window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("infra-capacity-economics", {"topics":[{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/gpu-infrastructure","sources":["Official docs: NVIDIA GPU Operator","Official docs: NVIDIA Container Toolkit","Official docs: Kubernetes (device plugins and extended resources)"],"why":"- **GPUs break CPU-server assumptions.** CPU nodes are interchangeable: any node runs any container, the\n  OS hides the hardware, the scheduler places work anywhere. A GPU is a separate computer on PCIe with\n  its own memory, instruction set and driver, and the host kernel does not virtualise it like CPU time\n  and RAM.\n- **Four things must line up.** An inference container only works if the node's driver, the CUDA toolkit\n  in the image, the device plugin that advertised the card and the scheduler's placement all agree.\n- **The naive approach collapses at the second node.** \"Install the driver, `pip install torch`, run it\"\n  works on one laptop. Then driver versions drift, images pull CUDA builds the driver cannot run, pods\n  land on nodes without a card, and two pods silently share one card and OOM each other.\n- **Nobody can see utilisation.** Without exported GPU metrics, nobody can say whether the fleet is 30%\n  or 90% busy.\n- **Every idle hour is expensive.** One high-end accelerator costs more per hour than a rack of web\n  servers, so every misplaced pod is a capacity bug. GPU infrastructure is the set of layers that turn a\n  card into a reliable, schedulable, observable, billable resource.","how":"Walk the stack bottom-up. Each layer answers one question; sizing, bandwidth, card selection,\nserverless and autoscaling each have their own lesson.\n\n| # | Layer | Question it answers | Typical failure |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Accelerator | What does the compute look like? | Model does not fit; decode slow |\n| 2 | Node | What feeds the card? | Slow weight loads, CPU-bound serving |\n| 3 | Driver and CUDA | Will the code run at all? | \"driver version is insufficient\" |\n| 4 | Container runtime | How does a container see the device? | `nvidia-smi` fails in the pod |\n| 5 | Scheduling | Which workload gets which card? | Pods on wrong node, shared-card OOM |\n| 6 | Procurement | Who pays, and how? | \"insufficient capacity\", quota wait |\n| 7 | Observability | Is it actually busy? | \"100% utilised\" while idle; XID faults |\n\n### 1. The accelerator: what does the compute look like?\n- **Shape.** Thousands of simple cores grouped into streaming multiprocessors (SMs), each with tensor\n  cores for matrix maths, fed by on-package memory (HBM on A100/H100, GDDR6 on L4).\n- **Two numbers dominate.** Memory capacity decides whether the model fits (see *GPU sizing*); memory\n  bandwidth bounds how fast decode runs (see *GPU utilization & memory bandwidth*).\n- **Form factor.** SXM modules sit on a baseboard with NVLink/NVSwitch between GPUs and run at higher\n  power. PCIe cards slot into ordinary servers, draw less power, and talk to each other only over PCIe\n  unless bridged. Which card to buy is *A100 / H100 / L4 selection*.\n\n### 2. The node: what feeds the card?\nA GPU node is a host with one to eight accelerators plus the CPU, RAM, storage and network that keep\nthem busy. Each can be the bottleneck:\n- **CPU and host RAM.** Tokenisation, request handling, data loading and the engine's scheduler run on the\n  CPU. Host RAM stages weights on the way to the device, so a 64 GB host struggles to load a 140 GB\n  checkpoint.\n- **Storage.** Weights are read from disk at start-up: 140 GB over a 100 MB/s network volume takes about\n  23 minutes; local NVMe at a few GB/s takes under a minute. Cold-start time is mostly a storage decision.\n- **Intra-node interconnect.** Tensor parallelism exchanges activations between GPUs every layer. NVLink\n  gives hundreds of GB/s per GPU (NVIDIA quotes 900 GB/s for H100 SXM); PCIe Gen4 x16 gives roughly\n  32 GB/s per direction. Sharding across PCIe-only cards works but leaves bandwidth on the table.\n- **Network.** Multi-node training needs RDMA (InfiniBand or RoCE) so gradient all-reduce does not stall in\n  the host networking stack. Serving needs it only when one replica spans machines (a model too large for\n  one eight-GPU node, or disaggregated prefill and decode shipping KV caches between nodes).\n- **Otherwise Ethernet is enough,** *because* only prompts and tokens cross the network when each request\n  stays inside one node.\n\n### 3. Driver and CUDA: will the code run at all?\n- **One driver per node.** The kernel-mode NVIDIA driver is the only piece that touches hardware.\n- **Everything else ships in the image.** CUDA runtime, cuBLAS, cuDNN, NCCL and the PyTorch build must be\n  versions the node's driver supports.\n- **Backward, not forward, compatible.** Drivers run older CUDA runtimes, not newer ones. \"CUDA driver\n  version is insufficient for CUDA runtime version\" means the image is newer than the node.\n- **Two escape hatches, neither a plan.** CUDA minor-version compatibility lets, say, a 12.x runtime run on\n  an older 12.x driver with some features (PTX JIT) missing. The `cuda-compat` forward-compatibility\n  package works only on data-centre GPUs.\n- **Rule.** Pin both, and upgrade drivers fleet-wide *before* bumping base images.\n\n### 4. Container runtime: how does a container see the device?\n1. Containers do not include the driver.\n2. When a container requests GPUs, the NVIDIA Container Toolkit's runtime hook mounts the device files\n   (`/dev/nvidia*`) and the driver's user-space libraries in at start.\n3. That is why a GPU image is portable across driver versions, and why `nvidia-smi` works inside a\n   container that never installed it.\n\n### 5. Scheduling: which workload gets which card?\n- **Integer extended resource.** A device plugin advertises GPUs as `nvidia.com/gpu` in integer units.\n  Extended resources cannot be overcommitted and requests must equal limits.\n- **Why no overcommit.** The kubelet treats an extended resource as an opaque count it cannot enforce.\n  There is no cgroup-style controller that guarantees a container a fraction of a GPU or caps its device\n  memory, as CFS quotas and memory cgroups do for CPU and RAM.\n- **Time-slicing still happens.** The GPU does time-slice between processes by default; what is missing\n  is enforced shares and isolation.\n- **GPU Operator.** Packages driver, toolkit, device plugin, feature discovery (node labels such as the\n  card model) and the DCGM metrics exporter as one install.\n- **Placement.** Taints keep ordinary pods off expensive nodes; labels let a pod target the right tier.\n\nSharing one card between small workloads:\n\n| Mechanism | How it shares | Memory isolation | Fault isolation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| MIG | Hardware-partitions an A100 or H100 into up to seven instances with separate memory | Hardware-enforced | Yes |\n| Time-slicing | Plugin advertises N virtual GPUs sharing one memory pool | None | No |\n| MPS | Processes run kernels concurrently on one card | Opt-in per-client cap on Volta and later | No: one shared fault domain |\n\nOnly MIG gives hardware-enforced memory and fault isolation, so only MIG reliably protects one tenant\nfrom another's OOM.\n\n### 6. Procurement: who pays, and how?\n- **Same card, many contracts.** On-demand instances, reserved or committed-use discounts,\n  spot/preemptible capacity, bare-metal leases, GPU-specialist clouds, or serverless per-second billing.\n- **Quotas gate all of them.** A fresh cloud account often cannot launch a single H100 without a request.\n- **Lead time is capacity planning.** Days for quota, weeks for reservations. The economics live in *cost\n  modeling*, *TCO* and *serverless GPUs*.\n\n### 7. Observability: is it actually busy?\n- **DCGM exporter.** Publishes per-GPU utilisation, memory use, temperature, power, ECC and XID error\n  counts to Prometheus. Without it the fleet is a black box.\n- **\"100% utilised\" is often a lie.** It frequently means one kernel at a time on an otherwise idle chip,\n  which the utilization lesson unpacks.\n- **GPUs fail in ways the OS does not hide.** Uncorrectable ECC errors, retired memory rows, a card that\n  drops off the PCIe bus. The driver logs these as XID events in the kernel log; DCGM exports them.\n- **Why it matters for scheduling.** Kubernetes still sees the device plugin's advertised count, *because*\n  nothing else tells it the card is bad. Unless a health check cordons the node, the scheduler keeps\n  placing pods on a card that will crash them.\n- **Automated cordon-on-XID** (or the GPU Operator's health checks) is part of the stack, not an optional\n  extra.","example":"### Deployment that fails fast on a bad node\nAsks for one GPU on a node labelled with a card model and tolerates the GPU taint.\n- The init container runs `nvidia-smi`, which only succeeds if the toolkit mounted the driver libraries.\n- Official `nvidia/cuda` images set `NVIDIA_REQUIRE_CUDA`, so the toolkit refuses to start the container\n  on a driver too old for that CUDA version.\n- Use the same CUDA version as the server image, and a bad node fails the pod before the server tries to\n  load 16 GB of weights.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: embedder\nspec:\n  replicas: 1\n  selector: { matchLabels: { app: embedder } }\n  template:\n    metadata: { labels: { app: embedder } }\n    spec:\n      nodeSelector:\n        nvidia.com/gpu.product: NVIDIA-L4        # label written by GPU feature discovery\n      tolerations:\n      - key: nvidia.com/gpu\n        operator: Exists\n        effect: NoSchedule\n      initContainers:\n      - name: gpu-check\n        image: nvidia/cuda:12.4.1-base-ubuntu22.04   # same CUDA version as the server image\n        command: [\"nvidia-smi\"]                   # fails the pod if the device is not visible\n        resources: { limits: { nvidia.com/gpu: 1 } }\n      containers:\n      - name: server\n        image: ghcr.io/example/embedder:1.4       # ships its own CUDA runtime, never the driver\n        resources:\n          limits: { nvidia.com/gpu: 1 }           # integer; requests are forced equal to limits\n          requests: { cpu: \"4\", memory: 16Gi }    # CPU for tokenisation, RAM to stage weights\n        volumeMounts:\n        - { name: weights, mountPath: /models, readOnly: true }\n      volumes:\n      - name: weights\n        persistentVolumeClaim: { claimName: model-weights }   # NVMe-backed, not object storage\n```\n\n### Cold-start calculator: storage decides ready-time\nWeight loading time is size divided by read throughput. Plug in your checkpoint and volume.\n\n```python\ndef load_minutes(weights_gb: float, read_mb_per_s: float) -> float:\n    return weights_gb * 1000 / read_mb_per_s / 60\n\ncheckpoint_gb = 140                       # e.g. a 70B model in fp16\nfor volume, mb_s in [(\"network volume\", 100), (\"local NVMe (illustrative)\", 3000)]:\n    print(f\"{volume:28s} {load_minutes(checkpoint_gb, mb_s):5.1f} min\")\n# network volume                23.3 min\n# local NVMe (illustrative)      0.8 min\n```\n\n### Worked triage: \"the GPU is slow\" ticket, layer by layer\n| Symptom seen | Layer | Check | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CrashLoopBackOff only on some nodes | Driver/CUDA | Logs say \"driver version is insufficient\" | Upgrade driver fleet-wide, or pin to upgraded nodes |\n| Running-but-not-Ready for tens of minutes | Node storage | Weight read throughput | Local NVMe or pre-staged weights |\n| CUDA OOM in one deployment when another is busy | Scheduling | Two models time-sliced on one card | MIG, or separate cards |\n| \"95% busy\" but low tokens/s | Observability | SM activity, not GPU-Util | Batch more, fix the bottleneck |\n| \"unknown error\" or illegal-memory-access on one node | Hardware | XID entries in that node's kernel log | Cordon and drain the node |\n| Queue grows 20 minutes after scale-up | Procurement | Cloud says \"insufficient capacity\" | Reservations, quota headroom, fallback region |","patterns":["**Fail-fast GPU init container.** Use on every GPU workload. Run `nvidia-smi` from an `nvidia/cuda` base image with the same CUDA version as the server, as in the example above. Monitor init-container failure counts per node: a cluster of failures marks a mis-provisioned node pool.","**Driver first, image second.** Use whenever you bump a base image. Roll the new driver across every GPU node pool, verify, then release the image with the newer CUDA runtime. Monitor driver version per node (feature discovery labels or the node image version) and block the rollout while versions are mixed.","**Cordon on XID.** Use on any fleet larger than a few nodes. Alert on the DCGM exporter's XID error metric (`DCGM_FI_DEV_XID_ERRORS`) and cordon and drain the node automatically, or enable the GPU Operator's health checks. Monitor how many nodes are cordoned so lost capacity is visible to the autoscaler and on-call.","**Taint GPU nodes, select by label.** Use on any shared cluster. Taint GPU nodes with `NoSchedule` on the `nvidia.com/gpu` key, add the matching toleration only to GPU workloads, and select the card tier with the `nvidia.com/gpu.product` label. Monitor for CPU-only pods on GPU nodes.","**Weights on fast local storage.** Use when pod ready-time matters for autoscaling. Mount an NVMe-backed volume or pre-stage weights on the node instead of pulling from object storage at start. Monitor pod ready-time from scheduling to readiness.","**Measure SM activity, not GPU-Util.** Dashboard DCGM's SM activity profiling field (`DCGM_FI_PROF_SM_ACTIVE`; enable it in the exporter's metric list if it is not on) next to tokens per second. Monitor GPU hours used versus purchased."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"kubectl apply: pod asks for nvidia.com/gpu: 1\"] --> B[\"Scheduler: taints, labels, free GPU count\"]\n    B --> C[\"Node with card and NVIDIA driver\"]\n    C --> D[\"Container Toolkit mounts driver libs and /dev/nvidia*\"]\n    D --> E{\"Image CUDA runtime supported by driver?\"}\n    E -->|\"no\"| F[\"Crash: driver version is insufficient\"]\n    E -->|\"yes\"| G[\"Load weights from storage\"]\n    G --> H[\"Engine serves first token\"]\n    H --> I[\"DCGM exporter: SM activity, XID errors\"]\n    I -.->|\"XID fault: cordon node\"| B","caption":"Follow one GPU pod from `kubectl apply` to its first token; each box is a layer that can fail on its own,\nand the dotted edge is the health loop that keeps bad cards out of the pool."},"interview":[{"q":"Walk me through what happens between kubectl apply and the first token on a GPU pod.","a":"- **Scheduling:** the device plugin advertises `nvidia.com/gpu` as an integer resource; the scheduler\n  picks a node with a free card that the pod's tolerations and label selectors allow.\n- **Runtime:** the NVIDIA Container Toolkit mounts the node's driver libraries and `/dev/nvidia*` into the\n  container, so the image's CUDA runtime must be one that driver supports.\n- **Start-up:** the server reads weights from storage into host RAM and then GPU memory, so storage\n  throughput sets ready-time; only then does it serve the first token."},{"q":"Why does Kubernetes hand out whole GPUs, and how would you share one card between small models?","a":"- **No enforcement primitive:** there is no cgroup-style controller for a GPU share or device memory cap,\n  so the kubelet treats GPUs as an opaque count with requests equal to limits.\n- **Sharing options:** MIG for hardware-isolated slices on A100 or H100; time-slicing or MPS for higher\n  packing without memory or fault isolation.\n- **Rule of thumb:** use MIG when tenants must not OOM each other; time-slice only workloads whose summed\n  memory footprint you have checked."},{"q":"A dashboard shows the fleet at 95% GPU utilisation but throughput is poor. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Question the metric:** `nvidia-smi` GPU-Util only says a kernel was running in the sample window, not\n  how much of the chip it used.\n- **Look at SM activity:** DCGM's SM activity profiling field next to tokens per second shows real\n  occupancy; a low value means the card is mostly idle.\n- **Then find the feeder:** small batches, CPU-bound tokenisation or request handling, or slow data\n  loading are the usual causes, fixed by batching more or adding host CPU."},{"q":"When would you run inference on PCIe cards instead of SXM nodes with NVLink?","a":"- **PCIe wins** when each model fits on one card: cards are cheaper, available singly and fit ordinary\n  servers, and only prompts and tokens cross the network.\n- **SXM wins** once a model must be tensor-parallel across cards, because NVLink gives hundreds of GB/s\n  per GPU versus roughly 32 GB/s per direction for PCIe Gen4 x16.\n- **The cost of SXM** is fixed eight-card blocks and higher power, so small models waste much of the node."},{"q":"Your 140 GB checkpoint takes 23 minutes to become Ready. What do you change, and what does it buy?","a":"- **Diagnose:** 140 GB at 100 MB/s is about 23 minutes, so the network volume is the bottleneck, not the GPU.\n- **Fix:** move weights to local NVMe or pre-stage them on the node; at a few GB/s the load drops under a\n  minute.\n- **Why it matters:** autoscaling is only useful if new replicas are ready before the queue grows, so\n  ready-time is a capacity number, not a convenience."}],"tradeoffs":["**SXM/NVLink vs PCIe.** SXM nodes make multi-GPU sharding fast but come in fixed eight-card blocks. PCIe cards are cheaper and available singly, which wins until a model must be split across cards.","**Managed driver stack vs control.** A GPU Operator or managed node pool makes the driver stack someone else's problem but ties you to their driver cadence. Bare metal gives control and costs an ops team.","**Card sharing vs per-tenant performance.** MIG, time-slicing or MPS raises utilisation for small models but caps per-tenant performance and complicates scheduling. It stops paying off once one model saturates the card.","**Spot vs reliability.** Spot and preemptible capacity is far cheaper but can vanish mid-job. Fine for checkpointed training and batch inference, wrong for latency-SLO serving unless on-demand fallback exists."],"pitfalls":["**Image CUDA runtime newer than the node driver.** Symptom: CrashLoopBackOff on exactly the nodes that have not had the driver upgrade, with \"driver version is insufficient\" in the logs. Fix: driver first, image second.","**Loading weights from object storage or a slow network disk on every start.** Symptom: pods stuck in Running-but-not-Ready for tens of minutes while the readiness probe fails. Fix: NVMe-backed volumes.","**Time-slicing two models onto one card without checking combined memory.** Symptom: CUDA OOM errors in one deployment that correlate with traffic to another. Fix: sum both footprints, or use MIG.","**Judging fleet health by `nvidia-smi` GPU-Util.** Symptom: \"95% busy\" dashboards while tokens per second sit at a fraction of what the card can deliver. Fix: track DCGM SM activity.","**Leaving a node with a faulty GPU in the pool.** Symptom: CUDA \"unknown error\" or illegal-memory-access on one node while identical pods elsewhere run, with XID entries in that node's kernel log. Fix: cordon-on-XID.","**Treating quota and node lead time as instant.** Symptom: the autoscaler requests a node, the cloud answers \"insufficient capacity\", and the request queue grows for twenty minutes. Fix: plan quota and reservations ahead."],"when":"You do not choose whether to have GPU infrastructure; you choose how many layers to own.\n- **Buy the stack when:** the team is small and the workload is standard inference. Managed node pools\n  with the GPU Operator, or a serverless GPU platform, come with driver stack, device plugin and metrics\n  pre-wired.\n- **Own more layers when:** you need a specific NVLink topology, custom kernels or drivers, bare-metal\n  pricing, or on-prem placement.\n- **Measure three things:** pod ready-time (storage and image size), GPU hours used versus purchased\n  (procurement), and DCGM SM activity (whether \"busy\" is real).\n- **Day to day:** a CrashLoopBackOff you must attribute to the right layer.\n- **Interviews:** \"walk me through what happens between `kubectl apply` and the first token\"; naming each\n  layer and its failure mode is the expected answer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the pod spec, pins CUDA and driver versions, mounts weights from fast storage, and reads DCGM metrics to tell a real bottleneck from an idle card.","architect":"Decides which layers to own versus buy, the node shapes (SXM vs PCIe, sharing policy), and the procurement mix of reserved, on-demand and spot capacity.","fde":"Audits a customer's cluster layer by layer — driver, toolkit, device plugin, taints, metrics — before promising that a model will run there on day one."},"check":{"q":"After a base-image bump, new inference pods crash on half the cluster with \"CUDA driver version\nis insufficient\" and run fine on the other half. What happened, and how would you structure the fleet\nso it cannot recur?","a":"The image now carries a CUDA runtime newer than the kernel driver on the older half of the\nnodes; drivers support older runtimes, not newer ones, so only un-upgraded nodes fail. Immediate fix:\nroll the image back or pin the pods to upgraded nodes with a label. Structural fix: manage drivers with\nthe GPU Operator or node-pool images so every node runs one known version, label nodes with the driver\nversion, roll driver upgrades fleet-wide before base-image upgrades, and add an init container that\nruns `nvidia-smi` so a mismatch fails fast and visibly instead of after a long weight load. Capacity\nalso matters: if half the fleet is unusable, the autoscaler should be told, not left to discover it."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can `nvidia.com/gpu` not be overcommitted on Kubernetes the way CPU can?","options":["GPUs are too expensive for the scheduler to risk contention","There is no cgroup-style mechanism to enforce a fractional share or memory cap on a GPU, so whole devices are handed out","The NVIDIA driver forbids more than one process per card","Extended resources are only counted at node level, not pod level"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CPU is overcommittable because CFS quotas and cgroups can enforce each container's share. A GPU has no such host-side enforcement, so Kubernetes exposes it as an opaque integer extended resource with requests equal to limits. Multiple processes per card are possible (MPS, time-slicing) but without enforced shares or hard memory isolation; the driver does not forbid them."},{"q":"Which sharing mechanism protects one tenant from another tenant's out-of-memory error on the same card?","options":["Time-slicing configured in the device plugin","CUDA MPS","MIG hardware partitions","Setting memory requests equal to limits on both pods"],"answer":2,"explanation":"MIG partitions an A100 or H100 into instances with their own memory and compute slices. Time-slicing shares one memory pool with no limits; MPS shares the pool and the fault domain (its per-client memory cap is opt-in), so one process can starve or crash another. Pod memory limits govern host RAM only."},{"q":"A GPU container image contains the CUDA runtime but no driver. Where does the driver come from at run time?","options":["It is downloaded from the registry as a hidden image layer","PyTorch bundles a user-space driver in its wheel","The NVIDIA Container Toolkit mounts the host node's driver libraries and device files into the container","Kubernetes injects it through the device plugin's gRPC socket"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The kernel driver lives on the node and must match the kernel; the toolkit's runtime hook bind-mounts its user-space libraries and `/dev/nvidia*` into any container that requests GPUs. The device plugin only tells the scheduler which devices exist and which ones a container was allocated."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing","sources":["Rajbhandari et al., 2020 — ZeRO: Memory Optimizations Toward Training Trillion Parameter Models","Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Official docs: vLLM (engine arguments, gpu_memory_utilization)"],"why":"- **A GPU cannot page to disk.** When the device runs out of memory the engine does not slow down\n  gracefully; it throws a CUDA out-of-memory error and the process dies, often minutes into start-up\n  after loading most of a checkpoint.\n- **The quiet failure is over-buying.** A team provisions eight 80 GB cards for a model whose weights\n  occupy 16 GB, then wonders why the bill is high and throughput is low. Both failures come from treating\n  VRAM as a vibe instead of a sum.\n- **\"Parameters in billions ≈ GB\" is wrong both ways.** In fp16 every parameter is two bytes, so a\n  70-billion-parameter model needs 140 GB for weights alone, nearly twice the naive estimate.\n- **Weights are only the fixed part.** Every in-flight request grows a KV cache proportional to its\n  context length, prefill needs transient activation memory, and the CUDA context and engine allocations\n  take space before the first request arrives.\n- **Fine-tuning flips it again.** Gradients and optimizer state make a full fine-tune of that 70B model\n  need hundreds of gigabytes more than serving it. VRAM math writes each term down, with the model's real\n  shapes, before buying or promising anything.","how":"### The inference budget\n```\nVRAM        ≈ weights + KV cache + activations/workspace + runtime overhead\nweights      = n_params × bytes_per_param\nKV per token = 2 × n_layers × n_kv_heads × head_dim × bytes_per_element\nKV cache     = KV per token × tokens in flight (summed over all concurrent sequences)\n```\n\n| Precision | Bytes per parameter |\n|---|---|\n| fp32 | 4 |\n| fp16 / bf16 | 2 |\n| int8 / fp8 | 1 |\n| int4 | roughly 0.5 plus a few percent for scales and zero-points (call it 0.55) |\n\n- **The factor 2** in the KV term is one key and one value vector.\n- **`n_kv_heads`, not `n_heads`,** is why grouped-query attention matters: Llama-3 models use 8 KV heads\n  against 32 or 64 query heads, which cuts the cache 4–8× relative to full multi-head attention.\n- **Runtime overhead** is everything that is neither weights nor cache: the CUDA context, cuBLAS/cuDNN\n  workspaces, captured CUDA graphs and the engine's own buffers. Illustrative order of magnitude: around a\n  gigabyte per process, present before any request.\n- **Activations** during prefill scale with (tokens in the batch × hidden size) per layer and are\n  transient. Engines bound them by capping how many prompt tokens one forward pass may contain (chunked\n  prefill), so the practical rule is to leave headroom rather than compute them exactly.\n\n### How the engine spends the budget\n1. vLLM's `gpu_memory_utilization` (default 0.9) is the fraction of the card the engine may use,\n   *because* other processes and driver allocations need the rest.\n2. At start-up it profiles weights plus peak activations once.\n3. Everything remaining goes to KV-cache blocks.\n4. That space divided by KV-per-token is the total number of tokens the server can hold across all\n   concurrent requests: the real capacity number.\n\n### Two models, worked\n| Model | Params | Layers | KV heads | head_dim | bf16 weights | KV per token |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Llama-3-8B | about 8.0B | 32 | 8 | 128 | 16 GB | 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 131,072 bytes = 128 KB |\n| Llama-3-70B | about 70.6B | 80 | 8 | 128 | 141 GB | 2 × 80 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 327,680 bytes = 320 KB |\n\nFits, taking a 0.9 utilisation budget and ~1 GB overhead per card:\n\n| Setup | Arithmetic | KV room | Capacity |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 8B bf16 on a 24 GB L4 | 21.6 − 16 − 1 | ≈ 4.6 GB | ≈ 35k tokens ≈ eight or nine 4k-token conversations |\n| 8B bf16 on an 80 GB H100 | 72 − 16 − 1 | = 55 GB | ≈ 420k tokens ≈ a hundred 4k conversations; bandwidth, not capacity, now limits |\n| 70B bf16 on two 80 GB H100s | 144 − 141.2 − 2 | ≈ 0.8 GB | ≈ 2.4k tokens *total*: it \"fits\" but can barely serve one request |\n| 70B fp8 on the same two cards | 144 − 71 − 2 | = 71 GB | ≈ 220k tokens, a comfortable server |\n| 70B int4 (~39 GB) on one 80 GB card | 72 − 39 − 1 | = 32 GB | ≈ 100k tokens, with a quality cost that must be evaluated, not assumed |\n\n- **The 70B bf16 row** is the case the formula catches and intuition misses.\n- **Long context is the other surprise.** One 128k-token prompt on the 8B model is 128k × 128 KB = 16 GiB\n  (about 17 GB) of cache, as much as the weights, and a single such request can evict everything else.\n\n### The training budget\nFull fine-tuning with mixed precision and Adam keeps, per parameter (the accounting the ZeRO paper uses):\n\n| Term | Bytes per parameter |\n|---|---|\n| bf16 weights | 2 |\n| bf16 gradient | 2 |\n| fp32 master weight | 4 |\n| Adam first and second moments | 4 + 4 |\n| **Total** | **16** |\n\n- **8B full fine-tune ≈ 128 GB before activations,** so it does not fit one 80 GB card and must be sharded\n  (ZeRO/FSDP) across several.\n- **Activations** add batch × sequence × hidden per layer; attention without a fused kernel adds a\n  sequence-squared term.\n- **Gradient checkpointing** trades roughly a third more compute for storing only layer boundaries.\n- **LoRA** changes the equation *because* the base weights are frozen: they cost 2 bytes per parameter\n  with no gradient or optimizer state. Only the adapter (typically well under 1% of parameters) carries\n  the 16-byte burden, so the 8B model fine-tunes in roughly 16 GB plus activations.\n- **QLoRA** stores the frozen base in 4-bit, about 4.5 GB for 8B, which is what makes 24 GB cards viable.\n- **Eight-bit optimizers** (for example bitsandbytes' 8-bit Adam) store each Adam moment in one byte with\n  block-wise scales, cutting the optimizer term from 8 bytes per parameter to about 2.\n\n### Multi-GPU arithmetic\n- **Tensor parallelism** divides both weights and KV cache by the TP degree, while each card keeps its own\n  overhead. The head count must divide evenly (8 KV heads means TP up to 8 without replication).\n- **Pipeline parallelism** divides layers instead. It is used when cards lack NVLink or the model spans\n  nodes; each stage holds the KV cache only for its own layers.\n- **Either way,** \"fits on N cards\" means the per-card sum, not the total, is under budget.\n\n### Mixture-of-experts: size by total, not active, parameters\n- **Memory follows total parameters.** An MoE model routes each token through a few experts, so compute per\n  token follows the active count, but memory follows the total, *because* every expert must be resident\n  for whichever token picks it.\n- **Mixtral-8x7B:** about 46.7B total and 12.9B active parameters. In bf16 it needs ~93 GB for weights,\n  like a dense 47B model, even though each token does the arithmetic of a 13B one.\n- **Its KV cache follows the attention shape** (32 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128): 128 KB per token,\n  the same as Llama-3-8B.\n- **Bandwidth sits between the two.** At batch 1 a decode step reads only the active experts; at serving\n  batch sizes different tokens hit different experts and the step reads close to all of them.\n- **Rule:** size the card by total parameters; estimate latency with the batch you will run.\n\n### Order of decisions\n1. **Precision first,** because it halves or quarters the dominant term.\n2. **Card and count** (capacity).\n3. **`max_model_len`** (the worst-case single request).\n4. **Concurrency:** what remains ÷ KV per token ÷ typical context.\n\nReverse the order and you will size the card to a concurrency figure that the precision choice later\ninvalidates.","example":"### Planner that reproduces the worked numbers\nA twenty-line planner. It uses decimal gigabytes (1e9) to match how vendors quote card memory; engines\nreport GiB, so expect a 7% difference in their logs.\n\n```python\nGB = 1e9\n\ndef kv_per_token(n_layers, n_kv_heads, head_dim, bytes_el=2):\n    return 2 * n_layers * n_kv_heads * head_dim * bytes_el        # one K and one V per layer\n\ndef plan(name, params_b, n_layers, n_kv_heads, head_dim, vram_gb, n_gpus=1,\n         bytes_w=2, util=0.9, overhead_gb=1.0, ctx=4096):\n    weights = params_b * GB * bytes_w\n    budget = vram_gb * GB * util * n_gpus                       # engine's usable share\n    kv_room = budget - weights - overhead_gb * GB * n_gpus\n    tok = kv_per_token(n_layers, n_kv_heads, head_dim)\n    tokens = max(kv_room, 0) / tok\n    print(f\"{name:26s} weights={weights/GB:6.1f}GB KV/tok={tok/1024:.0f}KB \"\n          f\"KV room={kv_room/GB:5.1f}GB ~{tokens:,.0f} tok ≈ {tokens/ctx:.0f} seqs@{ctx}\")\n\nplan(\"8B bf16 / L4 24GB\",       8.0, 32, 8, 128, 24)             # ~35k tok, ~9 seqs\nplan(\"8B bf16 / H100 80GB\",     8.0, 32, 8, 128, 80)             # ~420k tok, ~100 seqs\nplan(\"70B bf16 / 2xH100\",      70.6, 80, 8, 128, 80, n_gpus=2)   # ~2.4k tok: unusable\nplan(\"70B fp8 / 2xH100\",       70.6, 80, 8, 128, 80, n_gpus=2, bytes_w=1)     # ~220k tok\nplan(\"70B int4 / 1xH100\",      70.6, 80, 8, 128, 80, bytes_w=0.55)            # ~100k tok\n```\n\n### Fine-tuning budget for the 8B model, four ways\nSame model, four training set-ups, using the per-parameter accounting above. All figures exclude\nactivations, which you add on top and control with batch size, sequence length and checkpointing.\n\n| Set-up | Bytes per parameter (state) | State for 8B | Fits one 80 GB card? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Full fine-tune, mixed-precision Adam | 2 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 16 | ~128 GB | No: shard with ZeRO/FSDP |\n| Full fine-tune, 8-bit Adam | 2 + 2 + 4 + 2 = 10 | ~80 GB | No: no room left for activations |\n| LoRA, bf16 base | 2 (base frozen) + adapter | ~16 GB + adapter | Yes, with room for activations |\n| QLoRA, 4-bit base | ~0.55 (base frozen) + adapter | ~4.5 GB + adapter | Yes; also viable on a 24 GB card |\n\n### Measuring the real peak during a training step\nEstimates miss allocator fragmentation and lazily allocated optimizer state, so measure one step.\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\ndef peak_step_gb(model, batch, optimizer):\n    torch.cuda.reset_peak_memory_stats()\n    loss = model(**batch).loss\n    loss.backward()\n    optimizer.step()               # Adam allocates its moments here, on the first step\n    optimizer.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\n    torch.cuda.synchronize()\n    return torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated() / 1e9\n\n# Run it twice: the first call includes optimizer-state allocation, the second shows steady state.\n# Compare with the table above; a large gap is activations or fragmentation.\n```","patterns":["**Read the capacity line from the engine log.** Use on every deployment change. vLLM logs the KV-cache size it allocated (in tokens or blocks) at start-up; compare it with the planner's estimate. Monitor the gap: a large shortfall means an overhead term (CUDA graphs, a co-located process) you missed.","**Cap the worst case explicitly.** Use whenever traffic mixes short chats and long documents. Set `--max-model-len` to the longest request you will accept and `--max-num-seqs` to the concurrency the KV room supports. Monitor KV-cache usage and preemption counts in the engine's metrics.","**Quantise before adding cards.** Use when weights leave too little KV room, as in the 70B bf16 on two cards case. Try fp8 weights (vLLM `--quantization fp8`) and an fp8 KV cache (`--kv-cache-dtype fp8`), then rerun your eval set. Monitor task quality alongside the gain in resident tokens.","**Leave headroom on shared cards.** Use when anything else runs on the GPU (a sidecar, a second model, a profiler). Lower `gpu_memory_utilization` below the 0.9 default rather than raising it. Monitor device memory used per process and OOM restarts.","**Measure one training step before a long run.** Use before any fine-tune longer than an hour. Run a single forward, backward and optimizer step at the real batch and sequence length and record `torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()`. Monitor that peak against card memory, keeping headroom."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Model config and target context\"] --> B[\"Pick precision: bf16, fp8, int4\"]\n    B --> C[\"Weights = params x bytes\"]\n    C --> D[\"Pick card and count, per-card budget\"]\n    D --> E[\"KV room = budget - weights - overhead\"]\n    E --> F{\"KV room holds max_model_len?\"}\n    F -->|\"no\"| B\n    F -->|\"yes\"| G[\"Tokens = KV room / KV per token\"]\n    G --> H[\"Concurrency = tokens / typical context\"]\n    H --> I{\"Meets target?\"}\n    I -->|\"no: quantise or add cards\"| B\n    I -->|\"yes\"| J[\"Set engine limits and verify start-up log\"]","caption":"Read it top to bottom: each decision feeds the next, and a failed fit loops back to precision or card\ncount rather than to wishful concurrency targets."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is \"a 70B model needs about 70 GB\" the wrong way to size a GPU?","a":"- **Bytes, not parameters:** bf16 stores two bytes per parameter, so 70B needs about 140 GB for weights\n  alone; only int8 or fp8 gets near the naive figure.\n- **Weights are the fixed part:** KV cache grows with every in-flight token, plus activations and roughly\n  a gigabyte of runtime overhead per process.\n- **So the right answer is a sum** with the model's real shapes, checked per card."},{"q":"Derive the KV cache per token for Llama-3-8B and turn it into a concurrency figure on a 24 GB L4.","a":"- **Per token:** 2 × 32 layers × 8 KV heads × 128 head_dim × 2 bytes = 131,072 bytes, 128 KB.\n- **Room:** 0.9 × 24 = 21.6 GB, minus 16 GB of bf16 weights and ~1 GB overhead, leaves ≈ 4.6 GB.\n- **Capacity:** 4.6 GB ÷ 128 KB ≈ 35k tokens, so about eight or nine 4k-token conversations; int4\n  weights would roughly triple that."},{"q":"A 70B bf16 model loads on two 80 GB cards but the server crawls. What is going on and what do you do?","a":"- **Diagnose:** 144 GB usable minus 141.2 GB weights and 2 GB overhead leaves ≈ 0.8 GB, about 2.4k\n  tokens of KV cache in total, so requests queue or get preempted.\n- **Fix options:** fp8 weights leave ≈ 71 GB for ≈ 220k tokens; or move to four cards with TP=4.\n- **Verify** with the engine's start-up capacity line, not with \"it loaded\"."},{"q":"When would you choose LoRA or QLoRA over a full fine-tune, and when not?","a":"- **Choose adapters** when memory is the constraint: an 8B full fine-tune needs ~128 GB of state, LoRA\n  about 16 GB plus activations, QLoRA about 4.5 GB, which fits a 24 GB card.\n- **Pay for full fine-tuning** only when an adapter measurably underperforms on your eval, because it needs\n  ZeRO/FSDP sharding across several cards.\n- **QLoRA's cost** is slower steps from dequantisation, on top of a smaller parameter update."},{"q":"How do you size memory for a mixture-of-experts model like Mixtral-8x7B?","a":"- **Memory by total parameters:** about 46.7B total means ~93 GB of bf16 weights, because every expert\n  must be resident.\n- **Compute by active parameters:** 12.9B active per token, so arithmetic looks like a 13B model.\n- **Bandwidth depends on batch:** batch 1 reads only active experts, serving batches read close to all of\n  them, so benchmark latency at the batch you will run."}],"tradeoffs":["**Lower weight precision vs quality and speed.** It frees memory for cache and raises concurrency, but int4 costs measurable quality on some tasks and dequantisation kernels can slow decode. It pays when memory, not quality, is the binding constraint.","**Higher `gpu_memory_utilization` vs slack.** More KV blocks, but less slack for activation spikes and co-located processes; push it only on a card nothing else touches.","**More tensor-parallel cards vs communication.** It multiplies KV capacity and bandwidth, yet adds per-layer communication and per-card overhead. It stops helping once the model already fits with KV headroom.","**LoRA and QLoRA vs update capacity.** They make fine-tuning fit small cards at the cost of a smaller parameter update and, for QLoRA, slower steps from dequantisation. Full fine-tuning is only worth its multi-card cost when the adapter measurably underperforms."],"pitfalls":["**Counting weights and forgetting overhead and activations.** Symptom: an OOM at start-up or on the first long prompt even though \"the model fits\". Fix: add the overhead and headroom terms.","**Sizing concurrency on the average context instead of `max_model_len`.** Symptom: sudden request preemption or queueing whenever a few long documents arrive together. Fix: size the worst case first.","**Assuming a fine-tune needs the same memory as serving.** Symptom: an OOM in the first optimizer step after the forward pass succeeded, *because* Adam state is allocated lazily. Fix: use the 16-byte rule.","**Dividing total memory across cards instead of checking per-card budgets with TP.** Symptom: one rank OOMing while the others wait on NCCL. Fix: check the per-card sum.","**Sizing an MoE model by its active parameter count.** Symptom: an OOM at weight load on a card the \"13B-class\" label said was ample. Fix: size by total parameters.","**Treating int4 as \"half of fp16\".** Symptom: a card that fits the weights but throughput below the fp16 figure, *because* dequantisation dominates at the batch sizes used. Fix: benchmark at real batch."],"when":"- **Run the arithmetic when:** a model, card, precision, context limit or concurrency target changes,\n  and before any procurement conversation.\n- **Inputs are public:** parameter count and layer/head shapes from the model config, memory from the\n  card datasheet.\n- **Reach for it when someone asks:** \"how many users can this card serve\", \"why did we OOM at 2 a.m.\" or\n  \"can we fine-tune this on what we have\".\n- **Measure:** the engine's reported KV-block count against your estimate; a large gap means an overhead\n  term you missed.\n- **Interviews:** deriving bytes-per-token from the config and turning it into a concurrency figure, then\n  adjusting for precision and TP, is the expected depth for both engineering and architecture roles.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Computes weights and KV per token from the model config before setting `max_model_len`, `gpu_memory_utilization` and batch limits, and validates against the engine's start-up log.","architect":"Owns the fit table — which models run on which card tier at which precision — and uses it to size clusters and to decide when quantisation or TP is mandatory.","fde":"Translates a customer's \"we have four A100s\" into which models and context lengths are realistic, and shows the arithmetic when a 70B request will not fit as asked."},"check":{"q":"You have one 80 GB card and a customer wants Llama-3-70B serving twenty concurrent 8k-token\nchats. Walk through whether it fits and what you would change.","a":"bf16 weights are 141 GB, so no. fp8 weights are 71 GB; with a 72 GB budget at 0.9 utilisation\nand ~1 GB overhead that leaves nothing for cache, so still no. int4 is about 39 GB, leaving ~32 GB\nfor KV at 320 KB per token, roughly 100k tokens; twenty chats at 8k need 160k, so about twelve fit.\nOptions: quantise the KV cache to 8-bit, doubling capacity to ~200k tokens, which meets the target if\nquality evaluation passes; cap concurrency at twelve and queue; or add a second card and run fp8 with\nTP=2 for ~220k tokens and better throughput. A strong answer also notes that twenty concurrent 8k\ncontexts will be bandwidth-bound on one card, so latency targets need checking too."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a full fine-tune need roughly eight times the memory of serving the same model in bf16?","options":["Training must keep a copy of every activation for the whole dataset","Gradients, fp32 master weights and two Adam moments add about 14 bytes per parameter on top of the 2-byte weights","Backpropagation requires the weights in fp64 for numerical stability","The KV cache must be stored for every training sequence simultaneously"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Mixed-precision Adam keeps bf16 weights and gradients plus fp32 master weights and two fp32 moments, 16 bytes per parameter versus 2 for inference, before activations. Training does not use a KV cache and master weights are fp32, not fp64."},{"q":"Llama-3-70B in bf16 (141 GB) is loaded on two 80 GB cards with tensor parallelism. What is the practical problem?","options":["The head count is not divisible by two, so TP fails","NCCL cannot shard models larger than one card's memory","After weights and overhead only about a gigabyte remains for KV cache, so the server can hold only a few thousand tokens","bf16 is not supported across tensor-parallel ranks"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The usable budget is about 144 GB at 0.9 utilisation; weights take 141 GB and overhead about 2 GB, leaving under a gigabyte, roughly 2.4k tokens of cache at 320 KB per token. The model technically loads but cannot serve real traffic, which is why fp8 or four cards are the usual choices."},{"q":"Which single change most increases the number of concurrent 4k-token requests an 8B bf16 model can hold on a 24 GB card?","options":["Raising the number of CPU threads for tokenisation","Switching from grouped-query to multi-head attention","Quantising the weights to int4, freeing roughly 11 GB for KV cache","Increasing `max_model_len` to 32k"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With ~4.5 GB of cache room, int4 weights (~4.4 GB instead of 16 GB) more than triple the space for KV blocks. Multi-head attention would multiply KV per token by four and reduce concurrency; a larger `max_model_len` changes the worst case per request, not the total capacity."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","sources":["Williams, Waterman & Patterson, 2009 — Roofline: An Insightful Visual Performance Model for Multicore Architectures","Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Chowdhery et al., 2022 — PaLM: Scaling Language Modeling with Pathways (model FLOPs utilization)","Official docs: NVIDIA DCGM (profiling metrics)"],"why":"- **The spec sheet and the dashboard disagree.** Teams buy GPUs for their FLOPs, then an H100 serving a\n  chat model produces tokens at a rate that would embarrass its spec sheet, while the dashboard shows\n  98% utilisation.\n- **For decode the GPU is a memory device.** Every decode step must stream every weight byte from HBM\n  into the compute units to produce one token per sequence. With one sequence in flight each byte is used\n  for a single multiply-add, and the tensor cores sit idle most of the cycle, waiting on memory.\n- **Both naive fixes waste money.** \"Utilisation is high, so we need more GPUs\" replicates idle hardware.\n  \"Buy the card with the most TFLOPs\" buys the wrong axis.\n- **The roofline asks the right question.** How many operations does this workload do per byte it moves\n  (its arithmetic intensity), and is that above or below the point where the card switches from\n  bandwidth-limited to compute-limited?\n- **One ratio explains a lot.** Why batching multiplies throughput almost for free, why quantisation\n  speeds decode even when the math is not faster, why prefill and decode differ, and which utilisation\n  metric to trust. Without it capacity planning is guesswork dressed up as a dashboard.","how":"### The roofline\nA kernel's attainable throughput is the lower of two ceilings:\n```\nattainable FLOP/s = min( peak FLOP/s , arithmetic intensity × memory bandwidth )\narithmetic intensity = FLOPs performed / bytes moved from memory   (FLOP per byte)\nridge point          = peak FLOP/s / bandwidth                      (FLOP per byte)\n```\n- **Below the ridge** the kernel is memory-bound and runs at `intensity × bandwidth`.\n- **Above it** the kernel is compute-bound and runs near peak.\n\nRidge points from NVIDIA's datasheet figures (dense bf16 tensor throughput and memory bandwidth):\n\n| Card | Peak bf16 | Bandwidth | Ridge (FLOP/byte) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A100 80 GB SXM | 312 TFLOP/s | 2.0 TB/s | ≈ 156 |\n| H100 SXM | 989 TFLOP/s | 3.35 TB/s | ≈ 295 |\n| L4 | 121 TFLOP/s | 0.3 TB/s | ≈ 400 (bandwidth small relative to compute) |\n\nThe number to remember is \"hundreds\": a workload must do hundreds of operations per byte before the\ntensor cores are the bottleneck.\n\n### Decode intensity: why batch size is the lever\n1. **Per step, each weight is multiplied by one activation and accumulated:** two FLOPs per parameter per\n   sequence.\n2. **In bf16 each parameter is two bytes,** read once per step regardless of how many sequences share it.\n3. **So a batch of *B* sequences** does about `2 × B` FLOPs per 2 bytes in the dense layers, an\n   intensity of roughly *B* FLOP/byte.\n4. **At *B* = 1** that is 1 FLOP/byte against a ridge of ~300: the H100 uses well under 1% of its\n   arithmetic, limited entirely by how fast HBM can stream 16 GB of 8B-model weights.\n5. **Doubling the batch doubles tokens per second** at almost no per-token latency cost, until *B*\n   approaches the ridge or, usually sooner, the sequences' KV-cache reads rival the weight reads. This is\n   the whole case for continuous batching.\n\nThe lower bound on a decode step is `bytes read ÷ bandwidth`. For an 8B bf16 model:\n\n| Card | Arithmetic | Step floor | Single-stream ceiling |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| H100 | 16 GB ÷ 3.35 TB/s | ≈ 4.8 ms | at most ~200 tokens/s |\n| L4 at 300 GB/s | 16 GB ÷ 0.3 TB/s | ~53 ms | at most ~19 tokens/s |\n\nReal engines land below these bounds, but the ratio between cards holds, which is why decode speed\ntracks bandwidth rather than TFLOPs.\n\n### Attention does not batch the same way\n- **Weights are shared across sequences; KV caches are not.** Each sequence's attention reads its own\n  cache, so attention's bytes grow with *B* × context and its intensity stays low at any batch.\n- **The cache can dominate.** A hundred 4k-token sequences on the 8B model read ~54 GB of cache per step\n  (100 × 4096 × 128 KB) on top of 16 GB of weights.\n- **Byte-cutting levers:** grouped-query attention, KV quantisation and FlashAttention's tiling (which\n  avoids writing the full attention matrix to HBM) all exist to reduce those bytes.\n\n### Prefill is the other regime\n- **Each weight byte serves every prompt token** in the pass, so intensity is roughly the number of\n  tokens in the pass.\n- **A 2,000-token prompt** is far above the ridge and compute-bound.\n- **Consequence:** time-to-first-token scales with prompt length and TFLOPs, while time-per-output-token\n  scales with bandwidth, which is why engines schedule prefill and decode differently.\n\n### What the utilisation metrics measure\n| Metric | What it measures | Reading it |\n|---|---|---|\n| `nvidia-smi` **GPU-Util** (DCGM `DCGM_FI_DEV_GPU_UTIL`) | Fraction of the sample period in which *any* kernel was executing | One tiny kernel per millisecond reads as 100% |\n| DCGM **SM activity** (`DCGM_FI_PROF_SM_ACTIVE`) | Fraction of time at least one warp was resident on an SM | Better than GPU-Util |\n| **SM occupancy** (`DCGM_FI_PROF_SM_OCCUPANCY`) | How full the SMs were | |\n| **Tensor pipe active** (`DCGM_FI_PROF_PIPE_TENSOR_ACTIVE`) | Whether the matrix units did work | Low in decode-bound serving |\n| **DRAM active** (`DCGM_FI_PROF_DRAM_ACTIVE`) | Memory bandwidth in use | High in decode-bound serving |\n\n- **The signature:** a decode-bound server shows high DRAM activity and low tensor activity; a\n  prefill-heavy one shows the reverse.\n- **Model FLOPs utilisation (MFU):** observed tokens/s × FLOPs per token ÷ peak FLOP/s. The honest single\n  number for training and prefill. For decode it is naturally low, *because* the roofline caps a\n  memory-bound step far below peak FLOPs, so a low decode MFU is not by itself a problem.\n- **Model bandwidth utilisation (MBU):** bytes a step must read (weights plus every sequence's KV cache)\n  × steps per second ÷ peak bandwidth. The decode counterpart of MFU: how close the server is to its real\n  ceiling.\n- **Reading MBU:** high MBU with poor tokens/s means you need fewer bytes or bigger batches; low MBU means\n  something other than the GPU is in the way.\n\n### Other things that masquerade as GPU limits\n- **Kernel-launch overhead** when batches are tiny; CUDA graphs fix it.\n- **Python-side scheduling and tokenisation** that leave the device starving.\n- **Host-to-device copies** over PCIe.\n- **The tell:** low DRAM *and* low tensor activity together. The GPU is waiting on the host, and no card\n  upgrade will help.","example":"### Roofline estimator for decode\nTakes datasheet bandwidth and peak compute and the bytes a step must read (weights plus the KV cache of\nevery sequence in the batch). Reports the bandwidth-bound step time, the tokens/s ceiling and whether\nthe step is memory- or compute-bound. Outputs for an 8B bf16 model reproduce the figures above; they are\nupper bounds, not measurements.\n\n```python\nCARDS = {  # datasheet-level figures, rounded: (memory bandwidth B/s, dense bf16 FLOP/s)\n    \"L4\":       (300e9,  121e12),\n    \"A100-80\":  (2.0e12, 312e12),\n    \"H100-SXM\": (3.35e12, 989e12),\n}\n\ndef decode_step(card, params=8.0e9, bytes_per_param=2, batch=1, ctx=4096, kv_per_token=131072):\n    bw, peak = CARDS[card]\n    weight_bytes = params * bytes_per_param\n    kv_bytes = batch * ctx * kv_per_token              # every sequence reads its own cache\n    bytes_read = weight_bytes + kv_bytes\n    flops = 2 * params * batch                          # dense layers only; attention omitted\n    intensity = flops / bytes_read\n    t_mem, t_compute = bytes_read / bw, flops / peak\n    t = max(t_mem, t_compute)                           # roofline: the slower ceiling wins\n    ridge = peak / bw\n    print(f\"{card:9s} B={batch:4d} ctx={ctx:5d} read={bytes_read/1e9:6.1f} GB \"\n          f\"intensity={intensity:6.1f} ridge={ridge:4.0f}  step={t*1e3:6.1f} ms \"\n          f\"tok/s={batch/t:8.0f} bound={'memory' if t_mem >= t_compute else 'compute'}\")\n\ndecode_step(\"H100-SXM\", batch=1)                  # ~4.8 ms, ~200 tok/s, intensity ~1\ndecode_step(\"L4\", batch=1)                        # ~53 ms, ~19 tok/s\ndecode_step(\"H100-SXM\", batch=64)                 # weights amortised; KV now a large share\ndecode_step(\"H100-SXM\", batch=100, ctx=4096)      # ~54 GB of KV per step dominates\ndecode_step(\"H100-SXM\", batch=8, bytes_per_param=0.55)  # int4: fewer bytes, faster step\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: batch scaling with short vs long contexts\nOutput of the estimator above for the 8B bf16 model on an H100 SXM (upper bounds). Every row is still\nmemory-bound, so throughput is set by bytes read per step.\n\n| Batch | Context | Bytes read per step | Step floor | Aggregate ceiling |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 4096 | 16.5 GB | 4.9 ms | ~203 tok/s |\n| 8 | 4096 | 20.3 GB | 6.1 ms | ~1,321 tok/s |\n| 32 | 4096 | 33.2 GB | 9.9 ms | ~3,231 tok/s |\n| 64 | 4096 | 50.4 GB | 15.0 ms | ~4,257 tok/s |\n| 64 | 512 | 20.3 GB | 6.1 ms | ~10,564 tok/s |\n\n- **Batching pays most when contexts are short:** at 512 tokens, 64 sequences add only ~4 GB of KV reads,\n  so the ceiling rises about 50x over batch 1.\n- **At 4k contexts the cache eats the gain:** batch 64 reads three times the weight bytes, so per-sequence\n  latency rises and the ceiling grows only about 20x.","patterns":["**Dashboard profiling fields, not GPU-Util.** Use on every serving fleet. Enable `DCGM_FI_PROF_DRAM_ACTIVE`, `DCGM_FI_PROF_PIPE_TENSOR_ACTIVE` and `DCGM_FI_PROF_SM_ACTIVE` in the DCGM exporter's metric list and chart them next to tokens/s per card. Monitor the DRAM-vs-tensor signature to classify each service.","**Autoscale on queue depth or tokens/s.** Use instead of any utilisation-based scaling rule. Scale on the engine's waiting-requests gauge (vLLM exposes `vllm:num_requests_waiting`) or on tokens/s per card against a tested ceiling. Monitor per-card throughput after scale-out: flat means you replicated idle hardware.","**Raise the batch limit before buying cards.** Use when MBU is low and KV room allows. Increase the engine's concurrent-sequence cap (vLLM `--max-num-seqs`) in steps under a load test at target concurrency. Monitor time-per-output-token p95 and preemptions; stop when latency SLOs bend.","**Keep CUDA graphs on.** Use for latency-sensitive decode with small batches. vLLM captures CUDA graphs by default; avoid `--enforce-eager` outside debugging, because it brings kernel-launch overhead back. Monitor step time at batch 1 against the bandwidth floor.","**Track MBU in a benchmark per release.** Use whenever the engine, model or card changes. Run a fixed load test, compute bytes per step × steps per second ÷ peak bandwidth, and record it. Monitor regressions: a drop in MBU with unchanged hardware points at host-side or engine changes."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Decode slower than expected\"] --> B[\"Floor = bytes per step / bandwidth\"]\n    B --> C{\"Observed step time near floor?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes: bandwidth-bound\"| D[\"Batch more, quantise, faster memory\"]\n    C -->|\"no: far above floor\"| E[\"Check DCGM profiling fields\"]\n    E --> F{\"DRAM active high?\"}\n    F -->|\"yes, tensor low\"| G[\"KV traffic dominates: GQA, KV quant\"]\n    F -->|\"no, tensor low too\"| H[\"Host-bound: CUDA graphs, CPU, scheduling\"]\n    F -->|\"tensor high\"| I[\"Compute-bound: prefill-heavy or big batch\"]","caption":"The triage path for \"decode is slow\": compute the bandwidth floor first, then let the DCGM signals say\nwhether to batch, cut bytes, or look at the host."},"interview":[{"q":"Explain the roofline model and where LLM decode sits on it.","a":"- **Definition:** attainable FLOP/s is the minimum of peak compute and arithmetic intensity times memory\n  bandwidth; the ridge point is peak divided by bandwidth, in the hundreds of FLOP/byte on A100 and H100.\n- **Decode:** two FLOPs per two-byte bf16 weight per sequence gives an intensity of roughly the batch size,\n  so at small batches it is far below the ridge and memory-bound.\n- **Prefill:** intensity is roughly the prompt length, so it sits above the ridge and is compute-bound."},{"q":"Why would an L4 decode an 8B model much more slowly than an H100 even though both \"fit\" it?","a":"- **Floor per step:** bytes read divided by bandwidth; 16 GB at 300 GB/s is ~53 ms on an L4 versus about\n  4.8 ms at 3.35 TB/s on an H100.\n- **So single-stream ceilings** are roughly 19 versus 200 tokens/s, an 11x gap that tracks bandwidth.\n- **Capacity is not speed:** fitting in memory says nothing about tokens per second, so compare cards on\n  bandwidth for decode-heavy services."},{"q":"When does increasing batch size stop helping decode throughput?","a":"- **While memory-bound and weight-dominated,** each extra sequence reuses the same weight read, so tokens/s\n  grows almost linearly.\n- **It flattens** when per-sequence KV reads rival the weight bytes (long contexts) or when intensity nears\n  the ridge and the step becomes compute-bound.\n- **Then the levers change:** GQA, KV quantisation or shorter contexts cut bytes; past that point, more\n  cards or a faster card are the answer."},{"q":"How would you compute MBU for a running server, and what does a low value tell you?","a":"- **Formula:** bytes per step (weights plus every in-flight sequence's KV cache) times steps per second,\n  divided by peak bandwidth.\n- **Low MBU** means the GPU is not the bottleneck: look for host-side scheduling, tokenisation, kernel-launch\n  overhead or small batches.\n- **High MBU with poor tokens/s** means the card is near its ceiling, so reduce bytes per token or batch more."},{"q":"Your autoscaler adds GPUs whenever GPU-Util exceeds 80%. What is wrong with that policy?","a":"- **GPU-Util is a time fraction:** it reads near 100% whenever any kernel runs, even one tiny kernel on an\n  otherwise idle chip.\n- **So it scales on noise:** the fleet grows while per-card tokens/s stays flat and the bill climbs.\n- **Better signals:** queue depth or waiting requests, and tokens/s per card against a tested ceiling, with\n  DCGM profiling fields to explain why a card is slow."}],"tradeoffs":["**Larger batches vs per-sequence latency.** Tokens/s rises nearly linearly while memory-bound, but each sequence's latency grows once the step becomes compute-bound or KV traffic dominates. The flip is near the ridge point or when cache bytes exceed weight bytes.","**Weight quantisation vs dequantisation cost.** It cuts bytes per step and speeds decode, yet dequantisation kernels can erase the gain at large batches where compute, not bandwidth, is the limit.","**Speculative decoding vs wasted compute.** It converts spare compute into more tokens per weight read, which pays when decode is bandwidth-bound and acceptance rates are high, and costs throughput otherwise.","**Faster memory vs price.** A faster-memory card (H100 over A100) buys decode speed roughly in proportion to bandwidth, not price; for prefill-heavy or training workloads the FLOPs gap matters more."],"pitfalls":["**Reading `nvidia-smi` GPU-Util as \"busy\".** Symptom: a fleet at 95% utilisation that delivers a small fraction of the tokens/s the bandwidth bound predicts. Fix: chart DCGM profiling fields and MBU.","**Scaling out on utilisation instead of on queue depth or tokens/s.** Symptom: more cards, same per-card throughput, and a bill that doubled. Fix: scale on queue depth.","**Benchmarking at batch 1 and extrapolating.** Symptom: a capacity plan off by an order of magnitude, *because* batching was never measured. Fix: benchmark at target concurrency.","**Ignoring attention's share of memory traffic.** Symptom: throughput collapsing as contexts get long, even though weights never changed. Fix: count KV bytes per step.","**Blaming the GPU for host-side stalls.** Symptom: low DRAM *and* low tensor activity together, with a single Python process pegged at 100% CPU. Fix: profile the host before touching hardware."],"when":"- **Reach for the roofline when:** throughput is below expectation, when choosing between cards, or when\n  someone proposes \"more GPUs\" as the fix.\n- **Method:** compute the bytes a decode step must read, divide by bandwidth, and compare with observed\n  time-per-output-token.\n- **Close to the floor:** you are bandwidth-bound and need batching, quantisation or faster memory. Far\n  worse: look at the host, kernel launches or scheduling.\n- **Monitor:** DCGM SM activity, tensor activity and DRAM activity rather than GPU-Util, and track\n  tokens/s per card as the business metric.\n- **Interviews:** \"why is decode memory-bound and what does batching do to it\" is a standard question; the\n  strong answer derives the intensity of ~B FLOP/byte and names the ridge point.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Tunes batch limits, enables CUDA graphs and quantisation, and uses DCGM profiling metrics to prove whether a slow server is bandwidth-, compute- or host-bound.","architect":"Chooses cards by bandwidth for decode-heavy services and by FLOPs for training or prefill-heavy ones, and sets capacity targets in tokens/s per card rather than utilisation.","fde":"Shows a customer why their \"fully utilised\" cluster is idle in practice, and sizes the batching change that recovers throughput before recommending new hardware."},"check":{"q":"A customer's H100 serves an 8B bf16 model at 40 tokens/s per request with four concurrent\nrequests, and `nvidia-smi` shows 97% utilisation. They want to buy three more H100s. What do you tell\nthem?","a":"The bandwidth bound for one decode step is about 16 GB ÷ 3.35 TB/s ≈ 5 ms, so a single stream\ncould approach 200 tokens/s and a batch of four should deliver far more than 160 aggregate. At 40\ntokens/s per stream the step takes ~25 ms, five times the bound: ~16 GB read every 25 ms is about\n0.65 TB/s, an MBU near 20% of 3.35 TB/s. GPU-Util at 97% only means some\nkernel was always running. Check DCGM: low DRAM activity and low tensor activity point to host-side\nstalls, kernel-launch overhead or a misconfigured engine, which more cards will not fix. Raise\nconcurrency and batch size, enable CUDA graphs, and confirm the engine is using fused attention.\nBuy hardware only after per-card tokens/s approaches the bandwidth bound at the target batch."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does doubling the batch size roughly double decode throughput on a bandwidth-bound GPU?","options":["The GPU clocks up under heavier load","Weights are read from memory once per step and shared by every sequence in the batch, so the dominant cost is amortised","Larger batches compress the KV cache","Tensor cores only activate above a minimum batch size"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A decode step must stream all weights from HBM regardless of batch size; with more sequences each byte does more useful work, raising arithmetic intensity. KV traffic still grows per sequence, so the gain is not unlimited."},{"q":"Which DCGM signal most directly reveals that a serving GPU is memory-bandwidth-bound?","options":["High GPU-Util with low power draw","High SM occupancy","High DRAM activity together with low tensor-pipe activity","Low PCIe transmit throughput"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Bandwidth-bound decode keeps the memory system busy while the matrix units wait, so DRAM activity is high and tensor activity low. GPU-Util cannot distinguish that state from a fully compute-bound one."},{"q":"Prefill of a 3,000-token prompt and decoding of its reply run on the same GPU. Which statement is correct?","options":["Both phases are memory-bound because they read the same weights","Prefill is compute-bound because every weight serves thousands of tokens per pass, while decode is memory-bound at one token per sequence per step","Decode is compute-bound because it runs more steps","Prefill is memory-bound because it must write the whole KV cache"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Arithmetic intensity in prefill is roughly the number of tokens in the pass, far above the ridge point; in decode it is roughly the batch size, usually far below it. Writing the KV cache is small relative to the matrix multiplications over thousands of tokens."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/a100-h100-l4-selection","sources":["Official docs: NVIDIA A100, H100 and L4 datasheets","Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference"],"why":"- **Price and speed differ by an order of magnitude.** Three cards from the same vendor can differ that\n  much in price and in decode speed, and the cheapest per hour is frequently the most expensive per\n  token, or simply cannot run the model.\n- **Picking by hourly price fails.** Teams end up with L4s that cannot hold a 70B model or that deliver\n  15 tokens per second to a chat user.\n- **Picking by prestige fails too.** An 8B internal tool on an H100 idles 95% of the day. Neither error\n  shows up until the bill or the latency complaints arrive.\n- **\"Buy the newest card\" misreads the workload.** Inference is usually bound by memory capacity and\n  bandwidth, not by the compute that generation-over-generation marketing emphasises. Cost per token\n  depends on how full the card is, which depends on your traffic, not the card.\n- **\"Buy the cheapest card that fits\" confuses fitting with serving.** A card that holds the weights with\n  no KV room serves one user; 300 GB/s of bandwidth cannot reach interactive decode on anything beyond a\n  few billion parameters. Selection is a short procedure over four numbers — memory, bandwidth, price,\n  expected load — plus a feature checklist.","how":"### The cards, by the numbers that matter\nRounded figures from NVIDIA's datasheets. Variants differ (A100 40 GB has ~1.55 TB/s, H100 PCIe ~2 TB/s\nand lower TFLOPs, H100 NVL 94 GB, H200 141 GB at ~4.8 TB/s), so confirm the exact SKU.\n\n| | L4 | A100 80 GB SXM | H100 80 GB SXM |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Architecture | Ada Lovelace | Ampere | Hopper |\n| Memory | 24 GB GDDR6 | 80 GB HBM2e | 80 GB HBM3 |\n| Bandwidth | ~300 GB/s | ~2.0 TB/s | ~3.35 TB/s |\n| Dense bf16 tensor | ~120 TFLOP/s | ~312 TFLOP/s | ~990 TFLOP/s |\n| FP8 | yes | no | yes (Transformer Engine) |\n| MIG partitions | no | up to 7 | up to 7 |\n| Interconnect | PCIe only, no NVLink | NVLink 600 GB/s (SXM) | NVLink 900 GB/s (SXM) |\n| Power / form | 72 W, single-slot PCIe | 400 W SXM (PCIe variant 300 W) | up to 700 W SXM (PCIe 350 W) |\n\n- **Bandwidth is the decode column.** The H100 streams weights ~11× faster than the L4 and ~1.7× faster\n  than the A100, and single-stream tokens/s follow those ratios almost directly.\n- **Memory is the fit column.**\n- **FP8 matters** *because* fp8 weights halve the bytes per decode step and double the KV room with far\n  less quality loss than int4.\n- **The A100 has no fp8 tensor cores.** It falls back to int8 or int4 schemes, or to engine-specific fp8\n  *weight-only* kernels that dequantise to bf16 on the fly, which save memory and bandwidth but not compute.\n- **MIG** matters for packing many small models onto one card with isolation; the L4 cannot do it, but at\n  its price you simply buy more L4s.\n\n### The procedure\n1. **Fit.** Weights at your precision plus KV headroom, from the sizing lesson. Anything that does not fit\n   ends the discussion. Roughly:\n   - an L4 holds an 8B model in bf16 with a few concurrent 4k contexts, or a ~13B model in int4;\n   - an 80 GB card holds 8B with hundreds of contexts, 30B-class models in bf16, or 70B in int4;\n   - 70B in fp8 or bf16 needs two or four 80 GB cards with NVLink.\n2. **Per-stream latency.** Decode step time is at least `bytes per step ÷ bandwidth`. For 8B bf16: ~53 ms\n   on L4 (≤19 tokens/s), ~8 ms on A100 (≤125), ~5 ms on H100 (≤200). If the SLO needs 30 tokens/s per\n   user, the L4 is out regardless of price.\n3. **Cost per token at your load.** Estimate the throughput ceiling at the batch your memory allows,\n   multiply by expected utilisation, divide price by it. The expensive card wins when traffic keeps it\n   busy; the cheap card wins when traffic is sparse. The examples below run this.\n4. **Features.**\n   - FP8 (H100, L4) for cheap precision reduction;\n   - MIG (A100, H100) for isolated sharing;\n   - NVLink (SXM) for tensor parallelism;\n   - L4's low power and single-slot size for edge boxes and ordinary servers, and its video encode/decode\n     engines for vision pipelines.\n5. **Availability and procurement.** Quotas, regions, reservation terms and spot pricing differ by card;\n   A100 supply is typically easier than H100, and L4s are widely available in small counts.\n\nCloud families to look for (check the current catalogue before planning):\n\n| Cloud | A100 | H100 | L4 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| AWS | p4d/p4de (A100 40/80 GB) | p5 | g6 |\n| GCP | a2 | a3 | g2 |\n| Azure | ND A100 v4 | ND H100 v5 | |\n\n### Training versus serving\n- **Training and full fine-tuning are compute- and interconnect-bound.** H100's bf16 and fp8 throughput\n  and NVLink make it the default; A100 remains cost-effective for smaller runs.\n- **The L4 is only sensible for LoRA on small models,** *because* 24 GB and PCIe-only links cap\n  everything else.\n- **For serving, steps 2 and 3 dominate.** That is why \"A100 is obsolete\" is wrong for inference: its\n  bandwidth is 60% of an H100's at a typically lower price, and for models under 30B it is often the best\n  value.\n\n### Neighbours you will see\n| Card | Memory | Bandwidth | Where it sits |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A10G | 24 GB | ~600 GB/s | Between L4 and A100 in price and bandwidth |\n| L40S | 48 GB | ~860 GB/s | Between L4 and A100 in price and bandwidth |\n| H200 | more and faster than H100 | | An H100 with more memory, for long contexts |\n\nNewer generations exist and follow the same procedure with their own datasheet numbers.","example":"### Worked comparison: an 8B chat model at two traffic levels\nIllustrative on-demand prices of roughly $0.80 per hour for an L4 and $4.00 per hour for an H100\n(ballpark only; prices vary by provider and over time).\n\n**Ceilings** (both are upper bounds):\n\n| | L4 | H100 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Batch the KV room allows | about eight 4k-token contexts, so batch 8 | about a hundred contexts |\n| Bytes per step | 16 GB weights + ~4.3 GB cache | 16 GB + ~54 GB cache |\n| Step time | ≈ 68 ms at 300 GB/s | ≈ 21 ms at 3.35 TB/s |\n| Aggregate ceiling | ≈ 120 tokens/s | ≈ 4,800 tokens/s |\n\n**Cost per million tokens at full load:**\n- **L4:** 120 × 3,600 ≈ 430k tokens per hour, so $0.80 ÷ 0.43M ≈ $1.90 per million.\n- **H100:** 4,800 × 3,600 ≈ 17M tokens per hour, $4.00 ÷ 17M ≈ $0.23 per million.\n- **So** at full load the H100 is roughly eight times cheaper per token despite costing five times more\n  per hour.\n\n**Two scenarios:**\n\n| Scenario | Demand | L4 | H100 | Winner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A, internal tool | 0.3 requests/s × 300 output tokens ≈ 90 tokens/s | about 75% of ceiling, $0.80/h | under 2% of ceiling, $4.00/h | L4, five to one; ≤19 tokens/s per user is acceptable internally |\n| B, public product | 10 requests/s × 300 tokens = 3,000 tokens/s | 25 L4s, $20/h | one H100 at ~60% of ceiling, $4/h | H100, and users get several times faster per-stream decode |\n\nIn scenario B the batch-1 bandwidth ratio is ~11×; at these batch sizes the per-user gap is smaller.\n\n**Where the crossover sits:**\n- **Base case.** The crossover is wherever the H100's higher hourly price is covered by the tokens it\n  actually serves. One H100 costs the same as five L4s, which together top out near 600 tokens/s, so the\n  crossover is roughly 500–600 tokens/s of sustained demand.\n- **Redundancy moves it.** A production service needs at least two replicas, and two H100s ($8/h) cost as\n  much as ten L4s, pushing the crossover toward ~1,200 tokens/s unless the second replica can be shared\n  with other models.\n- **Latency can remove the L4 entirely.** L4s at batch 8 deliver ~15 tokens/s per user; if the product SLO\n  is higher, the L4 is out at any demand and the comparison is H100 against A100.\n- **Bursty demand:** read the serverless lesson before buying either.\n\n### Card planner: cards, cost and utilisation for a demand figure\nReproduces the worked comparison with the bandwidth-bound ceiling and a two-replica minimum. Prices and\nbatch limits are the illustrative figures above; swap in your own quotes and sizing results.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\nCARDS = {  # (bandwidth B/s, illustrative $/hour, max concurrent 4k contexts for 8B bf16)\n    \"L4\":   (300e9,  0.80, 8),\n    \"H100\": (3.35e12, 4.00, 100),\n}\n\ndef ceiling_tps(bw, batch, weights=16e9, ctx=4096, kv_per_token=131072):\n    step = (weights + batch * ctx * kv_per_token) / bw     # bandwidth-bound step time\n    return batch / step\n\ndef plan(card, demand_tps, min_replicas=2):\n    bw, price, batch = CARDS[card]\n    cap = ceiling_tps(bw, batch)\n    n = max(min_replicas, math.ceil(demand_tps / cap))\n    per_m = price / (cap * 3600 / 1e6)\n    print(f\"{card:5s} ceiling={cap:6.0f} tok/s  ${per_m:.2f}/M at full load  \"\n          f\"{n:3d} cards  ${n*price:5.2f}/h  util={demand_tps/(n*cap):4.0%}\")\n\nfor demand in (90, 3000):          # scenario A, scenario B (tokens/s)\n    for card in CARDS:\n        plan(card, demand)\n# L4    ceiling=   118 tok/s  $1.88/M at full load    2 cards  $ 1.60/h  util= 38%\n# H100  ceiling=  4807 tok/s  $0.23/M at full load    2 cards  $ 8.00/h  util=  1%\n# L4    ceiling=   118 tok/s  $1.88/M at full load   26 cards  $20.80/h  util= 98%\n# H100  ceiling=  4807 tok/s  $0.23/M at full load    2 cards  $ 8.00/h  util= 31%\n```\n\nThe planner's 26 L4s versus the prose's 25 is rounding (118 versus 120 tokens/s). Running near 98% of a\nbandwidth ceiling is not realistic, which strengthens the case for the H100 in scenario B.","patterns":["**One node pool per card tier.** Use when more than one card type serves production. Create a pool per card and steer workloads with the `nvidia.com/gpu.product` label from GPU feature discovery, so a model cannot silently land on the wrong tier. Monitor pods per tier against the card-tier matrix.","**Benchmark the shortlist at real batch and context.** Use before any reservation or purchase. Run the engine's serving benchmark with your prompt and output lengths at target concurrency on each candidate, and record tokens/s per dollar. Monitor measured throughput against the bandwidth ceiling.","**Reserve the baseline, burst elsewhere.** Use when traffic has a steady floor and peaks. Commit to reserved capacity for the floor on the card that is cheapest per token when busy, and absorb peaks with on-demand or serverless capacity. Monitor reserved-card utilisation; idle reservations are sunk cost.","**Pack small models with MIG.** Use for embeddings, rerankers or several small models that need isolation on A100 or H100. Expose MIG profiles through the GPU Operator and request them as separate resources. Monitor per-instance memory and latency, because each slice gets a fraction of bandwidth.","**Re-run the selection on a schedule.** Use whenever prices, quotas, models or SLOs change, and at least at each budget cycle. Keep the planner inputs in version control next to the capacity plan. Monitor actual cost per million tokens against the plan's figure."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Model, precision, SLO, traffic forecast\"] --> B{\"Fits with KV headroom?\"}\n    B -->|\"no\"| C[\"Quantise or add NVLink cards\"]\n    C --> B\n    B -->|\"yes\"| D{\"Per-stream floor meets tokens/s SLO?\"}\n    D -->|\"no\"| E[\"Drop the low-bandwidth card\"]\n    E --> F[\"Cost per token at expected load\"]\n    D -->|\"yes\"| F\n    F --> G{\"Sustained load keeps big card busy?\"}\n    G -->|\"yes\"| H[\"H100 or A100\"]\n    G -->|\"no\"| I[\"L4, A100, or serverless\"]\n    H --> J[\"Check FP8, MIG, NVLink, quota\"]\n    I --> J","caption":"The procedure as a decision path: each gate can eliminate a card before price is even considered, and\nonly the survivors are compared on cost per token at your load."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is memory bandwidth, not TFLOPs, the first spec to compare for an LLM serving card?","a":"- **Decode streams every weight byte per step,** so the step floor is bytes divided by bandwidth; at small\n  batches the arithmetic units mostly wait.\n- **Numbers:** 8B bf16 is ~53 ms per step on an L4 at ~300 GB/s, ~8 ms on an A100 at ~2.0 TB/s and ~5 ms\n  on an H100 at ~3.35 TB/s.\n- **TFLOPs matter** for prefill-heavy and training workloads, which is why the answer depends on the workload."},{"q":"A team wants to standardise every workload on H100s. When would you push back?","a":"- **Sparse traffic:** an 8B internal tool at 0.3 requests/s keeps an H100 under 2% busy, so its hourly\n  price buys idle throughput while an L4 suffices.\n- **Small models with real concurrency:** an A100 often gives the best value under 30B, at 60% of the H100's\n  bandwidth for a typically lower price.\n- **Availability:** H100 quota or region limits can block launch where the data must stay."},{"q":"How do you find the traffic level at which an H100 becomes cheaper than a fleet of L4s?","a":"- **Compute each card's ceiling** at the batch its memory allows (about 120 versus 4,800 tokens/s for 8B\n  bf16 in the worked example).\n- **Equal-cost comparison:** one H100 costs as much as five L4s, which top out near 600 tokens/s, so the\n  crossover is roughly 500–600 tokens/s sustained.\n- **Then correct it:** two-replica redundancy pushes it toward ~1,200 tokens/s; a per-user tokens/s SLO\n  above what an L4 delivers removes the L4 at any demand."},{"q":"You validated fp8 serving on H100 but the production region only has A100s. What is your plan?","a":"- **Recognise the gap:** the A100 has no fp8 tensor cores, so the fp8 results do not transfer.\n- **Options:** int8 or int4 schemes, or fp8 weight-only kernels that dequantise to bf16 where the engine\n  offers them, which save memory and bandwidth but not compute.\n- **Re-evaluate** quality and speed on the A100 with the new kernels before committing, and recheck fit\n  and KV headroom at the new precision."},{"q":"Which card would you pick for a 70B model serving a public product, and why?","a":"- **Fit:** 70B needs two or four 80 GB cards in fp8 or bf16, or one 80 GB card in int4 with a quality cost.\n- **Interconnect:** tensor parallelism across cards needs NVLink, so SXM H100 or A100 nodes, not PCIe-only\n  cards.\n- **Load:** a public product with sustained demand keeps H100s busy, where they are cheapest per token,\n  and fp8 on H100 gives KV room with less quality loss than int4."}],"tradeoffs":["**H100 lowest cost per token vs needing load.** It delivers the lowest cost per token and fastest decode, but only when load keeps it busy; at low or bursty utilisation its hourly price makes the L4 or an API provider cheaper.","**A100 value vs no FP8.** It is often the best value for sub-30B serving and small training runs, but its lack of FP8 forces int8/int4 for memory relief, with the quality evaluation that implies.","**L4 cheap and available vs bandwidth and size caps.** Low-power and easy to get, yet its 300 GB/s bandwidth caps per-stream decode and its 24 GB caps model size, so it flips from \"obvious choice\" to \"cannot do it\" at roughly 8B–13B.","**SXM/NVLink vs flexibility.** NVLink makes tensor parallelism efficient for 70B-class models, at the cost of buying eight-card blocks; PCIe cards are flexible but make multi-card sharding slow."],"pitfalls":["**Choosing by hourly price alone.** Symptom: a fleet of L4s that cannot meet a per-user tokens/s SLO or cannot load the model the product team actually wants. Fix: run fit and latency gates first.","**Choosing by TFLOPs for an inference workload.** Symptom: paying H100 prices for decode throughput that an A100 would have delivered at 60%. Fix: compare on bandwidth and cost per token.","**Forgetting the A100 has no FP8.** Symptom: a quantisation plan validated on H100 that must be redone with int8/int4 kernels and re-evaluated for quality. Fix: validate on the target card.","**Sizing to the 80 GB figure without KV headroom.** Symptom: a 70B bf16 model that loads on two cards and serves one request at a time. Fix: use the sizing arithmetic.","**Ignoring quota and region availability.** Symptom: an architecture built around H100s that cannot be launched where the data must stay. Fix: confirm quota per region before design sign-off."],"when":"- **Run the five-step procedure when:** a new model, SLO or traffic forecast arrives, and re-run it when\n  prices or quotas change.\n- **Reach for the L4 for:** embeddings, rerankers, vision models, small LLMs and anything with sparse\n  traffic.\n- **Reach for the A100 when:** a 7B–30B model needs real concurrency at the best price, or when H100\n  quota is unavailable.\n- **Reach for the H100 for:** 70B-class serving, fp8, training, or any service whose sustained load would\n  keep it busy.\n- **Measure:** tokens/s per card against the bandwidth ceiling, and actual utilisation against the plan.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"which card for X and why\"; the strong answer names the binding constraint (fit,\n  bandwidth or cost per token) before naming the card.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Benchmarks the candidate card at the real batch size and context, confirms fp8 or int4 kernels exist for the engine, and reports tokens/s per dollar rather than utilisation.","architect":"Owns the card-tier matrix (which workloads go on L4, A100, H100), the procurement mix, and the policy for when a workload must move tiers.","fde":"Maps a customer's available hardware and quotas to what each model can realistically do there, and defends the recommendation with fit and bandwidth arithmetic."},"check":{"q":"A customer runs a 13B model in bf16 for 200 daily-active users and asks whether to buy L4s,\nA100s or H100s. Reason it through.","a":"Fit first: 13B in bf16 is ~26 GB of weights, so the L4 is out unless the model is quantised to\nint4 (~7 GB), which needs a quality evaluation. Both 80 GB cards fit it with tens of gigabytes of KV\nheadroom. Latency next: decode on the A100 is bounded near 26 GB ÷ 2 TB/s ≈ 13 ms per step, around\n75 tokens/s per stream, comfortably interactive; the H100 would be ~1.7× faster. Load: 200 daily\nusers rarely produce more than a handful of concurrent requests even at peak, well inside one A100's\nceiling, so an H100's extra throughput would sit idle. The int4 L4 route also needs its own latency and\nKV check: ~7 GB ÷ 300 GB/s ≈ 23 ms per step, under ~40 tokens/s per stream before batching, and if the\n13B model uses full multi-head attention rather than GQA its per-token cache is several times larger,\nso the remaining ~13 GB holds only a few long contexts. Recommendation: one A100 80 GB (two for redundancy), or an int4 L4 pair if\nthe quality evaluation passes and budget dominates; revisit if traffic grows past the A100's ceiling\nor fp8 becomes necessary."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which card property most directly determines single-stream decode tokens per second for a given model and precision?","options":["Peak bf16 TFLOP/s","Memory bandwidth","Number of streaming multiprocessors","PCIe generation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each decode step must stream all weights (and the sequence's KV cache) from memory, so the step time floor is bytes ÷ bandwidth. TFLOPs matter for prefill and training, and PCIe only affects host transfers and multi-card traffic."},{"q":"A team validated fp8 serving of a 70B model on H100s and now must deploy on A100s. What changes?","options":["Nothing; fp8 checkpoints run on any card with 80 GB","The A100 runs fp8 tensor-core matmuls at about half the H100's rate","The A100 has no fp8 tensor cores, so they need a different quantisation path and must re-evaluate quality and speed","They must use the A100 40 GB variant, which supports fp8"],"answer":2,"explanation":"FP8 tensor cores arrived with Hopper and Ada (H100, L4); Ampere lacks them. Memory relief must come from int8/int4 schemes, or from fp8 weight-only kernels that dequantise to bf16 where the engine offers them; either way the kernels and numerics differ, which invalidates the earlier evaluation."},{"q":"An internal tool serves an 8B model at 0.3 requests per second. Why is an L4 usually the better choice than an H100 here?","options":["The L4 has higher memory bandwidth for small models","The H100 cannot run models smaller than 30B efficiently","At that load the H100 would sit almost idle, so its higher hourly price buys throughput nobody uses, while the L4's decode speed is adequate","The L4 supports MIG and the H100 does not"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cost per token depends on utilisation. The H100 is far cheaper per token at full load, but at a few percent utilisation the hourly price dominates and the L4's lower bandwidth still meets an internal tool's latency needs. The L4 does not support MIG."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/serverless-gpus","sources":["Official docs: Modal","Official docs: RunPod Serverless","Official docs: Google Cloud Run (GPU support)"],"why":"- **A dedicated GPU costs the same idle or busy.** It costs the same per hour whether it serves ten\n  thousand requests or none.\n- **Most early traffic is bursty, diurnal or small.** A document-processing pipeline that runs two hours\n  each night, an internal assistant used during working hours in one time zone, a feature with a hundred\n  requests a day. Paying for 730 hours a month to use 20 is the single largest waste line in many AI\n  budgets, and it is invisible because the card shows up as \"in use\".\n- **\"Autoscale the Kubernetes deployment to zero\" is too slow.** A GPU node takes minutes to provision,\n  pull a multi-gigabyte image and load weights; by the time the pod is Ready the user has gone, and the\n  cluster autoscaler may not even have the quota to add a node.\n- **\"Keep one replica warm\" is a dedicated GPU with extra steps.**\n- **What the platforms buy you.** They make scale-from-zero fast enough to be usable — pre-provisioned\n  fleets, cached images, weight caches on fast storage, process snapshots — and bill in seconds, which\n  makes bursty use cheap. They do not remove the cold start; they shrink it and hide the node layer.","how":"### The request path\n1. **A request arrives at the platform's router.**\n2. **Warm path:** if a warm container for your function exists and has spare concurrency, it runs there\n   immediately.\n3. **Cold path:** the platform takes a GPU from its pooled fleet, starts your container, runs your\n   initialisation, and only then serves the request. It keeps nodes and often images pre-warmed *because*\n   node provisioning is the slowest step and amortises across tenants.\n4. **Scale-down:** after the container has been idle for a configurable scale-down window, it is stopped\n   and billing ends.\n5. **Scale-out** is the same path repeated: when in-flight requests exceed per-container concurrency,\n   another container is started.\n\n### Anatomy of a cold start\nEach stage has its own mitigation, and the total is the sum:\n\n| # | Stage | Typical cost | Mitigation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Scheduling and container start | Seconds on a pooled fleet; minutes if a node must be created | Platform's warm pool |\n| 2 | Image pull | Tens of seconds for a multi-gigabyte CUDA image | Lazy-loading image formats; keep weights *out* of the image |\n| 3 | Process start and imports | Several seconds for PyTorch, CUDA init and engine setup | Memory snapshots after imports (some platforms); GPU-state snapshots are newer and provider-specific |\n| 4 | Weight load (storage → host RAM → VRAM) | 16 GB bf16 from local NVMe cache: seconds; from a remote bucket: minutes | Volumes or model caches on fast storage |\n| 5 | Engine warm-up | CUDA graph capture, kernel compilation, `torch.compile`: tens of seconds for some engines | Accept it, disable it for serverless, or snapshot after it |\n\n- **A network bucket on every start is the classic mistake;** platforms offer fast-storage volumes\n  precisely to move stage 4 from minutes to seconds.\n- **Illustrative order of magnitude:** a tuned small-model function cold-starts in 10–30 seconds; an\n  untuned 70B deployment can take ten minutes.\n- **Measure yours;** it is the key input to every decision below.\n\n### The knobs\n- **Scale-down window (idle timeout).** Longer keeps containers warm through traffic gaps at the cost of\n  paying for idle seconds; shorter saves money and causes more cold starts.\n- **Minimum warm containers.** Removes cold starts for the base load, billed continuously at the\n  serverless rate, which is usually above on-demand; this is where \"serverless\" quietly becomes\n  \"expensive dedicated\".\n- **Concurrency per container.** Letting one container handle several requests at once lets the engine\n  batch them, raising throughput per GPU-second; set it from the batching lesson, not to 1.\n- **GPU type.** Most platforms let you pick L4/A10-class through H100; the selection lesson's arithmetic\n  applies unchanged, except that per-second billing makes low utilisation free.\n\n### Billing shape\n- **Per second of container lifetime,** not per request, often for CPU and memory as well.\n- **The GPU rate is usually above like-for-like on-demand hourly pricing** (compare the same card from the\n  same class of provider): the premium funds the idle pool and the fast storage.\n- **Cold-start seconds are usually billed.**\n- **So is the idle tail of every scale-down window:** each container that scales to zero was first paid\n  for while it sat idle for the full window.\n- **Egress, storage volumes and request overhead** may be separate lines.\n\n### What it is not\n- **Serverless GPUs are *your container on their pooled GPUs*.**\n- **Per-token model APIs are a different layer:** no container, no GPU choice, billed per token; they are\n  the right answer for standard open models at modest scale.\n- **Not every \"serverless\" product has GPUs.** AWS Lambda does not, and SageMaker's serverless inference\n  option has been CPU-only. Google Cloud Run and Azure Container Apps have added GPU options, with\n  constraints that change, so check current support before designing around any of them.","example":"### Worked scenario: an 8B model on an L4-class GPU\nIllustrative prices, ballpark only: dedicated on-demand $0.80 per hour ($584 for a 730-hour month);\nserverless $1.60 per hour-equivalent, billed per second, a 2× premium. Measured cold start after tuning:\n45 seconds. Scale-down window: 5 minutes.\n\n**Workload A, internal document tool:** 1,000 requests per day at ~3 GPU-seconds each.\n\n| Line | Arithmetic | Monthly |\n|---|---|---|\n| Busy seconds | 3,000 busy seconds a day ≈ 25 hours a month | $40 |\n| Cold starts | about 40 a day (working-hour clusters with gaps): 40 × 45 s = 30 minutes a day ≈ 15 hours | $24 |\n| Idle tail | 40 scale-downs, each after a billed 5-minute window: 200 minutes a day ≈ 100 hours | $160 |\n| **Serverless total** | | **roughly $225** vs $584 dedicated, about 2.6× cheaper |\n\n- **The idle tail is the line people forget,** and the biggest one here, so the scale-down window is a\n  cost knob: at 2 minutes it falls to ~$64 (if the number of cold starts does not rise much), at\n  10 minutes it doubles to ~$320.\n- **Latency is fine:** a 45-second cold start is tolerable for a tool that already takes seconds.\n\n**Workload B, steady product traffic at 40% busy:**\n- 292 busy hours × $1.60 = $467; cold starts are negligible because containers stay warm. Serverless still\n  wins by 20%.\n- **Break-even** is where the rates cross: 0.80 ÷ 1.60 = 50% utilisation, minus whatever cold starts and\n  idle windows add.\n- At 60% busy, serverless costs $700 against $584 and dedicated wins.\n\n**Workload B with a latency SLO** (p95 response under 2 seconds):\n- **Cold starts break it.** Any cold-start rate above 5% puts 45-second responses at the p95, and even 1%\n  puts them at the p99, which most teams also track.\n- **A warm container is too expensive.** Keeping one always warm costs 730 × $1.60 = $1,168, double the\n  dedicated card.\n- **The right design** is a dedicated card (or Kubernetes replica) for the base load and serverless only\n  for bursts above it, where the cold start affects a small tail of requests during ramps. The arithmetic,\n  not the branding, picks the layout.\n\n### Monthly cost calculator with the idle tail included\nReproduces workload A and shows the scale-down window as a cost knob. Rates are the illustrative figures\nabove.\n\n```python\nDAYS = 30\n\ndef serverless_month(busy_s_day, cold_starts_day, cold_s=45, window_min=5, rate_h=1.60):\n    hours = {\n        \"busy\": busy_s_day * DAYS / 3600,\n        \"cold\": cold_starts_day * cold_s * DAYS / 3600,               # start-up seconds are billed\n        \"idle\": cold_starts_day * window_min * 60 * DAYS / 3600,      # each scale-down waits a full window\n    }\n    return {k: round(v * rate_h) for k, v in hours.items()} | {\"total\": round(sum(hours.values()) * rate_h)}\n\ndedicated = 730 * 0.80                                                 # $584 a month\nfor window in (2, 5, 10):\n    print(f\"window={window:2d} min\", serverless_month(3000, 40, window_min=window), f\"dedicated=${dedicated:.0f}\")\n# window= 2 min {'busy': 40, 'cold': 24, 'idle': 64, 'total': 128} dedicated=$584\n# window= 5 min {'busy': 40, 'cold': 24, 'idle': 160, 'total': 224} dedicated=$584\n# window=10 min {'busy': 40, 'cold': 24, 'idle': 320, 'total': 384} dedicated=$584\n\nbreakeven = 0.80 / 1.60                                                # busy fraction where rates cross\nprint(f\"break-even utilisation (no cold starts): {breakeven:.0%}\")     # 50%\n```\n\n### Production snippet: load once, cached weights, batched concurrency (Modal)\nThe structure that keeps cold starts short on any platform: weights on a volume, load in a start-up hook,\nseveral requests per container. Shown with Modal's Python SDK (1.x parameter names); check the current\ndocs, since these names have changed between versions.\n\n```python\nimport modal\n\napp = modal.App(\"doc-tool\")\nweights = modal.Volume.from_name(\"model-weights\", create_if_missing=True)\nimage = modal.Image.debian_slim().pip_install(\"vllm\")       # weights stay out of the image\n\n@app.cls(gpu=\"L4\", volumes={\"/models\": weights},\n         scaledown_window=300,      # 5-minute idle window: the cost knob from the calculator\n         min_containers=0)          # scale to zero; raise only if the SLO demands it\n@modal.concurrent(max_inputs=8)     # let the engine batch, never 1\nclass Model:\n    @modal.enter()\n    def load(self):                 # runs once per container, not per request\n        from vllm import LLM\n        self.llm = LLM(model=\"/models/llama-3-8b-instruct\")\n\n    @modal.method()\n    def generate(self, prompt: str) -> str:\n        return self.llm.generate([prompt])[0].outputs[0].text\n```","patterns":["**Weights on a cached volume, never in the image or a bucket.** Use on every serverless GPU function. Mount the platform's volume or model cache and load from it in a start-up hook. Monitor cold-start time by stage; weight load should be seconds, not minutes.","**Tune the scale-down window from the idle-tail line.** Use when the bill has more idle than busy hours. Compute cold starts per day times the window, as in the calculator, and shorten the window until cold starts begin to rise. Monitor billed idle seconds and cold-start count together.","**Batch inside each container.** Use whenever the engine supports concurrent requests. Set per-container concurrency to what the batching lesson and KV room allow (for example eight concurrent inputs), not 1. Monitor GPU-seconds per request and per-container queue time.","**Hybrid base plus burst.** Use when there is a latency SLO and a steady floor of traffic. Serve the base load on a dedicated card or Kubernetes replica and route only overflow to serverless. Monitor the fraction of requests that hit a cold start and p95/p99 with cold starts included.","**Snapshot after imports.** Use where the platform offers memory snapshots and imports dominate start-up. Enable the snapshot after imports and weight loading to CPU, then move to the GPU at restore. Monitor cold-start p50 before and after.","**Capacity fallback for bursts.** Use for any customer-facing endpoint. Configure a second GPU type or region, or a per-token API, for capacity errors. Monitor capacity or quota errors per GPU type and region."],"diagram":{"code":"stateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> Cold\n    Cold --> Starting : request arrives, no warm container\n    Starting --> Loading : pull image, run imports\n    Loading --> Warm : weights in VRAM, engine warmed\n    Warm --> Warm : requests within concurrency\n    Warm --> Idle : no requests, idle time billed\n    Idle --> Warm : request inside scale-down window\n    Idle --> Cold : window expires, billing stops","caption":"The request lifecycle on a scale-to-zero platform: the warm path is cheap and fast, the cold path pays\nevery start-up stage, and the idle window is billed before each scale-down."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a serverless GPU, and how is it different from a per-token model API?","a":"- **Serverless GPU:** your container on the provider's pooled GPUs, billed per second of container\n  lifetime, scaling to zero after an idle window.\n- **Per-token API:** no container and no GPU choice, billed per token; best for standard open models at\n  modest scale.\n- **Why choose serverless GPUs:** custom models, custom engines or weights you control, with bursty traffic\n  that would leave a dedicated card idle."},{"q":"Walk me through the stages of a cold start and which one you would fix first.","a":"- **Stages:** scheduling and container start, image pull, process start and imports, weight load, engine\n  warm-up; the total is the sum.\n- **Fix weight load first** when weights come from a remote bucket: moving 16 GB to a cached volume turns\n  minutes into seconds.\n- **Then** keep weights out of the image, snapshot after imports where offered, and decide whether engine\n  warm-up such as CUDA graph capture is worth its tens of seconds."},{"q":"At what point would you move a workload off serverless onto dedicated GPUs?","a":"- **Utilisation:** when busy fraction exceeds the dedicated rate divided by the serverless rate, 50% with a\n  2× premium, and lower once cold starts and idle tails are billed.\n- **Latency:** when a contractual p95 or p99 cannot absorb cold starts, keep a dedicated base and burst to\n  serverless.\n- **Shape:** when the model spans multiple GPUs or nodes, which most platforms limit."},{"q":"Your serverless bill doubled but request volume did not change. What do you look at?","a":"- **Idle tail:** cold starts per day times the scale-down window; a longer window or more frequent gaps can\n  make idle seconds the largest line, as in the worked $160 idle versus $40 busy.\n- **Cold-start seconds** are billed too, so a regression in image size or weight loading raises cost.\n- **Warm minimums and concurrency:** a raised minimum or concurrency set to 1 multiplies GPU-seconds per\n  request."},{"q":"Why is \"just keep one container warm\" often the wrong fix for a latency SLO?","a":"- **It is billed continuously** at the serverless rate: 730 × $1.60 = $1,168 a month in the example, double\n  a dedicated card at $584.\n- **Better:** a dedicated replica for the base load and serverless for bursts, so cold starts only touch a\n  small tail during ramps.\n- **First,** tune the cold start itself, since a 10–30 second start may already meet a relaxed SLO."}],"tradeoffs":["**Per-second billing vs rate premium.** It makes low and bursty utilisation cheap, but the premium makes steady high utilisation dearer than on-demand; the flip is near the ratio of the two prices.","**Scale-to-zero vs cold starts.** It removes idle cost but introduces cold starts; right when the SLO tolerates them or traffic gaps are rare, wrong for tight tail-latency targets.","**Warm minimums vs cost.** Warm minimums and long idle windows buy latency with money, and past a point you are paying dedicated prices for a platform you do not control.","**Pooled third-party hardware vs data control.** It removes node operations but puts your weights and prompts in the provider's regions and tenancy model; for regulated data the compliance review, not the price, often decides.","**Managed pooling vs control.** It hides nodes, drivers and images, at the cost of vendor-specific SDKs, less control over GPU topology and limits on multi-GPU or multi-node models."],"pitfalls":["**Loading weights from a remote bucket on every start.** Symptom: cold starts of minutes that no scale-down tuning fixes, visible as a bimodal latency histogram. Fix: a cached volume.","**Baking weights into the container image.** Symptom: image pulls that take longer than the model load and rebuilds for every checkpoint change. Fix: keep weights on a volume.","**Setting per-container concurrency to one.** Symptom: many containers each running a batch of one, so GPU-seconds per request are several times higher than on a batched dedicated server. Fix: allow batching.","**Forgetting that cold-start seconds are billed.** Symptom: a bill where start-up time rivals serving time, which happens whenever the scale-down window is short relative to request gaps. Fix: model it.","**Assuming serverless capacity is unlimited.** Symptom: requests queueing or failing with capacity or quota errors during a burst, *because* the provider's pool for that GPU type in that region, or your account's container limit, is exhausted exactly when everyone's traffic peaks. Fix: a fallback.","**Promising a tight p95 on a scale-to-zero deployment.** Symptom: an SLO breach on the first request after every lull. Fix: hybrid base plus burst."],"when":"- **Use when:** utilisation would be low or bursty (internal tools, nightly batch, prototypes, long-tail\n  models that are rarely hit), latency can absorb a cold start or the warm pool makes it rare, and you\n  want no node operations.\n- **Prefer dedicated capacity or Kubernetes when:** sustained utilisation exceeds the price ratio, tail\n  latency is contractual, or the model spans multiple GPUs.\n- **Measure:** cold start by stage, busy seconds per day, and p95 with cold starts included; those three\n  numbers decide.\n- **Day to day:** the \"why did the first request take a minute\" ticket.\n- **Interviews:** the expected answer walks the cold-start anatomy and the break-even calculation.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Structures the function so imports and weight loading happen once at container start, keeps weights on a cached volume, tunes concurrency and idle windows, and measures cold-start stages.","architect":"Decides which workloads live on serverless, which on dedicated capacity, and where the hybrid boundary sits, using measured utilisation and the SLO rather than vendor claims.","fde":"Prototypes a customer pilot on serverless to avoid procurement delays, then presents the utilisation data that justifies moving to dedicated hardware when traffic becomes steady."},"check":{"q":"A customer's serverless LLM endpoint shows p50 latency of 1.2 s and p95 of 48 s. They ask\nwhether to raise the minimum warm containers to five. What do you investigate and recommend?","a":"A 48-second p95 against a 1.2-second p50 is a cold-start signature: at least 5% of requests\nstart a container. First measure the cold start by stage (image pull, imports, weight load, warm-up)\nand fix the cheap ones: weights on a cached volume instead of a bucket, snapshotting after imports,\ndisabling or pre-capturing warm-up. Then look at traffic: if gaps are frequent, a longer scale-down\nwindow may cut cold starts more cheaply than warm containers. If base load is steady, one warm\ncontainer removes most cold starts; five warm containers is dedicated pricing for five cards and\nprobably exceeds an on-demand deployment. Recommend tuning first, then one warm container, and move\nthe base load to dedicated capacity if the utilisation arithmetic says so."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do serverless GPU platforms charge a per-second rate above on-demand hourly pricing?","options":["Per-second billing is more expensive to meter","The premium pays for the idle pooled fleet, cached images and fast storage that make scale-from-zero fast enough to use","GPUs run at higher clocks in serverless mode","Serverless containers get larger VRAM allocations"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The platform must keep provisioned GPUs and warm infrastructure waiting for tenants' bursts; that idle capacity is shared and amortised through the rate premium. Customers accept it because they pay only for busy seconds."},{"q":"Which change most reduces cold-start time for a function whose 16 GB weights currently download from object storage on every start?","options":["Increasing per-container concurrency","Choosing an H100 instead of an L4","Keeping the weights on a platform volume or local cache so they load from fast storage","Shortening the scale-down window"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Weight loading from remote storage is usually the dominant cold-start stage; moving it to cached NVMe-class storage cuts minutes to seconds. A faster GPU does not speed a network download, and a shorter idle window causes more cold starts, not faster ones."},{"q":"At what utilisation does serverless stop being cheaper than a dedicated card, ignoring cold starts?","options":["Always above 10%, because of the premium","Never; per-second billing is always cheaper","Roughly where busy fraction equals the dedicated hourly rate divided by the serverless hourly-equivalent rate","At 100%, when both bill the same hours"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With a 2× premium the crossover is 50% busy; cold starts and idle windows are billed too, so the real crossover is lower. Above it the dedicated card's constant price covers more tokens."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/keda-autoscaling","sources":["Official docs: KEDA","Official docs: Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler"],"why":"- **The HPA assumes CPU rises with load.** The built-in Horizontal Pod Autoscaler was designed for\n  stateless web services. An LLM server breaks that twice.\n- **CPU barely moves.** The work happens on the GPU, and the Python process mostly waits on CUDA kernels,\n  so a CPU-based HPA never scales.\n- **GPU utilisation saturates immediately.** The `DCGM_FI_DEV_GPU_UTIL`-style \"percent of time a kernel\n  was running\" reads near 100% once one request is decoding, *because* a single sequence keeps kernels\n  busy while using a sliver of the card's arithmetic. A GPU-utilisation HPA scales at the first request\n  and then cannot tell 4 requests from 40.\n- **Demand is the right signal, but plumbing it is work.** Requests waiting in the engine's queue,\n  requests in flight per replica, or broker messages for batch workers track pain. The HPA can consume\n  such \"external\" metrics only if something serves them through the Kubernetes external metrics API, and\n  it cannot scale below one replica in a stock cluster (scale-to-zero is behind an alpha feature gate).\n- **KEDA packages the fix.** Teams used to bolt on a Prometheus adapter, write custom controllers for\n  zero and duplicate credentials per queue. KEDA ships dozens of \"scalers\" (queues, databases, cloud\n  services, Prometheus), an external-metrics server and its own 0↔1 activation. For GPUs, where an idle\n  replica can cost dollars per hour, both halves matter: the right signal and releasing the card.","how":"### Components\nKEDA runs as an operator plus a metrics API server in the cluster. You declare intent with custom\nresources:\n\n| Resource | Targets | Use it for |\n|---|---|---|\n| **ScaledObject** | Anything with a `/scale` subresource (usually a Deployment or StatefulSet) | Long-running servers |\n| **ScaledJob** | Creates Kubernetes Jobs in proportion to pending work; each Job runs to completion | Batch inference, where a pod should not be killed mid-item |\n| **TriggerAuthentication / ClusterTriggerAuthentication** | References to secrets or workload identity for the scalers | Keeping the queue password out of every manifest |\n\n### Two control loops, split at one replica\nKEDA does not replace the HPA; it drives it. The split is the most important thing to understand.\n\n**1. Activation (0↔1), owned by KEDA:**\n1. Every `pollingInterval` seconds (default 30) the operator asks each trigger whether it is active,\n   meaning above `activationThreshold`.\n2. If any is active and the target is at zero, KEDA sets replicas to `minReplicaCount` or 1.\n3. When all triggers have been inactive for `cooldownPeriod` seconds (default 300), it scales back to zero.\n4. `cooldownPeriod` governs *only* this last step.\n\n**2. Scaling (1↔N), owned by an HPA that KEDA creates:**\n1. KEDA registers each trigger as an external metric and generates an HPA pointing at it.\n2. For an `AverageValue` target (the default for most scalers), `desired = ceil(total_metric / threshold)`,\n   clamped to `[minReplicaCount, maxReplicaCount]`, with a default tolerance of about 10% to avoid flapping.\n3. **Scale-down** is damped by the HPA `behavior` stabilisation window (default 300 s).\n4. **Scale-up** has no stabilisation window by default but is rate-limited by the default policy to the\n   larger of +4 pods or +100% every 15 seconds.\n5. Tune both through `advanced.horizontalPodAutoscalerConfig.behavior`.\n\n**Several triggers:** the HPA computes a replica count per metric and takes the maximum, so you can combine\n\"queue depth\" with a `cron` trigger that pre-warms capacity before the morning peak.\n\n### Why queue depth beats latency as a trigger\n- **Latency is what the SLO says,** which makes scaling on p95 tempting.\n- **But latency lags and saturates.** It barely moves until the server is nearly full, then explodes (the\n  queueing knee). Adding replicas changes it only after new pods serve, which for a GPU pod can be several\n  minutes, so a controller acting on it over-reacts and oscillates.\n- **In-flight plus waiting requests lead, roughly linearly.** By Little's law, concurrency = arrival rate ×\n  time in system, so it rises the moment traffic does. Dividing it by a measured per-replica capacity\n  gives a replica count directly.\n\n### When request count misleads\n- **It treats a 200-token chat and a 30k-token RAG prompt as equal load.**\n- **KV-cache usage measures the real resource but is a poor *primary* trigger,** *because* it saturates:\n  it sits near 100% whenever the server is full, whether one more request is waiting or a thousand, so\n  `ceil(metric / threshold)` cannot size the fleet from it.\n- **Fix 1:** keep waiting requests as the proportional trigger and add KV usage as a second trigger (the\n  HPA takes the maximum).\n- **Fix 2:** scale on a token-weighted signal such as prompt plus generation tokens in flight, when your\n  gateway exports one.\n- **Either way,** re-measure per-replica capacity whenever the prompt-length distribution changes.\n\n### What KEDA does not do\n- **It changes a number in a Deployment.** If the new pod requests `nvidia.com/gpu: 1` and no node has a\n  free GPU, the pod sits Pending until the cluster autoscaler or Karpenter adds a node, subject to cloud\n  quota and stock.\n- **Then the image is pulled and the weights loaded.** KEDA cannot shorten any of that; it only decides\n  earlier.\n- **Plain HTTP scale-to-zero needs a request holder** while the first pod starts. The separate KEDA HTTP\n  add-on provides an interceptor proxy for that, and serverless GPU platforms exist because doing it well\n  is hard.","example":"### ScaledObject for a vLLM chat deployment\nA vLLM Deployment serving an 8B model. A load test showed one replica holds its TTFT target up to about 16\nconcurrent sequences (illustrative: measure your own). The trigger sums running and waiting requests across\nreplicas and targets 12 per replica, keeping headroom for the minutes a new pod takes to arrive.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: keda.sh/v1alpha1\nkind: ScaledObject\nmetadata:\n  name: chat-llm\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    name: vllm-chat                 # the Deployment\n  minReplicaCount: 1                # 0 only if a cold start fits the SLO\n  maxReplicaCount: 8                # match the GPU quota you actually have\n  pollingInterval: 15\n  cooldownPeriod: 900               # only for the final 1 -> 0 step\n  advanced:\n    horizontalPodAutoscalerConfig:\n      behavior:\n        scaleUp:   {stabilizationWindowSeconds: 0}\n        scaleDown:\n          stabilizationWindowSeconds: 600\n          policies: [{type: Pods, value: 1, periodSeconds: 120}]\n  fallback:                         # if Prometheus is unreachable, hold 4 replicas\n    failureThreshold: 3\n    replicas: 4\n  triggers:\n    - type: prometheus\n      metadata:\n        serverAddress: http://prometheus.monitoring:9090\n        query: >\n          sum(vllm:num_requests_running{model_name=\"chat-8b\"})\n          + sum(vllm:num_requests_waiting{model_name=\"chat-8b\"})\n        threshold: \"12\"\n        activationThreshold: \"0\"\n```\n\nWalkthrough of a day:\n\n| Time | Load | What happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| 09:00 | 70 concurrent requests | HPA computes ceil(70 / 12) = 6 replicas and gets there within one or two 15-second policy periods (no stabilisation window; the default scale-up policy allows +4 pods or +100% per period, and the `scaleUp` block above keeps those default policies) |\n| 09:00 | only two GPU nodes free | four pods go Pending until new nodes join, which is why a cron trigger setting 4 replicas at 08:45 is often cheaper than SLO misses |\n| 18:00 | load falls to 10 | HPA removes at most one pod every two minutes after a ten-minute window, so a brief lull does not throw away warm replicas that cost minutes to rebuild |\n\nThe vLLM metric names follow its Prometheus exporter and can change between versions; check `/metrics`.\n\n### Replica calculator: the HPA rule KEDA relies on\nA pure-Python model of the 1↔N loop, handy for choosing a threshold before touching the cluster. It applies\n`ceil(total / threshold)`, the ~10% tolerance and the default scale-up rate limit.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef desired(total, threshold, current, lo=1, hi=8, tolerance=0.1):\n    want = math.ceil(total / threshold)\n    if current and abs(total / (threshold * current) - 1) <= tolerance:\n        want = current                                   # inside tolerance: no change\n    return max(lo, min(hi, want))\n\ndef scale_up_limit(current):\n    return max(current + 4, current * 2)                 # default: +4 pods or +100% per 15 s\n\nreplicas, t = 1, 0\nfor demand in [70, 70, 70]:                              # 09:00 ramp from one warm replica\n    target = desired(demand, 12, replicas)\n    replicas = min(target, scale_up_limit(replicas)) if target > replicas else replicas\n    print(f\"t={t:3d}s demand={demand} target={target} replicas={replicas}\")\n    t += 15\n# t=  0s demand=70 target=6 replicas=5\n# t= 15s demand=70 target=6 replicas=6\n# t= 30s demand=70 target=6 replicas=6\n\nprint(desired(66, 12, 6), desired(80, 12, 6))           # 6 (within 10%), 7 (scale up)\n```","patterns":["**Activate from a signal that exists at zero.** Use whenever `minReplicaCount` is 0. Point the activation trigger at a gateway queue, broker depth or the HTTP add-on's interceptor, not at the engine's own `/metrics`, because those series vanish with the pods. Monitor time from first request to first token after an idle period.","**Cron pre-warm before known peaks.** Use when traffic has a daily shape and GPU nodes take minutes to join. Add a second trigger of type `cron` with a timezone, start and end schedule and a desired replica count (for example 4 replicas from 08:45 on weekdays); the HPA takes the maximum. Monitor TTFT during the morning ramp.","**Cap at real quota and alert on Pending.** Use on every GPU ScaledObject. Set `maxReplicaCount` to the GPUs you can actually get and alert when GPU pods stay Pending (kube-state-metrics pod phase) for more than a few minutes. Monitor replica count against Pending pods so a capacity problem is not reported as a scaling success.","**Drain before scale-down.** Use for any streaming endpoint. Set `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` longer than your longest generation and add a preStop hook that stops new requests and waits for in-flight ones. Monitor truncated or reset streams during scale-down.","**Fallback replicas when the metrics source fails.** Use whenever the trigger depends on Prometheus. Set the `fallback` block (`failureThreshold`, `replicas`) to a safe mid-day count, as in the example. Monitor KEDA scaler errors so the fallback is not silently permanent.","**ScaledJob for queue-drained batch work.** Use for nightly or bursty batch inference. Create one Job per batch of messages from a queue scaler so work is never killed mid-item, with the Job's GPU request in its template. Monitor queue age and Job failures."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    M[\"Demand metric: running + waiting requests\"] --> K[\"KEDA operator, every pollingInterval\"]\n    K -->|\"0 to 1 or 1 to 0 after cooldownPeriod\"| D[\"Deployment replicas\"]\n    M --> MS[\"KEDA metrics server\"]\n    MS --> H[\"Generated HPA: ceil total / threshold\"]\n    H -->|\"1 to N, behavior policies\"| D\n    D --> P{\"Free GPU on a node?\"}\n    P -->|\"no\"| CA[\"Pending: cluster autoscaler adds node\"]\n    CA --> L[\"Image pull and weight load\"]\n    P -->|\"yes\"| L\n    L --> R[\"Ready, serving\"]\n    R -.->|\"metric falls\"| M","caption":"The two loops and what sits after them: KEDA only flips 0↔1 and feeds the HPA, which sets replica counts;\nGPU nodes, image pulls and weight loads still decide when capacity is actually serving."},"interview":[{"q":"What does KEDA add on top of the Kubernetes HPA, and why does an LLM service need it?","a":"- **External signals without plumbing:** ready-made scalers for queues, Prometheus and cloud services, plus\n  its own external-metrics server, so the HPA can scale on demand rather than CPU.\n- **Scale-to-zero:** KEDA owns the 0↔1 activation that a stock HPA cannot do.\n- **Why LLMs:** CPU barely moves and GPU utilisation saturates at one request, so neither tells the\n  autoscaler how much demand there is."},{"q":"Explain how a ScaledObject decides to go from 1 to 6 replicas, and from 6 back to 0.","a":"- **1 to 6:** the generated HPA computes ceil(total metric / threshold), for example ceil(70 / 12) = 6,\n  limited by the scale-up policy of +4 pods or +100% per 15 seconds.\n- **6 to 1:** the HPA again, damped by its scale-down stabilisation window and policies, not by\n  `cooldownPeriod`.\n- **1 to 0:** KEDA itself, once every trigger has been below `activationThreshold` for `cooldownPeriod`."},{"q":"Traffic spiked, KEDA scaled to 8 replicas, but TTFT still breached the SLO for 15 minutes. Debug it.","a":"- **Check Pending pods:** if no GPU node was free, pods waited for the cluster autoscaler, cloud quota and\n  stock; KEDA only decided earlier.\n- **Check ready-time:** image pull and weight loading from slow storage can add minutes per pod.\n- **Fixes:** a cron pre-warm before known peaks, a lower threshold for more headroom, faster weight storage,\n  and `maxReplicaCount` matched to quota with alerts on Pending."},{"q":"When would you not use KEDA for a GPU inference service?","a":"- **Sub-second bursty HTTP traffic** that needs request buffering while scaling from zero; a serverless GPU\n  platform handles that better.\n- **You already run Ray Serve,** whose own autoscaler fills the same role.\n- **You hope it fixes cold starts:** it cannot shorten node provisioning, image pulls or weight loads."},{"q":"How would you pick the threshold for a request-count trigger, and when would you revisit it?","a":"- **Load-test one replica** at realistic prompt and output lengths and find the concurrency where TTFT\n  still meets target (about 16 in the example).\n- **Set the threshold below that knee** (12 in the example) to leave headroom for the minutes a new pod\n  takes to arrive.\n- **Revisit** when the prompt-length distribution shifts, for example longer RAG contexts, or add a\n  KV-usage or token-weighted trigger alongside it."}],"tradeoffs":["**Queue depth vs threshold drift.** It tracks demand linearly and early, but needs a measured per-replica capacity; when prompt lengths shift (longer RAG contexts), the same request count means more load and the threshold drifts.","**Scale-to-zero vs first-request wait.** It saves the full idle cost of a GPU, but the first request after idle waits for node provisioning, image pull and weight loading. It flips to worthwhile only when idle hours dominate and callers tolerate minutes.","**Slow scale-down vs idle cost.** It keeps warm replicas through short lulls at the price of paying for idle GPUs; with per-second billing and fast-loading small models, a shorter window becomes the better bet.","**One KEDA layer vs owning the rest.** It is simpler than adopting Ray Serve or a serverless platform, but you own cold-start mitigation, request buffering and node provisioning yourself."],"pitfalls":["**Scaling a scaled-to-zero vLLM Deployment on vLLM's own metrics.** With zero pods the series vanish, the query returns nothing, and the service never wakes. Fix: activate from a signal that exists without pods (gateway queue, broker depth).","**Writing a per-pod average query while the trigger uses `AverageValue`.** The HPA divides an already-averaged number again; symptom: a deployment stuck near one replica under heavy load. Fix: sum.","**Setting `maxReplicaCount` above real GPU quota.** Symptom: pods Pending for hours while dashboards claim the autoscaler \"scaled up\", hiding a capacity problem behind a scaling one. Fix: cap at quota.","**Expecting `cooldownPeriod` to slow scale-down from 6 to 2 replicas.** It only applies to the last replica; symptom: replicas dropping after five minutes despite a long cooldown. Fix: HPA `behavior`.","**Letting the HPA remove a pod mid-generation** without a long `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` and a drain hook. Symptom: truncated streamed responses every time load falls. Fix: graceful drain."],"when":"- **Use when:** a Kubernetes workload's demand is visible in some external signal and CPU is not it — LLM\n  servers, embedding workers draining a queue, nightly batch inference as ScaledJobs, or anything that\n  should sit at zero outside working hours.\n- **Avoid when:** you want it to fix cold starts (it cannot), for sub-second bursty HTTP traffic that needs\n  request buffering (look at serverless GPUs), or when you already run Ray Serve, whose own autoscaler\n  fills the same role.\n- **Measure:** per-replica capacity under realistic prompt lengths before choosing a threshold, then the\n  replica count against queue depth, Pending pods, and TTFT during ramps.\n- **Interviews:** the signal is explaining why GPU utilisation is the wrong metric and where the 0↔1 and\n  1↔N loops split.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the ScaledObject, chooses the Prometheus query and threshold from a load test, and adds graceful drain so scale-down never cuts off a streaming response.","architect":"Decides which services may scale to zero, the scaling signal per workload class, and how pod autoscaling couples to node provisioning, GPU quota and warm-pool policy.","fde":"Installs KEDA inside a customer's existing cluster, maps their queue or gateway metrics to triggers, and explains why the first morning request is slow unless a cron pre-warm is added."},"check":{"q":"A team autoscales its vLLM pods with an HPA on GPU utilisation targeting 70%. The deployment\nsits at its maximum replica count even overnight, when traffic is a fraction of the daytime peak, and\nthe GPU bill is huge.\nExplain what is happening and design a better setup.","a":"GPU \"utilisation\" reports the fraction of time any kernel is running, so it reads near 100% as\nsoon as one or two requests are decoding on each replica and says nothing about how many more could fit. The\nHPA sees every replica above target, scales up, and never finds a reason to scale down; the signal\ncarries no proportional information, so capacity is set by the cap rather than by demand. Better: a\nKEDA ScaledObject on running plus waiting requests summed across\nreplicas, threshold set from a load test at below-knee concurrency, fast scale-up, slow damped\nscale-down, `maxReplicaCount` equal to GPU quota, a cron trigger to pre-warm before peaks, and a node\nautoscaler with GPU node pools. If scale-to-zero is wanted, activate from a gateway or queue metric."},"quiz":[{"q":"In a KEDA ScaledObject, which mechanism controls scaling from 5 replicas down to 3?","options":["The `cooldownPeriod` field","The HPA that KEDA generates, damped by its scale-down behavior and stabilisation window","The `pollingInterval` field alone","The cluster autoscaler removing GPU nodes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"KEDA itself handles only activation between zero and one replica, and `cooldownPeriod` applies to that final step. Between one and N, the generated HPA decides, using its behavior policies and window."},{"q":"Why is in-flight plus waiting request count a better trigger for an LLM server than p95 latency?","options":["Latency metrics cannot be exported to Prometheus","KEDA does not support latency-based triggers","Concurrency rises linearly and immediately with demand, while latency lags and spikes near saturation, causing oscillation","Request counts are unaffected by prompt length"],"answer":2,"explanation":"By Little's law concurrency tracks arrival rate times service time, so it is a leading signal. Latency stays flat until the queueing knee, then jumps, and reacts only after slow GPU pods arrive."},{"q":"A ScaledObject scales a vLLM Deployment to zero using a Prometheus query over vLLM's own metrics. What happens when traffic returns?","options":["KEDA buffers the requests and replays them once a pod is ready","The service may never wake, because with zero pods the metric series do not exist to cross the activation threshold","The HPA scales from zero to one on its own","The cluster autoscaler detects pending requests and starts a pod"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A trigger must be observable while nothing is running. Activate from a gateway, queue or broker metric, or use the HTTP add-on; the stock HPA does not scale from zero, and nothing buffers requests by default."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/ray-kuberay","sources":["Moritz et al., 2018 — Ray: A Distributed Framework for Emerging AI Applications","Official docs: Ray","Official docs: KubeRay"],"why":"- **AI workloads rarely fit one box or one shape.** Batch inference over ten million documents needs CPU\n  workers to read and tokenise, GPU workers that each load a model once and keep it resident, and\n  back-pressure so GPUs are neither starved nor flooded. Hyperparameter search launches hundreds of short\n  trials; multi-node serving needs GPUs placed together and started as a unit; RL interleaves simulators,\n  learners and inference.\n- **Job queues have no notion of a resident model.** Celery or SQS workers cannot express \"this worker holds\n  a 16 GB model and should keep it\"; each task either reloads weights or you build actor management yourself.\n- **Spark fights the GPU pattern.** It was built for CPU data-parallel stages with a barrier between them;\n  feeding GPUs a continuous stream and keeping a model warm per executor fights its execution model.\n- **Raw Kubernetes and multiprocessing stop short.** Kubernetes gives pods, not a way for one Python program\n  to fan out ten thousand fine-grained calls and pass large arrays without serialising through a database.\n  Multiprocessing stops at one machine.\n- **Ray plus KubeRay closes the gap.** Ray offers tasks, actors and objects with a resource-aware scheduler,\n  plus libraries for data, training, tuning and serving. KubeRay creates head and worker pods, wires up\n  autoscaling and upgrades serving clusters without downtime via CRDs, so Kubernetes stays the control plane\n  while ML code stays Python.","how":"### Core primitives\n| Primitive | What it is | Why it matters |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Task** | A `@ray.remote` function. Calling `f.remote(x)` returns immediately with an `ObjectRef` (a future); the scheduler places it on any node with the requested resources | Stateless, so it can be retried elsewhere on failure |\n| **Actor** | A `@ray.remote` class. Instantiating it starts a dedicated worker process that keeps state between method calls | Exactly how a model stays loaded on a GPU across thousands of batches |\n| **Object** | Task results in a per-node shared-memory object store | Passing an `ObjectRef` moves bytes node-to-node only when needed; same-node readers share memory without copies |\n\nResults that do not fit the object store spill to disk, which is slow but avoids crashes.\n\n### Scheduling by declared resources\n- **Declare, then place.** Each task or actor states `num_cpus`, `num_gpus`, memory or custom resources,\n  and the scheduler only places it where those are free.\n- **Reservations are *logical*.** `num_gpus=1` sets `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` for the worker but does not stop\n  it using memory it was not granted; `num_gpus=0.5` lets two actors share a card with no memory isolation.\n- **Placement groups** reserve bundles atomically (gang scheduling). Strategies:\n  - `STRICT_PACK`: all bundles on one node, for tensor parallelism over NVLink (`PACK` is only a\n    best-effort preference for that);\n  - `SPREAD`: fault isolation.\n- **Engines like vLLM** use Ray for multi-node tensor and pipeline parallelism for exactly this.\n\n### Cluster anatomy\n- **Head node:** runs the Global Control Service (GCS, cluster metadata; the original paper called it the\n  Global Control Store), the dashboard, the job-submission API and the autoscaler.\n- **Worker nodes:** each runs a raylet (local scheduler and object store).\n- **The head is a single point of failure:** lose it and, by default, the cluster's state goes with it.\n- **The autoscaler scales on *pending logical demand*, not utilisation.** If tasks are queued asking for GPUs\n  that no node has free, it adds workers; idle workers are removed after a timeout.\n\n### Failure semantics\nRay's recovery model follows from the primitives:\n\n| What fails | What Ray does | What you must do |\n|---|---|---|\n| Task | Retried (by default a few times) on any node with resources, *because* it is stateless | Make batches idempotent |\n| Lost object | Rebuilt by re-running the tasks that produced it (lineage reconstruction) | Works for task outputs, not for objects created with `ray.put` |\n| Actor | Can be restarted (`max_restarts`), but its in-memory state is gone | A model actor reloads its weights; checkpoint per-actor progress in your code |\n| Owner worker | Every object has an owner, the worker that created the reference; if it dies, its objects are lost even if copies survive elsewhere | Avoid long-lived refs owned by short-lived workers |\n| Head node | Not automatically survivable | Treat it as the one component that needs explicit fault tolerance |\n\nDesign batch pipelines so a retried batch is idempotent and long runs checkpoint their output.\n\n### Libraries\n- **Ray Data** streams datasets through CPU and GPU stages with back-pressure, the usual tool for offline\n  batch inference and embedding.\n- **Ray Train** and **Ray Tune** wrap distributed training and hyperparameter search.\n- **Ray Serve** runs deployments as replica actors with request-based autoscaling (a target of ongoing\n  requests per replica), model composition and multiplexing.\n\n### KubeRay\nKubeRay adds three CRDs:\n\n| CRD | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **RayCluster** | Declares a head group and worker groups (each with pod template, `minReplicas`, `maxReplicas`). With in-tree autoscaling enabled, an autoscaler sidecar in the head pod edits worker-group replica counts; a node autoscaler (cluster autoscaler or Karpenter) supplies GPU nodes |\n| **RayJob** | Creates a cluster, submits an entrypoint and can tear the cluster down when it finishes, so a nightly batch run pays for GPUs only while it runs |\n| **RayService** | Runs a Serve application. Cluster-spec upgrades bring up a new cluster, wait until it is healthy, then switch traffic; changes to only the Serve config are applied in place on the running cluster |\n\n- **Why an operator and not a Deployment:** scale-down. Ray must choose *which* worker to remove (an idle one\n  holding no actors or primary object copies).\n- **So the autoscaler names specific pods** for KubeRay to delete, instead of decrementing a replica count and\n  letting Kubernetes pick one at random.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: ray.io/v1\nkind: RayJob\nmetadata: {name: nightly-embed}\nspec:\n  entrypoint: python embed.py\n  shutdownAfterJobFinishes: true\n  rayClusterSpec:\n    enableInTreeAutoscaling: true\n    headGroupSpec: {rayStartParams: {}, template: {...}}         # CPU-only head\n    workerGroupSpecs:\n      - groupName: gpu\n        minReplicas: 0\n        maxReplicas: 8\n        rayStartParams: {}\n        template: {...}                                          # requests nvidia.com/gpu: 1\n```","example":"### Batch embedding of 10 million documents with Ray Data\nCPU stages read and clean; four GPU actors each load the model once and receive batches as they are\nready. Under the RayJob above, the autoscaler sees demand for four GPUs, raises the worker group to four,\nand the job deletes the cluster when done.\n\n```python\nimport ray\n\nclass Embedder:\n    def __init__(self):\n        # Runs once per actor: the weights stay resident on that actor's GPU.\n        from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n        self.model = SentenceTransformer(\"all-MiniLM-L6-v2\", device=\"cuda\")\n\n    def __call__(self, batch: dict) -> dict:\n        texts = [str(t)[:2000] for t in batch[\"text\"]]\n        batch[\"embedding\"] = self.model.encode(texts, batch_size=128)  # (n, 384) array\n        return batch\n\nray.init()  # inside a RayJob this connects to the cluster it created\n\nds = ray.data.read_parquet(\"s3://corpus/docs/\")                    # streamed, CPU tasks\nds = ds.filter(lambda row: row[\"text\"] is not None)                # CPU tasks\nds = ds.map_batches(Embedder, batch_size=512, num_gpus=1, concurrency=4)  # 4 GPU actors\nds.write_parquet(\"s3://corpus/embeddings/\")                         # triggers execution\n\n# Ray Data executes lazily and streams: reading, filtering and embedding overlap, and\n# back-pressure stops readers from filling the object store when GPUs fall behind.\n```\n\n- **Rough cost reasoning (illustrative):** if one GPU actor sustains about 1,000 documents per second, four\n  actors finish 10 million in roughly 40 minutes plus cluster start-up.\n- **`shutdownAfterJobFinishes`** means you pay for about that long, not for an always-on pool.\n- **Version note:** the argument names (`concurrency`, `num_gpus`) follow recent Ray Data releases and have\n  shifted between versions; check yours.\n\n### Worked sizing: actor count versus wall-clock and GPU-hours\nSame illustrative 1,000 documents per second per GPU actor, 10 million documents. Start-up (pods, image\npull, model load) is a fixed cost per run, shown here as an assumed 5 minutes.\n\n| GPU actors | Embedding time | Wall-clock with start-up | GPU-hours billed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 10,000 s ≈ 167 min | ≈ 172 min | ≈ 2.9 |\n| 4 | 2,500 s ≈ 42 min | ≈ 47 min | ≈ 3.1 |\n| 8 | 1,250 s ≈ 21 min | ≈ 26 min | ≈ 3.4 |\n\n- **More actors buy wall-clock, not savings:** GPU-hours stay roughly flat because the work is the same;\n  start-up overhead grows with actor count.\n- **Check the CPU side first:** eight GPU actors only help if reads and filtering keep up; otherwise the\n  dashboard shows idle actors and you pay for waiting GPUs.\n\n### Ray Serve deployment with request-based autoscaling\nA Serve application whose replicas are GPU actors and whose replica count follows ongoing requests, the\nRay-native counterpart to a KEDA queue-depth trigger. Parameter names follow recent Ray 2.x releases.\n\n```python\nfrom ray import serve\n\n@serve.deployment(\n    ray_actor_options={\"num_gpus\": 1},          # one card per replica actor\n    autoscaling_config={\n        \"min_replicas\": 1,\n        \"max_replicas\": 4,                       # match the GPU worker group's maxReplicas\n        \"target_ongoing_requests\": 8,            # in-flight requests per replica before scaling\n    },\n)\nclass Reranker:\n    def __init__(self):\n        from sentence_transformers import CrossEncoder\n        self.model = CrossEncoder(\"cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2\", device=\"cuda\")\n\n    async def __call__(self, request):\n        body = await request.json()\n        pairs = [(body[\"query\"], d) for d in body[\"docs\"]]\n        return {\"scores\": self.model.predict(pairs).tolist()}\n\napp = Reranker.bind()       # deploy with `serve run module:app`, or from a RayService spec\n```","patterns":["**Pin one Ray and Python version everywhere.** Use on every cluster. Build head, worker and client images from the same Ray release and Python minor version, and pin the image tag in the RayCluster or RayJob spec. Monitor job-submission failures after any image change, since mismatches surface as cryptic deserialisation errors.","**Ephemeral RayJob clusters for batch.** Use for nightly or ad-hoc batch inference. Set `shutdownAfterJobFinishes: true`, GPU worker groups with `minReplicas: 0`, and an upper bound matching quota. Monitor GPU-hours per run against the sizing table, and cluster start-up time as a fixed overhead.","**GCS fault tolerance for RayService.** Use for any production Serve application. Enable KubeRay's GCS fault tolerance backed by an external Redis so a head-pod restart does not take every Serve replica with it. Monitor head-pod restarts and Serve availability during them.","**Keep the dashboard and job API internal.** Use always. Expose the dashboard and job-submission service as cluster-internal only, behind a network policy, and reach it through port-forwarding or an authenticated proxy. Monitor for unknown job submissions.","**Declare GPUs to match what code uses.** Use for every actor and task. Set `num_gpus` from what the code actually loads, and use fractional values only for models whose summed memory fits the card. Monitor actor busy time and GPU memory per process in the dashboard.","**Separate worker groups per GPU tier.** Use when jobs need different cards. Define one worker group per card type with a node selector, plus custom resources so actors target the right group. Monitor pending demand per group to see which tier is the bottleneck."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    J[\"RayJob custom resource\"] --> O[\"KubeRay operator\"]\n    O --> H[\"Head pod: GCS, dashboard, autoscaler\"]\n    H --> Q[\"Pending actors ask for num_gpus 1\"]\n    Q --> A[\"Ray autoscaler: unmet logical demand\"]\n    A -->|\"raise gpu worker group replicas\"| O\n    O --> W[\"GPU worker pods\"]\n    W -->|\"no free GPU node\"| N[\"Node autoscaler adds GPU node\"]\n    N --> W\n    W --> R[\"Actors load model once, process batches\"]\n    R -->|\"job finishes\"| T[\"shutdownAfterJobFinishes: cluster deleted\"]","caption":"How a RayJob turns pending GPU demand into running actors: Ray's autoscaler reacts to unplaceable\nresource requests, KubeRay creates the named pods, and the node autoscaler supplies the GPU machines."},"interview":[{"q":"Explain tasks, actors and objects in Ray, and which you would use for GPU inference.","a":"- **Tasks** are stateless remote functions returning `ObjectRef` futures, retried elsewhere on failure.\n- **Actors** are long-lived worker processes that keep state across calls, so a model loads once and stays\n  resident on its GPU; that makes them the right primitive for inference workers.\n- **Objects** live in a per-node shared-memory store and move between nodes only when a consumer needs them."},{"q":"Why does the Ray autoscaler react to pending demand rather than GPU utilisation, and what goes wrong because of it?","a":"- **Ray schedules logical resources:** a task or actor declares `num_gpus` and waits until a node can satisfy\n  it, so unmet declarations are the demand signal.\n- **The failure mode:** declarations that do not match the code. Over-declaring holds idle GPU nodes;\n  under-declaring with fractional GPUs lets two processes OOM one card.\n- **So** sizing `num_gpus` correctly is a capacity decision, not a formality."},{"q":"A RayService head pod restarted and every Serve replica went down. What happened and how do you prevent it?","a":"- **Cause:** the head runs the GCS, which holds cluster metadata; without fault tolerance its loss takes the\n  cluster state, and the Serve application, with it.\n- **Prevention:** enable KubeRay's GCS fault tolerance with an external Redis so workers and replicas\n  survive a head restart.\n- **Also** keep the head CPU-only and resource-guarded so GPU work cannot starve or evict it."},{"q":"When would you choose a plain Deployment with KEDA over Ray Serve on KubeRay?","a":"- **One stateless model per pod:** an inference engine Deployment scaled on queue depth is less to operate,\n  with no second scheduler or head node.\n- **Choose Ray Serve** when you need model composition, multiplexing, multi-node replicas with placement\n  groups, or one Python codebase spanning batch and online.\n- **Cost of Ray:** coupling to its versioning and head-node fault tolerance, which a platform team must own."},{"q":"How would you size a RayJob to embed 10 million documents overnight, and what would you monitor?","a":"- **Measure one actor's throughput,** for example an illustrative 1,000 documents per second, then pick actor\n  count from the deadline: four actors take about 42 minutes, eight about 21.\n- **GPU-hours stay roughly flat** as actors increase, so the choice trades wall-clock for start-up overhead\n  and quota.\n- **Monitor** actor busy time (CPU stages keeping up), object-store spilling, and per-GPU throughput before\n  scaling out further."}],"tradeoffs":["**One Python program vs a second scheduler.** It can express a whole CPU-to-GPU pipeline with stateful model workers, but you take on a second scheduler inside Kubernetes; for a single stateless model server, a Deployment plus KEDA is simpler.","**Ray Serve's features vs coupling.** It gives request-aware autoscaling, composition and multi-node replicas in one framework, but ties serving to Ray's versioning and head node; a dedicated engine behind a gateway wins when one model per pod suffices.","**Fractional GPUs vs isolation.** They pack small models densely and raise utilisation, but sharing is unenforced; it flips to harmful when one actor's memory grows and OOMs its neighbour.","**Ephemeral RayJob clusters vs start-up cost.** They cost nothing while idle, but every run pays start-up and image pull; for frequent short jobs a long-lived RayCluster with autoscaling to zero workers is cheaper."],"pitfalls":["**Mismatched Ray or Python versions** between the client, head image and worker images. Symptom: cryptic connection or deserialisation errors at job submission rather than a clear version message. Fix: pin one version everywhere.","**Exposing the dashboard or job-submission port beyond the cluster.** These endpoints have historically had no authentication by default and accept arbitrary code (check what your Ray version offers). Symptom: unknown jobs (often crypto-mining) on your GPUs. Fix: internal-only access.","**Declaring `num_gpus` that do not match what the code uses.** The autoscaler provisions by declarations, so the symptom is idle GPU nodes held by actors or, conversely, two processes OOMing one card. Fix: declare what the code loads.","**Running GCS on the head without fault tolerance for a RayService.** Symptom: a head-pod restart taking every Serve replica with it. Fix: KubeRay supports external-Redis GCS fault tolerance for this.","**Returning huge objects from tasks and holding the refs.** Symptom: object-store spilling to disk and a pipeline that slows by orders of magnitude without erroring. Fix: write large outputs to storage, stream."],"when":"- **Use Ray when:** the workload is Python, distributed and heterogeneous — batch inference or embedding over\n  large datasets with GPU actors, hyperparameter sweeps, RL, multi-node model serving, or pipelines that mix\n  CPU and GPU stages with state.\n- **Use KubeRay when:** that runs on Kubernetes and you want the cluster lifecycle declared in Git.\n- **Avoid when:** it is a single stateless model endpoint (an inference engine Deployment with KEDA is less\n  to operate) or SQL-shaped ETL (Spark or a warehouse).\n- **Measure:** GPU-actor busy time and object-store pressure in the dashboard.\n- **Interviews:** explain tasks versus actors, logical-resource scheduling, and why the autoscaler reacts to\n  pending demand.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes Ray Data pipelines and Serve deployments, sizes `num_gpus`, batch size and actor count, and reads the dashboard to find whether the CPU or GPU stage is the bottleneck.","architect":"Decides whether Ray becomes a platform layer on Kubernetes or stays a per-job tool, and owns head-node fault tolerance, version pinning, network exposure and GPU quota per worker group.","fde":"Deploys KubeRay into a customer's cluster, adapts worker-group templates to their GPU node pools and security policy, and locks the dashboard behind internal networking before handover."},"check":{"q":"A team runs nightly embedding of 20 million chunks with a Celery queue: each task loads the model,\nembeds 100 chunks and exits. GPUs show low utilisation and the job takes all night. How would you\nrestructure it with Ray on KubeRay, and why would it be faster and cheaper?","a":"The waste is reloading weights per task and idle GPUs between tasks, with CPU-side work (reads,\ntokenisation) serialised in front of the GPU. With Ray Data, model loading moves into an actor\nconstructor, so each GPU loads once; `map_batches` with a fixed actor pool streams large batches,\noverlapping S3 reads and filtering on CPU tasks with GPU encoding under back-pressure. A RayJob with an\nautoscaling GPU worker group sized from measured per-actor throughput, minimum zero and\n`shutdownAfterJobFinishes`, means GPUs exist only during the run. Validate by checking actor busy time,\nobject-store spilling, and per-GPU throughput before scaling out."},"quiz":[{"q":"What distinguishes a Ray actor from a Ray task?","options":["Actors run on GPUs while tasks run only on CPUs","An actor is a long-lived worker process that keeps state across method calls, while a task is a stateless function call","Tasks are scheduled by Kubernetes while actors are scheduled by Ray","Actors cannot return ObjectRefs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Both can request any resources. The difference is state and lifetime: an actor holds, for example, a loaded model between calls, which is why GPU inference workers are actors rather than tasks."},{"q":"On what signal does the Ray autoscaler (as used by KubeRay) add worker nodes?","options":["Average GPU utilisation across the cluster","CPU usage of the head pod","Pending tasks and actors whose declared resource requests cannot be placed on existing nodes","The number of HTTP requests reaching the dashboard"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ray schedules logical resources, so the autoscaler reacts to unmet declared demand. Real utilisation is not consulted, which is why wrong `num_gpus` declarations directly cause over- or under-provisioning."},{"q":"A team gives two model actors `num_gpus=0.5` each on one 24 GB GPU. What does Ray guarantee?","options":["Each actor is limited to 12 GB of GPU memory","The GPU is time-sliced so each actor gets exactly half the compute","Only that both are scheduled onto the same GPU; memory and compute are not isolated","Ray will refuse the placement because GPUs cannot be shared"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fractional GPUs are bookkeeping for placement. The processes share the card freely, so if one grows its memory use, the other can hit out-of-memory errors; isolation needs MIG or framework-level memory caps."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/inference-clusters","sources":["Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Zhong et al., 2024 — DistServe: Disaggregating Prefill and Decoding for Goodput-optimized LLM Serving","Official docs: vLLM","Official docs: Kubernetes"],"why":"- **One replica has a hard ceiling.** Weights plus KV cache must fit in VRAM, decode is bound by memory\n  bandwidth, and the number of concurrent sequences that fit sets both throughput and tail latency. Past\n  that, the only moves are a bigger GPU (there is a largest one) or more replicas, which means a cluster.\n- **Requests are not interchangeable.** The naive fix is \"same load balancer as the API tier, autoscale\n  on CPU\". But one request may be a 200-token chat turn and the next a 60,000-token document summary;\n  round-robin sends the long one to a saturated replica and everyone's tail latency there collapses.\n- **CPU, even GPU utilization, is the wrong signal.** The useful capacity signal is how full the KV cache\n  is and how deep the engine's waiting queue is, since a GPU at 40% SM utilization can still be unable\n  to admit another sequence.\n- **The cache makes replicas stateful.** A request whose prefix is cached on replica A costs a full\n  prefill on replica B, so routing affects cost, not just balance.\n- **Some models exceed one GPU.** A 70B model in fp16 is ~140 GB of weights, so it must be split across\n  several GPUs acting as one replica: a topology decision, not a scaling knob. A cluster answers all of\n  these on purpose.","how":"### The layers\nA cluster is organised as nodes → replicas → engines, with a router in front and a scheduler behind.\nEach layer answers a different question.\n\n1. **Replica.** One running instance of a serving engine with one copy of the model. If the model does\n   not fit on one GPU, the replica spans several GPUs on the same node using tensor parallelism: each\n   layer's matrices are sliced across GPUs and the shards exchange activations every layer.\n2. **Model pool.** All replicas of one model and one configuration (quantization, max context,\n   adapters). Scaling happens per pool, because a 7B chat model and a 70B reasoning model have\n   completely different capacity curves and traffic.\n3. **Router / gateway.** Receives requests, picks a pool by model name, picks a replica inside the pool,\n   streams tokens back. This is where LLM-aware routing lives.\n4. **Scheduler inside the engine.** Continuous batching admits new sequences into the running batch each\n   step as others finish; it is the reason throughput grows with concurrency until the KV cache is full.\n5. **Orchestrator.** Kubernetes (often with KubeRay or a serving layer such as KServe) places replicas on\n   nodes with the right GPU type and count, restarts failures, and runs the autoscaler.\n\n### Where tensor parallelism stops\n- **TP stays inside a node** because it needs an all-reduce per layer per token, which is only affordable\n  over NVLink-class bandwidth. Crossing the network would make every decode step wait on the slowest link.\n- **Rack-scale systems** whose NVLink domain spans several trays move that boundary from \"node\" to\n  \"NVLink domain\", but the rule is the same.\n- **Pipeline parallelism across nodes** is used only when a model cannot fit in one node or NVLink domain.\n\n### Routing that knows what a request costs\nA good router does not round-robin. The usual signals, in rough order of value:\n\n| Signal | What it does | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Queue depth and in-flight sequences per replica (\"least-loaded\") | Avoids replicas at their concurrency cap | A long request on a full replica queues everyone behind it |\n| Prefix-cache affinity | Hashes shared system prompts or conversations to the replica holding that prefix | Turns a full prefill into a cache hit |\n| Session stickiness | Keeps a multi-turn chat on one replica | A special case of prefix affinity |\n| Priority classes | Admits interactive traffic ahead of batch jobs sharing the pool | Batch can wait, users cannot |\n\n- **Replicas expose the load signals as metrics.** vLLM publishes running/waiting request counts and\n  KV-cache usage.\n- **Simple affinity** is consistent (or rendezvous) hashing on a fixed-length prompt prefix, so adding a\n  replica remaps only a small share of keys.\n- **Advanced affinity** tracks which prefixes each replica actually holds, for example a radix tree of\n  cached prefixes.\n- **Always spill over** to least-loaded when the preferred replica is saturated, *because* affinity is\n  worth little if it adds queueing delay.\n\n### Disaggregated prefill and decode\n- **The phases differ.** Prefill is compute-bound (big matrix multiplies over the whole prompt); decode is\n  bandwidth-bound (one token per step, reading the weights and the whole cache).\n- **Sharing a replica hurts decode.** A 40k-token prefill stalls every decode stream on that GPU for\n  hundreds of milliseconds to several seconds depending on model size, which shows up as TPOT jitter.\n- **First mitigation: chunked prefill.** It splits a long prompt into pieces interleaved with decode\n  steps, inside one replica.\n- **At scale: split the phases.** Prefill replicas compute the KV cache and ship it over a fast\n  interconnect to decode replicas, each class sized and scaled separately. The cost is the KV transfer\n  and more moving parts, so it pays only at scale with long prompts.\n\n### Scaling on the right signal\n- **Read engine metrics:** waiting-queue length, KV-cache utilization, TTFT, or tokens/second against the\n  replica's measured capacity.\n- **CPU is meaningless and GPU utilization misleading,** since a replica with its KV cache full of\n  idle-but-live sessions is \"available\" by utilization and \"full\" by admission.\n- **Scale-up is slow.** Pulling tens of GB of weights and warming the engine can take minutes, so the\n  cluster needs headroom and predictive or scheduled scaling rather than purely reactive rules.\n- **Scale-down must drain** in-flight streams before killing a pod.\n\n### Node topology and placement\n- **Pin replicas to the right node pool.** Replicas request whole GPUs (or MIG slices for small models),\n  because a 70B replica scheduled onto an L4 node simply fails.\n- **Spread vs pack.** Spreading a pool's replicas across nodes and zones limits blast radius from a node\n  failure; packing them raises the chance that one node loss removes a whole pool. Both are deliberate.\n\n### Admission control, failure and rollout\n- **Something must say no, and it should be the gateway.** When every replica is at its concurrency cap,\n  engines queue internally without bound by default. Overload looks like slowly rising TTFT until clients\n  time out and retry, which adds load.\n- **Bound the wait.** Reject with 429 and a `Retry-After` once the expected queue time exceeds the TTFT\n  budget, or shed low-priority traffic first, *because* a request that will miss its deadline is cheaper\n  to refuse than to serve late.\n- **Rollouts are a capacity event.** A rolling update of a pool with no spare removes one replica per\n  step, and the new replica arrives with a cold prefix cache, so the pool is smaller and slower until it\n  warms. That is why the N+1 spare doubles as rollout surge capacity.\n- **Model version changes go blue/green.** New weights and behaviour ship as a separate pool with traffic\n  shifted by weight, so rollback is a routing change rather than a redeploy that pulls tens of GB again.","example":"### Sizing a pool and routing inside it\nThe capacity numbers are illustrative placeholders you would replace with your own load-test results.\n\n```python\nimport math, hashlib\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass\nclass Replica:\n    name: str\n    max_seqs: int            # concurrency at which p95 TPOT still meets the budget (load-tested)\n    running: int = 0\n    hot_prefixes: set = field(default_factory=set)\n\ndef replicas_needed(peak_out_tok_s, out_tok_s_per_replica, headroom=1.3, n_plus=1):\n    \"\"\"Pool size from peak decode demand, with headroom for burst and N+1 for a node loss.\"\"\"\n    return math.ceil(peak_out_tok_s * headroom / out_tok_s_per_replica) + n_plus\n\n# Peak 6,000 output tok/s; a 2xH100 TP=2 replica sustains ~1,500 tok/s within budget (illustrative).\nprint(\"replicas:\", replicas_needed(6_000, 1_500))      # -> ceil(5.2)+1 = 7\n\npool = [Replica(f\"r{i}\", max_seqs=48) for i in range(7)]\n\ndef route(prompt: str, prefix_chars: int = 2048) -> Replica:\n    # Affinity only works if the shared prefix (system prompt) is at least prefix_chars long.\n    key = hashlib.sha1(prompt[:prefix_chars].encode()).hexdigest()\n    preferred = pool[int(key, 16) % len(pool)]  # modulo for brevity; use consistent hashing in prod\n    if preferred.running < preferred.max_seqs:\n        preferred.hot_prefixes.add(key); preferred.running += 1\n        return preferred\n    # Affinity is not worth queueing for: spill to the least-loaded replica with room.\n    cands = [r for r in pool if r.running < r.max_seqs]\n    if not cands:\n        raise RuntimeError(\"pool saturated: shed or queue at the gateway\")\n    best = min(cands, key=lambda r: r.running); best.running += 1\n    return best\n\ndef finished(r: Replica):             # call when the stream ends, or slots leak and the pool \"fills\"\n    r.running -= 1\n```\n\n- **Assumption 1, made explicit:** a replica's capacity is the concurrency at which it still meets the\n  latency budget, not its raw maximum.\n- **Assumption 2:** one extra replica covers a node loss without breaching the budget. If one node hosts\n  several replicas, N+1 must be counted in nodes, not replicas.\n- **Routing:** the router prefers the replica that already holds the prompt prefix, but only while it has\n  admission room.\n- **The modulo hash is a shortcut.** When the pool resizes, almost every key moves and cache affinity\n  resets, which is why real routers use consistent or rendezvous hashing.\n\n### Worked scenario: N+1 counted in nodes, not replicas\nSame demand as above (illustrative numbers), placed on 8-GPU nodes where each TP=2 replica uses 2 GPUs,\nso one node hosts 4 replicas.\n\n| Step | Calculation | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Demand with headroom | 6,000 tok/s × 1.3 | 7,800 tok/s |\n| Replicas for demand | 7,800 ÷ 1,500 = 5.2, rounded up | 6 replicas |\n| Naive N+1 in replicas | 6 + 1 | 7 replicas = 14 GPUs on 2 nodes |\n| What one node loss removes | 4 replicas on that node | only 3 left, 4,500 tok/s: budget breached |\n| N+1 in nodes | ceil(6 ÷ 4) = 2 nodes, plus 1 spare node | 3 nodes, 12 replicas, 24 GPUs |\n\n- **The lesson:** the spare must be the unit that fails. Spreading 7 replicas across more, smaller nodes\n  is the other way to shrink what one failure takes out.","patterns":["**Scale on queue depth, not CPU.** For any pool with variable traffic, drive a KEDA `ScaledObject` with a Prometheus trigger on the engine's waiting-request metric (vLLM exports `vllm:num_requests_waiting`), threshold taken from the replica's load test, minimum replicas at N+1. Monitor queue depth, TTFT p95 and how long a new pod takes to become ready.","**Gate readiness on the engine, not the container.** Point the Kubernetes readiness probe at the engine's health endpoint and give a startup probe enough time for weight loading and warm-up, so new replicas take traffic only when serving. Monitor errors and timeouts in the minutes after scale-up.","**Drain before termination.** Remove the pod from the router first, then let in-flight streams finish: a `preStop` hook plus `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` longer than your longest expected stream. Monitor truncated streams and client retries on each scale-down.","**Prefix affinity with spillover.** Hash a fixed-length prompt prefix with consistent or rendezvous hashing and fall back to least-loaded when the preferred replica is at its cap. Monitor prefix-cache hit rate and per-replica load skew.","**Admission at the gateway.** Return 429 with `Retry-After` once estimated queue time exceeds the TTFT budget, shedding batch priority first. Monitor 429 rate per priority class and client retry volume.","**Blue/green pools for model versions.** Deploy the new weights as a second pool and shift traffic by weight at the gateway; rollback is a routing change. Monitor error rate, latency and eval scores per pool during the shift."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Client request\"] --> G[\"Gateway: admission control\"]\n    G -->|\"expected wait over TTFT budget\"| R[\"429 with Retry-After\"]\n    G --> P[\"Pick pool by model name\"]\n    P --> A{\"Prefix replica has room?\"}\n    A -->|\"yes\"| H[\"Prefix-affinity replica\"]\n    A -->|\"no\"| L[\"Least-loaded replica\"]\n    H --> E[\"Engine: continuous batching\"]\n    L --> E\n    E --> KV[(\"KV cache\")]\n    E -.->|\"queue depth, KV usage\"| S[\"Autoscaler\"]\n    S -.->|\"add or drain replicas\"| P","caption":"Follow one request: the gateway decides whether to admit it, the router prefers the replica holding its\nprefix but spills over when that replica is full, and engine metrics drive the autoscaler."},"interview":[{"q":"What are a replica, a model pool and a node in an inference cluster, and why separate them?","a":"- **Replica:** one engine instance with one copy of the model, possibly spanning several GPUs with tensor\n  parallelism. It is the unit of capacity you load-test.\n- **Model pool:** all replicas of one model and configuration. It is the unit you scale and route to,\n  because different models have different capacity curves.\n- **Node:** the machine the replicas run on. It is the unit that fails, so N+1 spares and zone spreading\n  are planned in nodes."},{"q":"When would you run a model as a few large tensor-parallel replicas instead of many TP=1 replicas?","a":"- **When weights do not fit on one GPU,** TP is mandatory: a 70B fp16 model needs ~140 GB.\n- **When per-replica throughput or latency matters more** than fine-grained scaling, larger TP groups can\n  help, but they are not N independent GPUs and a failure removes more capacity.\n- **Keep TP inside a node or NVLink domain,** because it needs an all-reduce every layer for every token;\n  use pipeline parallelism only when one node is not enough."},{"q":"Users report TPOT jitter whenever someone submits a very long document. What is happening and what do you do?","a":"- **Diagnosis:** a long prefill (for example 40k tokens) is compute-bound and stalls every decode stream\n  sharing that GPU for hundreds of milliseconds or more.\n- **First fix:** enable chunked prefill so the prompt is processed in pieces interleaved with decode steps.\n- **At scale:** route long-document traffic to its own pool, or disaggregate prefill and decode replicas,\n  accepting the KV-transfer cost and an extra pool to run."},{"q":"Peak demand is 6,000 output tokens per second. How do you size the pool?","a":"- **Measure first:** load-test one replica shape to find the throughput at which p95 TPOT still meets the\n  budget, say ~1,500 tok/s for a TP=2 replica (illustrative).\n- **Add headroom:** 6,000 × 1.3 ÷ 1,500 = 5.2, so 6 replicas for demand.\n- **Add the spare in failure units:** +1 replica if each node hosts one, or a whole spare node if a node\n  hosts several replicas."},{"q":"How do you ship a new model version on a GPU pool without a capacity dip or a slow rollback?","a":"- **Do not rolling-update a pool with no spare:** each step removes a replica and the new one has a cold\n  prefix cache, so the pool is smaller and slower until it warms.\n- **Go blue/green:** stand up the new version as a separate pool, gate readiness on warm-up, and shift\n  traffic by weight at the gateway.\n- **Rollback is a routing change,** so you never wait to pull tens of GB of weights again under pressure."}],"tradeoffs":["**Many small replicas vs a few large TP replicas.** TP=1 replicas give fine-grained scaling and isolation. Large TP replicas give higher per-replica throughput and are mandatory once weights exceed one GPU, but a single failure removes a bigger slice of capacity.","**Prefix affinity vs load concentration.** Affinity cuts prefill cost for shared prompts, but it concentrates load; when one tenant's prefix is hot, you must spill to other replicas or that replica becomes the bottleneck.","**Disaggregation vs operating cost.** Splitting prefill from decode stabilises TPOT and lets each phase scale on its own signal, at the price of KV-cache transfer and a second pool to operate; below a few replicas it is not worth it.","**Headroom vs paid idle GPU time.** Idle capacity buys tail-latency protection and absorbs slow scale-up, but every point of headroom is paid GPU time. The right level depends on how spiky traffic is and how long a cold replica takes to become ready.","**Zone spreading vs cost.** Spreading replicas across zones improves availability but costs cross-zone traffic and sometimes loses access to the cheapest GPU capacity pool, which flips the decision for batch workloads."],"pitfalls":["**Autoscaling on CPU or GPU utilization.** Symptom: replicas report \"healthy\" and under 50% utilization while TTFT climbs into seconds because the KV cache or waiting queue is full. Fix: scale on queue depth and KV usage.","**Round-robin routing across replicas.** Symptom: p99 latency far above p50 with no correlation to overall load, caused by long requests piling onto already-busy replicas. Fix: least-loaded routing.","**Killing pods on scale-down without draining.** Symptom: a burst of truncated streams and client retries every time the autoscaler steps down. Fix: drain in-flight streams first.","**Treating a tensor-parallel replica as N independent GPUs** in the capacity model. Symptom: a plan that promises N× the throughput a TP group actually delivers. Fix: load-test the replica shape.","**Wrong node pool for the replica.** Scheduling onto a node with the wrong GPU type or too little host RAM for weight loading. Symptom: CrashLoopBackOff with OOM during model load rather than at serving time.","**No readiness gate that waits for the engine's warm-up.** Symptom: a wave of errors and timeouts right after every scale-up or rollout because traffic hits a replica still loading weights."],"when":"- **Use when** one replica cannot meet peak demand within the latency budget, when you need to survive a\n  node loss without an outage, or when you serve more than one model.\n- **Avoid when** traffic is below that: one well-tuned replica (or a hosted API) is simpler and cheaper.\n- **Decide** the replica shape per model (TP degree, quantization, max context), the routing policy\n  (least-loaded first, prefix affinity second), the scaling signal (queue depth and KV utilization, never\n  CPU), and the headroom.\n- **Measure** a replica's capacity curve under load before writing any autoscaling rule, and keep TTFT,\n  TPOT, queue depth and KV usage on one dashboard.\n- **Interviews:** expect to be asked why you would not use the normal load balancer and autoscaler, and to\n  reason from KV-cache memory and prefill/decode asymmetry to the answer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures the engine (TP degree, max sequences, max context), exposes its metrics, writes the router's selection logic and the autoscaler's queries, and load-tests each replica shape.","architect":"Decides pool boundaries per model, TP versus more replicas, whether to disaggregate prefill and decode, zone spreading, and the headroom policy that balances cost against tail latency.","fde":"Sizes a customer's cluster from their traffic shape and GPU inventory, explains why their existing Kubernetes autoscaler misbehaves with LLM pods, and sets up the right metrics and drain behaviour."},"check":{"q":"A team runs eight identical vLLM replicas behind an ordinary round-robin load balancer with\nCPU-based autoscaling. Under load, p50 latency is fine but p99 TTFT is 20× p50 and the autoscaler\nnever fires. Diagnose the cause and describe the fix, including what you would measure.","a":"Round-robin ignores that LLM requests vary 100× in length and occupy a replica for seconds to\nminutes, so long requests pile onto replicas that are already at their concurrency cap; those\nreplicas queue new arrivals, which is the p99 tail. CPU never rises because decode is GPU-bandwidth\nbound and the pods are waiting on the GPU, so a CPU rule sees an idle fleet. The fix is to route on\nengine state (least in-flight sequences, with prefix affinity when a prefix is hot) and to scale on\nengine metrics: waiting-queue length and KV-cache utilization, with a TTFT SLO as the alarm. Before\nchanging rules, load-test one replica to find the concurrency at which p95 TPOT still meets budget\nand treat that as its capacity; size the pool from peak tokens/second with headroom and one spare\nreplica, and add a readiness gate so new replicas take traffic only after warm-up."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is tensor parallelism normally kept inside a single node rather than spread across nodes?","options":["Kubernetes cannot schedule a pod across two nodes","It needs an all-reduce every layer for every token, which is only fast enough over intra-node interconnects","Cross-node tensor parallelism would duplicate the model weights on every node","The KV cache must be contiguous in memory, which a network cannot provide"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tensor parallelism slices each layer and exchanges partial results per layer per step; over a network that round-trip would dominate every decode step (rack-scale NVLink domains extend the boundary but do not change the reasoning). Pipeline parallelism is the cross-node option because it exchanges activations only at stage boundaries."},{"q":"Which signal is the best basis for autoscaling an LLM model pool?","options":["Average CPU utilization of the serving pods","GPU SM utilization reported by the driver","Engine queue depth and KV-cache utilization measured against the replica's load-tested capacity","Number of HTTP connections open on the load balancer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Admission is limited by KV-cache memory and the engine's scheduler, not by CPU; GPU utilization can be low while the cache is full of live sessions. Queue depth and cache usage reflect whether the replica can admit more work."},{"q":"Prefix-affinity routing improves cost; under what condition should the router ignore it?","options":["When the prompt is shorter than the model's context window","When the replica holding the prefix is in a different availability zone","When the preferred replica is at its concurrency cap, so spilling to a less-loaded replica avoids queueing delay","When the request uses streaming, since streamed responses cannot reuse a cache"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A cache hit saves prefill compute, but sending a request to a saturated replica trades that saving for queueing time and tail-latency damage. Affinity should be a preference with a spillover rule, not a hard constraint."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","sources":["Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Sheng et al., 2023 — S-LoRA: Serving Thousands of Concurrent LoRA Adapters","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"- **A GPU is efficient only when busy.** Continuous batching reaches its throughput at tens of concurrent\n  sequences; at two or three the GPU spends most of each decode step reading weights for almost no\n  output, so cost per token can be an order of magnitude higher than at full batch.\n- **Pooling fills the batch.** A tenant peaking at five concurrent requests cannot fill a replica alone.\n  Because many tenants' peaks fall at different times, the pool needs capacity for the peak of the sum,\n  which is much less than the sum of the peaks.\n- **The pull is strong.** For a platform serving dozens of internal teams, dedicated replicas per team can\n  multiply the GPU bill several times for the same total tokens.\n- **\"Share everything\" fails fast.** One tenant's batch job of 50k-token documents fills the KV cache and\n  everyone's TTFT jumps. Nobody can say whose fault it is or stop it, because the engine's scheduler is\n  first-come-first-served by default (some engines offer a request-priority policy) and knows nothing\n  about tenants or fair shares.\n- **And again on guarantees.** A regulated tenant asks whether their prompts ever shared a GPU with another\n  company's; another needs a different model, context limit, fine-tuned adapter or region. Sharing without\n  isolation quietly removes fairness, blast-radius control and contractual guarantees. So decide which\n  properties each tenant needs, and the cheapest isolation level that provides them.","how":"### The isolation spectrum\nFrom cheapest to most isolated, each level buys a specific guarantee:\n\n| Level | What it is | Buys | Costs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Shared pool, no tenant awareness | One pool, no limits | Maximum utilization | No guarantees: one team's own traffic or a prototype only |\n| 2. Shared pool with logical isolation | Gateway limits, priorities, fair-share admission, per-tenant metrics | Fairness and attribution, batching economics kept | Cannot govern what happens after admission |\n| 3. Reserved capacity in a shared cluster | Guaranteed minimum, burst into the shared remainder | A floor of capacity | Some reserved idle time |\n| 4. Dedicated replicas or node pool | Separate engine processes, possibly separate GPUs | Latency isolation, own model, context or quantization | Idle capacity whenever the tenant is quiet |\n| 5. Dedicated cluster, VPC or cloud account | Separate control plane, network and identity | Strongest compliance story | Highest operating cost: every platform component duplicated |\n\n- **Level 2 detail.** Per-tenant rate limits and token budgets at the gateway, priority classes, fair-share\n  admission so one tenant cannot hold more than a share of in-flight sequences, plus per-tenant metrics and\n  chargeback. It exists *because* the engine scheduler is FCFS or at best priority-ordered, never\n  tenant-fair; fairness must be enforced before requests reach it.\n- **Level 3 detail.** The tenant gets their own replicas or a reserved share of the pool. This is the\n  self-hosted analogue of what hosted providers sell as provisioned throughput next to a pay-as-you-go pool.\n- **Level 4 detail.** A tenant's KV cache and queue are physically theirs.\n- **Level 5 detail.** Data residency, no shared control plane, independent blast radius.\n\n### The multiplexing arithmetic\nThe saving from sharing has a precise form. With tenant loads Lᵢ(t) in concurrent sequences and a replica\nholding C sequences within the latency budget:\n\n```\nisolated replicas = Σᵢ ( ceil(max_t Lᵢ(t) / C) + spareᵢ )\nshared replicas   = ceil( max_t Σᵢ Lᵢ(t) / C ) + spare\n```\n\n1. **Rounding.** Every isolated tenant pays for at least one whole replica (and its own spare) even if it\n   peaks at a tenth of one.\n2. **Multiplexing.** The peak of the sum is lower than the sum of the peaks whenever tenants peak at\n   different times. For many independent tenants the aggregate's relative fluctuation shrinks roughly as\n   1/√N, so a large pool needs proportionally less headroom.\n3. **The second effect is the one that flips.** When peaks are correlated (one time zone, the same\n   month-end process, the same upstream event), the peak of the sum approaches the sum of the peaks and\n   sharing saves little beyond rounding and spares.\n4. **So the input is a per-tenant load time series,** not a per-tenant average.\n\n### What is actually shared at each level\n- **Sharing a replica** means sharing GPU memory: tenants' KV caches sit in the same pool of blocks and the\n  batch holds their sequences side by side. Within a correct engine one sequence cannot read another's\n  cache, so this is not a data-leak path in itself.\n- **The real risks** are side-channel timing, engine bugs and, more practically, prefix caching.\n- **The prefix-cache side channel.** If a cached prefix keyed only by its text can be hit by another\n  tenant's identical text, the cached KV is never returned to the caller. But the faster TTFT of a hit is\n  a timing side channel that can reveal someone else recently sent that prefix.\n- **So cross-tenant cache sharing is an explicit decision.** The usual answer is to salt the cache key with\n  the tenant id (some engines accept a per-request cache salt).\n- **Sharing a node but not a replica** removes cache co-residency and leaves shared host RAM, network and\n  the node's failure domain.\n- **Sharing a cluster** leaves a shared control plane and shared operators.\n- **The compliance question is always \"what exactly is shared\",** and the answer should be precise,\n  because \"dedicated\" is sold at every level.\n\n### Multi-LoRA: sharing the base, isolating the behaviour\n- **Expensive choice:** a dedicated replica per fine-tune.\n- **Cheaper design:** one shared base model, with each tenant's LoRA adapter loaded into the same engine.\n  Adapters are small and swapped per request; base weights are shared.\n- **What it gives:** behavioural isolation (each tenant's outputs come from their adapter) with shared\n  economics.\n- **The limit:** all tenants must agree on the base model, context limit and quantization.\n\n### Logical isolation mechanics\n- **At the gateway:** per-tenant token-bucket limits in requests and tokens per minute, a concurrency cap\n  (in-flight sequences per tenant), and a priority tag.\n- **At the pool:** weighted fair queueing across tenants before admission, so a tenant with a batch backlog\n  cannot starve interactive users, and the option to route a tenant's batch traffic to a separate\n  low-priority pool.\n- **In observability:** every request carries a tenant id into metrics and traces so cost and tail latency\n  can be attributed. Without attribution you cannot even argue about whether a tenant deserves dedicated\n  capacity.\n\n### How the decision is made\nScore each tenant on four questions:\n\n1. Does their traffic shape threaten others (long contexts, bursty batch jobs)?\n2. Do they need guarantees others would violate (latency SLO, data separation, region)?\n3. Do they need a different model configuration?\n4. Would their dedicated capacity sit idle most of the day?\n\n- **Reading the score.** A \"yes\" to any of the first three is a reason for separation; a \"no\" to the last\n  means separation will be expensive.\n- **Contract or regulation wins regardless of cost,** and the cost goes on the contract.\n- **A traffic-shape problem** is first attacked with logical isolation or a separate low-priority pool, and\n  becomes dedicated capacity only when the tenant can fill it.\n- **Everyone else** lives in the shared pool behind logical isolation. The platform ends up with a shared\n  pool plus a short list of exceptions, each with a documented reason.","example":"### Peak of the sum vs sum of the peaks, in code\nA runnable model of the multiplexing formula. Loads are illustrative hourly concurrency per team.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef isolated_replicas(loads, cap, spare=1):\n    \"\"\"loads: {tenant: [concurrent sequences per hour]}; cap: sequences per replica within budget.\"\"\"\n    return sum(math.ceil(max(series) / cap) + spare for series in loads.values())\n\ndef shared_replicas(loads, cap, spare=1):\n    combined = [sum(hour) for hour in zip(*loads.values())]   # peak of the sum, not sum of peaks\n    return math.ceil(max(combined) / cap) + spare\n\nhours, cap = range(24), 48\n# Ten teams, each peaking at 20 sequences and idling at 4 (illustrative).\nspread = {f\"team{i}\": [20 if h in (2 * i, 2 * i + 1) else 4 for h in hours] for i in range(10)}\nsame = {f\"team{i}\": [20 if h in (14, 15) else 4 for h in hours] for i in range(10)}\n\nfor name, loads in ((\"peaks spread over the day\", spread), (\"all peak at 14:00\", same)):\n    iso = isolated_replicas(loads, cap, spare=0)          # before any per-team spare\n    iso_n1 = isolated_replicas(loads, cap, spare=1)       # each team gets its own N+1\n    sh = shared_replicas(loads, cap, spare=1)\n    print(f\"{name}: shared={sh}, isolated={iso} ({iso_n1} with spares), \"\n          f\"shared costs {sh / iso:.0%} of isolated\")\n# peaks spread over the day: shared=3, isolated=10 (20 with spares), shared costs 30% of isolated\n# all peak at 14:00: shared=6, isolated=10 (20 with spares), shared costs 60% of isolated\n```\n\n- **Spread peaks:** the combined peak is 20 + 9 × 4 = 56 sequences, so two replicas plus a spare.\n- **Correlated peaks:** the combined peak is 200, so the saving falls back to rounding and shared spares.\n\n### Worked comparison: ten product teams on one 70B model\nNumbers are illustrative and come from the kind of measurements you would make yourself. Each team peaks\nat 20 concurrent sequences, but peaks are spread over the day; the measured combined peak is 80\nconcurrent sequences, not 200. A replica holds 48 concurrent sequences within the latency budget.\n\n| Design | Replicas | Utilization | Cost vs isolated |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Isolated per team | 10, one per team, since no team's traffic can be combined with another's | each averages perhaps 6 of 48 slots busy: near 12%, before any per-team N+1 spare | 100% |\n| Shared with logical isolation | ceil(80 / 48) = 2, plus one for N+1 and headroom = 3 | the same 60 average busy slots on 144 slots instead of 480: about 42% (roughly 3.3x higher) | 3/10 = 30% |\n| Hybrid | 3 shared + 1 dedicated batch replica = 4 | fairness preserved | 40% |\n| All ten teams peak in the same hour | combined peak close to 200: ceil(200 / 48) = 5, plus a spare | sharing saves 40% instead of 70% | 60% |\n\n- **The hybrid case.** One team runs nightly document batches with 60k-token prompts that would fill the\n  KV cache during the batch window. Give that team a dedicated replica for batch (or route their batch\n  traffic to a low-priority pool) and keep their interactive traffic in the shared pool.\n- **The regulated case.** If one team handles regulated data and its compliance rule says \"no shared GPU\",\n  none of this satisfies it: that team needs level 4 or 5, at least two replicas of its own for N+1, and a\n  cost line attached.\n- **What matters:** peak-of-sum versus sum-of-peaks, and whether a tenant's shape breaks the shared pool.\n  Whenever the second is true, the first stops mattering for that tenant.\n- **Correlated peaks:** the saving is almost all from rounding and shared spares rather than multiplexing.","patterns":["**Per-tenant token buckets at the gateway.** For any shared pool, enforce requests-per-minute, tokens-per-minute and an in-flight concurrency cap per tenant id (for example counters in Redis keyed by tenant), returning 429 when exceeded. Monitor 429s and in-flight sequences per tenant.","**Fair-share queue in front of the engine.** Keep one queue per tenant at the gateway and dequeue by weight, because the engine itself is FCFS. Monitor per-tenant p95 TTFT against the pool-wide p95.","**Tenant-salted prefix cache.** Where the engine supports a per-request cache salt, pass the tenant id so cache hits never cross tenants; share only deliberately public prefixes. Monitor prefix-cache hit rate per tenant to see what the salt costs.","**Multi-LoRA on one base.** In vLLM, start the server with `--enable-lora`, register adapters with `--lora-modules name=path` and bound residency with `--max-loras`; requests pick an adapter by model name. Monitor TTFT per adapter and how often adapters are swapped in.","**Separate low-priority batch pool.** Route requests tagged as batch to their own pool sized for throughput, so batch windows never touch interactive replicas. Monitor batch backlog and interactive TTFT during the batch window.","**Exception register with quarterly review.** Every reserved or dedicated tenant has an owner, a reason and a cost line. Monitor each exception's utilization and demote tenants that no longer fill their capacity."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"Tenant request for capacity\"] --> R{\"Contract or regulation forbids sharing?\"}\n    R -->|\"yes\"| D5[\"Level 4 or 5: dedicated nodes, cluster or account\"]\n    R -->|\"no\"| C{\"Needs a different model config?\"}\n    C -->|\"only an adapter\"| ML[\"Multi-LoRA on the shared base\"]\n    C -->|\"different base or context\"| D4[\"Level 4: dedicated replicas\"]\n    C -->|\"no\"| S{\"Traffic shape hurts others?\"}\n    S -->|\"no\"| L2[\"Level 2: shared pool, logical isolation\"]\n    S -->|\"yes\"| F{\"Can they fill a replica most of the day?\"}\n    F -->|\"yes\"| L3[\"Level 3 or 4: reserved or dedicated\"]\n    F -->|\"no\"| LP[\"Low-priority batch pool\"]","caption":"Walk a tenant down the decision path: hard requirements first, then configuration, then traffic shape;\neveryone who reaches the bottom stays in the shared pool."},"interview":[{"q":"Explain peak-of-sum versus sum-of-peaks, and when the saving from sharing disappears.","a":"- **Definition:** isolated capacity is sized for each tenant's own peak; shared capacity is sized for the\n  peak of the combined load, which is lower when tenants peak at different times.\n- **Why it grows with N:** for many independent tenants the aggregate's relative fluctuation shrinks\n  roughly as 1/√N, so a big pool needs proportionally less headroom.\n- **When it disappears:** correlated peaks (one time zone, month-end, one upstream event); then sharing\n  saves only rounding and spares."},{"q":"A security reviewer asks whether tenants can learn anything from each other through your shared pool. What do you answer?","a":"- **No direct read path:** in a correct engine one sequence cannot read another's KV cache, even in the\n  same batch.\n- **Real risks:** engine bugs, side-channel timing and, most practically, a shared prefix cache whose\n  faster TTFT reveals that someone recently sent the same prefix.\n- **Mitigation and precision:** salt the cache key with the tenant id, and state exactly what is shared\n  (replica, node, cluster) rather than saying \"dedicated\"."},{"q":"Ten teams want their own fine-tuned version of the same 8B model. Dedicated replicas or multi-LoRA?","a":"- **Multi-LoRA by default:** one shared base with per-team adapters swapped per request keeps batching\n  economics while each team gets its own behaviour.\n- **Dedicated replicas when** a team needs a different base model, context limit or quantization, or has\n  a contractual reason not to share.\n- **Cost of dedicated:** ten replicas sized for ten separate peaks, mostly idle outside each team's peak."},{"q":"What would you measure before agreeing to give a tenant dedicated capacity?","a":"- **Their load shape:** a per-tenant time series of in-flight sequences and tokens, not an average, to see\n  whether they could fill a replica most of the day.\n- **Their effect on others:** pool-wide KV utilization and other tenants' p95 TTFT during their peaks.\n- **Their requirement type:** a contract or regulation wins regardless of cost; a traffic-shape problem is\n  first tried with caps, fair-share admission or a batch pool."},{"q":"Ten teams each peak at 20 concurrent sequences and a replica holds 48. How many replicas shared vs isolated?","a":"- **Isolated:** 10 replicas (one each), near 12% utilization, before giving each team a spare.\n- **Shared with spread peaks:** the measured combined peak of 80 gives ceil(80 / 48) = 2, plus one spare =\n  3 replicas, about 30% of the isolated bill.\n- **Shared with correlated peaks:** a combined peak near 200 needs 5 plus a spare, so sharing saves 40%\n  instead of 70%."}],"tradeoffs":["**Utilization vs latency isolation.** Sharing raises utilization and lowers cost per token, but removes latency isolation; the balance flips when one tenant's traffic shape (long prompts, batch bursts) dominates the pool.","**Logical isolation vs hard SLOs.** Gateway isolation preserves batching economics and gives fairness, but cannot prevent cache contention once requests are admitted, so it does not satisfy hard latency SLOs.","**Dedicated replicas vs idle capacity.** They give predictable latency and freedom to change model configuration, but capacity sits idle outside the tenant's peak; worthwhile when the tenant's own traffic can fill a replica most of the day.","**Multi-LoRA vs configuration freedom.** Many customised models ride one base at shared cost, but every tenant is constrained to the same base model, context and quantization, which breaks when one needs a different model.","**Dedicated clusters vs operating burden.** They give the strongest compliance story, yet duplicate every platform component and operator burden; justified by contract or regulation, rarely by performance."],"pitfalls":["**Promising \"dedicated\" without stating the level.** Symptom: a security review discovers the tenant's \"dedicated\" model shares a node, control plane or prefix cache with others. Fix: name what is not shared.","**A shared pool without per-tenant concurrency caps.** Symptom: platform-wide TTFT spikes that line up with one tenant's cron schedule. Fix: caps and fair-share admission.","**Keying a shared prefix cache on prompt text alone** without deciding whether cross-tenant hits are acceptable. Symptom: an auditor asks why tenant B's request was faster because tenant A had sent the same prompt. Fix: salt with the tenant id.","**No tenant id in metrics.** Symptom: a cost-allocation argument nobody can settle and an isolation decision made on anecdotes instead of load shape.","**Giving every tenant who asks their own replica.** Symptom: a fleet averaging single-digit utilization and a GPU bill that scales with the number of customers rather than with tokens.","**Treating isolation as permanent.** Symptom: a tenant that outgrew or shrank below the threshold is still paying (or still being subsidised) a year later. Fix: review exceptions."],"when":"- **Start shared with logical isolation:** gateway limits, fair-share admission, per-tenant metrics. That is\n  the default for internal platforms and for SaaS tiers without hard SLOs.\n- **Move to reserved or dedicated capacity** when a tenant's traffic shape measurably damages others, when\n  a contract specifies latency or data-separation guarantees you cannot meet in the pool, or when they need\n  a different model configuration.\n- **Move to a dedicated cluster or account** only for regulatory or contractual reasons, and attach the cost\n  to the contract.\n- **Measure** per-tenant p95 TTFT, in-flight sequences and tokens, and pool utilization; revisit each\n  exception quarterly.\n- **Interviews:** explain why continuous batching makes sharing cheap and why that same batching makes noisy\n  neighbours hard to contain after admission.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements tenant-tagged rate limits, concurrency caps and fair-share queueing at the gateway, and ensures tenant ids flow into engine metrics and traces for attribution.","architect":"Owns the isolation policy: which guarantees each tier of tenant gets, at which level of the spectrum, and the cost line that goes with every dedicated exception.","fde":"Translates a customer's \"we need our own model\" into the precise isolation level they need, prices it, and configures reserved or dedicated capacity inside their environment."},"check":{"q":"A SaaS company serves forty customers from one shared vLLM pool. One enterprise customer\ndemands \"dedicated infrastructure\" after a week of slow responses. How do you decide what to give\nthem, and what would you check first?","a":"First check whether the slowness is caused by them or by someone else: per-tenant p95 TTFT\nand in-flight sequences over the week, correlated with pool-wide KV utilization. If another tenant's\nbatch jobs caused it, the fix is logical isolation for everyone (concurrency caps, fair-share\nadmission, a low-priority batch pool), which solves the enterprise customer's problem without\ndedicated hardware. If their own traffic shape is the cause, or their contract names a latency SLO,\ngive them reserved capacity: a dedicated replica for their guaranteed minimum with burst into the\npool. Only if the demand is about data separation or residency do you move to a dedicated node\npool, cluster or account, and then the precise level of what is not shared goes into the contract\nalong with its cost. Dedicated capacity is reviewed against their utilization every quarter."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a shared model pool usually cost far less per token than dedicated replicas per tenant?","options":["Shared pools can use cheaper GPUs because the model is loaded once","Continuous batching only reaches full efficiency at high concurrency, and tenants' peaks rarely coincide, so capacity is sized for the peak of the sum","Dedicated replicas must each keep a separate copy of the training data","Shared pools skip the prefill phase for every tenant after the first"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A GPU at low concurrency spends most of each decode step reading weights for little output. Pooling fills the batch and sizes capacity for combined peak rather than the sum of individual peaks, which is where the saving comes from."},{"q":"Per-tenant rate limits and concurrency caps at the gateway are in place, yet one tenant's long-context jobs still slow others. Why?","options":["Rate limits only count requests, never tokens","The gateway cannot see which tenant a request belongs to","Once admitted, sequences share the replica's KV cache and batch, so long contexts consume memory and bandwidth that gateway limits do not govern","Long-context requests bypass the gateway and reach the engine directly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Logical isolation controls admission, not what happens inside the batch; long sequences take many KV blocks and lengthen every step. Latency isolation needs separate replicas or routing long jobs to a separate pool."},{"q":"Which situation most justifies a fully dedicated cluster or cloud account rather than dedicated replicas?","options":["The tenant's p95 latency target is tighter than the pool's","The tenant wants a different context length limit","The tenant's contract requires data residency or no shared control plane","The tenant's traffic is the largest in the pool"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Latency and configuration needs are met by dedicated replicas inside a shared cluster. A separate cluster or account is justified by what must not be shared at all: region, control plane, operators and network."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/cloud-ai-architecture","sources":["Official docs: Amazon Bedrock","Official docs: Azure OpenAI Service","Official docs: Google Cloud Vertex AI"],"why":"- **A vendor's public API triggers reviews unrelated to the model.** Who approved this vendor, where does\n  the data go, which network path, how is it authenticated, where are the logs, who pays under which\n  contract. Each is a review, and each review is slow.\n- **The company already has those answers for its cloud.** IAM or Entra identities, private networking,\n  audit logging, consolidated billing against a committed spend, and a signed compliance package. The cloud\n  AI platforms let the model inherit them, so to a security reviewer it is \"just another cloud service\".\n- **\"Same model, use the vendor API\" fails on the review calendar,** not on technology, and later on data\n  terms (training opt-outs, retention, residency) that the cloud contracts fix in writing.\n- **It also fails on capacity.** Committed cloud customers can buy provisioned throughput in their own\n  region, which a public pay-as-you-go API does not offer in the same form.\n- **\"Self-host everything on our GPUs\" fails** for most teams on model quality and operations cost, which\n  is why the managed route is the default in large organisations.","how":"### What a call looks like on each platform\n| Platform | A request is | Network | Logging |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bedrock | A SigV4-signed AWS API call | Can travel over a VPC endpoint | Opt-in model invocation logging to CloudWatch Logs or S3, billed on the AWS invoice |\n| Azure OpenAI | A call to a deployment, a resource in a resource group with Entra RBAC | Private endpoint | Diagnostic settings |\n| Vertex AI | A Google Cloud API call under a project's IAM | VPC Service Controls | Cloud Audit Logs |\n\n### The common shape: seven layers\nAll three platforms present the same layers, and your design decisions live in each.\n\n1. **Identity.** No API keys from the model vendor. Bedrock uses IAM principals and policies per model;\n   Azure OpenAI uses Entra ID (keys exist but are the weaker option); Vertex uses Google Cloud IAM and\n   service accounts. *Decide:* which workload identities may call which models.\n2. **Network.** Private connectivity so traffic never crosses the public internet: VPC endpoints\n   (PrivateLink) for Bedrock, Private Endpoints for Azure OpenAI, Private Service Connect and VPC Service\n   Controls for Vertex. *Decide:* where the endpoint sits and which networks can reach it.\n3. **Model catalog and endpoint.** See the next subsection. *Decide:* which models, which versions, pinned\n   how.\n4. **Capacity model.** Pay-as-you-go vs reserved, below. *Decide:* which workloads run on shared quota and\n   which need reserved capacity.\n5. **Region.** Models are available per region, and quotas are per region. *Decide:* which regions satisfy\n   the data-residency rule, and whether cross-region routing is permitted.\n6. **Governance and logging.** Invocation logging to the cloud's log services, content filters (Bedrock\n   Guardrails, Azure's built-in content filtering, Vertex safety settings), and the data terms.\n7. **Adjacent building blocks.** Managed retrieval (Bedrock Knowledge Bases, Vertex AI Search and RAG\n   tooling, Azure AI Search), agent frameworks, evaluation and fine-tuning services. They are convenient\n   and deepen lock-in; treat each as a separate build-versus-buy decision.\n\n### Model catalogs\n- **One idea on three clouds:** frontier and open-weight models served as a managed API inside your\n  account's perimeter. The architecture work is placing the endpoint inside that perimeter and planning\n  capacity per region.\n- **Bedrock** offers models from several vendors (Amazon's own, Anthropic, Meta, Mistral and others)\n  behind one API, with the Converse API as the model-agnostic interface.\n- **Azure OpenAI** hosts OpenAI models; you create a named *deployment* of a model version with a capacity\n  setting. The wider Azure AI Foundry catalog (since rebranded Microsoft Foundry; these product names\n  change often) adds other vendors' models.\n- **Vertex** serves Gemini natively and partner models (Anthropic, Meta, Mistral and others) through Model\n  Garden.\n\n### Capacity modes and regions\n- **Two modes everywhere, with different names.** Pay-as-you-go shares a regional pool under quotas\n  (requests and tokens per minute per model per region per account or subscription).\n- **Reserved capacity buys guaranteed throughput:** Bedrock Provisioned Throughput in model units, Azure\n  Provisioned Throughput Units (PTU), Vertex Provisioned Throughput. It exists *because* the shared pool\n  cannot promise throughput at peak; you pay for dedicated model capacity in exchange for predictability.\n- **Batch modes** exist for offline work at a discount.\n- **Cross-region routing when one region is full:** Bedrock cross-region inference profiles, Azure \"Global\"\n  and \"Data Zone\" deployment types that process within a wider geography, and a global endpoint option on\n  Vertex for some models. These trade residency precision for availability.\n\n### Data terms\n- **No training on your data:** all three state that customer prompts and outputs are not used to train the\n  base models.\n- **Azure abuse monitoring** may retain prompts for a limited period unless an exemption is granted, which\n  matters for regulated data and must be checked against current docs.\n\n### Where the differences bite\n- **Model lag.** New models and features usually reach the vendor's own API first and the cloud platforms\n  days to months later, sometimes with missing features. If the roadmap depends on the newest model, that\n  lag is a real cost.\n- **Capacity semantics.** Azure's quota is a TPM allocation per model per region per subscription that you\n  split across deployments; Bedrock's on-demand quotas are per model per region per account; Vertex has\n  moved Gemini pay-as-you-go toward a dynamically shared quota rather than fixed per-project limits.\n- **Same consequence everywhere:** plan capacity per model per region and request increases early, since\n  increases are tickets, not API calls.\n- **Version pinning and retirement.** Each platform retires model versions on its own schedule; Azure\n  deployments can be set to auto-upgrade or pinned, Bedrock and Vertex pin by model id.\n- **A pin is not forever.** When a pinned version reaches its retirement date you must migrate (or, on\n  Azure, you may be upgraded), so the retirement calendar is part of the plan. Unplanned upgrades change\n  behaviour under your evals, so pinning plus a scheduled re-evaluation is the safe pattern.\n\n### SDK shape\n| Platform | SDK | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bedrock | Its own SDK (boto3) | Two call styles: `InvokeModel`, whose request body is each vendor's native format, and the Converse API, one message format across models; vendor SDKs can also use Bedrock as a backend |\n| Azure OpenAI | The OpenAI SDK with an Azure client | `model` means the deployment name. Azure has also added a versionless \"v1\" endpoint usable from the standard OpenAI client, so check which style the current docs recommend |\n| Vertex | The Google Gen AI SDK, or vendor SDKs with a Vertex backend | Authenticates with Application Default Credentials and a project/location |\n\n- **Hide the differences.** An internal gateway or an abstraction such as LiteLLM usually hides this so that\n  application code is not rewritten when a model moves.\n\n### Choosing the capacity mode\nReserved capacity is a fixed hourly or monthly price for a throughput ceiling; pay-as-you-go is a\nper-token price with no ceiling guarantee. The comparison is the same arithmetic as owning GPUs:\n\n```\non-demand cost  = tokens actually served × price per token\nreserved cost   = units × price per unit-hour × hours (paid whether used or not)\nbreak-even utilization u* = reserved cost ÷ (tokens the reservation could serve at full use × on-demand price)\n```\n\n- **Above u\\*, reserved is cheaper per token;** below it, you are paying for a guarantee.\n- **\"Tokens the reservation could serve\" is the hard number,** because each platform's unit (model unit,\n  PTU) delivers a throughput that depends on the model and on the input/output mix: prompt-heavy and\n  generation-heavy traffic consume a unit differently.\n- **Load-test it.** Each platform publishes sizing guidance or a calculator, but the reliable figure comes\n  from a load test of your own traffic mix against a small reservation.\n- **A latency SLO at peak can justify reserved capacity below u\\*,** since the alternative is 429s at the\n  worst moment.\n- **Commitment terms** (hourly, monthly, longer) mean a forecast error is paid for the length of the term.\n\n### The reference architecture\nApplication → internal AI gateway (auth, routing, quotas, logging, prompt and response capture) → private\nendpoint → cloud AI platform in the approved region → optional cross-region or second provider fallback.\nThe gateway is where you keep the option to move between platforms, which answers the second-layer\nlock-in these services create.","example":"### The same one-turn request on all three platforms\nNotice what differs: Bedrock has no key and signs with the IAM identity in the environment; Azure needs an\nendpoint, an API version and the *deployment* name; Vertex authenticates with Application Default\nCredentials and a project/location. Model identifiers are placeholders to look up in each catalog.\n\n```python\nimport os, boto3\nfrom openai import AzureOpenAI\nfrom google import genai\n\nQUESTION = \"Summarise our refund policy in one sentence.\"\n\ndef via_bedrock(model_id: str) -> str:\n    rt = boto3.client(\"bedrock-runtime\", region_name=os.environ[\"AWS_REGION\"])  # IAM creds, SigV4\n    r = rt.converse(\n        modelId=model_id,                                   # or a cross-region inference profile id\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [{\"text\": QUESTION}]}],\n        inferenceConfig={\"maxTokens\": 200},\n    )\n    return r[\"output\"][\"message\"][\"content\"][0][\"text\"]\n\ndef via_azure(deployment_name: str) -> str:\n    client = AzureOpenAI(\n        azure_endpoint=os.environ[\"AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT\"],   # https://<resource>.openai.azure.com\n        api_key=os.environ[\"AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY\"],            # prefer Entra ID token auth in prod\n        api_version=os.environ[\"AZURE_OPENAI_API_VERSION\"],\n    )\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=deployment_name,                               # deployment, not the model family name\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": QUESTION}], max_tokens=200,\n    )\n    return r.choices[0].message.content\n\ndef via_vertex(model_name: str) -> str:\n    client = genai.Client(vertexai=True, project=os.environ[\"GCP_PROJECT\"],\n                          location=os.environ[\"GCP_LOCATION\"])  # ADC supplies the identity\n    r = client.models.generate_content(model=model_name, contents=QUESTION)\n    return r.text\n```\n\n- **In production none of these calls live in application code.** They sit behind the gateway, which tags\n  each request with tenant, feature and region so quota use and cost can be attributed and a fallback can\n  be configured without touching the application.\n\n### Worked scenario: reserved vs pay-as-you-go\nIllustrative prices, not any platform's list price. A reservation costs $30,000 a month; your load test\nshows it sustains about 1.5 billion tokens a month of your mix at full use. The same tokens on\npay-as-you-go cost $40 per million, so full use would cost $60,000 on demand.\n\n| Average utilization | Reserved | On demand | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Break-even u\\* | 30,000 ÷ 60,000 | = 50% | the line to beat |\n| 70% | $30,000 | $42,000 | reserved wins |\n| 30% | $30,000 | $18,000 | only a peak-latency SLO justifies it |\n\n- **The usual design** reserves for the base and lets the burst above it spill to a pay-as-you-go\n  deployment, which the gateway does by trying the reserved endpoint first and falling over on a 429.\n\n### Gateway spill from reserved to pay-as-you-go\nA minimal version of that spill on Azure, with two deployments of the same model in one resource. The\nreserved call disables the SDK's automatic retries so a 429 spills at once instead of waiting.\n\n```python\nimport openai\n\ndef complete_with_spill(client: openai.AzureOpenAI, reserved: str, paygo: str, messages, **kw):\n    \"\"\"Base load on the provisioned deployment; burst spills to pay-as-you-go on 429.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        r = client.with_options(max_retries=0).chat.completions.create(\n            model=reserved, messages=messages, **kw)\n        return r, \"reserved\"\n    except openai.RateLimitError:\n        r = client.chat.completions.create(model=paygo, messages=messages, **kw)\n        return r, \"paygo\"          # count these: a rising spill rate means the reservation is too small\n```","patterns":["**Reserve the base, spill the burst.** When load has a steady floor plus peaks, size provisioned capacity for the floor and route overflow to a pay-as-you-go deployment on 429, as in the snippet above. Monitor reservation utilization against u\\* and the spill rate.","**Private-only endpoints.** Disable public network access on the Azure OpenAI resource and use a Private Endpoint; reach Bedrock through a VPC interface endpoint with an endpoint policy; put Vertex inside a VPC Service Controls perimeter. Monitor denied calls in the audit logs.","**Least-privilege model access.** Grant workload identities only the models they need, for example an IAM policy allowing `bedrock:InvokeModel` on specific model ARNs (the Converse API uses the same permission). Monitor access-denied errors after each new model rollout.","**Pin versions with a retirement calendar.** Turn off auto-upgrade on Azure deployments and pin model ids on Bedrock and Vertex; track each pinned version's retirement date and re-run evals before switching. Monitor eval scores and output-parse failures per model version.","**Invocation logging on from day one.** Enable Bedrock model invocation logging (it is opt-in), Azure diagnostic settings and Vertex audit logs, routed to the security team's workspace. Monitor that every gateway request has a matching platform log entry.","**Quota headroom per model per region.** Keep a sheet or config of quota, current peak TPM and lead time for each model-region pair, and file increases weeks before launch. Monitor 429 and throttle rates."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Application\"] --> G[\"Internal AI gateway\"]\n    G -.->|\"tenant, feature, region tags\"| T[(\"Logs, quota and cost telemetry\")]\n    G -->|\"private endpoint\"| R[\"Reserved deployment, approved region\"]\n    R -->|\"429 at peak\"| P[\"Pay-as-you-go deployment, same region\"]\n    P -->|\"429 or outage\"| F{\"Residency allows another region?\"}\n    F -->|\"yes\"| X[\"Cross-region profile or second provider\"]\n    F -->|\"no\"| Q[\"Queue or fail fast\"]","caption":"The request path of the reference architecture: the gateway sends base load to reserved capacity, spills\nbursts to pay-as-you-go, and leaves the region only if the residency rule allows it."},"interview":[{"q":"What do you gain and give up by calling a model through Bedrock, Azure OpenAI or Vertex instead of the vendor's own API?","a":"- **Gain:** the model inherits identity (IAM, Entra), private networking, audit logging, consolidated\n  billing and the signed compliance package, so security review treats it as another cloud service.\n- **Gain:** provisioned throughput in your own region and written data terms on training and retention.\n- **Give up:** days to months of model and feature lag, per-region quotas you must request, and a second\n  layer of lock-in to the cloud's catalog and adjacent services."},{"q":"Walk me through deciding between provisioned throughput and pay-as-you-go.","a":"- **Compute break-even:** u\\* = reserved cost ÷ (tokens the reservation serves at full use × on-demand\n  price); for a $30,000 reservation worth $60,000 on demand, u\\* is 50%.\n- **Get the capacity number by load test,** because a PTU or model unit delivers different throughput for\n  prompt-heavy and generation-heavy mixes.\n- **Then weigh the SLO and term:** a peak-latency SLO can justify reserving below u\\*, and a long commitment\n  makes forecast errors expensive."},{"q":"An Azure OpenAI call returns 404 \"deployment not found\" although the model is available in that region. What is wrong?","a":"- **Most likely** the code passes the model family name as `model`; on Azure, `model` is the deployment\n  name you created.\n- **Check also** the endpoint, region and API version, since the deployment lives in one resource.\n- **Prevent it** by keeping the deployment mapping in the gateway's config so application code never names\n  Azure deployments directly."},{"q":"Outputs from a model you depend on changed without any deploy on your side. How do you stop that happening again?","a":"- **Cause:** an auto-upgrade or default version moved under you, or a pinned version reached retirement.\n- **Fix:** pin explicit versions (disable auto-upgrade on Azure, pin model ids on Bedrock and Vertex) and\n  track each version's retirement date.\n- **Process:** treat every version change as a release: run the eval set and output-parsing tests before\n  switching traffic."},{"q":"How would you keep a credible exit from a cloud AI platform?","a":"- **Put a gateway in front** so application code speaks one interface (your own, or an abstraction such as\n  LiteLLM) and models can move between platforms by config.\n- **Treat adjacent managed services** (knowledge bases, agents, guardrails) as separate build-versus-buy\n  decisions, adopting them only where they are replaceable behind an interface.\n- **Prove the exit:** keep a second provider or region wired as fallback and exercise it, so moving is a\n  routing change rather than a rewrite."}],"tradeoffs":["**Cloud perimeter vs model lag.** You inherit the cloud's identity, network, logging and compliance perimeter, but accept model and feature lag behind the vendor's own API; the balance flips when the product depends on the newest model capabilities.","**Reserved capacity vs paying for idle.** PTU, provisioned throughput and model units buy predictable throughput for peak, but are paid whether used or not; worth it only when steady load fills most of it or an SLO makes shared-pool 429s unacceptable.","**Consolidated billing vs multi-cloud freedom.** Billing against committed spend simplifies procurement and can discount the model bill, but ties model choice to one cloud's catalog and makes a multi-cloud model strategy harder.","**Managed adjacent services vs lock-in.** Knowledge bases, agents and guardrails accelerate delivery, but every one adopted deepens lock-in and narrows the exit path; the trade flips when the component is commodity and replaceable behind an interface.","**Cross-region routing vs residency.** Cross-region and global options raise availability, but loosen the residency guarantee; where regulation names a region, they must be disabled."],"pitfalls":["**Passing the model family name instead of the deployment name to Azure OpenAI.** Symptom: a 404 \"deployment not found\" even though the model is clearly available in the region.","**Sizing capacity in one region and discovering the model is not offered there** or the quota is lower. Symptom: a launch delayed by a quota-increase ticket. Fix: check availability and quota first.","**Leaving auto-upgrade or default model versions in place.** Symptom: a silent behaviour change that breaks evals or output parsing with no deploy on your side. Fix: pin and re-evaluate.","**Enabling cross-region inference or global deployment types on a workload with a residency requirement.** Symptom: an audit finding months later rather than an error at request time.","**Calling the platform from application code directly.** Symptom: a multi-week rewrite when a model moves clouds or a second provider is added for fallback. Fix: a gateway.","**Assuming the cloud's data terms cover abuse-monitoring retention and human review.** Symptom: a compliance review rejects a workload handling regulated data until the exemption is arranged."],"when":"- **Use a cloud AI platform when** the organisation already runs on that cloud, when identity, private\n  networking and compliance scope are review gates, when spend commitments can absorb the model bill, or\n  when you need reserved capacity in a chosen region.\n- **Prefer the vendor's direct API when** you need the newest models or features first and your data terms\n  are satisfied by the vendor's contract.\n- **Prefer self-hosting when** open-weight models meet the bar and volume makes it cheaper, or when the data\n  cannot leave your own hardware.\n- **Measure** quota headroom per model per region, 429 and throttle rates, and time-to-new-model when a\n  release matters.\n- **Interviews:** explain the identity, network, capacity and region layers and how a gateway keeps the\n  exit open.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Wires the gateway to each platform's SDK, pins model versions, handles each platform's throttling and error shapes, and tags requests for cost and quota attribution.","architect":"Chooses platform versus direct API versus self-host per workload, places endpoints inside the network and identity perimeter, and decides reserved versus shared capacity per region.","fde":"Deploys the solution inside the customer's existing cloud account, works through their quota, region and private-endpoint constraints, and documents which data terms apply."},"check":{"q":"A bank on Azure wants a customer-facing assistant on a frontier model, with EU data residency,\na p95 latency target and a launch in six weeks. Walk through the architecture decisions and the\nrisks you would raise.","a":"Use Azure OpenAI deployed in an EU region, reached through a Private Endpoint from the\napplication's VNet, with Entra ID identities and diagnostic logging to the bank's workspace. Check\nmodel availability in that region first, then request quota early; for the latency target, size\nprovisioned throughput for peak and keep a pay-as-you-go deployment for overflow, or accept 429s\nwith queueing if the SLO allows. Use regional deployment types (regional Standard and regional\nProvisioned) and avoid Global types; an EU Data Zone deployment keeps processing inside the EU and\nmay be acceptable, but only if legal confirms that the wider geography satisfies the residency rule. Pin the model version and schedule\nre-evaluation before any upgrade. Confirm the abuse-monitoring retention terms and apply for the\nexemption if required. Put all calls behind a gateway so a second region or provider can be added\nas fallback. The biggest risks are quota lead time, model availability in the region, and\nbehaviour drift from version upgrades."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the main reason enterprises route model calls through Bedrock, Azure OpenAI or Vertex AI rather than the model vendor's public API?","options":["The cloud platforms serve the models with lower latency","The model inherits the cloud account's identity, private networking, logging, billing and compliance perimeter","The cloud platforms always offer newer model versions","The cloud platforms do not enforce rate limits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The technical model is the same; what changes is that security, procurement and compliance are already solved for the cloud provider. Model versions usually lag, and quotas still apply."},{"q":"Why do all three platforms sell a reserved or provisioned capacity mode alongside pay-as-you-go?","options":["Reserved capacity uses a different, faster model build","Pay-as-you-go cannot be used from a private endpoint","The shared regional pool is governed by quotas and cannot guarantee throughput at peak, so predictability has to be bought as dedicated capacity","Reserved capacity is required for logging and audit features"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Shared pools give best-effort throughput under per-region quotas; a workload with a latency SLO at peak needs capacity that is reserved for it, which is what PTU, provisioned throughput and model units provide."},{"q":"A workload with a strict single-country data-residency rule is deployed with a cross-region or global routing option enabled. What is the consequence?","options":["Requests fail immediately with a residency error","Latency improves and nothing else changes","Requests may be processed in another region, violating the rule without any runtime error","The quota is halved because two regions share it"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cross-region and global modes are designed to route to wherever capacity exists; they do not know your legal constraints. The violation surfaces in an audit, not in an error, so it must be prevented by configuration."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","sources":["Official docs: OpenAI rate limits","Official docs: Anthropic rate limits","Official docs: Azure OpenAI quotas and limits","Official docs: Amazon Bedrock quotas"],"why":"- **The provider's fleet is finite and shared.** Without limits one customer's spike would degrade every\n  other customer, and the provider could not size its fleet. Rate limits are the provider's admission\n  control; quotas are the allocation of that control per customer.\n- **Each boundary has a reason.** Per model because models have different cost and capacity, per region\n  because the fleet is regional, per account or subscription because that is the billing and abuse\n  boundary. Tiers rise with spend because payment history is the provider's best signal that a customer\n  is real and predictable.\n- **\"The API is infinite, just retry\" fails, first, on retries.** A 429 under sustained demand is not\n  transient: retries add load to a limiter already rejecting you, raising latency for every request\n  without increasing throughput.\n- **Second, the limit is in tokens, including ones never generated.** Several major providers count\n  `max_tokens` against the budget at admission, so a team that never set it deliberately can find its\n  effective capacity is a fraction of what the quota implies.\n- **Third, quotas are regional.** The model may be missing, or have a lower quota, in the only region\n  the residency rule allows. A launch has been delayed by a quota ticket more often than by a model's\n  quality, so treat quota as a planned resource like GPU memory: measurement, headroom, a growth process.","how":"### The units\n| Unit | What it caps | When it binds |\n|---|---|---|\n| **RPM** (requests per minute) | The simplest cap | Workloads with many small requests |\n| **TPM** (tokens per minute) | Tokens; some providers count input and output together, others keep separate input-TPM and output-TPM | Usually, for LLM traffic. Long-prompt RAG and long-output generation exhaust different budgets |\n| **TPD / RPD** (per day) | A budget rather than a rate | Lower tiers on some providers |\n| **Concurrency** | Simultaneous in-flight requests, instead of or in addition to rates | Long generations |\n\n- **Azure OpenAI** allocates TPM per model per region per subscription and you split it across deployments.\n- **Bedrock** on-demand quotas are per model per region per account, raised through the service-quotas\n  process.\n- **Vendor APIs** set limits per organisation per model by usage tier.\n- **Vertex** has moved toward dynamically shared quota for some models, where the fixed per-project cap\n  gives way to a pool shared across customers.\n\n### How counting works\n1. **Token bucket or sliding window.** The budget refills continuously; a request is admitted if the\n   bucket holds enough.\n2. **Otherwise 429,** usually with a `Retry-After` (or `retry-after-ms`) header saying when to try again.\n3. **Output is reserved at admission** on providers that do so: the cost is estimated from prompt length\n   plus `max_tokens` (or a default when absent), *because* the provider must reserve capacity before it\n   knows the real output length.\n4. **Accounting afterwards differs.** Some adjust when the response completes; others count only actual\n   output against an output-token limit.\n5. **Response headers report the remaining budget,** for example OpenAI's `x-ratelimit-remaining-tokens`\n   family and Anthropic's `anthropic-ratelimit-tokens-remaining` family. A gateway can read them to slow\n   down before the first 429 rather than after it.\n\n### The max_tokens trap\n- **Who documents it:** OpenAI, Azure OpenAI and Bedrock among others document that they count\n  `max_tokens` against the token budget at admission.\n- **The effect:** a request with `max_tokens=4096` that produces 50 tokens still holds 4,096 tokens of\n  budget when admitted. Some providers credit the unused part back when the response completes, others\n  do not.\n- **Caveat:** the exact rule differs per provider and has changed over time, so read the current docs for\n  the one you use.\n\n### The ceiling a quota actually gives you\nEvery limit converts into a request ceiling, and the lowest one binds:\n\n```\nmax RPM from TPM  = TPM quota ÷ tokens charged per request\ntokens charged    = prompt tokens + max_tokens     (on providers that reserve output)\nbinding ceiling   = min(RPM quota, max RPM from TPM, concurrency cap ÷ mean seconds per request × 60)\n```\n\n| Setting (400k TPM, 1,000-token prompts) | Tokens charged | Ceiling |\n|---|---|---|\n| `max_tokens=4000` | 5,000 | 80 requests per minute, even if answers average 300 tokens |\n| `max_tokens=800` | 1,800 | about 220 per minute, no change to quota or bill |\n| Concurrency cap of 50 in flight, 15 seconds per long generation | n/a | at most 200 per minute whatever the token quota says (Little's Law) |\n\nRunning this calculation per model per region is the core of quota planning.\n\n### Quota versus capacity\n- **A quota is a promise about *your* admission,** not about the pool's availability.\n- **A saturated regional pool fails you under quota:** overload or capacity errors (Anthropic's 529,\n  Bedrock's throttling exceptions, Azure 429s on a deployment that is under its TPM).\n- **Provisioned or reserved capacity fills this gap.** It buys dedicated throughput that does not compete\n  with the pool, which is why it is priced by the hour rather than by the token.\n\n### Regional limits\n- **Quotas are per region, which cuts both ways.** If residency allows, spread across several regions or\n  Azure deployments and multiply effective quota. If residency names one region, that region's quota is\n  the ceiling, and the plan must include lead time for increase requests.\n- **Model availability is regional too:** the newest model may reach some regions months after others.\n- **Borrowing capacity is a residency decision.** Cross-region inference profiles, global and data-zone\n  deployment types and global endpoints borrow capacity from elsewhere; using them is a residency\n  decision as much as a capacity one.\n\n### The client-side design\n1. **Measure demand in the provider's units.** Peak tokens per minute in and out, peak requests per minute,\n   per model, per region, from your own telemetry, with `max_tokens` included in the input-side estimate\n   when the provider reserves it.\n2. **Limit yourself below the quota.** A gateway-level token bucket at roughly 80–90% of each quota smooths\n   bursts, so the provider never sees a storm and the first rejection happens in your queue, where you\n   control priority.\n3. **Queue with priority and bound the wait.** Interactive traffic ahead of batch; shed or defer when the\n   queue exceeds the latency budget rather than letting everything time out.\n4. **Retry correctly.** Honour `Retry-After`, add jitter, cap attempts, and open a circuit breaker when the\n   failure rate shows the pool is overloaded, since retries cannot create capacity.\n5. **Spread and fall back.** Weighted routing across regions, deployments, accounts or providers where\n   allowed, with the fallback order chosen by residency and quality constraints.\n6. **Mind the terms when spreading across accounts.** It is legitimate when accounts map to real\n   organisational boundaries; creating accounts purely to multiply a limit can breach a provider's terms,\n   so check them and prefer a quota increase or reserved capacity.\n7. **Buy reserved capacity for the base load** and use pay-as-you-go for burst, with batch APIs for\n   offline work because they have separate limits and typically a lower price.\n8. **Operate the quota.** Alert on remaining-budget headers and 429 rates; file increases before growth,\n   not during the incident; keep a table of quota per model per region in the runbook.","example":"### Gateway limiter that budgets like the provider\nBudgets tokens the way the provider does (reserving `max_tokens`), routes across a pool of deployments,\nhonours `Retry-After`, and refuses to retry into an overloaded pool. Deployment names and quotas are\nplaceholders.\n\n```python\nimport time, random, threading\n\nclass TokenBucket:\n    def __init__(self, per_minute: int, share: float = 0.85):\n        self.cap = per_minute * share; self.tokens = self.cap; self.t = time.time(); self.lock = threading.Lock()\n    def take(self, n: int) -> bool:\n        with self.lock:\n            now = time.time(); self.tokens = min(self.cap, self.tokens + (now - self.t) * self.cap / 60); self.t = now\n            if self.tokens >= n: self.tokens -= n; return True\n            return False\n    def give(self, n: int):                      # refund a reservation that was never sent\n        with self.lock: self.tokens = min(self.cap, self.tokens + n)\n\nclass Deployment:\n    def __init__(self, name, tpm, rpm):\n        self.name, self.tpm, self.rpm, self.open_until = name, TokenBucket(tpm), TokenBucket(rpm), 0.0\n\npool = [Deployment(\"eu-west-a\", tpm=300_000, rpm=1_800), Deployment(\"eu-west-b\", tpm=150_000, rpm=900)]\n\ndef pick(need: int):\n    for d in random.sample(pool, len(pool)):   # random order spreads load; budget only the one used\n        if d.open_until > time.time() or not d.rpm.take(1): continue\n        if d.tpm.take(need): return d\n        d.rpm.give(1)                          # token budget short: return the request slot\n    return None\n\ndef call(send, prompt_tokens, max_tokens, attempts=4):\n    need = prompt_tokens + max_tokens          # count max_tokens, as the provider does at admission\n    for attempt in range(attempts):\n        d = pick(need)\n        if d is None:\n            time.sleep(min(2 ** attempt, 8) * random.uniform(0.5, 1.5)); continue   # wait in our queue\n        status, headers, body = send(d.name)\n        if status == 200: return body\n        if status == 429:                      # our estimate drifted or the pool is busy: back off\n            time.sleep(float(headers.get(\"retry-after\", 2 ** attempt)) * random.uniform(1.0, 1.5))\n        elif status in (503, 529):             # overloaded: trip this deployment for a while\n            d.open_until = time.time() + 30\n        else:\n            raise RuntimeError(f\"{d.name}: {status}\")\n    raise RuntimeError(\"quota exhausted: shed or defer this request\")\n```\n\n- **Admission matches the provider's check:** it admits only when both the request budget and the token\n  budget of the chosen deployment have room.\n- **It debits only the deployment actually used;** debiting every candidate would silently burn the\n  others' budgets.\n- **85% of quota** keeps a margin for estimation drift.\n- **A production version would also refund** the unused part of `max_tokens` when the response reports\n  its real usage, if the provider does the same.\n\n### Which limit binds? A ceiling calculator\nPlug in your quotas to see the request ceiling, and notice that fixing one limit exposes the next.\n\n```python\ndef binding_rpm(tpm, rpm, prompt_tokens, max_tokens, concurrency=None, secs_per_request=None):\n    \"\"\"Requests per minute you can actually get: the lowest of the limits binds.\"\"\"\n    limits = {\n        \"RPM quota\": rpm,\n        \"TPM quota / tokens charged\": tpm / (prompt_tokens + max_tokens),  # providers that reserve output\n    }\n    if concurrency:\n        limits[\"concurrency cap (Little's Law)\"] = concurrency / secs_per_request * 60\n    name = min(limits, key=limits.get)\n    return name, int(limits[name])\n\n# 400k TPM and 1,000-token prompts; the RPM quota of 10,000 is a placeholder.\nprint(binding_rpm(400_000, 10_000, 1_000, 4_000))   # ('TPM quota / tokens charged', 80)\nprint(binding_rpm(400_000, 10_000, 1_000, 800))     # ('TPM quota / tokens charged', 222)\nprint(binding_rpm(400_000, 10_000, 1_000, 800, concurrency=50, secs_per_request=15))\n# (\"concurrency cap (Little's Law)\", 200): fix max_tokens and the next limit binds\n```","patterns":["**Self-limit below quota.** For every deployment, run a gateway token bucket at 80–90% of TPM and RPM (as in the limiter above), so rejections happen in your priority queue. Monitor local queue wait and provider 429s, which should stay near zero.","**Throttle on remaining-budget headers.** Export `x-ratelimit-remaining-tokens` (OpenAI) or `anthropic-ratelimit-tokens-remaining` (Anthropic) as a gauge and slow admission as it nears zero. Monitor the gauge's minimum per minute alongside 429 counts.","**Right-size `max_tokens` per endpoint.** Set it from observed output-length percentiles plus a margin, not a large default. Monitor the truncation rate: responses ending because they hit the limit (`finish_reason` of `length` on OpenAI, `stop_reason` of `max_tokens` on Anthropic).","**Circuit-break overloaded deployments.** On 503 or 529, stop sending to that deployment for a cooldown and fail over within the residency rules. Monitor open circuits per deployment and overload rate while under quota.","**Batch APIs for offline work.** Send evaluations, backfills and nightly jobs to the provider's batch API, which has separate limits and typically a lower price. Monitor batch turnaround against the job's deadline.","**Quota register in the runbook.** One table of quota, current peak and increase lead time per model per region, reviewed before each launch. Monitor peak demand as a share of quota and file increases well before it nears the self-limit."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant A as App\n    participant G as Gateway limiter\n    participant D1 as Deployment A\n    participant D2 as Deployment B\n    A->>G: request, prompt plus max_tokens\n    Note over G: debit token and request buckets at 85% of quota\n    G->>D1: send\n    D1-->>G: 429 with Retry-After\n    Note over G: wait Retry-After plus jitter, cap attempts\n    G->>D1: retry\n    D1-->>G: 529 overloaded\n    Note over G: open circuit on A for 30 s\n    G->>D2: send to next deployment\n    D2-->>G: 200 plus remaining-budget headers\n    G-->>A: response","caption":"One request through a gateway that limits itself below quota: a 429 means wait as told, an overload\nerror means stop sending to that deployment for a while and try another."},"interview":[{"q":"Why do providers set limits per model, per region and per account, and what does that mean for your plan?","a":"- **Per model** because models differ in cost and capacity, **per region** because the GPU fleet is\n  regional, **per account** because that is the billing and abuse boundary.\n- **For your plan:** demand must be converted into each provider's units for each model-region pair, and\n  the lowest limit in the region you are allowed to use is your real ceiling.\n- **Tiers rise with spend,** so a new account may need a growth path before launch, not just a ticket."},{"q":"You are under quota but still getting errors at peak. What is going on?","a":"- **Quota is not capacity:** it governs your admission, not whether the shared regional pool has room.\n- **Signs:** overload or capacity errors (Anthropic's 529, Bedrock throttling exceptions, Azure 429s on a\n  deployment under its TPM) during provider-wide peaks.\n- **Response:** circuit-break and fail over where residency allows, and buy reserved capacity for the base\n  load if you have a peak SLO."},{"q":"How is retry logic for an LLM gateway different from retrying an ordinary HTTP call?","a":"- **A sustained 429 is not transient,** so blind retries become a retry storm that consumes the budget\n  fresh requests need.\n- **Do instead:** honour `Retry-After`, add jitter, cap attempts, queue by priority, and shed or defer when\n  the wait exceeds the latency budget.\n- **Treat overload separately:** open a circuit on a deployment returning 503 or 529, because retries\n  cannot create capacity."},{"q":"Your provider caps you at 50 concurrent requests and long generations take 15 seconds. What is your ceiling, and what moves it?","a":"- **Little's Law:** 50 ÷ 15 s × 60 = 200 requests per minute, whatever the token quota says.\n- **Shorter requests raise it:** a tighter `max_tokens` or smaller outputs cut seconds per request.\n- **Otherwise:** a higher concurrency cap, reserved capacity, or spreading across deployments where\n  residency allows."},{"q":"When would you not spread traffic across regions or accounts to get more quota?","a":"- **When residency names a region:** cross-region and global routing violate it silently and surface as\n  an audit finding.\n- **When accounts would exist only to multiply a limit:** that can breach the provider's terms; prefer an\n  increase or reserved capacity.\n- **When prefix caching or a reservation matters:** spreading splits those benefits across endpoints."}],"tradeoffs":["**Self-limiting vs unused quota.** Limiting below quota prevents 429 storms and lets you prioritise, but leaves a slice of paid-for quota unused; the margin should shrink as your token estimates get better.","**Spreading vs residency and cache benefits.** Spreading across regions or deployments multiplies quota and availability, but weakens the residency guarantee and splits prefix-cache and provisioned-capacity benefits across endpoints.","**Reserved capacity vs hourly billing.** It removes 429 and overload risk for the base load, but is billed by the hour whether used or not; it pays when steady load fills most of it or an SLO forbids shed traffic.","**Tight `max_tokens` vs truncation.** A tight value frees quota and lowers reservation waste, but a value below what real answers need truncates output; tune it per endpoint from observed output-length percentiles.","**Batch APIs vs turnaround.** They give separate limits and a lower price, but hours of turnaround; they flip from useful to useless when a workload is interactive or has a deadline inside the batch window."],"pitfalls":["**Retrying 429s immediately and without a cap.** Symptom: latency rising across every endpoint while throughput stays flat, because retries consume the budget that fresh requests need.","**Leaving `max_tokens` at a large default.** Symptom: hitting TPM at a fraction of the token volume the quota suggests, visible as 429s with low measured output tokens.","**Planning capacity in one region without checking model availability and quota there.** Symptom: a launch waiting on a quota-increase ticket.","**Treating a quota as a capacity guarantee.** Symptom: overload errors during a provider-wide peak while your dashboards show you well under quota.","**Ignoring remaining-budget headers.** Symptom: learning about exhaustion from the first 429 instead of throttling smoothly before it.","**Enabling cross-region or global routing to fix a quota problem on a residency-bound workload.** Symptom: an audit finding rather than a runtime error."],"when":"- **Applies to** every workload on a hosted model API.\n- **Plan quotas when you choose the model and region,** not at launch: convert peak demand into the\n  provider's units, compare with the current allocation per model per region, and file increases with\n  weeks of lead time.\n- **Day one in the gateway:** a self-limiter, priority queue and correct retry logic.\n- **Add later:** spreading across regions or deployments when residency allows and demand exceeds one\n  region's quota; reserved capacity when there is a steady base load or an SLO at peak.\n- **Measure** 429 and overload rates, remaining budget, queue wait time, and actual versus reserved output\n  tokens.\n- **Interviews:** expect questions on why retries do not fix sustained 429s and why `max_tokens` affects\n  throughput before it affects cost.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements token-aware limiting, priority queueing and `Retry-After` handling in the gateway, tunes `max_tokens` per endpoint and surfaces remaining-budget headers as metrics.","architect":"Decides the region, deployment and provider topology per workload, when to buy reserved capacity, and how residency constraints bound the spreading options.","fde":"Audits a customer's quotas per model per region before go-live, drives the increase requests early, and explains overload errors that occur while they are under quota."},"check":{"q":"A team on a hosted API has a quota of 400k TPM for their model in the one region their\nresidency rule allows. Their load test shows 429s starting at about 120k measured tokens per\nminute. Explain what is probably happening and how you would raise effective capacity without\nbreaking the residency rule.","a":"The provider reserves `max_tokens` at admission; if requests set `max_tokens` at 4,000 while\nreal answers average a few hundred tokens, each request reserves far more than it uses, so the\nprovider's limiter counts the full 400k as consumed while only about 120k tokens are actually\nprocessed (with prompts around 1,300 tokens, each request charges about 5,300 but uses about\n1,600, a ratio near 0.3). First, set `max_tokens` from\nobserved output-length percentiles per endpoint, which can roughly triple effective capacity on\nits own. Second, add a gateway token bucket at 85% of quota with a priority queue so interactive\ntraffic is admitted first and batch work is deferred rather than retried. Third, move offline work\nto the batch API, which has separate limits. Fourth, file a quota increase with usage evidence,\nand if a peak SLO exists, buy provisioned capacity in the same region for the base load. Spreading\nto other regions is off the table because of residency, so the plan is efficiency, prioritisation\nand reserved capacity in-region."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does retrying immediately after a 429 fail to improve throughput under sustained demand?","options":["429 responses are cached by the provider for one minute","The limiter is already rejecting at the quota, so retries consume budget that new requests need and only add latency","Retries are billed at double the token price","The provider blocks the account after repeated 429s"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A 429 under sustained load means the budget is exhausted; another request does not add capacity, it competes for the same refill. Back off with jitter, honour Retry-After, and prioritise in your own queue."},{"q":"Why does an overly large `max_tokens` reduce effective throughput on a tokens-per-minute quota?","options":["Larger outputs always take longer to generate","The provider charges for `max_tokens` whether used or not","Providers that reserve output count `max_tokens` against the budget at admission, so unused reservation still counts for that minute","Requests with large `max_tokens` are routed to a slower pool"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Billing is on actual tokens, but a limiter that reserves before it knows the output length uses `max_tokens` (OpenAI, Azure OpenAI and Bedrock document this; check your provider). Setting it from real output-length percentiles recovers the wasted budget."},{"q":"A residency-bound workload exceeds the quota available in its region. Which option is valid?","options":["Enable the provider's global routing so excess traffic is served elsewhere","Spread traffic across accounts in several regions","Improve efficiency, prioritise traffic, use batch APIs for offline work, and request reserved capacity or a quota increase in that region","Disable rate limiting in the client so requests are not delayed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Global or multi-region routing violates residency silently. The in-region toolkit is tighter token reservation, prioritisation, separate batch limits, and reserved capacity or increases with lead time."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/hybrid-on-prem","sources":["Official docs: vLLM","Official docs: Kubernetes"],"why":"- **Regulation and contracts push on-prem.** Health, financial, defence and public-sector data often may\n  not be processed outside a named facility, jurisdiction or the customer's own account, and \"the provider\n  promises not to train on it\" does not satisfy that rule.\n- **Physics and connectivity push on-prem.** A factory, ship, hospital or retail site may need inference\n  near the machines generating the data, or have links that are slow, metered or intermittent.\n- **Economics can push on-prem.** A workload running flat-out all day on open-weight models can cost less\n  on amortised owned GPUs than on per-token prices, once utilization is high enough.\n- **Two forces push back.** The strongest models are available only as hosted APIs, and most products want\n  them for some tasks. Owned GPUs are a fixed quantity bought on a procurement cycle measured in months, so\n  a spike cannot be met by \"buying more\" this week; and you own the operations (drivers, firmware, CUDA\n  stacks, failures, spares, power, cooling, patching).\n- **Both naive answers fail.** \"Everything on-prem\" leaves teams on last year's open-weight model, unable\n  to serve a launch spike, paying a platform team for work the cloud would do. \"Everything in the cloud\"\n  fails the first compliance review and the first site with a bad link. Hybrid makes it a routing policy,\n  enforced by a gateway, with the application unaware.","how":"### What \"on-prem\" covers\nThe term spans a spectrum, and the control, cost and operational burden differ at each point, so pin\n\"on-prem\" down in a requirement before designing anything.\n\n| Option | Who owns hardware and control |\n|---|---|\n| Your own datacenter | You, fully |\n| Colocation space with your hardware | You own hardware; the colo provides space and power |\n| Private cloud | You, through a cloud-like layer |\n| A customer's cloud account or VPC | Cloud hardware, customer control |\n| Cloud-provider on-premises extensions | The provider's managed stack in your facility (AWS Outposts, Azure's on-premises stack and Google Distributed Cloud are examples of this category) |\n| Fully air-gapped sites | You, with no external connectivity at all |\n\n- **Scope of this lesson:** the architecture that spans both sides. The deployment module covers the\n  customer-VPC and air-gapped cases in depth from the forward-deployed perspective.\n\n### The routing policy\nEvery request carries attributes the gateway can read: data classification of its inputs, latency\nbudget, model capability required, tenant and site. The policy maps those to a target:\n\n| Request | Target | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Restricted data** | On-prem pool only | Never bursts, no fallback to cloud |\n| **Internal data, frontier capability needed** | Cloud platform in the approved region | Through private connectivity |\n| **Public or synthetic data, bulk volume** | On-prem when capacity is free (already paid for) | Cloud burst when the on-prem queue exceeds a wait threshold |\n| **Site-local, low latency or offline** | The site's own small pool | Degraded mode when central systems are unreachable |\n\n- **Enforce at the gateway** *because* enforcing it in each application means every application is a\n  compliance risk.\n- **Classification comes from the data source** (document labels, database column tags, tenant contract),\n  not from inspecting the prompt at request time. Inspection is a detector with a miss rate, and the rule\n  must hold at the boundary regardless.\n\n### A uniform API surface\n- **Application code should not know which side served it.** On-prem engines (vLLM, TGI and others) expose\n  OpenAI-compatible endpoints; hosted platforms are reached through their SDKs.\n- **The gateway presents one interface** and holds the policy, the credentials for each side, the quotas\n  and the telemetry. That makes moving a workload between sides a configuration change.\n- **The exception is behaviour.** An open-weight model on-prem and a frontier model in the cloud answer\n  differently, so prompts and evals are per route, and a workload that may be served by either side must\n  pass its eval on both.\n\n### Bursting and backflow\n- **Cloud burst is an overflow queue.** When the on-prem queue wait exceeds a threshold, eligible requests\n  (eligibility is the classification again) go to the cloud.\n- **On-prem first, cloud overflow** is the right default for workloads allowed on both sides, because the\n  owned cost is already sunk while every cloud token is a marginal cost.\n- **Backflow is the reverse.** Deferrable eligible work (batch jobs, re-indexing, evaluation runs) is pulled\n  back onto owned GPUs whenever they would otherwise idle, for example overnight, so the fixed pool stays\n  busy instead of the cloud bill growing.\n\n### Sizing the owned side\nA fixed pool is only cheaper when busy, so on-prem capacity should not be sized for the peak of all\ntraffic. The sizing has two parts:\n\n1. **Restricted work has nowhere else to go,** so its *peak* (plus a spare for a node failure) must fit\n   on-prem.\n2. **Burst-eligible work fills the gaps** the restricted load leaves (nights, weekends, the spare's idle\n   hours), and its peaks go to the cloud.\n3. **Extra GPUs beyond the restricted requirement** are justified only by eligible load present nearly all\n   the time, *because* only load that is always there keeps an extra GPU above break-even utilization;\n   eligible load that comes and goes is cheaper rented.\n\n### Operating the owned side\n- **Hardware lifecycle:** procurement lead time, spares, replacement cadence, typically a three-to-five-year\n  amortisation for the TCO model.\n- **Serving-stack lifecycle:** engine upgrades, CVE patching, driver and CUDA compatibility.\n- **Monitoring** unified with the cloud side.\n- **Model lifecycle:** which open-weight models, which quantization, evaluated against which cloud baseline.\n- **Private connectivity between sides** (VPN, dedicated links or private endpoints).\n- **Secrets held only where used,** so a compromised on-prem node does not hold cloud credentials and vice\n  versa.\n\n### Economics in one line\n```\nowned cost per token = (amortised hardware + power + space + people) ÷ tokens actually served\n```\n- **It falls with utilization;** hosted cost per token is roughly flat.\n- **The crossover utilization is the break-even,** covered in its own lesson. The hybrid pattern's job is to\n  keep the owned side above it by giving it all the eligible work.","example":"### A gateway routing policy\nEnforces classification, routes for capability and bursts only eligible traffic. Thresholds and model\nnames are placeholders.\n\n```python\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom enum import Enum\n\nclass DataClass(Enum):\n    RESTRICTED = \"restricted\"   # may never leave the owned environment\n    INTERNAL = \"internal\"       # cloud allowed in the approved region, via private link\n    PUBLIC = \"public\"\n\n@dataclass\nclass Request:\n    data_class: DataClass\n    needs_frontier: bool        # task requires a capability only the hosted model has\n    latency_budget_ms: int\n    site: str | None = None     # set for site-local traffic\n\nONPREM_QUEUE_WAIT_MS = {\"central\": 0, \"plant-7\": 0}   # refreshed from engine metrics\nBURST_THRESHOLD_MS = 800\n\ndef route(r: Request) -> str:\n    if r.data_class is DataClass.RESTRICTED:\n        if r.needs_frontier:\n            raise PermissionError(\"restricted data cannot use the hosted frontier model\")\n        return \"onprem:central\"                          # no burst, no fallback, by policy\n    if r.site and r.latency_budget_ms < 300:\n        return f\"onprem:{r.site}\"                        # site-local pool, degraded mode if central is down\n    if r.needs_frontier:\n        return \"cloud:eu-region\"                         # private endpoint, approved region\n    if ONPREM_QUEUE_WAIT_MS[\"central\"] > BURST_THRESHOLD_MS and r.latency_budget_ms < 5_000:\n        return \"cloud:eu-region\"                         # interactive eligible burst only;\n                                                         # deferrable work waits for owned GPUs\n    return \"onprem:central\"                              # owned capacity is sunk cost: keep it busy\n\nprint(route(Request(DataClass.RESTRICTED, False, 2000)))        # onprem:central\nprint(route(Request(DataClass.INTERNAL, True, 2000)))           # cloud:eu-region\nONPREM_QUEUE_WAIT_MS[\"central\"] = 1500\nprint(route(Request(DataClass.PUBLIC, False, 2000)))            # cloud:eu-region (burst)\n```\n\n- **It refuses rather than degrades** when restricted data needs a capability only the cloud has. That\n  refusal is the signal to procure a better on-prem model, not a bug to route around.\n- **The order of the checks is the policy:** classification first, so no later rule (latency, capability,\n  burst) can move restricted data.\n\n### Worked scenario: sizing the owned pool\nIllustrative numbers, in replicas of one model.\n\n| Load | Daytime | Overnight |\n|---|---|---|\n| Restricted traffic | peaks at 3 replicas on weekday afternoons | falls to 1 |\n| Eligible interactive traffic | runs at 2 replicas, peaks at 6 | low |\n| Overnight batch | none | could use 4 replicas for 8 hours |\n\n1. **Own 3 + 1 spare = 4** for the restricted peak.\n2. **During the day,** the spare and any restricted slack take eligible interactive work, and the rest of\n   its peak bursts to the cloud.\n3. **Overnight,** restricted needs 1 and the batch takes the other 3, so the pool stays busy around the\n   clock.\n4. **Buying 2 more replicas** for the daytime eligible base makes sense only if those 2 also stay busy\n   overnight and at weekends. If not, they sit idle for over half the week and the cloud is cheaper for\n   that load.","patterns":["**Default-deny classification from source labels.** The data service attaches the record's label (document label, column tag, tenant contract) to the request, and the gateway rejects any request without one. Monitor rejections for missing labels and the share of traffic per class.","**OpenAI-compatible engines on the owned side.** Run vLLM's OpenAI-compatible server (`vllm serve <model>`) on-prem so the gateway uses the same client with a different base URL for each side. Monitor per-route latency and error rates on one dashboard.","**Burst on queue wait, by class.** The gateway reads the on-prem engine's waiting-request metric and sends only eligible interactive requests to the cloud above a wait threshold. Monitor burst volume and its cost per day.","**Backflow scheduling.** Run batch, re-indexing and eval jobs as low-priority work (for example a Kubernetes `CronJob` overnight) on owned GPUs. Monitor owned-pool utilization by hour of day and week.","**Per-route evals in CI.** Run the same eval suite against the on-prem and cloud routes before any prompt or model change ships. Monitor the score gap between routes, which predicts burst-time regressions.","**Credentials only where used.** Cloud credentials live with the cloud-facing gateway component, never on on-prem serving nodes. Monitor with secret scanning and access audits on both sides."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Request with source data label\"] --> C{\"Restricted data?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes\"| F1{\"Needs frontier model?\"}\n    F1 -->|\"yes\"| X[\"Refuse: signal to improve on-prem model\"]\n    F1 -->|\"no\"| OP[\"On-prem central pool\"]\n    C -->|\"no\"| S{\"Site-local with tight latency?\"}\n    S -->|\"yes\"| SP[\"Site pool, degraded mode if offline\"]\n    S -->|\"no\"| F2{\"Needs frontier model?\"}\n    F2 -->|\"yes\"| CL[\"Cloud, approved region, private link\"]\n    F2 -->|\"no\"| Q{\"On-prem queue over threshold?\"}\n    Q -->|\"yes and interactive\"| CL\n    Q -->|\"no, or deferrable\"| OP","caption":"The order of checks is the policy: classification first, so no later rule about latency, capability or\nburst can move restricted data off owned hardware."},"interview":[{"q":"A requirement says \"must be on-prem\". What do you ask before designing anything?","a":"- **Which point on the spectrum:** own datacenter, colo, private cloud, the customer's cloud account or VPC,\n  a cloud provider's on-premises extension, or an air-gapped site.\n- **Why it matters:** control, cost and operational burden differ at each point, and a customer VPC still\n  carries the customer's cloud bill and the provider's egress terms.\n- **Which data it applies to:** usually a named data class, which decides what may still use hosted APIs."},{"q":"Why enforce data classification at the gateway instead of in each application?","a":"- **One enforcement point:** if each application enforces the rule, every application is a compliance risk\n  and one mistake leaks restricted data.\n- **Deterministic labels:** the gateway reads labels from the data source, not a prompt detector with a\n  miss rate.\n- **Order of checks:** classification runs first, so no latency, capability or burst rule can move\n  restricted data."},{"q":"uality complaints arrive only during traffic peaks. What is your first hypothesis?","a":"- **Bursting changed the model:** at peak, eligible requests overflow from the open-weight on-prem model to\n  the cloud model (or the reverse during fallback), and a prompt tuned on one route regresses on the other.\n- **Confirm:** split quality metrics by route and correlate with burst volume.\n- **Fix:** keep prompts and evals per route and require a workload that may run on both sides to pass its\n  eval on both."},{"q":"Restricted traffic peaks at 3 replicas, eligible interactive at 6, and an overnight batch needs 4. How many replicas do you own?","a":"- **Own 4:** 3 for the restricted peak, which cannot burst, plus 1 spare for a node failure.\n- **Fill the gaps:** daytime slack takes eligible interactive work and its peak bursts to the cloud;\n  overnight, restricted needs 1 and the batch takes the other 3.\n- **Buy more only for always-present load:** 2 more replicas pay off only if they stay busy nights and\n  weekends too."},{"q":"When would you not build a hybrid setup?","a":"- **No residency or connectivity constraint and volume below break-even:** stay fully hosted, because owned\n  GPUs only pay when busy.\n- **Every hosted path forbidden and open-weight models good enough:** go fully on-prem rather than run two\n  stacks.\n- **Load spiky or growing fast:** months of procurement lead time make owned capacity a poor fit."}],"tradeoffs":["**Owned cost vs fixed quantity.** Owned GPUs lower cost per token at high utilization, but are a fixed quantity with months of lead time; the trade flips against ownership when load is spiky or still growing fast.","**Residency vs model quality.** On-prem satisfies residency and air-gap rules, but confines those workloads to open-weight models that typically trail the frontier; when the task needs frontier quality, the choice is a better on-prem model or a policy change, not a routing trick.","**Bursting vs data paths.** Bursting absorbs peaks without over-buying hardware, but every burst path is a data path that must be gated by classification, and prompts must be evaluated on both models.","**Uniform interface vs hidden behaviour.** One gateway interface makes migration a configuration change, but hides behavioural differences between routes, which is why evals and prompts stay per route.","**Provider on-prem extensions vs lock-in.** They reduce the operations burden of owned hardware, but keep the provider's pricing and lock-in inside your building."],"pitfalls":["**Sizing on-prem for the peak of all traffic,** including work that could burst. Symptom: a GPU fleet averaging well under a quarter utilization while the finance model assumed near-full.","**Classification by prompt inspection instead of by source.** Symptom: a restricted document found in cloud logs because a detector missed it.","**One prompt tuned on the cloud model and reused on-prem.** Symptom: a quality regression that only appears during bursts or on-prem fallback.","**No spare hardware or replacement plan.** Symptom: weeks of reduced capacity after a single GPU or node failure.","**Cloud credentials stored on on-prem nodes for \"convenience\".** Symptom: a security review finding that an on-prem compromise exposes the cloud account.","**Treating a customer VPC deployment as \"on-prem\" in the TCO model.** Symptom: a cost estimate that omits the customer's cloud bill and the provider's egress and support terms."],"when":"- **Use hybrid when** a named class of data or sites cannot use hosted APIs *and* other workloads need\n  frontier models or elastic capacity. The hard parts are the routing policy, a uniform API surface, and\n  keeping fixed capacity busy.\n- **Stay fully hosted when** no residency or connectivity constraint exists and volume is below the\n  break-even.\n- **Go fully on-prem only when** policy forbids every hosted path and open-weight models meet the quality\n  bar.\n- **Measure** owned-pool utilization, burst volume and its cost, per-route quality on shared evals, and the\n  number of policy refusals, which tells you where the on-prem model is falling short.\n- **Day to day** this shows up as the gateway's policy table and the quarterly review of what each side\n  costs.\n- **Interviews:** defend why classification is enforced at the gateway and why on-prem is sized for the\n  restricted peak plus only the always-present base of eligible work rather than for total peak.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the gateway policy and burst logic, runs OpenAI-compatible engines on the owned side, and maintains per-route prompts and evals so either side passes the same test suite.","architect":"Defines the data classes and their permitted targets, sizes the owned pool to the restricted peak plus the always-present eligible base, owns the TCO and break-even model, and signs off on the connectivity and secrets design.","fde":"Deploys the on-prem or customer-VPC side inside the customer's environment, maps their data classifications to routes, and sets up burst eligibility and monitoring that the customer's security team accepts."},"check":{"q":"A hospital group wants an assistant over patient records (must stay on-site), a general\nstaff assistant, and overnight batch summarisation of public research papers. They own eight\nGPUs. Design the hybrid layout and say what you would measure to know it is working.","a":"Patient-record traffic is restricted: it runs on the owned pool with an open-weight model,\nno burst, no fallback, enforced by the gateway from the record system's classification, and the\nmodel is evaluated on clinical tasks before launch. The staff assistant needs frontier quality and\ntouches internal but not restricted data, so it goes to a cloud platform in an approved region\nover a private endpoint. The research batch is public data: it runs on the owned pool overnight\nwhen the restricted workload is quiet, bursting to the cloud batch API if it cannot finish by\nmorning. Size the owned pool for the restricted workload's peak (it cannot burst) with one GPU\nor node of spare, and let the overnight batch fill the idle hours, not for the combined peak of all\nthree workloads.\nMeasure owned-pool utilization (target high, since cost per token depends on it), queue wait and\nburst volume, per-route eval scores, and policy refusals, which show where the on-prem model is\ninsufficient."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should data classification for routing be derived from the data source rather than from inspecting the prompt?","options":["Prompt inspection is too slow for interactive latency budgets","A detector has a miss rate, while source-level labels make the restriction hold at the boundary regardless of prompt content","Prompts are encrypted before they reach the gateway","Source labels are required by the serving engine to allocate KV cache"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Compliance rules are absolute; a classifier that misses one restricted document has already violated them. Labels attached to the record system, tenant or document travel with the request and are enforced deterministically."},{"q":"Which sizing rule keeps owned GPUs economical in a hybrid design?","options":["Size on-prem for peak so the cloud is never needed","Fit the restricted workload's peak, add capacity only for eligible load present nearly all the time, and send eligible peaks to the cloud","Size on-prem for the restricted workload's average load and send everything else to the cloud","Size on-prem to match the cloud quota in the approved region"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Owned cost per token falls with utilization, so the pool must be busy, but restricted work cannot burst, so its peak sets a floor. Filling the remaining capacity with the base of eligible work keeps the pool full, while elastic cloud capacity absorbs eligible peaks that would otherwise sit idle on owned hardware; sizing restricted work at its average would drop requests at its peak."},{"q":"A restricted-data workload needs a capability only the hosted frontier model has. What should the gateway do?","options":["Route to the cloud once with logging so the exception is auditable","Strip identifying fields from the prompt and route to the cloud","Refuse the request and surface it as a signal to improve the on-prem model or change policy","Route to the cloud only during off-peak hours when scrutiny is lower"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The restriction is on the data, not on the time or on identifiers that a detector might miss. The correct response is a policy refusal, which becomes evidence for procuring a stronger on-prem model or formally revisiting the rule."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/edge-ai-deployment","sources":["Frantar et al., 2022 — GPTQ: Accurate Post-Training Quantization for Generative Pre-trained Transformers","Official docs: llama.cpp","Official docs: ONNX Runtime"],"why":"- **A cloud call has four costs.** A network round trip, a need for connectivity, uploading the user's\n  data, and per-token billing. For many workloads none of that matters; for some it is disqualifying.\n- **The disqualifying cases.** A keyboard suggestion or voice assistant that waits 400 ms for a server\n  feels broken; a field technician in a basement has no signal; a factory camera stream cannot be shipped\n  to a datacenter every frame; health-app users will not accept data leaving the phone; and a product with\n  a hundred million installs cannot pay per-token prices for every interaction.\n- **\"Ship the cloud model\" fails on arithmetic.** A 70B model in fp16 is roughly 140 GB of weights; a phone\n  gives an app a few gigabytes at most. Even a laptop with 16 GB of unified memory comfortably holds only a\n  4-bit model of about 7–8B parameters (a 13–14B model is a squeeze) once the OS, other apps and the KV\n  cache are counted.\n- **Bandwidth and heat bind too.** Decode is bound by memory bandwidth, a small fraction of a datacenter\n  GPU's on a phone, so a model streaming 100 tokens per second in the cloud would crawl on device even if\n  it fit. Sustained inference throttles the chip and drains the battery.\n- **\"Quantize everything to 4-bit and hope\" fails on quality.** Compression without evaluation produces\n  models that pass a demo and fail in the field. Edge is a budgeting and engineering discipline, not a flag\n  on the cloud model.","how":"### Which edge\n| | On-device | On-site edge servers |\n|---|---|---|\n| Where | The user's phone, laptop, browser or an embedded controller | A small box or rack at a store, plant, hospital or cell site (a Jetson-class module, a workstation GPU, a telco edge node) |\n| Ownership | You do not own the hardware; it is shared with other apps | You own it |\n| Fleet | Heterogeneous | Fewer devices, known hardware, a few users each |\n| Planned like | This lesson, where the constraints are hardest | A tiny on-prem cluster |\n\nBoth give the same latency and connectivity benefits.\n\n### Start from the device budget\nThree numbers bound the design:\n\n| Budget | Rule | Illustration |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Memory** | Weights plus KV cache plus activations must fit in what the platform grants an app, with margin for the app itself. Weight bytes ≈ parameters × bits per weight ÷ 8 | A 3B model at 4-bit is about 1.5 GB and an 8B model about 4 GB, before the cache |\n| **Bandwidth** | Each decode step reads all the weights once, so tokens per second ≤ memory bandwidth ÷ model bytes | Illustrative order of magnitude: tens of GB/s and a 2 GB model cannot exceed a few tens of tokens per second, whatever the compute |\n| **Power and thermal** | Sustained throughput is lower than burst because the chip throttles | Plan on sustained figures from warm devices; battery cost per interaction is a product metric on phones and laptops |\n\n### Prefill on a device\n- **The datacenter split still applies, with a twist.** Decode is bandwidth-bound, but prefill is\n  compute-bound, and on a phone compute is scarce too.\n- **So TTFT grows with prompt length:** TTFT ≈ prompt tokens ÷ prefill tokens per second.\n- **Example:** a 2,000-token prompt (system prompt plus a retrieved document) on a device that prefills a\n  few hundred tokens a second waits several seconds before the first word appears.\n- **Hence the habits:** keep prompts short, reuse a cached system-prompt prefix across turns, and use the\n  NPU or GPU for prefill where the runtime supports it.\n- **Plan both numbers:** TTFT from prompt length, streaming speed from bandwidth.\n\n### Make the model fit\n- **Choose a small model first.** Down to about 4 bits per weight, a larger quantized model often beats a\n  smaller one at the same byte budget, so 4-bit is a common sweet spot.\n- **Below roughly 3 bits quality usually falls off sharply,** so a large model that only fits at 2-bit is\n  typically beaten by a small language model (hundreds of millions to a few billion parameters) at 4-bit,\n  especially one trained or distilled for the task. The crossover is model- and task-dependent; measure it.\n- **Quantize,** typically to 4-bit or 8-bit weights, with activations kept at higher precision.\n  Post-training methods (GPTQ, AWQ and the k-quant families used by llama.cpp) cut memory and raise\n  bandwidth-bound speed roughly in proportion to the bit reduction.\n- **Measure the quality cost per model and task:** it is small for many models at 8-bit and 4-bit but can\n  be severe for the smallest models.\n- **Distil** a large model's behaviour into a small one for the specific task, which is how most production\n  on-device models are made task-competent.\n- **Prune and adapt.** Structured pruning removes capacity the task does not need; LoRA adapters let one\n  base model serve several device features with small per-feature deltas.\n\n### Match the runtime to the silicon\nDevice accelerators are heterogeneous: CPU vector units, mobile GPUs, NPUs on phones and laptops, Apple's\nunified-memory GPU and Neural Engine, NVIDIA Jetson-class embedded GPUs. Runtimes map one model to many:\n\n| Runtime | Targets |\n|---|---|\n| llama.cpp with its GGUF format | CPU and GPU back ends across desktop and mobile |\n| ONNX Runtime | Execution providers per accelerator, including mobile |\n| Core ML | Apple |\n| LiteRT (formerly TensorFlow Lite) and ExecuTorch | Mobile and embedded, from the TensorFlow and PyTorch ecosystems |\n| TensorRT | NVIDIA embedded and desktop GPUs |\n| WebGPU-based runtimes | In-browser inference |\n\n- **Decide per device class,** because the fastest path on each is different.\n- **Test on real devices,** not a developer laptop.\n\n### Hybrid by default\n- **A small on-device model handles what it can;** requests needing more capability, context or knowledge\n  escalate to the cloud.\n- **The escalation rule is explicit** (confidence threshold, task type, user setting) and is itself a\n  privacy decision, since escalation uploads data.\n- **On-device first with cloud fallback** is the shape most consumer platforms have converged on, and it\n  preserves the latency and offline benefits for the common case.\n\n### Lifecycle on a fleet you do not control\n- **Model files are distributed like app assets:** downloaded on Wi-Fi, versioned, delta-updated, with\n  integrity checks.\n- **Many versions live at once,** so telemetry (opt-in, aggregated) must carry the model version, and the\n  server-side fallback must accept requests from old clients.\n- **Capability detection at install time** decides which model variant, if any, a device gets; low-end\n  devices get cloud-only mode.\n- **Rollouts are staged by device class** because a model that fits one phone can crash another.\n\n### Security on device\n- **Anything on the device belongs to the user,** including the weights, the prompts and any guardrail\n  logic.\n- **Treat model weights as extractable** and keep anything proprietary in the cloud model.\n- **Never rely on on-device prompts or filters** for decisions that matter to the server; re-check on the\n  server side when the request escalates.","example":"### Running a quantized GGUF model with a budget check\nllama.cpp through its Python binding, with the memory check first. The model file name is a placeholder;\nparameters and quantization are illustrative.\n\n```python\nimport os, time\nfrom llama_cpp import Llama\n\ndef fits(model_path: str, n_ctx: int, kv_bytes_per_token: int, budget_bytes: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Weights on disk approximate weights in memory for GGUF; add the KV cache for n_ctx.\"\"\"\n    weights = os.path.getsize(model_path)\n    need = weights + n_ctx * kv_bytes_per_token\n    print(f\"weights {weights/1e9:.2f} GB + cache {n_ctx*kv_bytes_per_token/1e9:.2f} GB vs budget {budget_bytes/1e9:.1f} GB\")\n    return need <= budget_bytes\n\nMODEL = \"small-chat-3b.Q4_K_M.gguf\"          # ~3B params at 4-bit: roughly 1.5-2 GB on disk\nN_CTX = 2048\n# kv_bytes_per_token = 2 * n_layers * n_kv_heads * head_dim * bytes; read these from the model card\nassert fits(MODEL, N_CTX, kv_bytes_per_token=2 * 28 * 8 * 128 * 2, budget_bytes=4 * 1024**3)\n\nllm = Llama(model_path=MODEL, n_ctx=N_CTX, n_threads=os.cpu_count() or 4,\n            n_gpu_layers=-1, verbose=False)   # offload all layers to Metal/CUDA/Vulkan if present\n\nt = time.time()                      # timing below includes prompt processing, not just decode\nout = llm.create_chat_completion(\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Reply in one sentence.\"},\n              {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Summarise why the pump alarm fired.\"}],\n    max_tokens=64,\n)\ntext = out[\"choices\"][0][\"message\"][\"content\"]\nn_out = out[\"usage\"][\"completion_tokens\"]\nprint(text); print(f\"{n_out/(time.time()-t):.1f} tok/s on this device\")\n# Measure sustained tok/s after several minutes on a warm device, not this first burst.\n```\n\n- **The `fits` check is the on-device capacity question:** weights plus cache against the budget the\n  platform grants.\n- **The printed tokens-per-second figure** decides whether streaming feels responsive, and it will drop as\n  the device heats up.\n\n### Device budget calculator: why long prompts feel slow\nPure Python, no model needed. The device figures (50 GB/s bandwidth, 300 tokens per second of prefill) are\nillustrative; replace them with measurements from your target hardware.\n\n```python\ndef edge_budget(params_b, bits, n_ctx, kv_bytes_per_token, bandwidth_gb_s, prefill_tok_s, prompt_tokens):\n    \"\"\"Rough on-device numbers: weights, cache, decode ceiling and time to first token.\"\"\"\n    weights_gb = params_b * bits / 8               # billions of params x bits / 8 = GB\n    cache_gb = n_ctx * kv_bytes_per_token / 1e9\n    max_decode_tok_s = bandwidth_gb_s / weights_gb  # each decode step reads all weights once\n    ttft_s = prompt_tokens / prefill_tok_s          # prefill is compute-bound, and compute is scarce\n    return dict(weights_gb=round(weights_gb, 2), cache_gb=round(cache_gb, 2),\n                max_decode_tok_s=round(max_decode_tok_s), ttft_s=round(ttft_s, 1))\n\nkv = 2 * 28 * 8 * 128 * 2       # bytes per token for the model shape used in the llama.cpp example\n# Illustrative device: 50 GB/s memory bandwidth, prefills 300 tokens per second.\nprint(edge_budget(3, 4, 2048, kv, bandwidth_gb_s=50, prefill_tok_s=300, prompt_tokens=2000))\n# {'weights_gb': 1.5, 'cache_gb': 0.23, 'max_decode_tok_s': 33, 'ttft_s': 6.7}\nprint(edge_budget(3, 4, 2048, kv, bandwidth_gb_s=50, prefill_tok_s=300, prompt_tokens=500))\n# {'weights_gb': 1.5, 'cache_gb': 0.23, 'max_decode_tok_s': 33, 'ttft_s': 1.7}\n```\n\n| Change | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| Prompt cut from 2,000 to 500 tokens (reuse the cached system-prompt prefix) | TTFT falls from 6.7 s to 1.7 s; streaming speed unchanged |\n| Same model at 8-bit instead of 4-bit | weights double to 3 GB, decode ceiling halves to about 17 tok/s |\n| 8B model at 4-bit | about 4 GB of weights: over a 3 GB app budget before the cache |","patterns":["**Capability detection at install.** Read available RAM and accelerator at install or first launch and pick a model variant, or cloud-only mode for low-end devices. Monitor the share of devices per variant and out-of-memory crash rate by device class.","**Model files as verified, versioned assets.** Download on Wi-Fi, verify a checksum before loading, and stage rollouts by device class. Monitor the distribution of model versions in the fleet and crash rate per version.","**Quantization eval gate.** Evaluate each candidate quantization (for example GGUF `Q8_0` and `Q4_K_M`) on the task set against the cloud model, and ship the smallest one that passes. Monitor field quality reports and escalation rate per variant.","**Thermal soak tests on real devices.** Run a fixed workload for several minutes on the target device matrix and record sustained tokens per second, peak memory and battery drain. Monitor the same figures from opt-in field telemetry.","**Stable system prompt for prefix reuse.** Keep the system prompt fixed and first so the runtime can reuse its cached prefix across turns instead of re-running prefill. Monitor TTFT by prompt length.","**Server-side re-check on escalation.** Every escalated request passes the same policy checks as a pure cloud request, whatever the device claims. Monitor server-side rejections of escalated requests."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    I[\"Install: detect RAM and accelerator\"] --> V{\"Device can hold a model variant?\"}\n    V -->|\"no\"| CO[\"Cloud-only mode\"]\n    V -->|\"yes\"| L[\"Small quantized model on device\"]\n    L --> C{\"Confident and in scope?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes\"| A[\"Answer locally\"]\n    C -->|\"no\"| P{\"User setting allows upload?\"}\n    P -->|\"no\"| D[\"Degraded local answer\"]\n    P -->|\"yes, offline\"| Q[\"Queue until connectivity returns\"]\n    P -->|\"yes, online\"| E[\"Escalate to cloud model\"]\n    Q --> E\n    E --> S[\"Server re-checks policy\"]","caption":"The common consumer shape: decide at install whether a device gets a local model, answer locally when\nconfident, and treat every escalation as an upload that policy must allow."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between on-device and on-site edge deployment, and why does it change the plan?","a":"- **On-device** runs on hardware you do not own (phones, laptops, browsers, controllers), shared with other\n  apps across a heterogeneous fleet, so memory budgets, testing and rollout are the hard parts.\n- **On-site edge servers** are boxes you own at a store, plant or cell site, with known hardware and few\n  users each.\n- **So** on-site edge is planned like a tiny on-prem cluster, while on-device needs capability detection,\n  per-device runtimes and staged rollouts."},{"q":"A laptop has 16 GB of unified memory. What is the largest model you would plan to run, and how fast will it be?","a":"- **Memory:** weights ≈ parameters × bits ÷ 8, so a 4-bit 7–8B model takes about 4 GB and fits comfortably\n  with the OS, apps and KV cache; a 13–14B model is a squeeze.\n- **Speed:** decode tokens per second ≤ memory bandwidth ÷ model bytes, so measure the device's bandwidth\n  and divide.\n- **Then measure sustained figures** on a warm machine, because thermal throttling lowers them."},{"q":"Users say an on-device summary feature \"freezes\" with long documents but streams fine afterwards. What is happening?","a":"- **TTFT, not decode:** prefill is compute-bound, so TTFT ≈ prompt tokens ÷ prefill tokens per second, and\n  a 2,000-token prompt at a few hundred tokens a second takes several seconds.\n- **Fixes:** shorter prompts, a stable system prompt whose cached prefix is reused, and prefill on the NPU\n  or GPU where the runtime supports it.\n- **Measure** TTFT by prompt length on real devices, separately from streaming speed."},{"q":"How do you ship a new on-device model version to millions of heterogeneous devices?","a":"- **Treat it like an app asset:** Wi-Fi download, versioning, delta updates and integrity checks before\n  loading.\n- **Stage by device class,** because a model that fits one phone can crash another, and keep cloud-only\n  mode for devices that cannot hold it.\n- **Carry the model version** in telemetry and requests, and keep the server fallback compatible with old\n  clients."},{"q":"When would you keep a feature in the cloud instead of moving it on device?","a":"- **Quality:** the task needs frontier reasoning, broad knowledge or large context that no small model can\n  match.\n- **Operations:** the device matrix is too fragmented to test, or the user base is small enough that the\n  saved cloud bill does not repay the engineering.\n- **Security:** the logic or weights are proprietary, since anything on the device is extractable."}],"tradeoffs":["**No network vs capped quality.** On-device inference removes network latency, cloud cost and data upload, but caps model quality at what fits the device's memory and bandwidth; the trade flips when the task needs frontier capability or broad knowledge.","**4-bit vs 8-bit.** Quantization to 4-bit roughly halves memory and raises speed versus 8-bit, but the quality loss grows as models get smaller; measure on the task before shipping.","**Escalation vs privacy and dependency.** A hybrid escalation path recovers quality for hard cases, but every escalation is a network dependency and a privacy event, so the rule must be visible to the user and the product.","**Per-device runtimes vs test matrix.** They extract the most from each accelerator, but multiply the build, test and update matrix; a single portable runtime is slower and simpler.","**Near-zero marginal compute vs slow operations.** Running on the user's hardware makes marginal compute cost near zero, but makes rollouts, debugging and telemetry slower and partial than on a server you control; for a small or internal user base the cloud bill it saves may not repay that engineering."],"pitfalls":["**Measuring only tokens per second and ignoring prompt length.** Symptom: a feature that streams quickly once started but sits blank for seconds whenever a long document is in context.","**Benchmarking on a developer laptop.** Symptom: a model that is fast in the demo and crawls or crashes on the median user's device.","**Measuring burst throughput.** Symptom: responsiveness collapses after a minute of use as the device throttles thermally.","**Forgetting the KV cache in the memory budget.** Symptom: out-of-memory kills on long conversations even though the weights \"fit\".","**Shipping a quantized model without task evals.** Symptom: a quality regression reported from the field that the cloud model never showed.","**Relying on on-device guardrails or prompts for policy.** Symptom: users bypass them by editing the app or its model files.","**No model-version field in telemetry and requests.** Symptom: a bug that reproduces on some devices and cannot be attributed to the variant they run."],"when":"- **Use when** latency must be below a network round trip, the device is often offline, the data must not\n  leave it, or per-interaction cloud cost at your install base is untenable, *and* a small or distilled\n  model can meet the quality bar on the task.\n- **Avoid when** the task needs frontier reasoning or large context, or the device matrix is too fragmented\n  to test.\n- **Measure** sustained tokens per second on target devices, memory high-water marks with the KV cache,\n  task-eval scores per quantization level, escalation rate to the cloud, and crash rates by device class.\n- **Interviews:** expect to size a model to a device from parameter count and bit width and to explain why\n  decode speed follows memory bandwidth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Quantizes and evaluates candidate models, integrates the runtime for each target platform, implements the memory check and the escalation path, and builds the device test matrix.","architect":"Sets the on-device versus cloud split per feature, the privacy rule for escalation, the model distribution and versioning scheme, and which capabilities stay server-side for security.","fde":"Fits a customer's workload to their actual devices (plant controllers, handhelds, kiosks), proves sustained performance on site, and designs the offline and degraded modes they need."},"check":{"q":"A logistics company wants a package-scanning assistant on handheld devices with 6 GB of RAM,\nof which the app may use about 3 GB, working in warehouses with poor connectivity. Walk through\nhow you would choose and validate the model.","a":"Budget first: 3 GB must hold weights, the KV cache for the longest expected interaction and\nthe app. A 3B-class model at 4-bit is about 1.5–2 GB of weights (k-quant formats spend a little\nmore than 4 bits per weight), and a 2k-token cache for a model of that shape is a few hundred MB,\nleaving room for the app; an 8B model at 4-bit (about 4 GB) does not fit. Then quality: distil or fine-tune the\nsmall model on the scanning tasks and evaluate it against the cloud model on a task set, at the\nexact quantization that will ship. Then speed: measure sustained tokens per second on the real\nhandheld after thermal steady state, since decode is bound by its memory bandwidth, and check the\nhigh-water memory mark on long sessions. Design offline-first with queued escalation to the cloud\nwhen connectivity returns for cases below a confidence threshold. Stage the rollout by device\nclass, carry the model version in telemetry, and keep any policy enforcement on the server."},"quiz":[{"q":"What primarily bounds decode tokens per second for an LLM on a phone?","options":["The number of CPU cores available to the app","Memory bandwidth divided by the bytes of weights read per step","The size of the KV cache in the longest conversation","Network latency to the model's update server"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each generated token requires reading all the weights once; a phone's memory bandwidth is a small fraction of a datacenter GPU's, so the model's byte size divided into that bandwidth gives the ceiling. Quantization helps precisely because it shrinks the bytes read."},{"q":"Why is a purpose-built small model at 4-bit usually better on device than a large model squeezed to 2–3 bits to fit the same memory?","options":["Small models do not need a KV cache","Large models cannot be quantized below 8-bit","Quality degrades sharply below about 4 bits per weight, so the extra parameters do not make up for it, while a small model distilled for the task keeps its quality at the same byte budget","Small models run on the CPU, which is always faster than the NPU"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At a fixed memory budget the question is where quality per byte is highest. Around 4 bits a larger model often wins, but pushing it to 2–3 bits degrades it sharply, whereas a distilled small model at 4-bit keeps task performance. The crossover still has to be measured per task."},{"q":"Which safeguard belongs on the server even when the model runs entirely on device?","options":["The tokenizer, so that vocabulary stays consistent","The model's quantization tables","Policy enforcement for decisions that matter, since on-device prompts, filters and weights can be modified by the user","The KV cache, to save device memory"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Everything on the device is under the user's control and can be extracted or altered. Guardrails that protect the business or other users must be re-checked server-side when requests escalate or produce consequential actions."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","sources":["Little, 1961 — A Proof for the Queuing Formula L = λW","Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"- **The web habit breaks.** A classic web service has fairly uniform requests: if one pod handles 500 RPS\n  at p99 under 100 ms, ten pods handle about 5,000. Teams carry that habit into LLM serving and it breaks\n  in three ways.\n- **Cost per request is wildly uneven.** A 200-token classification and a 30,000-token document summary\n  are both \"one request\", yet the second processes 150 times the input tokens (more in attention work,\n  which grows faster than linearly with context) and holds GPU memory far longer. An RPS figure says\n  nothing about which mix you will get.\n- **A request occupies a GPU for seconds, not milliseconds.** Output tokens come one at a time, so a\n  500-token answer at 30 ms per token holds a batch slot and its KV cache for 15 seconds. What saturates a\n  replica is sequences *in flight at once*, bounded by KV memory and promised per-token latency, not by\n  arrivals per second.\n- **The limiting units come from different places.** A self-hosted engine runs out of KV memory or decode\n  bandwidth; a managed API throttles requests and tokens per minute; your websocket tier and session store\n  care about open conversations. A plan in one unit misses the constraint that binds.\n- **The naive fixes fail.** Averaging tokens/sec over a day or adding GPUs until the alerts stop either\n  under-provisions the peak or pays for idle hardware without knowing which.","how":"### 1. Describe the workload as distributions, not averages\nCapacity starts from traffic, not hardware. Collect, per use case:\n\n- **Peak concurrent active users** and how often each sends a message.\n- **Model calls per message** (an agent may make several).\n- **Input tokens per call** (system prompt + history + retrieved context) and **output tokens per call**,\n  at p50 and p95.\n- **Peak-to-average ratio** of traffic over a day and a week.\n- **Why the tails:** a long-context minority can consume most of the KV memory while being a small share\n  of requests.\n\n### 2. Convert between the three units\n| Unit | Formula | What it sizes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Requests/sec (λ)** | active users × messages per user per second × calls per message | Arrivals, RPM quotas |\n| **Tokens/sec** | input TPS = λ × mean input tokens; output TPS = λ × mean output tokens | Prefill and decode work, TPM quotas |\n| **Concurrent in-flight sequences (L)** | Little's Law: L = λ × W, with W ≈ queue wait + TTFT + output tokens × TPOT (time per output token) | Batch slots and KV memory: the GPUs |\n| **Concurrent sessions** | Open conversations, mostly idle; sessions × duty cycle (fraction of wall time a session has a call running) ≈ in-flight sequences | Connection tier, session store, prefix-cache retention |\n\n- **Keep input and output separate** *because* they hit different resources: input is prefill,\n  compute-bound and parallel; output is decode, bandwidth-bound and sequential per request. Planning on\n  \"total tokens\" hides which one binds.\n- **Little's Law holds for any stable system in steady state,** whatever the arrival or service\n  distribution, which is *why* it is the safe bridge between units. It does not tell you queueing delay;\n  that is the job of the queueing-theory lesson.\n\n### The circularity: W depends on load\n- **TPOT rises as the batch grows,** so more in-flight sequences make each call longer, which makes L\n  larger still.\n- **Break the loop** by using TPOT and TTFT measured *at the concurrency you plan to run*, not at batch 1.\n  An unloaded TPOT undercounts in-flight sequences exactly when it matters.\n- **Consistency identity:** a replica running B sequences at TPOT t produces about B ÷ t output tokens per\n  second, so in-flight sequences, TPOT and output tokens/sec must agree with each other.\n\n### 3. Measure capacity per replica at the SLO\nPer-replica capacity is not a spec-sheet number.\n\n1. **Load-test with a realistic token mix and sweep concurrency.** Throughput rises with batch size while\n   per-request TPOT and TTFT degrade.\n2. **Find the latency knee.** Usable capacity is the highest concurrency (and its tokens/sec) where p95\n   TTFT and p95 TPOT still meet the latency budget, sometimes called goodput.\n3. **Cross-check against KV memory:** max sequences ≈ free memory after weights ÷ (KV bytes per token ×\n   mean context length).\n4. **The lower of the two is the replica's capacity.** Know which one it is, because the fixes differ: more\n   memory or a GQA/quantized cache vs. more replicas.\n\n### 4. Size, then add headroom\n```\nreplicas = max( peak in-flight sequences ÷ (per-replica sequence capacity × target utilization),\n                peak input tokens/sec ÷ per-replica prefill capacity at that load )   rounded up\n         + spares for N+1, rolling deploys and autoscaler lag\n```\n\n- **Usually one term dominates,** but a prefill-heavy mix (long RAG contexts, short answers) can be bound by\n  prefill while sequence slots sit free.\n- **Target utilization sits below 100%** *because* queueing delay grows sharply as utilization approaches\n  one.\n- **Add spares** for N+1 failure tolerance, rolling deploys and autoscaler lag (GPU nodes and model loading\n  can take minutes).\n- **Plan over the lead time.** The horizon must be at least as long as your procurement or quota lead time:\n  if reserved GPUs take a quarter to arrive and traffic grows 10% a month, today's plan must cover the peak\n  expected a quarter from now.\n- **For a managed API,** the same numbers become requests-per-minute and tokens-per-minute quotas per\n  region and per model. Check whether your provider counts the requested `max_tokens` against the token\n  quota at admission, because some do, which makes generous `max_tokens` settings consume quota you never\n  use.","example":"### A capacity plan in code\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef plan(active_users, msgs_per_user_min, calls_per_msg, in_tok, out_tok,\n         ttft_s, tpot_s, replica_max_seqs, target_util=0.7, spare=1):\n    rps = active_users * msgs_per_user_min / 60 * calls_per_msg     # λ\n    in_tps, out_tps = rps * in_tok, rps * out_tok\n    w = ttft_s + out_tok * tpot_s                                   # seconds per call\n    in_flight = rps * w                                             # Little's Law L = λW\n    duty = in_flight / active_users                                 # share of sessions busy\n    replicas = math.ceil(in_flight / (replica_max_seqs * target_util)) + spare\n    return dict(rps=rps, in_tps=in_tps, out_tps=out_tps, w=w,\n                in_flight=in_flight, duty=duty, replicas=replicas)\n\n# Support agent at peak: 1,200 open sessions, one message per user per minute,\n# 3 model calls per message, 3,000 input / 250 output tokens per call.\n# Load test (illustrative): one replica holds 64 sequences at p95 TPOT <= 30 ms.\nprint(plan(1200, 1, 3, 3000, 250, ttft_s=0.5, tpot_s=0.03, replica_max_seqs=64))\n# {'rps': 60.0, 'in_tps': 180000.0, 'out_tps': 15000.0, 'w': 8.0,\n#  'in_flight': 480.0, 'duty': 0.4, 'replicas': 12}\n```\n\n- **Reading the result:** 60 calls/sec, each lasting about 8 s (0.5 s TTFT + 250 × 30 ms), means 480\n  sequences in flight, so only 40% of the 1,200 sessions are busy at any instant.\n- **Replicas:** at 64 sequences per replica, 7.5 replicas would run at 100%; at a 70% target that is 11,\n  plus one spare gives 12.\n\n### Worked scenario: cross-checking the plan\nThree checks on the same plan; if any disagreed badly, one of the inputs would be wrong.\n\n| Check | Calculation | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Memory** | Llama-3-8B-shaped model (32 layers, 8 KV heads, head dim 128, fp16): 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 bytes = 128 KiB per token; 64 sequences × 3,250 tokens ≈ 27 GB | Fits beside ~16 GB of weights on an 80 GB GPU |\n| **Decode consistency** | 480 sequences over 12 replicas = 40 per replica; 40 ÷ 0.03 s ≈ 1,330 output tokens/sec per replica, about 16,000 across the fleet | Slightly above the 15,000 demanded, because part of each call's 8 s is TTFT rather than decode |\n| **Prefill** | 180,000 input tokens/sec across 12 replicas = 15,000 per replica | The load test must confirm it; if the 2,000-token system prompt is shared, prefix caching removes most of that work |\n\n### Worked scenario: planning over the lead time\nSame workload, but traffic grows 10% a month and reserved GPUs take a quarter to arrive.\n\n| Step | Calculation | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Growth over the lead time | 1.1 × 1.1 × 1.1 | ≈ 1.33× |\n| Peak in-flight sequences in a quarter | 480 × 1.33 | ≈ 639 |\n| Replicas for that peak | 639 ÷ (64 × 0.7) = 14.3, rounded up, plus 1 spare | 16 replicas |\n| What to order today | 16 − 12 | 4 more replicas, ordered now, not when alerts fire |","patterns":["**Load test with production-shaped prompts.** Replay token-length distributions sampled from production (not fixed-length synthetic prompts) and sweep concurrency, recording p95 TTFT and TPOT at each step. Monitor where the latency knee and the KV-memory bound fall, and re-run after every model or engine change.","**Reconcile predicted and observed concurrency.** Export the engine's running and waiting request counts (vLLM publishes both) and compare them with L = λW from your telemetry. Monitor the gap; a large one means W, the token mix or fan-out is mis-measured.","**Living capacity model.** Keep the plan as a script fed weekly by production histograms of arrivals, tokens, TTFT and TPOT, and alert when projected peak at lead time exceeds provisioned capacity. Monitor peak-to-average ratio and growth rate.","**Cap work per call.** Set `max_tokens` and a context cap per route so W and KV bytes per sequence stay bounded. Monitor input and output token p95 per route after each release.","**Headroom as a policy, not a guess.** Fix a target utilization (the example uses 70%) and N+1 per pool, and widen headroom where scale-up is slower than the traffic ramp. Monitor queue wait p95 and preemption counts.","**Order on lead time.** File quota increases and reservation requests for the peak expected at now plus lead time. Monitor provider throttle counts and remaining quota per region and model."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"Traffic: users, messages, calls, token p50 and p95\"] --> A[\"Arrivals: lambda requests per second\"]\n    A --> TK[\"Input and output tokens per second\"]\n    A --> L[\"In-flight sequences: L = lambda times W\"]\n    LT[\"Load test at the latency SLO\"] --> C[\"Replica capacity: latency knee or KV bound\"]\n    L --> S[\"Replicas from the binding unit\"]\n    TK --> S\n    C --> S\n    S --> H[\"Add N+1, deploy and autoscaler headroom\"]\n    H --> G[\"Project to peak at procurement lead time\"]\n    G -.->|\"managed API\"| Q[\"RPM and TPM quotas per region and model\"]","caption":"Read it top to bottom: traffic becomes three units, a load test turns hardware into a capacity number,\nand replicas fall out of peak demand divided by capacity, then grow for headroom and lead time."},"interview":[{"q":"A product manager asks how many GPUs you need for 5,000 users. What do you ask before answering?","a":"- **Traffic shape:** peak concurrent active users, messages per user per minute, and model calls per message\n  (agents fan out).\n- **Token distribution:** input and output tokens per call at p50 and p95, because one request can cost a\n  hundred times another.\n- **The SLO:** p95 TTFT and TPOT targets, since replica capacity is defined at the latency budget.\n- **Then reason:** λ, tokens/sec and L = λW, divided by a load-tested per-replica capacity, plus headroom."},{"q":"Why is requests per second not enough to plan LLM capacity?","a":"- **Uneven cost:** a 200-token classification and a 30,000-token summary are both one request.\n- **Long occupancy:** a 500-token answer at 30 ms per token holds a batch slot and KV cache for 15 seconds,\n  so in-flight sequences, not arrivals, saturate a replica.\n- **Different limits:** engines run out of KV memory or bandwidth, APIs throttle on RPM and TPM, and session\n  tiers care about open conversations."},{"q":"A benchmark says one replica does several thousand tokens per second, but production misses its TPOT SLO at a fraction of that. Why?","a":"- **Benchmark batch:** peak tokens/sec is usually measured at a huge batch where TPOT is far above an\n  interactive budget.\n- **Unrealistic prompts:** fixed-length synthetic prompts miss the long tail of contexts that fills KV\n  memory sooner.\n- **Fix:** sweep concurrency with production-shaped prompts and take the highest load where p95 TTFT and\n  TPOT still pass."},{"q":"Why plan for a target utilization below 100%, and how would you choose it?","a":"- **Queueing:** delay grows sharply as utilization approaches one, so a fleet planned at 100% misses its\n  TTFT budget at every burst.\n- **Choose by burstiness and scale-up time:** bursty traffic or a tight TTFT budget push the target down;\n  steady traffic and fast scale-up allow it higher.\n- **Spares are separate:** N+1, rolling deploys and autoscaler lag are added on top of the utilization\n  target."},{"q":"Traffic grows 10% a month and reserved GPUs take a quarter to arrive. How does that change your plan?","a":"- **Plan for the future peak:** over three months 1.1³ ≈ 1.33, so 480 in-flight sequences today become\n  about 639.\n- **Size for it now:** at 64 sequences per replica and a 70% target that is 15 replicas plus a spare, 16\n  instead of today's 12.\n- **Order today,** because anything ordered when alerts fire arrives a quarter too late."}],"tradeoffs":["**Higher target utilization vs queueing delay.** It cuts GPU cost, but queueing delay rises steeply near saturation; it flips toward low utilization when traffic is bursty or the TTFT budget is tight.","**p95 token counts vs means.** Planning to p95 over-provisions on quiet days but protects the tail; means are acceptable only when context length is tightly capped.","**Larger batches vs TPOT.** Larger per-replica batches raise tokens/sec per GPU but lengthen TPOT for every user; interactive chat favours smaller batches, offline batch jobs favour the largest batch memory allows.","**Static peak provisioning vs autoscaling.** Static is simple and fast to respond, but wastes money when peak-to-average is high; autoscaling saves cost only if scale-up time is shorter than the ramp of your traffic."],"pitfalls":["**Planning in RPS only.** Symptom: a plan that looks fine until one tenant sends long documents and KV-cache preemptions spike while RPS is unchanged.","**Using the engine's peak tokens/sec from a benchmark at huge batch.** Symptom: real interactive traffic misses its TPOT SLO at a fraction of that throughput.","**Forgetting agent fan-out,** counting user messages instead of model calls. Symptom: provider 429s on requests-per-minute at a user load the plan said was safe.","**Sizing to the daily average.** Symptom: queue depth and TTFT climb every weekday morning while the dashboard shows modest mean utilization.","**Load-testing with fixed-length synthetic prompts.** Symptom: a replica that met its SLO in the test misses it in production, where the long tail of contexts fills KV memory sooner.","**Treating open sessions as concurrent load.** Symptom: a GPU fleet several times larger than needed because idle conversations were counted as in-flight sequences."],"when":"- **Do it before** any launch, before signing a reserved-capacity or provisioned-throughput commitment, and\n  whenever the traffic mix changes: a new feature with longer context, an agent that adds tool-calling\n  steps, a large tenant onboarding.\n- **Keep it alive** as a spreadsheet or script fed by production telemetry: arrivals, input and output token\n  histograms, TTFT and TPOT percentiles, in-flight sequences, KV-cache utilization and provider throttle\n  counts.\n- **Managed APIs only?** The plan is shorter but still needed, because quotas are per region and per model\n  and raising them can take days.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"how many GPUs for N users\" questions; the strong move is to ask for the token\n  distribution and SLO first, then reason through Little's Law and a measured per-replica capacity.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments token counts, TTFT, TPOT and in-flight sequences per route, and runs the load test that finds the replica's knee at the latency SLO.","architect":"Owns the capacity model, headroom policy and the choice between reserved capacity, autoscaling and managed API quotas, including regional failover capacity.","fde":"Turns a customer's \"5,000 users\" into calls/sec and token mix from their real documents, and files quota increase requests in their cloud account before go-live, not after."},"check":{"q":"Traffic stays at 40 RPS, but after a release p95 TTFT doubles and the engine reports frequent\npreemptions. Nothing changed in hardware. What happened, and how do you confirm and fix it?","a":"RPS is unchanged, so look at the work per request. Likely the release raised input or output\ntokens per call: more retrieved chunks, longer history, or a higher `max_tokens`. Longer outputs raise W,\nso in-flight sequences L = λW rise even at constant λ; longer contexts raise KV bytes per sequence. Both\npush the replica past its memory bound, causing preemption and queueing. Confirm by comparing input and\noutput token histograms and in-flight sequence counts before and after. Fix by trimming context, capping\noutput, enabling prefix caching for shared prompts, or adding replicas sized from the new token mix."},"quiz":[{"q":"A service receives 20 calls/sec and each call takes 6 s end to end. How many sequences are in flight on average?","options":["20, because concurrency equals arrival rate","3.3, because 20 divided by 6","120, by Little's Law L = λW","It cannot be estimated without the arrival distribution"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Little's Law gives L = λ × W = 20 × 6 = 120 for any stable system in steady state, whatever the distribution. The distribution matters for queueing delay and tails, not for the mean in-flight count."},{"q":"Why should a capacity plan keep input and output tokens/sec separate instead of summing them?","options":["Providers bill them at the same rate, so splitting adds nothing","Input tokens drive compute-bound prefill while output tokens drive bandwidth-bound, sequential decode that holds GPU memory longer","Output tokens are always fewer, so they can be ignored","Input tokens are cached and therefore free after the first request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The two phases hit different resources. A summary workload is prefill-heavy, a long-generation workload is decode-heavy and occupies batch slots longer; summing them hides which constraint binds. Only shared prefixes, not all input, benefit from caching."},{"q":"A chat product has 10,000 open sessions, but each session has a model call running 5% of the time. What should size the GPU fleet?","options":["10,000 concurrent sequences, since every session might send at once","About 500 in-flight sequences at peak, plus headroom for bursts and failures","The number of sessions divided by the number of GPUs","Requests per second alone, since sessions are irrelevant to GPUs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GPUs are occupied by in-flight sequences, which here are sessions × duty cycle ≈ 500. Open sessions still size connections and session storage, and the plan adds headroom because peaks exceed averages."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","sources":["Kleinrock, 1975 — Queueing Systems, Volume 1: Theory","Little, 1961 — A Proof for the Queuing Formula L = λW","Harchol-Balter, 2013 — Performance Modeling and Design of Computer Systems"],"why":"- **The naive model is division.** A replica handles 4 requests per second, peak traffic is 40, so buy\n  10 replicas. That assumes evenly spaced arrivals and every request taking exactly the average time.\n  Neither is true.\n- **Arrivals are bursty and LLM service times vary wildly.** Users arrive in uncoordinated bursts, and\n  output length varies: one request generates 20 tokens, the next 2,000. When three long requests land\n  together everything behind them waits, and the server cannot \"bank\" idle time from a minute ago.\n- **Latency bends long before the cliff.** It is not flat until capacity; it curves upward well before\n  100% utilization. Teams that size to the mean see fine dashboards at 60% load and mysterious p99\n  explosions at 85%, with no code change.\n- **It quantifies the curve.** How much waiting a utilization buys, how variability makes it worse, and\n  how pooling, admission control and headroom make it better.\n- **It bridges two teams' numbers.** Throughput measured by serving and latency promised by product.\n  Without it, \"how much headroom do we need\" gets nothing better than a guess.","how":"### Vocabulary\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **λ (lambda)** | Arrival rate, requests per second |\n| **μ (mu)** | Service rate of one server, so mean service time is 1/μ |\n| **c** | Number of servers; for an LLM think *concurrency slots*, not machines (see below) |\n| **ρ = λ / (c·μ)** | Utilization, fraction of time servers are busy; must stay below 1 or the queue grows without bound |\n| **W, Wq, L** | Mean time in system, mean time waiting in queue, mean number of requests in system |\n\n### Little's law: L = λ·W\n- **What it says.** Average requests in flight equals arrival rate times average time each spends\n  inside. It holds for almost any stable system, regardless of arrival or service distribution.\n- **Why it holds.** Every request that enters eventually leaves, so long-run averages must balance.\n- **Forwards.** At 50 req/s and 6 s mean latency you have 300 requests in flight on average, so you need\n  at least 300 concurrent slots across the fleet before any headroom.\n- **Backwards.** If a gateway caps in-flight requests at L and latency is W, the best throughput you can\n  see is L/W.\n\n### Why waiting explodes: the 1/(1−ρ) term\n- **M/M/1** (one server, random Poisson arrivals, exponential service): W = 1/(μ − λ) and\n  Wq = ρ/(μ − λ).\n- **Rewritten:** Wq = (service time) × ρ/(1 − ρ).\n\n| ρ | Queue wait in service times |\n|---|---|\n| 0.5 | one |\n| 0.9 | nine |\n| 0.99 | ninety-nine |\n\n- **Headroom is not waste.** Because of the denominator, the last 10% of utilization costs more latency\n  than the first 80%.\n\n### Variability: Kingman's approximation\nReal systems are not exponential. For a single server with general distributions (G/G/1):\n\n```\nWq ≈ ( ρ / (1 − ρ) ) × ( (Ca² + Cs²) / 2 ) × mean_service_time\n```\n\n- **Ca and Cs** are coefficients of variation (std dev / mean) of inter-arrival times and service times.\n- **Three levers:** utilization; *arrival* variability (bursts, retry storms, batch jobs released on the\n  hour); *service* variability (output-length spread, tool calls, cache hits vs misses).\n- **LLM traffic is high-variance.** Cs is often around or above 1 (an exponential has exactly 1), and\n  agentic traffic with tool calls and long-tail outputs can push it higher. When Cs > 1, queues are worse\n  than a textbook M/M/1 at the same utilization.\n- **Measure Cs from your own traffic** rather than assuming it.\n- **Lower Cs to help everyone.** Capping `max_tokens` or splitting long jobs onto a separate queue reduces\n  latency for all other requests.\n\n### Many servers: M/M/c and pooling\nWith c servers sharing one queue, the Erlang C formula gives the probability an arriving request must\nwait, and the mean wait. Two results matter:\n1. **Larger pools tolerate higher utilization at the same wait**, because a burst is more likely to find\n   some server free. This is statistical multiplexing.\n2. **One shared queue beats several separate ones of the same total capacity**, because separate queues\n   can leave one pool idle while another is backed up. Every dedicated per-tenant or per-model pool pays\n   this tax.\n\n### Mapping it onto LLM serving\n- **A replica is many servers.** With continuous batching it serves many sequences at once, and each\n  sequence slows slightly as the batch grows.\n- **Workable approximation:** treat each replica as roughly `max_concurrent_sequences` servers whose\n  service time is the measured latency *at that concurrency*.\n- **The queue is wherever requests wait for a slot:** the engine's waiting list, the gateway, or a\n  provider's rate limiter.\n- **Measure under load.** Take service time and its variance from real traffic, not a single-request\n  benchmark, because per-request speed depends on load.\n\n### Tails, not means\nSLOs are written on p95 or p99, and the tail of queue wait grows faster than the mean. In M/M/c a\nrequest waits at all with probability P(wait) (Erlang C); if it waits, the extra delay is exponential\nwith rate (c·μ − λ):\n\n```\nP(Wq > t) = P(wait) × exp(−(c·μ − λ)·t)      →   t_p = ln(P(wait) / (1 − p)) / (c·μ − λ)\n```\n\n- **(c·μ − λ) is spare service capacity.** As it shrinks toward zero, every percentile stretches in\n  proportion.\n- **In the 16-slot example below** p99 queue wait is about 3 s at 75% utilization and about 22 s at 95%,\n  while the mean goes from 0.2 s to 3.9 s. Planning on the mean hides most of the damage an SLO will see.\n\n### Open versus closed load, and queue order\n- **Open system:** arrivals keep coming regardless of how slow you are. True of public traffic, and what\n  the formulas above assume.\n- **Closed system:** a fixed population waits for each response before sending the next (a load test with\n  N virtual users, or an agent calling the model, then a tool, then the model again).\n- **Closed systems self-throttle**, because slow responses delay the next request. So a closed-loop load\n  test reports gentler latency than real open traffic at the same throughput.\n- **FIFO hurts short requests.** One 2,000-token job can delay many 20-token ones.\n- **Priority classes or shortest-expected-job-first** cut mean wait for interactive traffic, at the price\n  of possible starvation for long jobs unless they are aged up or given reserved capacity.","example":"### Erlang C for a 16-slot fleet\nA fleet exposes 16 concurrency slots in total; a request holds a slot for 4 s on average. Erlang C shows\nhow waiting behaves as arrival rate rises, and what pooling buys. Exponential service is an idealization;\nLLM traffic with Cs > 1 will wait longer than this at every row.\n\n```python\nfrom math import factorial\n\ndef mmc(lam, mu, c):\n    \"\"\"M/M/c: returns (utilization, P(wait), mean queue wait Wq, mean time in system W).\"\"\"\n    a = lam / mu                      # offered load, in busy servers\n    rho = a / c\n    if rho >= 1:\n        return rho, 1.0, float(\"inf\"), float(\"inf\")\n    top = a**c / factorial(c) / (1 - rho)\n    p_wait = top / (sum(a**k / factorial(k) for k in range(c)) + top)   # Erlang C\n    wq = p_wait / (c * mu - lam)\n    return rho, p_wait, wq, wq + 1 / mu\n\nmu = 1 / 4.0                          # 4 s mean service per slot\nfor lam in (2.0, 3.0, 3.5, 3.8, 3.95):\n    rho, pw, wq, w = mmc(lam, mu, c=16)\n    print(f\"lam={lam}/s rho={rho:.2f} P(wait)={pw:.2f} Wq={wq:.2f}s L={lam*w:.1f}\")\n# lam=2.0/s rho=0.50 P(wait)=0.01 Wq=0.00s L=8.0\n# lam=3.0/s rho=0.75 P(wait)=0.20 Wq=0.20s L=12.6\n# lam=3.5/s rho=0.88 P(wait)=0.51 Wq=1.02s L=17.6\n# lam=3.8/s rho=0.95 P(wait)=0.78 Wq=3.90s L=30.0\n# lam=3.95/s rho=0.99 P(wait)=0.94 Wq=18.84s L=90.2\n\nprint(mmc(3.5, mu, 16)[2], mmc(1.75, mu, 8)[2])   # pooled 16 slots: ~1.0 s; split 2x8: ~2.5 s each\n```\n\n- **Non-linear.** Raising load from 3.0 to 3.8 req/s (27% more traffic) raises mean queue wait from\n  0.2 s to 3.9 s, about 20×.\n- **Pooling.** Splitting the same 16 slots into two dedicated pools of 8 at the same total traffic more\n  than doubles the wait.\n- **Little's law** holds throughout: L = λ·W in every row.\n\n### Sizing slots for a p99 queue-wait target\nThis uses the tail formula above with the numerically stable Erlang B recursion, so it works for\nhundreds of slots where `factorial` would overflow. Same M/M/c idealization.\n\n```python\nfrom math import log\n\ndef erlang_c(lam, mu, c):\n    a = lam / mu\n    if a >= c:\n        return 1.0\n    b = 1.0\n    for k in range(1, c + 1):          # Erlang B recursion, stable for large c\n        b = a * b / (k + a * b)\n    return b / (1 - (a / c) * (1 - b))\n\ndef wait_pct(lam, mu, c, p=0.99):\n    if lam / mu >= c:\n        return float(\"inf\")\n    pw = erlang_c(lam, mu, c)\n    return 0.0 if pw <= 1 - p else log(pw / (1 - p)) / (c * mu - lam)\n\ndef slots_for(lam, mu, target_s, p=0.99):\n    c = int(lam / mu) + 1              # smallest stable pool\n    while wait_pct(lam, mu, c, p) > target_s:\n        c += 1\n    return c\n\nprint(round(wait_pct(3.8, 1 / 4, 16), 1))   # 21.8 s p99 wait at rho 0.95\nprint(slots_for(3.8, 1 / 4, 1.0))           # 23 slots for p99 wait <= 1 s\nprint(slots_for(50, 1 / 6, 1.0))            # 319 slots: Little's law floor is 300\n```\n\n| Fleet | Little's law floor | Slots for p99 wait ≤ 1 s | Utilization at that size |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 3.8 req/s, 4 s service | 15.2 | 23 | ~0.66 |\n| 50 req/s, 6 s service | 300 | 319 | ~0.94 |\n\n- **Pooling in numbers.** The small fleet needs about 50% headroom over the floor; the large one needs\n  about 6%, because bursts average out across more slots.\n\n### Worked tail table for the 16-slot fleet\n| Arrival rate | ρ | P(wait) | Mean Wq | p99 Wq |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 3.0 req/s | 0.75 | 0.20 | 0.20 s | ~3.0 s |\n| 3.5 req/s | 0.88 | 0.51 | 1.02 s | ~7.9 s |\n| 3.8 req/s | 0.95 | 0.78 | 3.90 s | ~21.8 s |\n\nThe p99 grows faster than the mean, so an SLO on p99 binds long before the mean looks alarming.","patterns":["**Cap concurrency and reject fast.** When load can exceed capacity, cap in-flight sequences per replica (for example vLLM's `--max-num-seqs`) and bound the gateway queue; beyond it return 429 or 503 with a `Retry-After` header. Monitor queue depth, queue wait p99 and rejection rate.","**Set utilization targets from the SLO, not from cost.** Compute slots with Little's law, then add headroom from an M/M/c tail estimate like the one above. Autoscale on slot occupancy or queue wait rather than CPU, and alert when occupancy stays above the planned ρ.","**Separate queues for long and batch work.** Route requests with large expected output or offline batch jobs to their own pool or a lower-priority class, so interactive traffic sees lower Cs. Monitor each class's queue wait and the batch pool's idle time.","**Retry with exponential backoff, jitter and a budget.** Clients should never retry immediately on timeout; cap retries as a fraction of total traffic. Monitor the ratio of attempted requests to successful completions; a rising gap signals a retry storm.","**Deadline-aware queues.** Attach a deadline to each request and drop it from the queue once the client has given up, so the fleet does not do work nobody is waiting for. Monitor drops and client cancellations.","**Open-loop load tests.** Drive a fixed arrival rate (for example k6's `constant-arrival-rate` executor) and replay real output-length distributions, then sweep the rate up to find the knee."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Arrivals at rate lambda\"] --> G{\"Gateway: queue below cap?\"}\n    G -->|\"no\"| R[\"Reject fast: 429\"]\n    G -->|\"yes\"| Q[(\"Shared queue: Wq\")]\n    Q --> S{\"Free slot among c?\"}\n    S -->|\"no, wait\"| Q\n    S -->|\"yes\"| P[\"Hold slot for 1/mu\"]\n    P --> D[\"Response: W = Wq + 1/mu\"]\n    R -.->|\"client retry raises lambda\"| A\n    Q -.->|\"deadline passed\"| X[\"Drop stale request\"]","caption":"Requests arrive, pass admission, wait for a free slot, then hold it for one service time. Retries feed\nback into arrivals, which is why they make saturation worse."},"interview":[{"q":"What is Little's law, and why is it the first formula you reach for in capacity planning?","a":"- **Definition:** average in-flight requests L equals arrival rate λ times mean time in system W.\n- **Why first:** it holds for any stable system regardless of arrival or service distribution, so it\n  needs no modelling assumptions.\n- **Use:** at 50 req/s and 6 s latency you need at least 300 concurrent slots before headroom; run it\n  backwards to get the throughput ceiling L/W from a gateway cap."},{"q":"p99 latency spikes every weekday at 9 a.m., but there was no deploy. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Split the latency:** compare queue wait with service time. If queue wait jumps while service time is\n  flat, the fleet is saturating, not the model slowing.\n- **Check arrivals:** look for a scheduled batch job, a retry storm, or a burst that raises Ca at that\n  hour; check whether request rate climbs while completions stay flat.\n- **Fix in order:** spread or separately queue the batch arrivals, add backoff to retries, then add\n  capacity sized from the tail, not the mean."},{"q":"When would you not pool all tenants into one shared queue?","a":"- **When isolation is worth the capacity tax:** compliance, a contractual SLO, or a tenant whose bursts\n  would otherwise hurt everyone.\n- **When service-time spread is large:** a separate long-job queue lowers Cs for interactive traffic even\n  though it can sit idle.\n- **Otherwise pool**, because one shared queue never leaves a slot idle while requests wait elsewhere,\n  and larger pools run safely at higher utilization."},{"q":"Why is a load test with a fixed number of virtual users misleading?","a":"- **It is a closed system:** each user waits for a response before sending again, so slow responses lower\n  the arrival rate.\n- **Production is open:** users keep arriving regardless of latency, so queues grow without that brake.\n- **Result:** the test passes at a rate where real traffic hits a p99 cliff; use a constant-arrival-rate\n  generator instead."},{"q":"Why do LLM queues behave worse than the M/M/c numbers suggest, and what reduces it?","a":"- **Service variability:** output length ranges from 20 to 2,000 tokens, and tool calls add spread, so Cs\n  is often at or above 1; Kingman shows wait scales with (Ca² + Cs²)/2.\n- **Arrival variability:** bursts and retries raise Ca.\n- **Levers:** cap `max_tokens`, route long jobs to their own queue, smooth batch arrivals, and use\n  priority classes for interactive traffic."}],"tradeoffs":["**High utilization vs latency.** It lowers cost per request, but queueing delay grows as ρ/(1−ρ). The trade flips toward headroom as soon as a latency SLO, not throughput, is the binding requirement.","**Dedicated pools vs pooling.** Per-tenant or per-model pools give isolation and predictable noisy-neighbour behaviour but lose pooling efficiency. They win only when isolation or compliance is worth the extra capacity.","**Larger batches vs per-request time.** Throughput per GPU rises but each request's service time lengthens, so the right concurrency cap depends on whether the SLO is on time-to-first-token or total completion time.","**Separate long-job queue vs idle capacity.** It cuts service variability for interactive traffic, at the cost of a second pool that can sit idle. It pays off when output-length spread is large."],"pitfalls":["**Sizing capacity to mean traffic.** Symptom: p99 latency fine off-peak that spikes sharply during ordinary daily peaks with no deploy. Fix: size on peak arrival rate and the tail, with headroom.","**Benchmarking service time one request at a time.** Under real concurrency each request is slower, so the model underestimates ρ and the fleet saturates earlier than predicted. Fix: measure at target concurrency.","**Immediate client retries on timeout.** Retries raise λ exactly when the system is saturated, visible as request rate climbing while successful completions stay flat. Fix: backoff, jitter, retry budgets.","**Closed-loop load tests.** A fixed pool of virtual users waiting for each reply hides the knee, so the test passes at a rate where open production traffic shows a p99 cliff. Fix: open-loop arrival rates.","**Treating an unbounded queue as safety.** Requests wait past their useful deadline, so the system does work nobody is waiting for, seen as high latency together with high client-side cancellation. Fix: bound the queue and enforce deadlines."],"when":"- **Use when** someone proposes a utilization target, a replica count, a concurrency cap or a per-tenant\n  pool.\n- **Order of tools:** Little's law first, because it needs no distribution assumptions; then M/M/c or\n  Kingman to sanity-check headroom; then a load test replaying real request-length distributions.\n- **Measure** arrival rate, slot occupancy, queue depth and queue wait separately from service time,\n  since only the split tells you whether to add capacity or reduce variability.\n- **Interviews:** explaining why 90% utilization is dangerous, and deriving in-flight concurrency from\n  rate times latency, signals real production experience.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets engine concurrency caps and gateway queue limits, and instruments queue wait separately from model latency so saturation is visible before users report it.","architect":"Chooses utilization targets, pooling versus per-tenant isolation, and admission control, using the utilization-latency curve to justify headroom in the capacity budget.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their batch job at 9 a.m. slows the interactive assistant, and proposes spreading arrivals or a separate queue rather than buying more GPUs."},"check":{"q":"Your assistant runs at 60% average utilization with acceptable p99. Marketing plans a launch\nthat should add 40% traffic. Leadership asks whether latency will rise about 40%. What do you say?","a":"No: queueing delay is non-linear. 60% × 1.4 is 84% utilization, and the ρ/(1−ρ) factor goes from\n1.5 to about 5.25, so queue wait could rise three to four times even before accounting for bursts, and\nLLM service-time variance makes it worse (Kingman). Service time itself may also lengthen if batches\ngrow. I would compute required in-flight slots with Little's law at the new rate, add capacity to keep\nutilization near the current level, put a concurrency cap and fast rejection at the gateway, and\nvalidate with a load test replaying real output-length distributions before launch."},"quiz":[{"q":"A service handles 20 req/s with a mean latency of 3 s. How many requests are in flight on average?","options":["About 7, because 20 divided by 3 is roughly 7","60, by Little's law L = λ·W","It cannot be known without the service-time distribution","20, because that is the arrival rate per second"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Little's law holds for any stable system regardless of distributions: L = 20 × 3 = 60. The distribution matters for waiting time and tails, not for this average."},{"q":"Two teams each run a dedicated pool of 8 identical slots at 85% utilization. What happens if they merge into one pool of 16?","options":["Waiting time is unchanged because total capacity and load are unchanged","Waiting time doubles because each request now competes with twice as many others","Mean waiting time drops, because a burst in one team's traffic can use slots the other is not using","Utilization falls to about 42% because the slots are now shared"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pooling is statistical multiplexing: one shared queue never leaves a slot idle while requests wait elsewhere. Utilization stays 85%, but the probability and length of waits fall."},{"q":"Kingman's approximation separates utilization from variability. Which change shrinks the variability term (Ca² + Cs²)/2 rather than utilization?","options":["Raising the batch size so each replica processes more tokens per step","Capping output length or routing long jobs elsewhere to reduce service-time variability","Adding retries with no backoff so failed requests finish sooner","Moving to faster GPUs so the mean service time drops"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reducing service-time spread lowers Cs, so waiting falls even at the same utilization. Bigger batches and faster GPUs act on capacity and mean service time, i.e. on ρ, not on the variability term; immediate retries raise arrival burstiness (Ca) and make waiting worse."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","sources":["Dean & Barroso, 2013 — The Tail at Scale","Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"- **An AI request is a pipeline, not a call.** A RAG answer may pass an edge proxy, auth, input\n  moderation, a query rewrite with a small model, embedding, vector search, a reranker, prompt assembly,\n  a model queue, prefill, decode, an output guardrail and a logging hop.\n- **Each step was \"fast enough\" alone.** Added together, and at the tail rather than the median, they blow\n  through any product target.\n- **One end-to-end timeout gives nobody a number.** A 400 ms reranker gets added without anyone noticing\n  it consumed a fifth of the budget.\n- **One outer timeout fires too late.** The request has already burned GPU time, and every downstream step\n  kept working after the user gave up.\n- **Percentiles do not add.** Ten parallel calls each 1% likely to be slow make the request slow about 10%\n  of the time (1 − 0.99¹⁰ ≈ 9.6%); 100 fan-out calls make it about 63%. So budgets are set on\n  percentiles and verified end to end, never assembled from component medians.","how":"### 1. Start from the experience, not the system\n| Product | Metric the user feels |\n|---|---|\n| Streamed chat | Time-to-first-token (TTFT) plus a readable token rate |\n| Voice agent | Time to first audio |\n| JSON API | Total completion time |\n| Background agent | Possibly minutes per task |\n\n- **Fix the percentile (p95 or p99) and the load** at which it must hold. A target with no percentile or\n  no load is not a budget.\n- **Streamed output needs two numbers.** A TTFT budget for the wait before anything appears, and a\n  time-per-output-token (inter-token latency) budget for the stream, because a fast first token followed\n  by a stuttering stream still feels broken.\n- **They use different levers:** prompt length and queueing for TTFT; batch size and model size for\n  per-token time.\n\n### 2. Decompose the critical path\n- **Mark sequential versus parallel steps.** Only the critical path consumes budget; parallel branches\n  cost the slowest branch, not the sum.\n- **The biggest wins hide here.** Running moderation in parallel with retrieval instead of before it\n  removes its cost from the critical path entirely, at the price of occasionally doing retrieval work for\n  a request you then block.\n\n### 3. Allocate, with a reserve\n1. **Give each step a number** from measured p95 (or p99) under realistic load.\n2. **Hold back a reserve of 10–20%** (an illustrative rule of thumb) for network jitter, retries and\n   growth.\n3. **Split the model step** into queue wait, prefill (grows with prompt length) and decode (time per\n   output token × tokens).\n- **Prompt length is a latency decision as well as a cost one:** adding 20 retrieved chunks lengthens\n  prefill and TTFT.\n- **Queue wait is the most load-sensitive line**, because it grows as ρ/(1−ρ) with utilization (see\n  queueing theory). An allocation measured at 50% load is valid only up to the utilization you tested,\n  so state that utilization alongside the budget.\n\n### 4. Enforce with deadlines, not just timeouts\n- **A deadline is an absolute time** by which the response must be produced, created at the edge and\n  propagated with the request. gRPC deadlines do this natively; over HTTP, pass a header with the\n  remaining time.\n- **Each step's timeout** is the smaller of its own allocation and what is left minus what later steps\n  need.\n- **Why:** a step that started late should get less time, not the same time, and a downstream service\n  should stop working once the caller has given up.\n\n### 5. Degrade instead of failing\n- **Classify steps as required or optional.** When the remaining budget is short, skip optional ones\n  (query rewrite, reranking, a second retrieval hop) rather than squeezing them into a timeout they will\n  miss.\n- **Retry only when the remaining budget exceeds the attempt's p95.** Otherwise the retry has a large\n  chance of timing out and adds load exactly when the system is struggling.\n- **Hedged requests** (send a second copy after the p95 delay, take the first response) cut tails for\n  idempotent reads at a modest extra cost.\n\n### 6. Verify end to end\n- **Distributed tracing:** each span reports its duration against its allocation.\n- **Alert on the end-to-end percentile**, not on component averages.\n- **Review budgets like any interface contract**, because they drift as features land.","example":"### Worked allocation: RAG assistant, p95 TTFT ≤ 2.0 s at peak load\nIllustrative allocation from measured p95s:\n\n| Step | Allocation | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Edge and TLS | 100 ms | |\n| Gateway and auth | 30 ms | |\n| Input moderation | 120 ms | can move parallel to retrieval |\n| Query rewrite | 300 ms | optional |\n| Embedding | 50 ms | |\n| Vector search | 80 ms | |\n| Rerank | 200 ms | optional |\n| Prompt assembly | 20 ms | |\n| Model queue plus prefill | 800 ms | grows with load and prompt length |\n| Reserve | 300 ms | jitter, retries, growth |\n| **Sum** | **2.0 s** | |\n\n- **Parallelize moderation** with retrieval and 120 ms is freed.\n- **Decode is separate.** With 400 output tokens at 25 ms per token the full answer takes another 10 s:\n  acceptable streamed, not if an output guardrail must see the whole answer before display.\n\n### Deadline-aware stages in asyncio\n```python\nimport asyncio, time\n\nclass Deadline:\n    def __init__(self, seconds): self.end = time.monotonic() + seconds\n    def remaining(self): return self.end - time.monotonic()\n\nasync def stage(fn, budget, dl, keep=0.0, optional=False, fallback=None):\n    avail = dl.remaining() - keep           # leave room for the stages after this one\n    if optional and avail < budget:\n        return fallback                     # skip an optional step rather than squeeze it\n    try:\n        return await asyncio.wait_for(fn(), max(0.0, min(budget, avail)))\n    except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n        if optional:\n            return fallback\n        raise\n\nPREFILL_P95 = 0.8                           # measured queue + prefill at peak\n\nasync def first_token(query, remaining_s, rewrite, retrieve, rerank, generate):\n    dl = Deadline(remaining_s)              # from the propagated deadline header, not a fresh 2.0 s\n    q = await stage(lambda: rewrite(query), 0.30, dl, keep=0.15 + PREFILL_P95,\n                    optional=True, fallback=query)\n    docs = await stage(lambda: retrieve(q), 0.15, dl, keep=PREFILL_P95)\n    docs = await stage(lambda: rerank(q, docs), 0.20, dl, keep=PREFILL_P95,\n                       optional=True, fallback=docs[:5])\n    return await stage(lambda: generate(q, docs), dl.remaining(), dl)\n```\n\n- **`remaining_s` comes from the deadline set at the edge**, so upstream delay is already subtracted.\n- **Normal request:** about 1.87 s is left (2.0 s minus 130 ms of edge and gateway) and the rewrite runs.\n- **Slow gateway:** if the queue ate an extra 700 ms, only 1.17 s is left. The rewrite needs its 0.30 s\n  plus the 0.95 s kept for retrieval and generation, so it is skipped automatically, and generation's\n  0.8 s share stays protected.\n- **The bug this prevents:** creating `Deadline(2.0)` inside this function would silently ignore upstream\n  delay, which is exactly what deadline propagation exists to stop.\n\n### Percentiles do not add: a quick simulation\nPure Python; three sequential steps with skewed (lognormal) latencies.\n\n```python\nimport random\n\nrandom.seed(7)\ndef p(xs, q):\n    xs = sorted(xs)\n    return xs[int(q * (len(xs) - 1))]\n\nN = 100_000\nstt = [random.lognormvariate(-1.4, 0.4) for _ in range(N)]\nret = [random.lognormvariate(-1.7, 0.5) for _ in range(N)]\nllm = [random.lognormvariate(-0.8, 0.4) for _ in range(N)]\ntotal = [a + b + c for a, b, c in zip(stt, ret, llm)]\n\nprint(f\"sum of step p95s: {sum(p(x, 0.95) for x in (stt, ret, llm)):.2f} s\")   # ~1.76 s\nprint(f\"p95 of the sum:   {p(total, 0.95):.2f} s\")                               # ~1.43 s\nfor n in (1, 10, 50, 100):          # fan-out: chance at least one call hits its 1% tail\n    print(n, f\"{1 - 0.99 ** n:.0%}\")                                             # 1%, 10%, 39%, 63%\n```\n\n- **Sequential, independent steps:** the sum of p95s overstates the end-to-end p95, so it is a safe but\n  pessimistic budget.\n- **Fan-out:** waiting on the slowest branch makes tails far more common than any one branch's tail.\n- **Either way, measure the end-to-end percentile** rather than deriving it from components.","patterns":["**Propagate deadlines on every hop.** When a request crosses services, set the deadline once at the edge and pass it on: gRPC carries it natively; over HTTP use a header holding remaining milliseconds, and have each service subtract its elapsed time. Monitor requests arriving with negative or near-zero remaining budget.","**Cancel abandoned work.** When the client disconnects or the deadline passes, cancel downstream calls and stop decoding; in Starlette or FastAPI check `await request.is_disconnected()` while streaming. Monitor tokens generated for requests whose client already left.","**Run independent stages concurrently.** Start moderation and retrieval together with `asyncio.gather` and block on the moderation verdict before generation. Monitor how often retrieval work is wasted on blocked requests.","**Budget spans in tracing.** Tag each span with its allocation as an attribute, and alert on the end-to-end p95 and p99 rather than on component averages. Monitor the share of requests taking the degraded path.","**Hedge only idempotent reads, with a cap.** Send a second copy after the p95 delay for retrieval or cache reads, and limit hedges to a small fraction of traffic. Monitor extra load from hedging and turn it off under saturation.","**Re-budget on every pipeline change.** Treat the budget table as a reviewed artifact, so adding a reranker, a guardrail or more retrieved chunks requires updating the numbers. Monitor TTFT p95 after each \"quality\" change."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    E[\"Edge: deadline = now + 2.0 s\"] --> G[\"Gateway and auth\"]\n    G --> M[\"Input moderation\"]\n    G --> R[\"Embed and vector search\"]\n    M --> J[\"Join: slowest branch\"]\n    R --> J\n    J --> C{\"Time left covers rerank plus prefill?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes\"| K[\"Rerank (optional)\"]\n    C -->|\"no\"| F[\"Skip: top 5 docs\"]\n    K --> P[\"Model queue and prefill\"]\n    F --> P\n    P --> T[\"First token\"]\n    T -.->|\"span vs allocation\"| O[\"Tracing and p95 alert\"]","caption":"The edge sets one deadline; each stage checks the time left before running, and optional stages are\nskipped rather than squeezed."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a latency budget, and why can't you build one by adding component medians?","a":"- **Definition:** one user-facing target at a stated percentile and load, divided among every step on the\n  critical path plus a reserve.\n- **Medians hide the tail:** users feel p95 and p99, and tails compound across a chain or fan-out.\n- **So** allocate from measured p95s under load and verify the end-to-end percentile with tracing."},{"q":"Design a budget for a RAG chat feature that must show the first token within 2 s at p95.","a":"- **Choose the metric:** TTFT at p95 at peak load, plus a separate per-token budget for the stream.\n- **Shape the path:** run moderation parallel to retrieval, mark rewrite and rerank optional, and keep\n  about 10–20% as reserve.\n- **Protect the model step:** allocate queue plus prefill (for example 800 ms) and cap retrieved chunks so\n  prefill stays inside it.\n- **Enforce:** an edge deadline propagated to every hop, with optional stages skipped when time is short."},{"q":"TTFT p95 degrades every day at peak, but prompt sizes have not changed. Where do you look?","a":"- **Queue wait first:** it grows as ρ/(1−ρ), so a budget measured at 50% load breaks at peak utilization.\n- **Split the span:** compare queue wait with prefill time; if queue wait dominates, add capacity or\n  shed load rather than optimizing the prompt.\n- **Check retries and hedges:** they raise load exactly at peak."},{"q":"When would you not use hedged requests?","a":"- **Non-idempotent calls:** a duplicated tool call or write can cause side effects twice.\n- **Saturated systems:** hedging adds roughly the fraction of requests that exceed the hedge delay, which\n  deepens the queue that caused the tail.\n- **Expensive model calls:** a second GPU-heavy request doubles cost for that tail; prefer hedging cheap\n  reads such as retrieval or caches."},{"q":"The user closes the tab mid-answer. What should happen, and how do you verify it?","a":"- **Cancel everything downstream:** stop decoding and abort pending tool or retrieval calls, because the\n  deadline is gone and the work is wasted.\n- **Implement:** detect disconnect in the streaming handler, cancel the task, and propagate cancellation\n  to the model client.\n- **Verify:** track tokens or GPU time spent on requests whose client already left; it should fall\n  toward zero."}],"tradeoffs":["**Tight per-step allocations vs churn.** They make regressions visible early, but over-precise budgets become a burden when the pipeline changes weekly and owners spend time renegotiating numbers.","**Skipping optional steps vs quality.** It protects latency at the cost of answer quality: right for chat, wrong for workflows where a wrong answer costs more than a slow one.","**Hedged requests vs load.** They cut tail latency but add load, roughly the fraction of requests that exceed the hedge delay. They backfire on saturated systems or non-idempotent calls.","**Streaming vs guardrails.** Streaming hides decode time behind reading time, but forces output guardrails to work on partial text or hold tokens back, which reintroduces latency."],"pitfalls":["**Adding component p95s or medians and calling it the end-to-end p95.** Symptom: every service is green while users report slowness. Fix: measure the end-to-end percentile with tracing.","**Retrying inside a budget that cannot afford another attempt.** Symptom: retry counts rising together with timeouts, and more GPU work per successful request. Fix: retry only when time left exceeds the attempt's p95.","**Budgeting the model step from a single-request benchmark.** Under load, queue wait and prefill grow, seen as TTFT p95 degrading at peak hours while prompt sizes are unchanged. Fix: measure at peak load.","**Enforcing the deadline only at the edge.** The user sees a timeout while the model keeps decoding, visible as GPU or token spend on requests whose clients already left. Fix: propagate and cancel.","**Letting retrieval chunk counts creep up without re-budgeting prefill.** Symptom: TTFT rising after a \"quality\" change that never touched serving. Fix: re-budget prefill with each change."],"when":"- **Use when** a user-facing AI path has more than two hops, and before adding a new step such as a\n  reranker, guardrail or tool call.\n- **Measure** p95 and p99 per span under peak load, the end-to-end percentile, and the share of requests\n  that took a degraded path.\n- **Avoid formal budgets** for offline batch jobs, where throughput and cost per task matter more than\n  per-request latency.\n- **Interviews:** decompose the target into steps, point out that percentiles do not add, and propose\n  deadline propagation with optional-step degradation; that is the expected answer to \"make this feel\n  fast\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements deadline propagation, per-stage timeouts and degradation paths, and adds tracing spans so each step's latency is compared against its allocation.","architect":"Owns the end-to-end target and its split, decides what runs in parallel, which steps are optional, and when a slow component must be redesigned rather than tuned.","fde":"Accounts for the customer's network, proxies and on-prem retrieval systems, which often consume budget the vendor's demo never had to pay."},"check":{"q":"Your voice agent must start speaking within 1 s at p95. Measured p95s: speech-to-text 300 ms,\nretrieval 250 ms, LLM TTFT 600 ms, text-to-speech first audio 200 ms. What do you change?","a":"The sequential sum of p95s is 1.35 s, and the true p95 of the sum is usually somewhat lower than that\nsum for independent steps, but still clearly over budget. Options: stream speech-to-text and start retrieval on partial\ntranscripts or run it in parallel with endpointing; cut prompt length or use a smaller or\nprefix-cached model to reduce TTFT; stream LLM tokens into TTS sentence by sentence so first audio\ndepends on the first clause, not the full answer; and speak a short acknowledgement while retrieval\ncompletes. Then verify the end-to-end p95 with tracing under load, keeping a reserve for network\njitter."},"quiz":[{"q":"A request fans out to 50 parallel services, each with a 1% chance of exceeding its p99. Roughly how often does the request wait on at least one slow call?","options":["About 1%, since each service meets its p99","About 50%, one percent per service added linearly","About 40%, because 1 − 0.99⁵⁰ ≈ 0.39","Never, because parallel calls hide each other's latency"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The request waits for its slowest branch. The chance that all 50 are fast is 0.99⁵⁰ ≈ 0.61, so about 39% of requests hit at least one tail. Linear addition overestimates; ignoring fan-out underestimates."},{"q":"Why propagate an absolute deadline instead of giving every stage a fixed timeout?","options":["Fixed timeouts are not supported by most HTTP clients","A stage that starts late gets only the time actually left, and downstream services stop working once the caller has given up","Deadlines make each stage run faster by raising its priority","Deadlines remove the need to measure per-stage latency"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fixed timeouts let each stage spend its full allocation even after earlier stages overran, so the total exceeds the target and abandoned work keeps consuming capacity."},{"q":"The remaining budget is 300 ms and the model call's p95 is 700 ms. What should the pipeline do?","options":["Retry the previous slow stage once to recover time","Call the model anyway with a 300 ms timeout","Take the degraded path: skip or shortcut steps, fall back, or fail fast with a clear response","Extend the deadline because the model step is required"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A 300 ms timeout on a 700 ms p95 call fails a large share of the time while still consuming GPU time. Degrading or failing fast keeps the SLO honest and avoids wasted work."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/scalability","sources":["Amdahl, 1967 — Validity of the Single Processor Approach to Achieving Large Scale Computing Capabilities","Gunther, 2007 — Guerrilla Capacity Planning"],"why":"- **\"Twice the machines, twice the throughput\" rarely holds.** It holds for perfectly independent work\n  and almost nothing else.\n- **Every system has a serial part.** A single primary database, a global rate limit, a lock around a\n  shared index, a coordinator every request touches. Amdahl showed speedup is capped at one over the\n  serial fraction, no matter how many workers you add.\n- **Coordination makes it worse.** Distributed systems pay a cost that grows with the square of the worker\n  count, because workers exchange state (cache invalidation, consensus, connection fan-in). That produces\n  *retrograde* scaling: throughput peaks, then falls.\n- **AI adds its own non-linear limits.** Replicas come in large indivisible steps (one or several whole\n  GPUs for weights plus a useful KV cache), take minutes to start because weights must load, hit\n  tokens-per-minute quotas no client hardware raises, and keep KV and prefix caches local, so even\n  spreading destroys hit rate.\n- **Without a model, walls appear at launch**, after adding hardware that did not help.","how":"### Vertical vs horizontal\n| | Scaling up | Scaling out |\n|---|---|---|\n| **What** | Bigger GPU, more memory, tensor parallelism across GPUs in one node | More replicas behind a load balancer |\n| **Buys** | What one replica can do; often the only option for a model that does not fit smaller hardware | Total throughput and fault tolerance |\n| **Limit** | A ceiling, and usually a price per unit that rises with size | Only partitionable work, and only until a shared component saturates |\n| **LLM serving** | Tensor or pipeline parallelism of one model instance | Data-parallel replicas |\n\n### Amdahl's law\n```\nspeedup(N) = 1 / ((1 − p) + p/N)        p = parallel fraction, ceiling 1/(1 − p)\n```\n- **Example:** if 5% of each request's time is spent holding one shared lock or primary, no fleet size\n  gets past 20×, and 64 workers already give only about 15×.\n\n### The Universal Scalability Law (USL)\nGunther's USL adds coherency:\n\n```\nC(N) = N / (1 + α(N − 1) + β·N(N − 1))\n```\n\n- **α is contention** (queueing on a shared resource); **β is coherency** (pairwise coordination).\n- **With β = 0** this is exactly Amdahl rewritten (α = 1 − p), and capacity flattens toward 1/α.\n- **With β > 0** capacity peaks at N* = √((1 − α)/β) and declines after.\n- **Its value is diagnostic.** Fit α and β from a few load-test points; they tell you whether to remove a\n  serialization point (α) or reduce cross-node chatter (β), and roughly where adding nodes stops paying.\n\n### Where AI systems stop scaling\n- **Batch is the first axis, inside one replica.** With continuous batching, throughput rises steeply with\n  concurrent sequences, because each weight read is shared by the whole batch, while per-token latency\n  rises more slowly. Operate at the largest batch that meets the per-token target; adding replicas\n  before then buys unused GPUs.\n- **GPU memory per replica.** Concurrency per replica is bounded by KV-cache memory, so throughput per\n  replica plateaus; you scale by adding replicas, not by pushing more requests into one.\n- **Provider quotas.** Tokens-per-minute and requests-per-minute limits per key, per model and per region\n  form a hard ceiling; past it you need quota increases, more regions, or multiple providers.\n- **State and affinity.** Prefix caches and conversation state live on a replica. Pure round-robin loses\n  the cache hit rate; affinity routing (by session or prefix hash) keeps it but makes load uneven.\n- **Shared stores.** Vector indexes, metadata databases, and the logging pipeline that records every\n  prompt are classic α sources once the model tier scales.\n- **Elasticity lag.** GPU nodes and model loads take minutes, so autoscaling on CPU utilization reacts too\n  late. Scale on leading signals (queue depth, in-flight requests, KV-cache utilization) and keep warm\n  capacity for the time a new replica needs to become ready.\n\n### Designing for it\n1. **Keep the request tier stateless** and push state into stores designed to scale.\n2. **Partition by tenant or key** where work is independent.\n3. **Make the serial parts small and fast.**\n4. **Add backpressure** so overload produces fast rejection instead of collapse.\n5. **Test scaling explicitly** rather than assuming it.","example":"### Fitting the USL from load-test points\nA retrieval service is load-tested with N workers sharing one vector index and metadata database. The\nmeasurements are illustrative; the fitting method is standard USL linearization.\n\n```python\nfrom math import sqrt\n\nN = [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 24]                 # workers\nX = [40, 78, 148, 262, 410, 485]         # measured req/s\n\n# USL: X(N) = X1*N / (1 + a(N-1) + b*N(N-1)).  Linearise: X1*N/X(N) - 1 = a(N-1) + b*N(N-1)\nrows = [(n - 1, n * (n - 1), X[0] * n / x - 1) for n, x in zip(N, X)]\ns11 = sum(u * u for u, v, y in rows); s12 = sum(u * v for u, v, y in rows)\ns22 = sum(v * v for u, v, y in rows)\nt1 = sum(u * y for u, v, y in rows); t2 = sum(v * y for u, v, y in rows)\ndet = s11 * s22 - s12 * s12              # 2x2 least squares, no intercept\na = (t1 * s22 - t2 * s12) / det\nb = (s11 * t2 - s12 * t1) / det\n\nusl = lambda n: X[0] * n / (1 + a * (n - 1) + b * n * (n - 1))\nprint(f\"contention a={a:.3f} coherency b={b:.5f} peak near N={sqrt((1 - a) / b):.0f}\")\nfor n in (16, 32, 48, 64, 96):\n    print(f\"N={n:3d} predicted {usl(n):4.0f} req/s efficiency {usl(n) / (X[0] * n):.0%}\")\n# contention a=0.026 coherency b=0.00069 peak near N=38\n# N= 32 predicted 514 req/s ...  N= 64 predicted 473 req/s ...  N= 96 predicted 394 req/s\n```\n\n- **Peak:** throughput tops out near 38 workers at roughly 520 req/s, with per-worker efficiency already\n  down to about a third.\n- **Retrograde:** doubling from 32 to 64 workers would *lower* throughput.\n- **Fix:** attack β (for example, shard the index so workers stop coordinating on one store) or α (cache\n  hot metadata), not buy replicas.\n\n### Worked numbers: what a 5% serial fraction costs\nAmdahl with p = 0.95, computed from the formula above:\n\n| Workers N | Speedup | Efficiency (speedup / N) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 8 | ~5.9× | ~74% |\n| 16 | ~9.1× | ~57% |\n| 32 | ~12.5× | ~39% |\n| 64 | ~15.4× | ~24% |\n| unbounded | 20× | → 0 |\n\nGoing from 32 to 64 workers doubles cost for about 23% more throughput, so shrinking the serial part\nusually beats adding workers.\n\n### Autoscaling on queue depth instead of CPU\nA KEDA `ScaledObject` that scales a vLLM deployment on waiting requests scraped by Prometheus, keeping\nwarm replicas for the minutes a model takes to load. The threshold is illustrative.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: keda.sh/v1alpha1\nkind: ScaledObject\nmetadata:\n  name: llm-server\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    name: llm-server            # the Deployment running the model\n  minReplicaCount: 2            # warm capacity covers model-load time\n  maxReplicaCount: 12\n  cooldownPeriod: 600           # scale down slowly; scale-up is the expensive direction\n  triggers:\n    - type: prometheus\n      metadata:\n        serverAddress: http://prometheus.monitoring:9090\n        query: sum(vllm:num_requests_waiting{deployment=\"llm-server\"})\n        threshold: \"8\"          # target waiting requests per replica\n```","patterns":["**Scale on leading signals with warm floor capacity.** For GPU replicas, autoscale on queue depth or in-flight requests (as in the KEDA example) and set a minimum replica count that covers cold-start minutes. Monitor time from scale-out decision to ready replica, and timeouts during spikes.","**Affinity routing for cache-heavy traffic.** Hash on session id or prompt-prefix hash so repeats land on the same replica, for example nginx's `hash $http_x_session_id consistent;` upstream directive. Monitor prefix-cache hit rate and per-replica load skew; cap or spill over hot keys.","**Multi-size load tests with a USL fit.** Before a launch, test at four or five fleet sizes, fit α and β, and record the predicted peak. Monitor per-replica throughput as you scale; a falling line means a shared bottleneck.","**Track provider quota headroom.** Read rate-limit response headers (OpenAI returns `x-ratelimit-remaining-tokens`; Anthropic returns `anthropic-ratelimit-tokens-remaining`) and spread load across keys, regions or providers before the ceiling. Monitor 429 rate per key and region.","**Pool connections to shared stores.** Put a pooler such as PgBouncer in front of Postgres so replica count times pool size cannot exceed the database's connection limit. Monitor pool wait time and active connections as replicas scale.","**Backpressure at the gateway.** Cap in-flight requests per replica and reject fast with 429 when the queue is full, so overload degrades instead of collapsing. Monitor rejection rate against capacity."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Added replicas, throughput flat\"] --> B{\"Per-replica GPU busy falling?\"}\n    B -->|\"no\"| C[\"Replica-bound: raise batch or add GPUs\"]\n    B -->|\"yes\"| D[\"Something shared is the limit\"]\n    D --> E{\"429s from provider?\"}\n    E -->|\"yes\"| F[\"Quota: raise, add regions or providers\"]\n    E -->|\"no\"| G{\"Prefix-cache hit rate fell?\"}\n    G -->|\"yes\"| H[\"Add session or prefix affinity\"]\n    G -->|\"no\"| I[\"Trace critical path: store, gateway, pools\"]\n    I --> J[\"Fit USL: alpha or beta?\"]","caption":"A diagnostic path for \"we added replicas and throughput barely moved\": falling per-replica utilization\npoints away from the GPU tier toward something shared."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between Amdahl's law and the Universal Scalability Law?","a":"- **Amdahl** models only a serial fraction: speedup plateaus at 1/(1 − p), so 5% serial work caps you at\n  20×.\n- **USL** adds a coherency term β for pairwise coordination, which makes throughput peak at\n  N* = √((1 − α)/β) and then decline.\n- **Why it matters:** a plateau says remove a serial point; a decline says reduce cross-node chatter, and\n  stop adding nodes."},{"q":"How would you autoscale a self-hosted LLM deployment, and on what signal?","a":"- **Not CPU or raw GPU utilization:** they lag the real constraint, and a new replica takes minutes to\n  load weights.\n- **Leading signals:** queue depth, in-flight requests or KV-cache utilization per replica.\n- **Plus warm floor capacity** sized to cover model-load time, and a slow scale-down so spikes do not\n  thrash."},{"q":"When would you choose scale-up over scale-out for a model?","a":"- **When the model does not fit smaller hardware:** tensor or pipeline parallelism inside one node is the\n  only way to serve it at all.\n- **When per-request latency matters:** a bigger single instance can lower per-token time, whereas more\n  replicas only add throughput.\n- **Cost:** price per unit usually rises with size and there is a ceiling, so scale out once one replica\n  meets the latency target."},{"q":"A load test fits α = 0.026 and β = 0.00069. What do you tell the team planning to go from 32 to 64 workers?","a":"- **Peak is near 38 workers**, from √((1 − α)/β), at roughly 520 req/s.\n- **64 workers predict about 473 req/s**, lower than 32, so the plan buys hardware to lose throughput.\n- **Instead** shard the shared index to cut β or cache hot metadata to cut α, then re-test."},{"q":"Why can adding replicas raise cost per request in a prefix-cached system?","a":"- **Caches are local to a replica:** round-robin spreads requests sharing a prefix, so each cache sees\n  fewer repeats.\n- **Lower hit rate means more prefill**, which raises TTFT and GPU work per request.\n- **Fix:** route by session or prefix hash and watch per-replica skew so affinity does not create hot\n  spots."}],"tradeoffs":["**Scale-out vs coordination.** It buys fault tolerance and near-linear throughput for independent work, but adds coordination; it flips to a loss once coherency cost dominates, visible as a USL peak.","**Affinity routing vs hot replicas.** It preserves prefix and session caches, cutting cost and TTFT, but creates hot replicas; it stops paying when traffic is dominated by a few large tenants or sessions.","**Warm GPU capacity vs idle spend.** It hides cold-start minutes during spikes, but you pay for idle hardware; worth it when spikes are frequent and the latency SLO is strict.","**Sharding a shared store vs complexity.** It removes a contention point, but cross-shard queries and rebalancing add complexity; premature while the store is far from saturation."],"pitfalls":["**Load-testing one replica and multiplying by N.** Symptom: a launch where adding replicas leaves throughput flat because a shared store or quota was the real limit. Fix: test at several fleet sizes.","**Autoscaling GPU replicas on CPU or GPU utilization.** Symptom: replicas arriving after the spike has already caused timeouts, because model loading takes minutes. Fix: queue depth plus warm capacity.","**Round-robin across replicas with prefix caching.** Symptom: cache hit rate falling as replicas are added, and TTFT and cost per request rising with scale. Fix: session or prefix affinity.","**Ignoring provider quotas in the scaling plan.** Symptom: 429 errors at a traffic level far below what the application tier could handle. Fix: quota headroom in the capacity plan."],"when":"- **Use before** any launch, migration or \"just add replicas\" proposal.\n- **Measure:** run load tests at several sizes (at least four or five points), fit a USL curve, and find\n  which shared resource sets α and β. Track per-replica throughput, cache hit rate, queue depth and\n  quota headroom as you scale.\n- **Avoid** elaborate scale-out architecture for workloads that fit comfortably on one replica with\n  headroom; simplicity is cheaper.\n- **Interviews:** naming the specific shared bottleneck and the signal you would autoscale on beats\n  generic \"make it stateless and horizontal\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes stateless services, adds affinity routing and backpressure, and runs multi-size load tests so scaling claims come from measurements rather than assumptions.","architect":"Decides partitioning keys, scale-up versus scale-out for models, multi-region and multi-provider strategy, and where shared state lives so it does not cap growth.","fde":"Finds the customer-side ceilings, such as an on-prem search cluster or a single API key's quota, that will limit rollout long before the vendor's platform does."},"check":{"q":"Going from 8 to 16 inference replicas raised throughput by only 15%, and GPU utilization per\nreplica fell. What do you investigate, and in what order?","a":"Falling per-replica utilization means replicas are waiting on something shared, so the GPU tier is\nnot the bottleneck. I would trace one request's critical path under load and look for the component\nwhose latency rose: a vector store or metadata DB (contention), a gateway or rate limiter, a provider\nquota producing 429s, or connection pools sized for 8 replicas. I would also check load balancing:\nround-robin can collapse prefix-cache hit rates, increasing prefill work. Then fit α and β from load\ntests at several sizes to confirm whether the limit is a serial point or coordination cost before\nredesigning."},"quiz":[{"q":"In the Universal Scalability Law, what does a positive coherency coefficient β imply?","options":["Throughput grows linearly but with a lower slope","Throughput peaks at some N and then declines as more workers are added","Throughput plateaus at 1/β and stays flat","The system is limited by its serial fraction only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"β models pairwise coordination cost growing roughly with N², so beyond N* = √((1 − α)/β) each added worker costs more than it contributes. Contention alone (α) gives a plateau, not a decline."},{"q":"Why is CPU utilization a poor autoscaling signal for GPU inference replicas?","options":["CPU metrics are not exposed inside containers","GPU replicas never use the CPU","It does not track the real constraint, and new replicas take minutes to load, so scaling must react to leading signals like queue depth","Autoscalers can only read GPU metrics"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Inference load shows up in queue depth, in-flight requests and KV-cache utilization. Because model loading is slow, a lagging signal adds capacity after users have already timed out."},{"q":"A team adds replicas and sees prefix-cache hit rate fall. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Larger fleets use smaller cache sizes per replica by default","Requests sharing a prefix are spread across replicas, so each replica's local cache sees fewer repeats","Prefix caching only works with a single replica","More replicas increase prompt lengths"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prefix caches are local to a replica. Without prefix- or session-aware routing, traffic spreads thinly, so each cache sees fewer repeats; affinity routing restores the hit rate."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/cost-optimization","sources":["Chen et al., 2023 — FrugalGPT: How to Use Large Language Models While Reducing Cost and Improving Performance"],"why":"- **Every request has a marginal cost.** Often cents, scaling directly with usage and with design choices\n  such as context size and model tier, unlike most software.\n- **Pilots hide it.** A feature cheap in a pilot can become the largest line on the cloud bill at\n  production volume, and gross margin on an AI product can be negative if no one looks.\n- **\"Switch to the cheapest model\" backfires.** It saves per call but often lowers task success, pushing\n  work into retries, escalations and human review, so cost per *successful* task can rise.\n- **\"Tune kernels\" or \"negotiate a discount\" pulls the smallest lever first.** A 20% price cut is dwarfed\n  by not sending 60% of the tokens in the first place.\n- **Blind optimization misses.** Without attribution by feature and request type, teams optimize the\n  endpoint they happen to be looking at, not the one that dominates spend. The discipline applies effort\n  where the money is, in order of yield per unit of risk, with quality held constant by measurement.","how":"### 0. Measure and attribute first\n- **Tag every model call** with feature, tenant, model and request type.\n- **Record tokens by type:** fresh input, cached input, output, reasoning.\n- **Rank spend by feature and inspect the distribution:** often a few features and a long tail of large\n  requests dominate.\n- **Pair cost with an outcome metric** (task success, acceptance rate) so every later change is judged on\n  cost per successful task.\n\n### 1. Do not call the model\nThe cheapest token is the one never sent.\n- **Exact-match response caches** for repeated queries, keyed on normalized input plus the version of\n  every input that affects the answer.\n- **Deterministic code paths** for cases rules handle reliably.\n- **Skip steps** that do not change the outcome.\n- **Semantic caches** (reuse an answer for a *similar* question) save more but can return a wrong answer\n  for a subtly different question, so they need a strict threshold and evaluation.\n\n### 2. Send fewer tokens\nInput usually dominates RAG and agent cost.\n- **Retrieve fewer, better chunks:** reranking lets you cut top-k.\n- **Trim** system prompts and tool schemas, compact long histories, and stop pasting entire tool outputs\n  into context.\n- **Prompt caching for stable prefixes:** fixed instructions and schemas first, variable content last,\n  *because* caching only matches identical prefixes.\n- **Cap output** with concise formats and `max_tokens`, since output tokens are usually priced higher\n  than input.\n- **Reasoning (\"thinking\") tokens** are billed as output on most providers even when not shown, so a\n  reasoning budget or effort setting is a cost control.\n- **Agent loops** re-send the growing context every step, so cost per task grows faster than linearly\n  with step count; cutting one redundant tool round-trip can save more than trimming the prompt.\n\n### 3. Use a cheaper model where it is good enough\n- **Route easy requests to a smaller model** and escalate hard or low-confidence ones (routing or\n  cascading).\n- **Distill or fine-tune** a small model for a narrow, high-volume task.\n- **Why it pays:** price per token differs by one to two orders of magnitude across tiers.\n- **The risk:** routing errors cost quality, so measure success per route.\n\n### 4. Buy capacity more cheaply\n| Lever | Fits |\n|---|---|\n| Asynchronous batch APIs (many providers discount them) | Work that can wait |\n| Committed-use or reserved capacity | Steady baseline load |\n| Spot or preemptible instances | Interruptible batch jobs |\n| Self-hosting | Only where volume keeps GPUs highly utilized |\n\n### 5. Tune serving\nFor self-hosted models, once the fleet is large:\n- **Raise utilization** with continuous batching and right-sized concurrency.\n- **Quantize** weights or the KV cache where evals allow.\n- **Enable prefix caching**, right-size GPUs, and scale down idle replicas.\n\n### Guardrails on the process\n- **Levers compound multiplicatively.** Estimate each on the base left after the previous ones, not on\n  the original bill: a 50% context cut and a 50% routing saving give 75%, not 100%.\n- **Levers interact.** Routing traffic away from the large model shrinks the value of the large model's\n  prompt cache.\n- **Every change ships behind an eval** and, ideally, an A/B or shadow comparison. Acceptance criterion:\n  cost per successful task at equal or better quality, within the latency budget.","example":"### Worked scenario: document-QA lever stack\nAll figures illustrative: 1M requests per month, 8,000 input tokens per request (1,500 fixed instructions\nplus 6,500 retrieved context), 500 output tokens, on a large model at $3 per million input and $15 per\nmillion output.\n\nCost per request is $0.024 + $0.0075 = $0.0315, or $31,500 a month. Each lever is confirmed by the eval\nsuite.\n\n| Step | Change | Per request | Per month |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Baseline | — | $0.0315 | $31,500 |\n| 1. Exact-match cache | normalized question plus document version; measured 10% hit rate, billable requests fall to 900k | $0.0315 | $28,350 |\n| 2. Prompt caching | 1,500-token fixed prefix read at $0.30 per million (a 90% discount); saves 1,500 × $2.70/M ≈ $0.004 | $0.02745 | $24,705 |\n| 3. Rerank and cut context | 6,500 to 3,000 tokens, eval shows no drop; saves $0.0105 | $0.01695 | $15,255 |\n| 4. Route 60% to a small model | $0.25/$1.25 per million, about $0.00175 per request, escalating 10% of those | $0.0088 blended | about $7,960 |\n\n- **The prompt-caching discount and any cache-write premium vary by provider** and are ignored here.\n- **Total reduction is roughly 75%**, and none of it came from GPU tuning or price negotiation.\n- **Step 4 is the riskiest.** If escalation is triggered by low confidence that misses wrong answers,\n  success drops and the saving is illusory, so success rate per route is the number to watch.\n\n### The same stack as a calculator\nPlug in your own traffic, token counts and prices; each line applies a lever to what the previous left.\n\n```python\nM = 1_000_000\n\ndef per_req(fresh_in, cached_in, out, p_in, p_cached, p_out):\n    return (fresh_in * p_in + cached_in * p_cached + out * p_out) / M\n\nLARGE = dict(p_in=3.00, p_cached=0.30, p_out=15.00)    # $ per million tokens, illustrative\nSMALL = dict(p_in=0.25, p_cached=0.25, p_out=1.25)\n\nreqs = 1_000_000\nbase = per_req(8_000, 0, 500, **LARGE)\nbillable = reqs * (1 - 0.10)                            # 1. exact-match cache, 10% hits\ncached = per_req(6_500, 1_500, 500, **LARGE)            # 2. prompt-cache the 1,500 fixed tokens\ntrimmed = per_req(3_000, 1_500, 500, **LARGE)           # 3. rerank: context 6,500 -> 3,000\nsmall = per_req(4_500, 0, 500, **SMALL)                 # 4. small model on trimmed prompt\nblended = 0.4 * trimmed + 0.6 * (small + 0.10 * trimmed)\n\nfor name, monthly in [(\"baseline\", base * reqs), (\"cache\", base * billable),\n                      (\"prefix\", cached * billable), (\"trim\", trimmed * billable),\n                      (\"route\", blended * billable)]:\n    print(f\"{name:9s} ${monthly:>8,.0f}/month\")\nprint(f\"reduction {1 - blended * billable / (base * reqs):.0%}\")   # 75%\n```\n\n### A response-cache key that cannot leak across tenants\nKeying on question text alone can serve one tenant an answer built from another's documents.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json\n\ndef cache_key(question, tenant_id, permission_scope, source_versions, prompt_version, model):\n    normalized = \" \".join(question.lower().split())\n    payload = {\n        \"q\": normalized,\n        \"tenant\": tenant_id,\n        \"scope\": sorted(permission_scope),          # what this user may see\n        \"sources\": source_versions,                 # e.g. {\"doc-42\": \"v7\"}\n        \"prompt\": prompt_version,\n        \"model\": model,\n    }\n    return hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(payload, sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()\n```","patterns":["**Attribution at the gateway.** Route every model call through one gateway or wrapper that records feature, tenant, model and token counts by type from the provider's usage fields. Monitor spend per feature per day and alert on week-over-week jumps.","**Cache-friendly prompt layout.** Put instructions and tool schemas first and variable content last; with Anthropic mark the stable prefix with `cache_control: {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}`, while OpenAI caches long prefixes automatically. Monitor the cached-token share from usage fields (for example `cache_read_input_tokens` or `cached_tokens`) after every prompt change.","**Batch lanes for deferrable work.** Send evals, backfills and nightly summaries through the provider's batch endpoint (OpenAI's Batch API, Anthropic's Message Batches API) instead of the interactive path. Monitor batch completion time against the job's deadline.","**Cascades with a shadow period.** Before routing a share of traffic to a smaller model, run it in shadow on live traffic and compare success per route against the large model. Monitor escalation rate and success per route after launch.","**Output and reasoning caps per route.** Set `max_tokens` per feature and a reasoning budget or effort level where the provider supports one (for example Anthropic's `budget_tokens` for extended thinking, OpenAI's `reasoning_effort`). Monitor output and reasoning tokens per request and the truncation rate."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Attribute spend by feature and token type\"] --> B[\"Avoid calls: exact cache, rules\"]\n    B --> C[\"Fewer tokens: rerank, prefix cache, caps\"]\n    C --> D[\"Cheaper model for easy share\"]\n    D --> E[\"Cheaper capacity: batch, reserved, spot\"]\n    E --> F[\"Tune serving: batching, quantization\"]\n    B --> G{\"Eval gate: success held?\"}\n    C --> G\n    D --> G\n    E --> G\n    F --> G\n    G -->|\"yes\"| H[\"Ship, track cost per success\"]\n    G -->|\"no\"| I[\"Revert or tighten lever\"]","caption":"The levers in order of yield per unit of risk; every arrow into production passes the same eval gate."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is \"switch to the cheapest model\" often not a cost saving?","a":"- **Per-call cost falls, success may fall too:** failures turn into retries, escalations and human\n  review.\n- **The right denominator is successful tasks:** if success drops enough, cost per successful task rises\n  even though each call is cheaper.\n- **So** route only the easy share, escalate the rest, and measure success per route."},{"q":"Your agent's cost per task doubled after adding two tool steps. Why, and what do you do?","a":"- **Context grows every step:** each step re-sends the accumulated history and tool outputs, so cost\n  grows faster than linearly with step count.\n- **Diagnose from traces:** tokens per step, which tool outputs are pasted in full, and whether any step\n  is redundant.\n- **Fix:** summarize or truncate tool outputs, cache the stable prefix, and remove round-trips that do not\n  change the outcome."},{"q":"Two levers each promise a 50% saving. What is the combined saving, and why?","a":"- **75%, not 100%:** the second lever applies to the half left after the first.\n- **They may interact further:** routing traffic away from the large model shrinks that model's\n  prompt-cache value.\n- **So** estimate each lever on the remaining base, from traces, before building."},{"q":"When would you not use a semantic cache?","a":"- **When small wording changes alter meaning:** \"cancel my order\" versus \"cancel my order's insurance\".\n- **When answers depend on user or tenant data:** a shared cache risks serving one tenant's answer to\n  another unless the key includes tenant and permission scope.\n- **Prefer exact-match caching** with versioned keys, and add semantic caching only for stable FAQ-like\n  traffic with a strict threshold and an eval."},{"q":"A prompt change shipped and spend rose 30% with identical traffic. Where do you look first?","a":"- **Cached-token share:** variable content moved to the front of the prompt breaks prefix caching, so\n  instructions are billed at full input price again.\n- **Output and reasoning tokens:** a new instruction may have lengthened answers or raised reasoning\n  effort.\n- **Then** compare tokens by type per request before and after the deploy."}],"tradeoffs":["**Routing to smaller models vs routing errors.** Cost falls sharply but a routing error rate appears; the trade flips when failures are expensive (refunds, compliance) and cost per successful task rises.","**Context trimming vs recall.** It saves input tokens and latency, but can drop the one chunk the answer needed; it stops paying when retrieval recall is already marginal.","**Batch APIs and spot vs delay.** Cheaper, but add delay or interruption; they suit offline work and are wrong for anything on an interactive path.","**Semantic caching vs correctness.** It avoids calls for similar queries but risks serving a wrong cached answer; it fits stable FAQ-like traffic and fails where small wording changes alter meaning."],"pitfalls":["**Optimizing without attribution.** Symptom: a successful saving on one endpoint while total spend keeps rising because another feature dominated all along. Fix: rank spend by feature first.","**Measuring cost per call instead of per successful task.** Symptom: falling per-call cost alongside rising retries, escalations or human-review volume. Fix: divide by successes.","**Breaking prompt caching** by putting timestamps, user names or request IDs at the start of the prompt. Symptom: cached-token share drops to near zero after a prompt change. Fix: variable content last.","**Keying a response or semantic cache only on the question text.** One tenant or user is served an answer built from another's documents, found as a data-exposure incident rather than a cost metric. Fix: include tenant, permission scope and source versions in the key.","**Shipping a cheaper configuration without an eval gate.** Quality complaints or lower acceptance rates appear weeks later and are hard to trace to the change. Fix: eval plus shadow or A/B before rollout."],"when":"- **Use when** a feature has real traffic and material spend, or earlier if the cost model shows negative\n  unit economics at target volume.\n- **Order:** begin with attribution, then work down the lever list, estimating savings from traces before\n  building anything.\n- **Measure** cost per successful task, cached-token share, tokens per request by type, routing mix and\n  quality per route.\n- **Avoid** premature optimization in prototypes, where learning what users need is worth more than a\n  cheaper token.\n- **Interviews:** ordering the levers and insisting on an eval gate separates practitioners from people\n  who only say \"use a smaller model\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements caches, prompt-prefix ordering, context trimming and routing, and wires per-call cost and success metrics so each change can be evaluated.","architect":"Sets the cost target per unit of work, decides which levers the platform provides centrally (gateway caching, routing, batch lanes), and owns the quality bar changes must meet.","fde":"Finds customer-specific waste, such as oversized documents or redundant calls in their integration, and proves savings against the customer's own acceptance metrics."},"check":{"q":"Finance asks you to cut a RAG assistant's model spend by half within a month without hurting\nquality. How do you approach it?","a":"First attribute spend: tokens by type per feature and request class, and the cost distribution,\nso I know whether input context, output length, a few heavy tenants or an agent loop dominates. Then\nestimate each lever from traces: exact caching for repeats, prompt caching with a stable prefix,\nreranking to cut retrieved context, output caps, and routing easy queries to a smaller model. I would\nship them in order of savings per unit of risk, each behind the eval suite and a shadow or A/B check,\ntracking cost per successful answer, not per call. Price negotiation and GPU tuning come last."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which metric best guards against a cost optimization that secretly degrades the product?","options":["Cost per API call","Total monthly token count","Cost per successful task, measured alongside a quality eval","Average output length per response"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Per-call cost falls when a cheaper model fails more often, but retries, escalations and human review then push cost per success up. Tying cost to successful outcomes exposes that trade."},{"q":"A prompt change moved the current date and user name to the top of the system prompt. What cost effect is most likely?","options":["Output tokens increase because the model writes longer answers","Prompt-cache hits collapse, because the prefix now differs across users and days","Nothing, since the total prompt length is unchanged","Batch API discounts no longer apply"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prompt caching matches identical prefixes. Putting variable content first makes the prefix differ per user and per day, so the instructions after it are billed at the full input price again; total length being unchanged is irrelevant."},{"q":"In what order should levers generally be tried for an API-based RAG feature?","options":["Negotiate price, tune GPU kernels, then trim prompts","Switch everything to the smallest model, then fix quality later","Attribute spend, avoid calls, cut tokens, route to cheaper models, then buy capacity more cheaply","Add semantic caching first because it removes the most calls"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Attribution shows where the money is; avoiding calls and tokens is low-risk and often large; routing has higher quality risk; cheaper capacity (batch lanes, commitments) only applies to deferrable or steady load, so it comes after removing work. Semantic caching is not first because it carries a wrong-answer risk and its hit rate is unknown until measured."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","sources":["Official docs: provider pricing pages (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google)"],"why":"- **Spend is metered per token.** Cost is set by the shape of each request, not by servers running.\n- **Similar-looking features differ a hundredfold.** A one-shot classification with a 300-token prompt\n  versus a 20-turn agent that pastes tool results into its context each step.\n- **\"Requests per day × a typical price\" misses three things:**\n  1. **Input and output are priced differently,** and reasoning models emit billed tokens the user never\n     sees.\n  2. **Loops re-send everything each turn,** so a long session is not N times a short one but closer\n     to N².\n  3. **The distribution is heavy-tailed:** a small share of long documents, runaway agent loops or\n     retries can dominate the invoice.\n- **It works before launch,** when you can still change the design (cap turns, cache prefixes, route to a\n  smaller model) instead of explaining an invoice.\n- **It feeds everything else:** break-even analysis, pricing and unit economics all need a defensible\n  cost per request.","how":"### The per-call formula\n```\ncost = fresh_input_tokens × p_in\n     + cached_input_tokens × p_cached\n     + output_tokens × p_out            # includes reasoning/thinking tokens where billed\n     + non-model costs (embeddings, rerank, vector search, tool APIs, guardrail models)\n```\n- **Other price terms exist:** some providers charge a premium to *write* a prompt-cache entry, discount\n  asynchronous batch processing, or use different rates above a context-length threshold.\n- **Read prices from configuration, never hard-code them,** because pricing is vendor- and\n  model-specific and changes often.\n\n### Why output costs more\n| Phase | How it runs | GPU efficiency |\n|---|---|---|\n| Prefill (input) | Whole prompt in one parallel pass | Uses compute efficiently |\n| Decode (output) | One token at a time; each step reads full weights and KV cache for little arithmetic | Far fewer tokens per second than input |\n\n- **Providers price that:** output is commonly several times the input price.\n- **Design consequence:** verbosity is expensive, and a reasoning model that \"thinks\" for thousands of\n  tokens can cost more than a larger model that answers directly.\n\n### Tokens are not words, and not portable\n- **Counts depend on the tokenizer.** The same text yields different counts on different model families.\n- **Code, non-English text and JSON** tend to tokenize less efficiently than English prose.\n- **So** a per-word estimate built on one model is wrong on another; measure with the target model's\n  tokenizer.\n\n### Multi-turn growth\nTurn t sends the system prompt, tool schemas and every earlier message. If each turn adds d tokens:\n\n```\ntotal input over T turns ≈ T·prefix + d·T²/2\n```\n\n- **The quadratic term dominates long sessions.**\n- **Prompt (prefix) caching** bills repeated prefixes at a discount. It leaves the T² shape intact but\n  multiplies its coefficient by the cached-to-fresh price ratio (0.1 in the example below).\n- **Context compaction** shrinks the history itself, capping how large it can grow and turning the\n  quadratic back into roughly linear growth once the cap is reached.\n- **Caching needs the next turn within the cache lifetime.** Human think time between turns can exceed\n  it, so measure the cached-token share rather than assuming it.\n\n### Self-hosted token cost\n```\ncost per token = (GPU $/hour) / (tokens produced per hour at the utilization you actually achieve)\n```\n- **Utilization is decisive.** A fleet busy a third of the time pays three times as much per token as the\n  same fleet kept full.\n\n### Model the distribution\n1. **Build from logged traces:** per feature, the distribution of input, cached and output tokens and of\n   turns per session.\n2. **Report** p50, p95 and p99 cost per request and the share of spend from the top 1% of requests.\n3. **Forecast with the true mean:** forecast = Σ over features of volume × mean cost per request. It is\n   usually well above the cost of the \"typical\" request people reason from.\n4. **State the tail share,** because the mean alone hides the runaway sessions that budgets and step caps\n   must bound.","example":"### Per-call, agent-loop and self-hosted cost\nIllustrative prices: $3 per million input tokens, $15 per million output, $0.30 per million cached input\n(cache-write premiums ignored). Substitute your provider's current sheet.\n\n```python\nP_IN, P_OUT, P_CACHED = 3.00, 15.00, 0.30          # $ per million tokens (illustrative)\n\ndef call_cost(fresh_in, out, cached_in=0):\n    return (fresh_in * P_IN + cached_in * P_CACHED + out * P_OUT) / 1e6\n\ndef agent_run(turns, prefix=4_000, delta=600, out=200, cache=False):\n    \"\"\"Each turn re-sends the history: prefix + all earlier tool results and outputs.\"\"\"\n    total, history = 0.0, prefix\n    for t in range(turns):\n        reused = history - delta - out if (cache and t > 0) else 0   # last turn's prompt\n        total += call_cost(history - reused, out, reused)\n        history += delta + out\n    return total\n\nprint(f\"single RAG call: ${call_cost(6_000, 400):.4f}\")              # $0.0240\nfor turns in (4, 12, 24):\n    print(turns, round(agent_run(turns), 3), round(agent_run(turns, cache=True), 3))\n# 4 turns: $0.074 vs $0.036 cached | 12: $0.338 vs $0.101 | 24: $1.022 vs $0.228\n\ngpu_hr, tok_per_s = 4.00, 1_500                     # illustrative $/GPU-hour, aggregate output tok/s\nfor util in (0.9, 0.3):\n    print(util, round(gpu_hr / (tok_per_s * 3600 * util) * 1e6, 2), \"$/M output tokens\")\n# 0.9 -> $0.82/M ; 0.3 -> $2.47/M\n```\n\n| Run | No cache | Prefix cache |\n|---|---|---|\n| 4 turns | $0.074 | $0.036 |\n| 12 turns | $0.338 | $0.101 |\n| 24 turns | $1.022 | $0.228 |\n\n- **Quadratic:** 4 to 24 turns is 6× the turns but about 14× the cost without caching.\n- **Caching:** cuts the 24-turn run by more than 4×.\n- **Self-hosted:** utilization, not GPU list price, decides whether owning hardware is cheap.\n\n### Forecasting from a heavy-tailed distribution\nSynthetic lognormal token counts stand in for logged traces; with your own logs, replace the loop with\nreal per-request token counts.\n\n```python\nimport random\n\nrandom.seed(1)\nP_IN, P_OUT = 3.00, 15.00                          # $ per million tokens (illustrative)\n\ncosts = []\nfor _ in range(50_000):\n    fresh_in = random.lognormvariate(8.3, 0.9)     # median ~4,000 tokens\n    out = random.lognormvariate(5.7, 0.7)          # median ~300 tokens\n    costs.append((fresh_in * P_IN + out * P_OUT) / 1e6)\n\ncosts.sort()\nn = len(costs)\npct = lambda q: costs[int(q * (n - 1))]\nmean = sum(costs) / n\ntop1 = sum(costs[int(0.99 * n):]) / sum(costs)\n\nprint(f\"p50 ${pct(0.50):.4f}  p95 ${pct(0.95):.4f}  p99 ${pct(0.99):.4f}\")   # ~0.018, 0.061, 0.106\nprint(f\"top 1% of requests = {top1:.0%} of spend\")                           # ~6%\nprint(f\"forecast at 2M req/month: ${mean * 2_000_000:,.0f} \"                 # ~$47.8k\n      f\"(median-based: ${pct(0.5) * 2_000_000:,.0f})\")                       # ~$36.2k\n```\n\n- **The median underforecasts by about a quarter** even in this mild tail; real agent traffic with\n  runaway sessions has a fatter tail and a larger gap.\n- **p99 is almost 6× p50**, which is where per-session caps earn their keep.","patterns":["**Price table as versioned config.** Keep per-model prices (input, cached input, cache write, output, batch) in a config file with an effective date, and compute cost from it at query time. Monitor for calls to models missing from the table.","**Log provider usage fields, not estimates.** Record the token counts each response returns (for example Anthropic's `cache_read_input_tokens` and `cache_creation_input_tokens`, OpenAI's `reasoning_tokens` under completion token details) tagged with feature and request IDs. Monitor billed tokens against visible text length to catch hidden reasoning or schema bloat.","**Count tokens with the target tokenizer before launch.** Use the provider's counter (for example `tiktoken` for OpenAI models or Anthropic's token-counting endpoint) on real sample documents, not a words-to-tokens ratio. Re-run it on every model switch.","**Per-session budget guards.** Enforce maximum turns, maximum tokens and a cumulative-cost cap per agent session, stopping or escalating when hit. Monitor how often caps fire and the p99 cost per session.","**Monthly forecast review from traces.** Recompute volume × mean cost per feature and the top-1% share, and compare with the invoice. Monitor forecast error; a growing gap usually means a new tail."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[(\"Logged traces per feature\")] --> K[\"Tokens by type per call\"]\n    P[\"Price config by model\"] --> C[\"Per-call cost\"]\n    K --> C\n    N[\"Non-model costs\"] --> C\n    C --> S[\"Per-session cost: sum over turns\"]\n    S --> D[\"Distribution: p50, p95, p99\"]\n    D --> F[\"Forecast: volume x mean\"]\n    D --> R[\"Tail share: top 1 percent\"]\n    R --> L[\"Turn and token caps\"]","caption":"From traces to a forecast: per-call costs roll up into per-session costs, whose distribution gives both\nthe forecast (mean) and the risk (tail) that caps must bound."},"interview":[{"q":"Walk me through estimating the cost of one request to an LLM feature.","a":"- **Tokens by type:** fresh input, cached input and output including reasoning, each times its price.\n- **Plus non-model calls:** embeddings, reranking, vector search, tool APIs and guardrail models.\n- **From real data:** count with the target model's tokenizer on real samples, and read prices from\n  config because they change."},{"q":"Why does a 24-turn agent cost far more than 6 times a 4-turn one?","a":"- **Each turn re-sends the whole history,** so total input is about T·prefix + d·T²/2.\n- **In the illustrative example** 6× the turns is about 14× the cost.\n- **Levers:** prefix caching lowers the quadratic coefficient; compaction caps history growth so cost\n  becomes roughly linear; turn caps bound the tail."},{"q":"When is a reasoning model cheaper than a non-reasoning one despite more output tokens?","a":"- **When it replaces retries or chains:** one longer call that succeeds can beat several short calls that\n  fail or need a multi-step pipeline.\n- **When failure is expensive:** human review or escalation dwarfs token cost.\n- **Measure cost per successful task,** and cap reasoning with a budget or effort setting."},{"q":"Your monthly forecast was $36k and the invoice was $48k with the forecast volume. What went wrong?","a":"- **Likely a median-based forecast:** the mean of a heavy-tailed distribution sits well above the\n  typical request.\n- **Check the tail:** p99 cost and the top-1% share per feature, plus hidden reasoning and schema tokens.\n- **Fix:** forecast as volume × mean from traces, state the tail share, and add per-session caps."},{"q":"How do you compare self-hosting with an API on cost per token?","a":"- **Self-hosted cost per token** is GPU $/hour divided by tokens produced per hour at achieved\n  utilization.\n- **Utilization dominates:** in the illustrative example 90% gives $0.82 per million output tokens and\n  30% gives $2.47.\n- **Self-hosting wins** only with steady high utilization; low or spiky traffic favors the API because\n  its cost stays variable."}],"tradeoffs":["**Detailed per-feature model vs effort.** It catches expensive design choices, but costs time to build and maintain; for a prototype a spreadsheet of token counts per call is enough until traffic is real.","**Prompt caching vs reuse.** It cuts repeated-prefix cost sharply, but only pays when prefixes are byte-identical and reused within the cache lifetime; it flips to a small loss where writes carry a premium and reuse is rare.","**Reasoning models vs output tokens.** They raise output tokens per task, but can lower total cost when they replace several retries or a multi-step chain; measure cost per *successful* task, not per call.","**Self-hosting vs fixed cost.** It lowers marginal token cost at high utilization, but turns cost fixed; it flips to more expensive than an API when traffic is low or spiky."],"pitfalls":["**Estimating from a typical or hand-picked request instead of the logged distribution.** Symptom: an invoice several times the forecast, driven by a long tail of large documents or long agent sessions. Fix: forecast with the mean of the logged distribution and report the tail.","**Forgetting hidden reasoning tokens or tool-schema tokens in the prompt.** Symptom: billed input or output counts far above what the visible text suggests. Fix: log provider usage fields.","**Reusing a token-per-word ratio from one model family on another.** Symptom: the forecast drifts after a model switch even though traffic did not change. Fix: re-count with the new tokenizer.","**Ignoring non-model costs** such as embeddings, reranking, guardrail models and trace storage. Symptom: total spend per request well above the LLM line item. Fix: include them in the formula."],"when":"- **Use before** committing to any design that calls a model in a loop, ingests user-supplied documents,\n  or will run at volume; rebuild it from real traces after launch.\n- **Use it to** set max-turn and max-token limits, decide where prompt caching and smaller models pay off,\n  and feed pricing and break-even decisions.\n- **Measure** tokens by type (fresh, cached, output, reasoning) per feature and per request, and watch the\n  p95 and p99, not only the mean.\n- **Interviews:** deriving the quadratic growth of agent cost and the utilization term in self-hosted cost\n  shows real understanding.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Logs token counts by type for every call, tags them with feature and request IDs, and writes the cost function that dashboards and budget guards share.","architect":"Uses the model to choose between designs, set turn and context limits, and decide where caching, smaller models or self-hosting change the economics.","fde":"Builds a cost estimate from the customer's real document sizes and usage volumes, so the commercial proposal survives contact with production traffic."},"check":{"q":"A support agent averages 8 turns, but finance's forecast assumed cost = 8 × a single-call cost.\nThe invoice is 4× the forecast. Explain, and say what you would change.","a":"Each turn re-sends the system prompt, tool schemas and full history, so input tokens grow with\nthe turn number and total input grows roughly with the square of turns; multiplying a single call by 8\nundercounts badly. The tail also matters: a few 30-turn sessions dominate. I would rebuild the model from\ntraces with token distributions per turn, enable prompt caching for the stable prefix, compact or\nsummarize history past a threshold, cap turns and tokens per session, and route simple tickets to a\nsmaller model, then track p95 cost per resolved ticket rather than per call."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do hosted providers typically price output tokens higher than input tokens?","options":["Output tokens are larger than input tokens in bytes","Output is generated sequentially in decode steps that use the GPU far less efficiently than the parallel prefill of input","Output tokens must be checked by a safety model that input tokens skip","Input tokens are cached for free on every request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prefill processes the prompt in one parallel, compute-efficient pass; decode yields one token per step and is memory-bandwidth bound, so each output token consumes more GPU time."},{"q":"An agent's turns each add about the same number of tokens to history. How does total input cost scale with the number of turns, without caching or compaction?","options":["Linearly, since each turn adds the same number of tokens","Roughly quadratically, because each turn re-sends all earlier turns","Logarithmically, because providers discount long prompts","It stays constant after the first turn"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Turn t's prompt contains all t earlier increments, so total input is a sum of an arithmetic series, proportional to T². Each turn adding a constant amount is exactly what makes the total quadratic."},{"q":"A self-hosted GPU costs the same per hour regardless of load. What most changes its cost per token?","options":["The model's parameter count only","The provider's published per-token price","The utilization actually achieved, since cost per token is hourly cost divided by tokens produced per hour","Whether the prompt uses JSON output"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fixed hourly cost spread over fewer tokens raises the per-token price; a fleet at 30% utilization pays three times more per token than the same fleet at 90%."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","sources":[],"why":"- **Dashboards show cost per call because providers bill per token.** That answers the wrong question.\n- **The business buys outcomes, not calls:** resolved tickets, extracted invoices, merged pull requests,\n  qualified leads.\n- **Between a call and an outcome sit hidden costs:** retries, tool errors, verification passes,\n  escalations to a human and, worst, wrong outputs nobody caught that cost a refund, a compliance\n  incident or a customer.\n- **Cost per call rewards the wrong design.** A smaller model halves per-call cost and can triple cost\n  per resolved task once escalations are counted. Agents widen the gap: a run that loops for 40 steps\n  and then fails consumed real money and produced nothing.\n- **Undefined success drifts.** Without explicit acceptance criteria, \"success\" becomes whatever flatters\n  the metric, such as \"the model returned something\". One fixed ratio of cost to outcome lets model\n  choices, retry policies and human-in-the-loop designs be compared on what the business cares about.","how":"### 1. Define success first\n- **In terms the customer would accept:** ticket resolved and not reopened within a set window;\n  extraction matching the source on all required fields; code change passing tests and merged.\n- **Decide how it is measured:** automated checks, sampled human review, or a downstream signal. A metric\n  without a fixed definition drifts toward whatever is easy to count.\n- **Use closed cohorts.** Downstream signals arrive late (a ticket counts as resolved only once the reopen\n  window has passed), so compute on cohorts whose window has closed, or this week's number looks better\n  than it is.\n\n### 2. Count all the cost\nFor a period or a cohort of tasks, sum:\n- **Model tokens for every attempt,** including failed and abandoned ones, and reasoning tokens.\n- **Non-model calls:** retrieval, tools, guardrails, verifier or judge models.\n- **Human time** for review, escalation and correction, at a loaded hourly rate.\n- **Expected cost of undetected errors:** rate of silent failure × cost per incident.\n- **Amortized fixed costs,** if the comparison is about the whole product rather than a design change.\n\n### 3. Divide by successes\n```\ncost_per_successful_task = total_cost / tasks_meeting_acceptance\n```\n- **Expected-value shortcut:** if each attempt costs c and succeeds with probability p, and every failure\n  is reliably detected and retried, expected AI cost per success is c/p.\n- **So a model at 4× the price with 1.5× the success rate is not 4× worse.**\n- **Two terms usually dominate in real systems:** the share of failures handed to humans, and the share\n  of failures *not detected* at all.\n\n### 4. Compare with value\n- **Value per task:** human handling cost avoided, revenue per conversion, or time saved.\n- **Margin per task** = value − fully loaded cost.\n- **Break-even success rate** is the p at which margin reaches zero: how good the system must be before\n  automation pays.\n- **With a human fallback,** the baseline is the all-human cost h. With one attempt at cost c, a detector\n  catching a fraction d of failures, and a cost e per silent error, automation is cheaper when:\n\n```\nc + (1 − p)·(d·h + (1 − d)·e)  <  h     →     p  >  1 − (h − c) / (d·h + (1 − d)·e)\n```\n\n- **With perfect detection (d = 1)** this reduces to p > c/h, which is why cheap models clear the bar\n  easily when failures are always caught. As e grows and d falls, the required p climbs fast.\n\n### Caveats that change the answer\n- **Attempts are not independent.** A model that fails on a hard input often fails again on retry, so the\n  c/p retry math is optimistic.\n- **Detection is neither free nor perfect.** A verifier adds cost and misses some failures.\n- **The task mix shifts over time,** so recompute per task category, not one global number.","example":"### Fully loaded cost per task for two models\nA ticket-triage agent where a human handles anything the agent cannot (all figures illustrative): human\ncost $4.00 per ticket, a verifier costing $0.005 catches 80% of agent failures, and a failure that slips\nthrough costs $20 in rework and goodwill.\n\n```python\ndef per_task(c, p, k=1, verify=0.005, detect=0.8, human=4.00, error=20.00):\n    \"\"\"Expected fully loaded cost of one task: up to k attempts; a caught failure is retried\n    (then sent to a human after k); a missed failure costs `error`. Independence is optimistic.\"\"\"\n    cost, reach, solved, ai = 0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 0.0\n    for _ in range(k):\n        ai += reach * (c + verify)\n        cost += reach * (1 - p) * (1 - detect) * error   # silent failure reaches the customer\n        solved += reach * p\n        reach *= (1 - p) * detect                         # caught failure: try again\n    cost += ai + reach * human\n    return cost, solved, ai / solved\n\nfor name, c, p in ((\"small\", 0.02, 0.60), (\"large\", 0.08, 0.90)):\n    for k in (1, 3):\n        cost, solved, ai_per_win = per_task(c, p, k=k)\n        print(f\"{name} k={k}: ${cost:.3f}/task automated {solved:.1%} AI spend/success ${ai_per_win:.3f}\")\n# small k=1: $2.905/task automated 60.0% AI spend/success $0.042\n# small k=3: $2.442/task automated 85.3% AI spend/success $0.042\n# large k=1: $0.805/task automated 90.0% AI spend/success $0.094\n# large k=3: $0.529/task automated 97.8% AI spend/success $0.094\n```\n\n- **AI spend per success flatters the small model** by more than 2×. Fully loaded, it costs about 3.6×\n  more per task (k=1), because its silent failures and escalations dominate.\n- **Retries help only with *detected* failures,** and every extra attempt is another chance to slip past\n  the verifier. For the small model, k=1 to k=3 cuts human escalation from $1.28 to about $0.13 per task\n  but *raises* the silent-error term from $1.60 to about $2.28, which now dominates its bill.\n- **So improving detection is often worth more than adding retries,** or even than improving the model.\n- **Cost per task ≈ cost per success here,** because a human finishes every caught failure; silent\n  failures are the exception, charged through the error term.\n- **Break-even:** against the all-human $4.00, the formula gives p > 1 − 3.975 / 7.2 ≈ 0.45 for the small\n  model. At 60% it beats doing everything by hand, but by a thin margin a small rise in silent-error cost\n  would erase.\n\n### How detection moves the break-even bar\nThe same formula as a function, for the small model (c = $0.02 + $0.005 verifier).\n\n```python\ndef break_even_p(c, h, d, e):\n    \"\"\"Minimum success rate for one AI attempt plus fallback to beat the all-human cost h.\"\"\"\n    return 1 - (h - c) / (d * h + (1 - d) * e)\n\nfor d in (1.0, 0.8, 0.6):\n    print(d, f\"{break_even_p(0.025, 4.00, d, 20.00):.2f}\")\n# 1.0 0.01   (reduces to c/h)\n# 0.8 0.45\n# 0.6 0.62\n```\n\n| Detection rate d | Required success rate p |\n|---|---|\n| 1.0 | ~0.01 (c/h) |\n| 0.8 | ~0.45 |\n| 0.6 | ~0.62 |\n\nLosing 20 points of detection raises the bar by about 17 points of model accuracy.\n\n### Worked cohort: from invoice lines to one number\nAn illustrative month of 10,000 tickets whose 7-day reopen window has closed.\n\n| Line | Amount |\n|---|---|\n| Model tokens, all attempts including failed and abandoned | $420 |\n| Retrieval, tools and verifier calls | $90 |\n| Human escalations: 1,200 tickets × $4.00 | $4,800 |\n| Silent errors: 150 reopened tickets × $20 | $3,000 |\n| **Total** | **$8,310** |\n| Successful tasks (resolved, not reopened) | 9,850 |\n| **Cost per successful task** | **~$0.84** |\n\n- **The model line is 5% of the total;** escalations and silent errors are 94%.\n- **Against the $4.00 human baseline** the margin is about $3.16 per success, a number a per-call\n  dashboard could never show.","patterns":["**One task ID across every attempt.** Generate an ID when the task starts and attach it to every model call, tool call, verifier result and escalation (for example as a trace attribute). Monitor the share of spend not attributable to any task; it should be near zero.","**Cohort metrics after the window closes.** Compute cost per successful task weekly on cohorts whose reopen or acceptance window has passed, per task category and per tenant. Monitor it next to success rate and escalation rate.","**Calibrate detection with sampled review.** Have humans review a random sample of verifier-passed outputs to estimate the silent-failure rate (1 − d). Monitor that rate per category; it feeds the error term directly.","**Retry budget tied to detection.** Allow retries only after a verifier rejects an output, cap them at a small k, then route to a human. Monitor success on the second attempt; if it is far below the first, failures are correlated and retries are wasted.","**Break-even check before rollout.** For each new model or prompt, compute required p from c, h, d and e and compare with the eval success rate per category. Ship only where the margin is comfortably positive."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"Task arrives\"] --> A[\"AI attempt: cost c\"]\n    A --> V{\"Verifier passes?\"}\n    V -->|\"yes, correct\"| S[\"Success\"]\n    V -->|\"yes, but wrong\"| E[\"Silent error: cost e\"]\n    V -->|\"no, caught\"| K{\"Attempts left?\"}\n    K -->|\"yes\"| A\n    K -->|\"no\"| H[\"Human handles: cost h\"]\n    H --> S\n    S --> R[\"Cost per success = total cost / successes\"]\n    E --> R","caption":"One task's paths: only caught failures can be retried; missed failures skip straight to the error cost,\nwhich is why detection rate matters so much."},"interview":[{"q":"What is cost per successful task, and why not just report cost per call?","a":"- **Definition:** fully loaded cost (all attempts, tools, verifiers, human time, expected silent-error\n  cost) divided by tasks meeting an agreed acceptance bar.\n- **Why:** the business buys outcomes; a cheaper call that fails more moves cost into retries,\n  escalations and errors.\n- **Comparable to value:** only this number can be set against value per task to get a margin."},{"q":"Your team adds two retries and the automation rate rises, but total cost per task barely moves. Why?","a":"- **Retries act only on caught failures,** so they shift cost from human escalation to automation.\n- **Each attempt is another chance for a silent miss,** so the silent-error term grows; in the\n  illustrative example it rises from $1.60 to about $2.28 per task.\n- **Fix detection first:** a better verifier lowers both escalation and silent-error terms."},{"q":"When would you not add a verifier step?","a":"- **When silent failures are cheap:** for low-stakes tasks the verifier is pure overhead on every task.\n- **When it misses the failures that matter:** a verifier with low detection on the costly error class\n  adds spend without cutting e.\n- **Decide with the break-even formula,** comparing verifier cost per task with the expected silent-error\n  cost it removes."},{"q":"How do you set the minimum accuracy a model needs before you automate a workflow?","a":"- **Compute break-even p** from attempt cost c, human cost h, detection rate d and silent-error cost e:\n  p > 1 − (h − c) / (d·h + (1 − d)·e).\n- **Detection dominates:** in the illustrative example the bar moves from about 0.45 at d = 0.8 to about\n  0.62 at d = 0.6.\n- **Require a margin** above break-even per task category, measured on a closed cohort."},{"q":"This week's cost per successful ticket looks 30% better than last month's. What do you check?","a":"- **Cohort maturity:** tickets still inside the reopen window count as successes until they reopen.\n- **Definition drift:** whether \"success\" quietly loosened, or the task mix shifted to easier categories.\n- **Attribution gaps:** failed or abandoned runs missing a task ID drop out of the cost numerator."}],"tradeoffs":["**Stricter success definition vs headline number.** It makes the metric trustworthy but lowers the number and costs review effort; a looser one flips into self-deception once customers start reopening tasks.","**Verifier cost vs silent-failure cost.** A verifier costs money on every task but pays back whenever a silent failure costs much more than verification; for low-stakes tasks it can be pure overhead.","**More retries vs tokens and latency.** Retries raise automation rate, but each costs tokens and latency and helps only when failures are detected and not strongly correlated across attempts.","**Fully loaded vs marginal cost.** Including amortized fixed costs gives the true product margin but blurs comparisons between designs; use marginal cost to choose designs and fully loaded cost to price."],"pitfalls":["**Reporting cost per call as unit cost.** Symptom: a \"cheaper\" model rollout followed by more escalations and a higher total support bill. Fix: divide fully loaded cost by successes.","**Counting only successful runs' tokens.** Symptom: unit cost that ignores agent runs that looped and failed, so finance totals never reconcile with the metric. Fix: attribute every attempt to its task.","**Defining success as \"completed without error\".** Symptom: the metric looks excellent while customer reopen rates or downstream correction volume quietly climb. Fix: customer-accepted criteria.","**Treating the retry math as independent.** Symptom: real success after retries stays well below the predicted rate because the same hard inputs keep failing. Fix: measure second-attempt success."],"when":"- **Use when** comparing models, prompts, retry policies or human-in-the-loop designs, and when pricing an\n  AI feature or arguing for its ROI.\n- **Measure** per task category and per tenant rather than globally; track weekly next to success rate\n  and escalation rate, and validate the success definition with sampled human review.\n- **Avoid** during early prototyping, before there is a stable task definition.\n- **Interviews:** asking \"per successful what?\" when someone quotes a cost number is the mark of someone\n  who has run an AI product.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments each task end to end with an ID so every attempt, tool call, verifier result and escalation is attributed to one outcome.","architect":"Chooses model tier, retry budget, verifier and human-fallback design by comparing fully loaded cost per successful task against task value.","fde":"Agrees the success definition and the value per task with the customer, so the ROI case uses their numbers and survives their finance review."},"check":{"q":"Model X costs $0.01 per attempt with 70% success; model Y costs $0.05 with 92%. Failures go to a\nhuman at $3. Which is cheaper per task, and what assumption would you challenge?","a":"With one attempt and perfect detection, X costs 0.01 + 0.30 × 3 = $0.91 per task and Y costs\n0.05 + 0.08 × 3 = $0.29, so Y is about three times cheaper despite the 5× price. The human fallback\ndominates. I would challenge perfect detection: if some failures are not caught, the cost of each silent\nerror must be added, and that term usually favors the more accurate model further. I would also check\nwhether retrying X helps or whether its failures correlate on hard inputs, and compute the numbers per\nticket category."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is cost per API call a misleading unit cost for an AI feature?","options":["API calls are billed monthly rather than per call","It ignores failed attempts, retries, human fallback and undetected errors, which can dominate the cost of a good outcome","Token prices change too frequently to measure per call","It double-counts cached input tokens"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The business pays for outcomes. A cheaper call that fails more often moves cost into retries, escalations and error handling, so cost per successful task can rise while cost per call falls."},{"q":"Each attempt costs c, succeeds with probability p, and failures are always detected and retried. What is the expected AI cost per success?","options":["c × p","c / p","c / (1 − p)","c + p"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The number of attempts until success is geometric with mean 1/p, so expected cost per success is c/p. c/(1 − p) inverts the wrong probability: it is the expected cost until the first *failure*, a common mix-up."},{"q":"A verifier catches 80% of failures. Adding more retries mainly reduces which cost term?","options":["The cost of silent failures that reach the customer","The share of tasks escalated to a human after caught failures","The per-attempt token cost","The verifier's own cost per attempt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Retries only act on failures the verifier detects, so they shift work from human escalation to automation. Missed failures never trigger a retry, and each extra attempt is another chance of a silent miss, so the silent-error term does not fall and can even grow; it needs better detection."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","sources":["Official docs: FinOps Foundation (FinOps Framework)"],"why":"- **Invoices say how much, not who.** A hosted model invoice is one number per model per month; a\n  self-hosted GPU fleet costs the same per hour whether one tenant or fifty used it.\n- **Cost profiles differ wildly.** A tenant who uploads 500-page contracts and runs long agent sessions\n  can cost a hundred times more than one asking short questions on the same plan. Without allocation it\n  looks as profitable as everyone else until gross margin turns negative.\n- **Even or per-seat splits hide the problem,** because they subsidize heavy users with light ones.\n- **Direct-API-only allocation ignores shared infrastructure,** which for self-hosted or RAG-heavy systems\n  can be most of the spend.\n- **Retrofitting fails.** Attribution cannot be rebuilt later from logs when tenant and feature were never\n  recorded at call time, and adding tags across many services is slow. Allocation answers, with numbers\n  that reconcile to the invoice: what each tenant and feature costs, and where the margin is.","how":"### 1. Tag at the point of spend\n- **One gateway or client library** for all model, embedding and reranker calls, requiring `tenant_id`,\n  `feature` (or workflow) and a task or trace ID on every call; it rejects or flags calls missing them.\n- **Record per call:** model, tokens by type (fresh input, cached input, output, reasoning), latency, and\n  for self-hosted models the GPU-seconds or token-seconds consumed.\n- **Propagate tags** through agents, tools and background jobs as trace attributes, *because* the call\n  that spends money is often several hops away from the request that caused it.\n\n### 2. Price direct costs per call\n- **Compute cost at write time** from a versioned price table, so historical rows keep the price that\n  applied then.\n- **Apply the billing rules your provider actually uses** for cache, batch and long context.\n- **Cross-check at the source:** where possible, use separate provider API keys or projects per\n  environment or major feature so the invoice itself can be split.\n\n### 3. Allocate shared costs with explicit drivers\nShared pools need a driver that approximates causation:\n\n| Pool | Driver | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| **GPU fleet** | GPU-seconds or token-seconds per request; tokens weighted by phase (prefill vs decode) | Concurrent batching makes exact attribution impossible |\n| **GPU fleet, long-context traffic** | KV-cache occupancy (context tokens × seconds resident) | Memory, not compute, limits how many other requests fit |\n| **Shared prompt-cache prefixes** | Blended rate for every call | Literal billing rewards whoever arrives second |\n| **Commitments and discounts** | Effective (amortized) rate, not list price | An unused commitment is idle capacity under another name |\n| **Vector store** | Stored vectors (storage-bound); query count (compute-bound) | Matches what drives each cost |\n| **Embedding re-indexing** | The tenant whose data was re-embedded | They caused it |\n| **Idle and headroom capacity** | Spread by usage, or a visible platform-overhead line | Policy choice, see below |\n\n- **Long context crowds others out.** A 100k-token session that decodes slowly crowds out many short ones\n  while using little arithmetic, so occupancy is often the fairer driver.\n- **Prompt-cache prefixes:** the first request pays the cache write and later ones get the cheap read.\n  For a prefix shared across tenants (a common system prompt), pricing every call at the blended rate is\n  usually fairer.\n- **Idle capacity:** spreading makes tenant totals add up; keeping it separate shows the cost of\n  over-provisioning instead of hiding it.\n- **Write the driver choice down.** It is a policy decision, and changing it changes everyone's numbers.\n- **Platform-caused waste goes to platform overhead.** Retries after a provider 5xx or a bad deploy are\n  usually better charged there than to the tenant who happened to be calling.\n\n### 4. Reconcile and track the unallocated share\n- **Each month, allocations must sum to the invoices.**\n- **Track the unattributed share** (untagged calls, shared jobs) as a KPI and drive it down, because a\n  large unattributed bucket makes every per-tenant number suspect.\n\n### 5. Use it: showback, chargeback, pricing\n- **Showback** reports cost per tenant and feature to teams and account owners.\n- **Chargeback** actually moves the cost onto internal teams' budgets.\n- **External pricing is a separate decision** (usage-based tiers, overages) that this data informs.\n- **Most products start with showback,** then use the data to set tier limits, overage pricing,\n  per-tenant quotas, and to flag tenants whose cost exceeds their revenue.","example":"### Monthly allocation in SQL\nA month of usage: hosted API calls priced at the gateway, plus a shared GPU fleet and vector store\nbilled as lump sums (PostgreSQL; numeric columns).\n\n```sql\nWITH usage AS (\n  SELECT COALESCE(tenant_id, 'UNATTRIBUTED') AS tenant_id,\n         COALESCE(feature,   'UNATTRIBUTED') AS feature,\n         COALESCE(SUM(api_cost_usd), 0)::numeric AS direct_usd,  -- priced per call at the gateway\n         SUM(gpu_seconds)::numeric       AS gpu_s,       -- self-hosted calls\n         SUM(retrieval_queries)::numeric AS rq\n  FROM usage_events\n  WHERE ts >= DATE '2026-09-01' AND ts < DATE '2026-10-01'\n  GROUP BY 1, 2\n),\npools AS (\n  SELECT SUM(amount_usd) FILTER (WHERE pool = 'gpu_fleet')    AS gpu_usd,\n         SUM(amount_usd) FILTER (WHERE pool = 'vector_store') AS vec_usd\n  FROM shared_invoices WHERE month = DATE '2026-09-01'\n),\nalloc AS (\n  SELECT u.tenant_id, u.feature, u.direct_usd,\n         COALESCE(p.gpu_usd * u.gpu_s / NULLIF(SUM(u.gpu_s) OVER (), 0), 0) AS gpu_usd,\n         COALESCE(p.vec_usd * u.rq / NULLIF(SUM(u.rq) OVER (), 0), 0)       AS vector_usd\n  FROM usage u CROSS JOIN pools p\n)\nSELECT tenant_id, feature,\n       ROUND(direct_usd, 2) AS direct, ROUND(gpu_usd, 2) AS gpu, ROUND(vector_usd, 2) AS vector,\n       ROUND(direct_usd + gpu_usd + vector_usd, 2) AS total\nFROM alloc\nORDER BY total DESC;\n```\n\n- **The `UNATTRIBUTED` row should be small;** if it is 15% of spend, fix tagging before trusting any\n  tenant number.\n\n### Worked scenario: the tenant that looked profitable\nIllustrative month: $40,000 of direct API spend plus a $30,000 GPU fleet ($70,000 in all; vector store\nleft out for simplicity). Tenant A's contract-review feature used 45% of GPU-seconds, and A holds 5% of\nseats.\n\n| View | A's cost | A's contract | Margin |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Per-seat split: 5% of $70,000 | $3,500 | $12,000 | +$8,500 |\n| Driver-based: $6,200 direct + 45% × $30,000 = $13,500 GPU | about $19,700 | $12,000 | about −$7,700 |\n\n- **The even-split view would have shown A as profitable.**\n- **Next steps the data supports:** a tier limit or overage price for long documents, a per-tenant quota,\n  or a dedicated pool priced into A's contract.\n\n### Pricing each call at write time\nA versioned price table means a provider price change never rewrites last quarter's tenant costs.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\n\n# $ per million tokens, illustrative. Rows are appended, never edited.\nPRICES = [\n    (\"large\", date(2026, 1, 1), {\"in\": 3.00, \"cached\": 0.30, \"out\": 15.00}),\n    (\"large\", date(2026, 9, 15), {\"in\": 2.50, \"cached\": 0.25, \"out\": 12.50}),\n]\n\ndef price_for(model, day):\n    rows = [(start, p) for m, start, p in PRICES if m == model and start <= day]\n    if not rows:\n        raise LookupError(f\"no price for {model} on {day}\")\n    return max(rows, key=lambda r: r[0])\n\ndef priced_event(call):\n    for tag in (\"tenant_id\", \"feature\", \"trace_id\"):\n        if not call.get(tag):\n            raise ValueError(f\"untagged model call: missing {tag}\")   # or flag as UNATTRIBUTED\n    start, p = price_for(call[\"model\"], call[\"day\"])\n    cost = (call[\"fresh_in\"] * p[\"in\"] + call[\"cached_in\"] * p[\"cached\"]\n            + call[\"out\"] * p[\"out\"]) / 1e6\n    return {**call, \"price_version\": start.isoformat(), \"api_cost_usd\": round(cost, 6)}\n\ncall = dict(tenant_id=\"acme\", feature=\"contract-review\", trace_id=\"t-91\", model=\"large\",\n            day=date(2026, 9, 10), fresh_in=12_000, cached_in=2_000, out=800)\nprint(priced_event(call)[\"api_cost_usd\"])                                 # 0.0486, old price\nprint(priced_event({**call, \"day\": date(2026, 9, 20)})[\"api_cost_usd\"])   # 0.0405, new price\n```","patterns":["**Mandatory tags at the gateway.** When several services call models, make the shared gateway or client reject (or loudly flag) any call without tenant, feature and trace ID. Monitor the unattributed share of spend daily and alert above a threshold such as a few percent.","**Propagate context through async work.** Carry tenant and feature as trace context (for example OpenTelemetry baggage or span attributes) into agents, tool calls, queues and background jobs, and copy them into job payloads. Monitor unattributed spend by service to find the hop that drops them.","**Split at the source with provider projects or keys.** Use separate OpenAI projects or Anthropic workspaces, or separate keys, per environment or major feature, so the invoice cross-checks the gateway's numbers. Monitor the gap between gateway-computed and invoiced cost per key.","**Monthly reconciliation job.** Sum allocations per pool and compare with each invoice before publishing showback. Monitor reconciliation error and the size of the platform-overhead line.","**Cost-versus-revenue alerts per tenant.** Join allocated cost with contract revenue and flag tenants whose cost exceeds a set share of revenue. Monitor the count of negative-margin tenants and feed it to pricing, quotas and isolation decisions."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Request with tenant and feature tags\"] --> G{\"Gateway: tags present?\"}\n    G -->|\"no\"| U[\"Flag as UNATTRIBUTED\"]\n    G -->|\"yes\"| E[\"Priced usage event\"]\n    P[\"Versioned price table\"] --> E\n    S[(\"Shared invoices: GPU, vector store\")] --> D[\"Split by driver\"]\n    E --> T[\"Cost per tenant and feature\"]\n    U --> T\n    D --> T\n    T --> C{\"Sums to invoices?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes\"| O[\"Showback, chargeback, pricing\"]\n    C -->|\"no\"| F[\"Fix tagging or drivers\"]","caption":"Direct costs are priced per tagged call; shared invoices are split by a driver; both meet in one\ntenant-by-feature table that must reconcile to the invoices."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between direct and shared AI costs, and how do you allocate each?","a":"- **Direct costs** are per call (hosted tokens): tag every call with tenant and feature and price it at\n  write time.\n- **Shared costs** are pools (GPU fleet, vector store, commitments): split by a written driver that\n  approximates causation, such as token-seconds or query count.\n- **Both must reconcile** to the invoices each month, with the unattributed share tracked as a KPI."},{"q":"Twenty percent of spend shows up as UNATTRIBUTED. How do you find and fix it?","a":"- **Break it down by service and call type:** usually agent tool calls, background re-embeddings and\n  retries that lost tenant context.\n- **Fix at the hop that drops it:** propagate tenant and feature as trace context and copy them into job\n  payloads.\n- **Then enforce:** make the gateway reject or flag untagged calls so the bucket cannot regrow."},{"q":"When would you give a tenant dedicated keys or a dedicated pool instead of shared infrastructure?","a":"- **Large enterprise tenants** whose contracts need exact attribution, isolation or a guaranteed quota.\n- **Cost:** dedicated pools lose pooling efficiency and add quota management.\n- **Not for the long tail:** small tenants are cheaper to serve from a shared pool with driver-based\n  allocation."},{"q":"A long-context tenant uses few GPU FLOPs but you suspect it costs a lot. How do you allocate fairly?","a":"- **Memory is the binding constraint:** a 100k-token session holds KV cache for its whole lifetime,\n  crowding out many short requests.\n- **Use KV-cache occupancy** (context tokens × seconds resident) as the driver for long-context traffic,\n  instead of request count or raw compute.\n- **Document the policy,** since switching drivers changes every tenant's numbers."},{"q":"Who should pay for retries caused by a provider outage or a bad deploy?","a":"- **Usually platform overhead,** because the tenant did not cause the extra spend.\n- **Charging the tenant** distorts their unit cost and erodes trust in showback.\n- **Record a cause field on retries** so the allocation job can route them, and watch the overhead line\n  to see the cost of incidents."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fine-grained drivers vs effort.** GPU-seconds by phase and per-query vector cost are fairer, but cost engineering effort; coarse token-proportional splits suffice while shared costs are a small share of spend.","**Spreading idle capacity vs visibility.** It makes totals reconcile, but hides over-provisioning; keep it separate when the platform team needs to see and own utilization.","**Chargeback vs disputes.** It changes behavior because internal teams pay from their own budget, but disputes over drivers follow; showback is the better start until the numbers are trusted.","**Per-tenant keys or dedicated pools vs pooling.** They make attribution exact, but lose pooling efficiency and add quota management; worth it for large enterprise tenants, not a long tail of small ones."],"pitfalls":["**Tagging only the user-facing call.** Symptom: a large unattributed bucket made of agent tool calls, background re-embeddings and retries that lost their tenant context. Fix: propagate tags as trace context.","**Repricing history with today's price table.** Symptom: last quarter's per-tenant costs change after a provider price update. Fix: price at write time with a versioned table.","**Allocating shared GPU cost by request count.** Symptom: long-document tenants look cheap while short-query tenants are overcharged, and cost per tenant does not track token volume. Fix: a token-based or occupancy driver.","**Never reconciling with invoices.** Symptom: per-tenant totals drift from actual spend and finance stops trusting the dashboard. Fix: a monthly reconciliation step before publishing."],"when":"- **Set up tagging before the first multi-tenant launch;** it is cheap at the start and painful to\n  retrofit.\n- **Formal shared-pool allocation pays** once shared infrastructure is a material part of spend or pricing\n  decisions depend on per-tenant cost.\n- **Measure** unattributed share, cost per tenant against revenue per tenant, cost per feature per active\n  user, and cost per successful task by tenant.\n- **Interviews:** explaining how to attribute shared GPU cost under batching, and why idle capacity is a\n  policy choice, shows depth beyond \"add a tenant label\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Enforces required tenant and feature tags in the gateway or client, propagates them through traces and jobs, and computes per-call cost from a versioned price table.","architect":"Chooses allocation drivers, decides between shared and dedicated pools per tier, and makes per-tenant cost an input to pricing, quotas and isolation design.","fde":"Gives a customer a defensible view of what their usage costs, and spots integration patterns such as oversized uploads that will push them past their contract."},"check":{"q":"Your platform shares one self-hosted model fleet across 40 tenants with continuous batching. How\ndo you allocate the fleet cost, and what do you do with idle capacity?","a":"Exact per-request GPU time is not observable under batching, so I would use a driver that\napproximates causation: tokens per request weighted by phase, since prefill and decode cost differently,\nor measured token-seconds from the engine, recorded per call with tenant and feature tags. Fleet cost is\nsplit proportionally to that driver. Idle and headroom capacity is a policy choice: spread it\nproportionally so tenant totals reconcile to the invoice, or report it as platform overhead so\nutilization stays visible. I would document the choice, reconcile monthly, and track the unattributed\nshare."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should tenant and feature tags be attached at the gateway on every model call rather than reconstructed later?","options":["Providers require tenant IDs in every API request","The call that spends money is often a tool or background step several hops away, and its tenant context is lost if not propagated at call time","Tags reduce token usage by shortening prompts","Logs cannot store more than one identifier per request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Agents, retries and background jobs issue calls far from the originating request. If context is not propagated and recorded when the call is made, the spend ends up unattributed."},{"q":"Allocating a shared GPU fleet by number of requests per tenant tends to do what?","options":["Allocate cost accurately, since every request uses the same GPU time","Undercharge tenants with long documents and overcharge tenants with many short requests","Overcharge tenants who use prompt caching","Make allocations fail to sum to the invoice"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GPU time scales with tokens processed, not request count. A request driver ignores size, so heavy long-context tenants are subsidized by light ones. Totals still reconcile; only fairness suffers."},{"q":"What is the main argument for reporting idle GPU capacity as a separate platform line rather than spreading it?","options":["It makes per-tenant totals sum exactly to the invoice","Tenants are not allowed to be billed for idle capacity","It keeps the cost of over-provisioning visible to the team that controls utilization","It removes the need for an allocation driver"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Spreading idle cost hides it inside every tenant's number. A separate line makes utilization a visible, owned cost; the trade-off is that tenant totals then do not add up to the invoice by themselves."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","sources":["Chen et al., 2023 — FrugalGPT: How to Use Large Language Models While Reducing Cost and Improving Performance"],"why":"- **The usual history.** A prototype ships on the largest model with a long, example-stuffed prompt. Then\n  three things happen at once: the bill grows linearly with traffic, a p95 of several seconds makes the\n  interface feel broken, and the first eval shows the \"best\" model is no better on this task than one an\n  order of magnitude cheaper, because the task saturates.\n- **\"Use the best model for everything\" fails on cost and latency.** Quality is task-relative and\n  plateaus, while cost and latency are paid on every single request.\n- **\"Use the cheapest model\" fails on quality.** A wrong answer has a cost too: a retry, a human\n  escalation, a lost customer. The right denominator is cost per *successful* task, and on that measure the\n  cheap model can be the expensive one.\n- **\"Tune one axis at a time\" is the most common failure.** A team trims the prompt to cut cost, quality\n  quietly drops, and nobody notices until a customer does, because quality was never a number on a\n  dashboard.\n- **The axes are coupled by mechanism, not by accident.** More reasoning tokens buy better answers on hard\n  problems and more decode time and spend; more retrieved context buys grounding and more prefill; a bigger\n  model buys capability and more memory bandwidth per token.\n- **So you need a method, not a hunch.** It holds all three in view, makes the exchange rate explicit, and\n  is re-run whenever a model, price or prompt changes. That is what separates features that scale from\n  features that get switched off when the invoice arrives.","how":"### Make all three measurable\nNothing below works without numbers for each axis on the *same* set of requests.\n- **Quality is a task-specific eval.** A golden set with a pass criterion: exact match for\n  classification, a rubric or LLM judge for open text, human grading for the ambiguous slice. Define it\n  first, *because* every cost or latency cut is a bet that quality survives, and bets need a scoreboard.\n- **Latency is what the user feels.** Time-to-first-token for streamed chat, end-to-end time for a\n  classifier or a tool call, and always a percentile (p95 or p99), not a mean. Means hide the tail, and the\n  tail is what users remember.\n- **Cost is cost per successful task.** Include retries, fallbacks, judge or verifier calls, cache misses\n  and the downstream cost of failures. Per-request cost is what the provider bills; per successful task is\n  what the business pays.\n\n### Where latency and cost actually come from\nDecompose each axis before pulling levers, *because* the decomposition tells you which lever can move it:\n\n```\ncost_per_call ≈ n_in_uncached·p_in + n_in_cached·p_cached + n_out·p_out\nTTFT          ≈ network + queueing + prefill(n_in)\nend_to_end    ≈ TTFT + n_out · TPOT          (TPOT = time per output token)\n```\n\n- **Input tokens are cheap in time.** Prefill processes the whole prompt in parallel.\n- **Output tokens are expensive in time and money.** Decode emits them one at a time, so `n_out · TPOT`\n  usually dominates end-to-end latency, and output tokens are typically priced several times higher than\n  input tokens.\n- **Illustrative numbers.** With a TPOT of 20 ms, a 300-token answer spends about 6 s in decode alone,\n  whatever the prompt length. Cutting the answer to 100 tokens saves about 4 s; halving a 2,000-token prompt\n  saves only a fraction of a second of prefill.\n- **So output caps and terse formats are often the strongest latency lever**, and prompt trimming mainly\n  buys cost and TTFT.\n- **Agents and chains multiply all of it** by the number of sequential calls.\n\n### Cost per success, and when quality *is* cost\n- **With a verifier and retries:** if each attempt succeeds with probability *q*, expected attempts are\n  `1/q`, so `cost_per_success = (c_call + c_verify) / q`.\n- **Caveat:** that assumes independent attempts. In practice the same hard input tends to fail again, so\n  retries recover less than `1/q` predicts.\n- **With human escalation:** `cost_per_task = c_call + (1 − q) · c_human`.\n\n| Model (illustrative) | Call cost | Pass rate | Escalation at $2 | Cost per task |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Small | $0.001 | 86% | 0.14 × 2 | 0.001 + 0.14 × 2 ≈ $0.28 |\n| Large | $0.018 | 94% | 0.06 × 2 | 0.018 + 0.06 × 2 ≈ $0.14 |\n\n- **When failure is expensive,** the quality axis collapses into the cost axis and the \"expensive\" model\n  is the cheap one.\n- **When failure is cheap** (a user rephrases), the axes stay separate and the frontier below matters.\n\n### Know which axis each lever moves\n| Lever | Quality | Latency | Cost | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bigger model tier | up | up | up | coarsest lever; quality gain saturates per task |\n| Shorter prompt / less context | risk | down (prefill) | down | every token is paid on every call |\n| Capped or terser output | neutral if instructed | down (decode) | down | decode is sequential, so output length often dominates total time |\n| Reasoning / thinking budget | up on hard tasks | up a lot | up a lot | wasted on easy tasks; gate by difficulty |\n| Prompt caching (shared prefix) | none | down (TTFT) | down (input) | removes work instead of trading it |\n| Response / semantic cache | none on hits | near zero on hits | near zero on hits | only for repeat queries; staleness risk |\n| Routing / cascade | ≈ large model | up for escalated requests | down on average | depends on escalation rate |\n| Batch / async endpoints | none | up (minutes to hours) | down sharply | for non-interactive work only |\n| Fine-tune / distill small model | up for small model | down | down | front-loaded effort; shifts the frontier |\n| Quantization, speculative decoding | small risk | down | down | serving-side; measure per model |\n| Streaming | none | perceived down | none | changes perception, not the number |\n\n- **Most levers move you *along* the frontier:** you give up some of one axis for another.\n- **A few *shift* the frontier:** caching, distillation, fine-tuning and task redesign, because they\n  remove work or make a cheaper model genuinely better at this task.\n- **When no frontier point satisfies the constraints,** only the second group can help; more tuning of\n  the first group cannot.\n\n### Build the frontier\n1. **Run every candidate configuration** against the same eval set and a replay of real traffic.\n2. **Record the triple** (quality, p95 latency, cost per successful task) for each.\n3. **Discard dominated configurations.** A is *dominated* if some B is at least as good on all three and\n   strictly better on one. What remains is the Pareto frontier.\n4. **Respect measurement noise.** A pass rate on *n* items has a standard error of about `√(q(1−q)/n)`; at\n   q = 0.92 and n = 200 that is roughly 2 points, so 0.92 and 0.93 are a statistical tie.\n5. **Treat configurations inside the noise band as equal on quality.** Compare them on the same items with\n   a paired test, or grow the eval, and let latency and cost decide. A p95 from a few hundred requests is\n   itself noisy.\n\nThe output turns a three-way argument into a short list with a visible exchange rate: \"moving from B to C\nbuys two points of quality for $0.003 per task and 450 ms of p95\". People can argue about whether that\ntrade is worth it; they can no longer argue about facts.\n\n### Choose under constraints\n- **Two axes are constraints, one is the objective.** Either *maximize quality subject to p95 ≤ L and\n  cost ≤ C*, or *minimize cost subject to quality ≥ Q and p95 ≤ L*.\n- **The second form is the common one,** because the quality floor is nearly always a product requirement\n  rather than a tunable.\n- **Pick the feasible frontier point with the best objective.** If the feasible set is empty, stop tuning\n  and reach for a frontier-shifting lever.\n\n### Do it per task, not per application\n- **One application holds many frontiers.** Intent classification saturates on a small model;\n  multi-document synthesis does not.\n- **Split traffic by task and optimize each separately.** It is usually the single largest win, which is\n  why routing comes before most other work.\n\n### Keep measuring\n- **Everything drifts.** Quality drifts when the provider updates a model or the input mix changes; prices\n  fall; traffic mix shifts; a prompt edit makes the model chattier.\n- **Re-run the frontier** on a cadence and on every change to prompt, model or retrieval.\n- **Alert on cost per task and p95 in production,** not just on total spend.","example":"### Frontier step for a triage task\nA small, runnable version of the frontier step. The measurements are illustrative numbers for a\nsupport-ticket triage task, not benchmarks; the structure is what matters.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Config:\n    name: str\n    quality: float        # pass rate on the task's golden set, 0..1\n    p95_ms: int           # end-to-end p95 on a traffic replay\n    cost_per_task: float  # USD per successful task, retries and judge calls included\n\nconfigs = [\n    Config(\"large, long prompt\",     0.94, 3800, 0.0180),\n    Config(\"large, trimmed prompt\",  0.93, 2900, 0.0110),\n    Config(\"small, trimmed prompt\",  0.86,  900, 0.0012),\n    Config(\"small, fine-tuned\",      0.92,  950, 0.0014),\n    Config(\"small -> large cascade\", 0.93, 1400, 0.0038),\n    Config(\"medium, trimmed prompt\", 0.90, 1700, 0.0045),\n]\n\ndef dominated(c, others):  # B dominates A: no worse on all three, strictly better on one\n    return any(o.quality >= c.quality and o.p95_ms <= c.p95_ms\n               and o.cost_per_task <= c.cost_per_task\n               and (o.quality > c.quality or o.p95_ms < c.p95_ms\n                    or o.cost_per_task < c.cost_per_task) for o in others)\n\nfrontier = [c for c in configs if not dominated(c, configs)]\nfeasible = [c for c in frontier if c.quality >= 0.90 and c.p95_ms <= 2000]\nbest = min(feasible, key=lambda c: c.cost_per_task)\nfor c in frontier:\n    print(f\"{c.name:<24} q={c.quality:.2f}  p95={c.p95_ms:>5} ms  ${c.cost_per_task:.4f}/task\")\nprint(\"choose:\", best.name)\n# large, long prompt       q=0.94  p95= 3800 ms  $0.0180/task\n# small, trimmed prompt    q=0.86  p95=  900 ms  $0.0012/task\n# small, fine-tuned        q=0.92  p95=  950 ms  $0.0014/task\n# small -> large cascade   q=0.93  p95= 1400 ms  $0.0038/task\n# choose: small, fine-tuned\n```\n\n- **Two configurations vanish immediately.** The trimmed large model is dominated by the cascade (same\n  quality, faster, cheaper); the medium model is dominated by the fine-tuned small one.\n- **Constraints decide the rest.** Under quality ≥ 0.90 and p95 ≤ 2 s only the fine-tuned small model and\n  the cascade survive, and the fine-tuned model wins on cost.\n- **What the table hides (1): the one-off fine-tuning cost.** It must be amortized over expected volume\n  before the choice is final.\n- **What the table hides (2): the cascade's tail.** Its p95 is driven by the escalated minority, so its\n  p50 is close to the small model's.\n- **The quality gap is noise.** 0.92 vs 0.93 is inside the noise of a few-hundred-item eval, which is\n  exactly why cost, not quality, should break the tie here.\n\n### Worked scenario: does the cascade pay?\nCascade cost per task is `s + e·l` (small cost *s*, large cost *l*, escalation rate *e*), so it beats the\nlarge model alone only while `e < 1 − s/l`. Illustrative prices:\n\n```python\ndef cascade_cost(s, l, e):\n    return s + e * l                 # every request pays s; escalated ones also pay l\n\ndef break_even_escalation(s, l):\n    return 1 - s / l                 # cascade wins while e is below this\n\nfor s, l in [(0.001, 0.010), (0.005, 0.010)]:   # 10x and 2x price gaps\n    print(f\"gap {l/s:.0f}x  break-even e < {break_even_escalation(s, l):.0%}\")\n    for e in (0.1, 0.3, 0.6):\n        c = cascade_cost(s, l, e)\n        print(f\"   e={e:.0%}  cascade ${c:.4f}  vs large ${l:.4f}  -> {'cascade' if c < l else 'large'}\")\n# output, condensed to one line per gap:\n# gap 10x  break-even e < 90%   (e=10%: $0.0020, e=30%: $0.0040, e=60%: $0.0070 -> cascade)\n# gap 2x   break-even e < 50%   (e=10%: $0.0060, e=30%: $0.0080 -> cascade; e=60%: $0.0110 -> large)\n```\n\n| Price gap | Break-even escalation | At 30% escalation | At 60% escalation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 10× | 90% | cascade, $0.0040 vs $0.0100 | cascade, $0.0070 vs $0.0100 |\n| 2× | 50% | cascade, $0.0080 vs $0.0100 | large wins, $0.0110 vs $0.0100 |\n\nThe narrower the price gap, the less escalation the gate can afford, and every escalated request also\npays both models' latency.","patterns":["**Cap output per route.** When: any interactive route, because decode time is `n_out · TPOT`. How: set the API's maximum output tokens (`max_tokens` on Anthropic's Messages API) per route and instruct a terse format. Monitor output-token p95 and the share of responses stopped for length.","**Stable prefix plus prompt caching.** When: many calls share a long system prompt or tool list. How: put stable content first and variable content last; on Anthropic mark the prefix with `cache_control`, while OpenAI caches long shared prefixes automatically. Monitor cached input tokens per call and TTFT.","**Small-first cascade with a gate.** When: traffic mixes easy and hard inputs. How: answer with the small model, escalate on a verifier failure or low confidence. Monitor escalation rate against `1 − s/l` and the p95 of escalated requests separately.","**Batch path for offline work.** When: nobody waits on the result (backfills, nightly enrichment). How: send it through the provider's batch endpoint, which trades a completion window of hours for a lower price. Monitor batch completion time against the downstream deadline.","**Three lines per feature.** When: always, once a feature has real traffic. How: tag every call with a feature id and plot quality on a sampled eval, p95, and cost per successful task. Alert on bands, not on the monthly total.","**Eval gate on every change.** When: any prompt, model or retrieval change. How: re-run the golden set in CI with a paired comparison on the same items, and block the merge if quality falls under the floor or p95 and cost per task leave their band."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    E[\"Define eval and quality floor\"] --> M[\"Measure quality, p95, cost per success\"]\n    M --> D[\"Discard dominated configs\"]\n    D --> F[\"Pareto frontier\"]\n    F --> C{\"Any point meets constraints?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes\"| P[\"Pick best objective\"]\n    C -->|\"no\"| S[\"Shift frontier: cache, distill, redesign\"]\n    S --> M\n    P --> R[\"Monitor in production\"]\n    R -->|\"model, price or prompt change\"| M","caption":"Read it as a loop: measure the triple, prune to the frontier, filter by constraints, and either ship a\npoint or reach for a lever that shifts the frontier."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the Pareto frontier in quality, latency and cost, and why not just pick the best model?","a":"- **Definition:** the set of configurations that no other configuration beats on all three axes at once;\n  everything else is dominated and can be discarded without argument.\n- **Why not the best model:** quality is task-relative and saturates, while cost and latency are paid on\n  every request. The best-scoring model is often tied, within eval noise, with one far cheaper and faster."},{"q":"A chat feature has a 6 s p95. How do you find which lever to pull?","a":"- **Decompose first:** end-to-end is TTFT plus `n_out · TPOT`, and TTFT is network, queueing and prefill.\n  Log TTFT, output tokens and TPOT per request.\n- **If decode dominates** (for example 300 output tokens at 20 ms each is about 6 s), cap output and ask\n  for a terser format; trimming the prompt would barely help.\n- **If TTFT dominates,** look at queueing and prompt length, and add prompt caching for the shared prefix."},{"q":"When would a small-first cascade be a bad idea?","a":"- **When escalation is high:** it wins on cost only while `e < 1 − s/l`; at a 2× price gap that is just\n  50%, and above it every escalated request pays for both models.\n- **When the tail matters:** escalated requests pay both latencies, so p95 rises even when average cost\n  falls.\n- **When the gate is unreliable:** a confidence signal that misses wrong answers silently lowers quality,\n  so the gate needs its own eval."},{"q":"Two configurations score 0.92 and 0.93 on a 200-item eval. The 0.93 one costs three times more. Which do you ship?","a":"- **They are tied on quality:** the standard error is about `√(q(1−q)/n)`, roughly 2 points at q = 0.92 and\n  n = 200.\n- **So cost and latency decide,** and the cheaper one ships unless a paired test on the same items, or a\n  larger eval, shows a real gap.\n- **State the exchange rate** to the product owner, so the decision is about whether a possible point is\n  worth 3× the spend."},{"q":"How do you account for a fine-tuned small model whose per-task cost looks tiny?","a":"- **Add the one-off cost:** data preparation, training runs, evals and the engineering time, amortized over\n  expected volume in the payback period.\n- **Add the recurring cost:** it must be redone when the task or base model changes, so plan for repeats.\n- **Then compare cost per successful task,** including any human escalation, against the current point on\n  the frontier at realistic monthly volume."}],"tradeoffs":["**Cascade: lower average cost vs higher tail latency.** It also adds a confidence gate to maintain. With small-model cost *s*, large-model cost *l* and escalation rate *e*, it beats the large model alone only while `s + e·l < l`, i.e. while `e < 1 − s/l`. At a 10× price gap that is 90%; at 2× it is only 50%.","**Trimmed context: cheaper and faster TTFT vs quality risk.** Removing context and few-shot examples lowers cost and TTFT on every call, but each removed token is a bet it was not needed. The trade flips the moment the eval shows quality under the floor.","**Reasoning budget: quality on hard inputs vs waste on easy ones.** It is favourable only when gated by task difficulty rather than applied uniformly.","**Fine-tuning or distillation: a shifted frontier vs front-loaded effort.** The engineering must be repeated when the task or base model changes. It pays only when monthly savings exceed the amortized effort, which is a volume threshold.","**Response caching: near-zero cost and latency on hits vs staleness.** It is worthless on long-tail inputs and harmful when answers must reflect fresh data; the trade flips with hit rate and staleness tolerance.","**Batch endpoints: much lower cost vs minutes-to-hours latency.** Good for offline work; the trade flips the instant a user is waiting on the result."],"pitfalls":["**Optimizing cost with no quality metric in place.** Symptom: a falling bill followed weeks later by rising escalation and complaint rates that nobody connects to the change. Fix: define the eval and floor first.","**Reporting mean latency.** Symptom: a green dashboard while users report hangs, because the p95 nobody plotted is three times the mean. Fix: plot p95 and p99.","**Comparing cost per request instead of per successful task.** Symptom: a \"cheaper\" model whose retries, judge calls and human review make the total higher. Fix: divide total spend, including verifier and escalation cost, by tasks that passed.","**Evaluating on a handful of hand-picked prompts.** Symptom: a configuration that shines in the demo and degrades on the real traffic distribution. Fix: add a traffic replay.","**Reading a one- or two-point eval difference as a real win.** Symptom: the \"winning\" configuration swaps places with the runner-up every time the eval is re-run or re-sampled. Fix: paired test or a bigger eval.","**Leaving output length unconstrained.** Symptom: cost and decode latency creep up after a prompt or model change made the model chattier. Fix: a per-route output cap and an alert on output-token p95.","**Treating the frontier as static.** Symptom: a once-optimal setup became dominated after a provider price cut or model update that nobody re-tested. Fix: re-run the frontier on a cadence and on every model, price or prompt change."],"when":"- **Use when** a feature first approaches production scale, whenever a new model or price list appears,\n  and whenever quality, p95 or cost per task moves outside its band in monitoring.\n- **Skip the full exercise** for throwaway prototypes and low-volume internal tools, where the cost of\n  measuring exceeds the cost of being wrong. Still write down the quality floor so it exists when volume\n  arrives.\n- **Day to day:** a dashboard with three lines per feature (quality on a sampled eval, p95, cost per\n  successful task) and a habit of re-running the eval on every prompt, model or retrieval change.\n- **Interviews:** name all three axes unprompted, insist on cost per successful task, separate\n  frontier-moving levers from frontier-shifting ones, and derive the cascade break-even condition from the\n  escalation rate.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns the eval harness, the traffic replay and the per-config measurement; implements routing, caching and output caps; keeps max_tokens, prompt versions and model ids under version control.","architect":"Sets the quality floor and latency budget per feature, decides which levers are allowed (cascade, distillation, batch paths) and signs off the frontier point the product will run on.","fde":"Rebuilds the frontier inside the customer's constraints, where the eval set is their data, the latency budget is their workflow and the cost ceiling is their contract margin, then defends the choice."},"check":{"q":"A feature runs on a large model at p95 of 4 s, $0.02 per task and 94% on its eval. Product wants\np95 under 2 s and cost under $0.005 per task while keeping quality at 90% or better. Which levers do you\ntry, in what order, and how do you know when you are done?","a":"Start with the levers that change the least: trim the prompt, cap and tighten the output, add\nprompt caching for the shared prefix, and re-measure all three numbers on the full eval and a traffic\nreplay. If that does not clear both constraints, route: a small model first with a confidence or verifier\ngate, escalating to the large model, and compute expected cost as small cost plus escalation rate times\nlarge cost, while checking that the escalated requests do not blow the p95. If escalation stays high, the\nfrontier itself must move: distill the large model's outputs into a fine-tuned small model and amortize\nthat effort against monthly savings at the expected volume. Done means a configuration on the frontier\nsatisfies all three constraints on the eval and the replay, with production monitoring of p95 and cost per\nsuccessful task so a regression is caught in days rather than at the next invoice."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which lever shifts the quality–latency–cost frontier rather than moving along it?","options":["Switching to a larger model tier","Distilling the large model's behaviour into a fine-tuned small model","Raising max_tokens so answers are more complete","Adding more few-shot examples to the prompt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Model tier, output length and prompt length trade one axis for another and stay on the existing frontier. Distillation makes a cheap, fast model genuinely better at this task, so it improves quality without paying in latency or cost: the frontier itself moves."},{"q":"Why is cost per successful task the right cost measure rather than cost per request?","options":["Because providers bill per task rather than per token","Because it includes retries, fallbacks, judge calls and the cost of failed outputs, which per-request cost hides","Because it is easier to compute from the invoice","Because latency is also measured per task"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A cheap model that fails more often incurs retries, verifier calls and human handling; per-request cost makes it look like a win while the business pays more. Per successful task counts what the outcome actually cost."},{"q":"A small model costs $0.001 per task and a large one $0.010. Below what escalation rate does a small-first cascade beat sending everything to the large model?","options":["10%","50%","90%","It always beats it, at any escalation rate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cascade cost is 0.001 + e × 0.010, which is below 0.010 only while e < 0.9. Above that, nearly every request pays for both models and the cascade is strictly worse on cost and on tail latency."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","sources":[],"why":"- **The question arrives with the first five-figure API invoice:** \"would it not be cheaper to run this\n  ourselves?\" The back-of-envelope answer divides a GPU's hourly price by its benchmark tokens per second\n  times 3,600, gets a cost per million tokens far below the API's list price, and concludes self-hosting is\n  several times cheaper.\n- **Wrong assumption 1: the GPU is saturated 24 hours a day.** Real interactive traffic is diurnal and\n  bursty. You provision for the peak with headroom to protect latency, so average utilization is a\n  fraction, and the effective cost per token is the list cost divided by that fraction.\n- **Wrong assumption 2: the GPU is the biggest line.** It is only the visible one. Someone has to build and\n  run the serving stack, carry the pager, upgrade the model and prove on your evals that an open-weight\n  model matches the hosted one.\n- **Wrong assumption 3: the comparison is like for like.** It compares a hosted frontier model against an\n  open-weight model, at different quality.\n- **\"Never self-host, it is a distraction\" is equally lazy.** At sustained high volume the fixed cost\n  amortizes until the marginal token is nearly free, and data residency, burst rate limits, latency control\n  or a model the vendor does not offer can decide it alone.\n- **What the analysis buys:** a curve and a sensitivity band in place of both reflexes. Most teams cross\n  break-even much later than per-token arithmetic suggests, and an API price cut can push the crossover to\n  infinity.","how":"### Two cost curves\nWrite monthly cost as a function of monthly volume *V* in tokens.\n\n```\nCost_api(V)  = p_in · V_in + p_out · V_out          ≈ p · V   for a stable input/output mix\nCost_self(V) = F + N(V) · G\n```\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `p` | blended price per token at *your* mix; output tokens usually cost several times input tokens, so blend per feature, not per company |\n| `F` | fixed monthly cost that does not scale with volume: people, platform tooling, eval upkeep |\n| `G` | monthly cost of one GPU replica |\n| `N(V)` | replicas needed to serve *V*; a step function, because you cannot buy a third of a GPU |\n\n### Replica count comes from the peak, not the average\n```\navg_rate  = V / seconds_per_month\npeak_rate = avg_rate · peak_to_average · (1 + headroom)\nN(V)      = ceil(peak_rate / T_slo)\n```\n\n- **`T_slo` is throughput at your latency target.** It is the sustained tokens per second one replica\n  delivers on your sequence lengths, not the throughput at maximum batch, because continuous batching\n  trades per-request latency for aggregate throughput: the batch size that maximizes tokens per second\n  gives a p95 nobody would ship.\n- **Measure it for your request *shape*** (typical input and output lengths), not as a generic\n  tokens-per-second figure, *because* a prompt token is processed in parallel during prefill and costs far\n  less GPU time than an output token generated one at a time in decode.\n- **The API's input/output price ratio is not your GPU's cost ratio,** so blend both sides on the same mix.\n- **Peak-to-average comes from your traffic logs.** Headroom covers failover and the fact that a replica\n  at 100% load is already past its latency target.\n\n### Utilization is the single most important number\n- **Achieved utilization** is `u = avg_rate / (N · T_slo)`, roughly `1 / (peak_to_average · (1 + headroom))`\n  once N is large.\n- **Interactive traffic** with a peak-to-average of 2–3 and 20% headroom lands around a third.\n- **Batch or offline workloads** can be scheduled flat and push *u* toward one, which is why self-hosting\n  pays off far earlier for them.\n\n### Effective cost per token and the sign test\n```\nc_self = G / (T_slo · seconds_per_month · u)\n```\n\n1. **Compare `c_self` to `p` before anything else.**\n2. **If `c_self > p`** at the utilization you can realistically achieve, there is no break-even at any\n   volume: fixed costs only widen the gap.\n3. **If `c_self < p`,** the continuous approximation of the crossover is `V* ≈ F / (p − c_self)`; the step\n   function makes the real crossover slightly later.\n4. **An API price cut attacks the sign test.** Halve `p` and a workload comfortably past break-even can be\n   on the wrong side of it permanently.\n\n### Payback on the switch\n- **Moving is a one-off cost `M`:** building the serving path, evaluating and possibly fine-tuning the\n  open-weight model, load testing, migrating traffic.\n- **Even past break-even,** the switch pays back only after `M / (Cost_api − Cost_self)` months.\n- **Compare with the horizon.** Put payback next to the reservation term and the expected life of the\n  model choice: a 14-month payback on a decision you expect to revisit within a year is a loss.\n\n### What goes into G and F\n| Line | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| `G` (cloud) | accelerator plus its share of the node (CPU, memory, local disk, network), ingress and load balancing; on-demand vs one- or three-year reserved pricing differs a lot |\n| `G` (on-prem) | adds amortized hardware, power, cooling, space and replacement of failed parts |\n| `F` | fraction of platform engineers and SREs, on-call rotation, serving-stack upgrades, security and compliance work, observability tooling, recurring eval and fine-tuning work to keep quality parity each time a new open-weight or hosted model appears |\n\n`F` is where most \"it would be cheaper\" spreadsheets go wrong, because it is the line nobody wants to\nwrite down.\n\n### Quality parity is a cost, not an assumption\n- **If the open-weight model scores below the hosted one on your evals,** either pay to close the gap\n  (fine-tuning: a one-off cost plus a recurring maintenance line in `F`) or accept lower quality and price\n  it through cost per successful task.\n- **Either way, compare at equal quality** or convert the gap into money; comparing at unequal quality is\n  comparing nothing.\n\n### Sensitivity, not a point\n- **Vary utilization** between the batch-flat and interactive-bursty cases.\n- **Vary `p`** across list and committed-use pricing and a plausible future price cut.\n- **Vary `G`** between on-demand and reserved.\n- **Time matters.** A reservation locks `G` for its term, while hosted prices have tended to fall and hosted\n  models to improve, so a marginal decision today is a bet on the next twelve months of price movement.\n\n### Non-cost factors that can decide it alone\n- **The list:** regulated data that cannot leave a region or a building; rate limits and quotas that cap\n  bursts during your peak; latency control through co-location; a specific open-weight or custom\n  fine-tuned model the vendor does not serve; deprecation and lock-in risk.\n- **Run the arithmetic anyway,** so the premium being paid for the constraint is known and owned.\n\n### Separate the model choice from the operating model\n- **\"Hosted API versus self-hosting\" bundles two decisions:** which model (proprietary or open-weight) and\n  who operates it. They can be unbundled.\n- **Per-token open-weight endpoints:** several providers serve open-weight models per token, giving the\n  open model's price and portability with no `F` for serving.\n- **Provisioned capacity:** some vendors sell dedicated capacity for a fixed fee, giving fixed cost and\n  guaranteed throughput without running the stack.\n- **Price these as separate rows,** *because* the self-hosting case often rests on the model being cheaper,\n  not on operating it yourself, and a managed endpoint captures that saving without the fixed costs.\n\n### The usual answer is hybrid\n- **By load:** self-host the predictable base load, where utilization is high and fixed cost amortizes\n  well; send bursts and rare high-complexity requests to the API, which prices variable demand well.\n- **By task:** high-volume simple tasks on a self-hosted small model, frontier-quality tasks on the API.\n- **Why it wins:** it puts each cost structure on the traffic shape it is built for, at the price of two\n  serving paths and two quality baselines.","example":"### Break-even calculator with three scenarios\nA runnable version of the model above, with every number illustrative. The output shows the curves crossing\nand, more importantly, how the crossover moves when one assumption changes.\n\n```python\nimport math\nSEC_MONTH = 30 * 24 * 3600\n\ndef api_cost(tokens_m, price_per_m):\n    return tokens_m * price_per_m\n\ndef self_host_cost(tokens_m, gpu_month, tok_per_s_at_slo, peak_to_avg, headroom, fixed):\n    avg_rate = tokens_m * 1e6 / SEC_MONTH                 # tokens/s averaged over the month\n    peak_rate = avg_rate * peak_to_avg * (1 + headroom)   # what you must provision for\n    replicas = max(1, math.ceil(peak_rate / tok_per_s_at_slo))  # cannot buy 0.3 of a GPU\n    return fixed + replicas * gpu_month, replicas\n\n# Illustrative: blended API price $2.00/M tokens; $3,000 per GPU replica-month (reserved);\n# 2,000 tok/s per replica at the latency target; $16,000/month of people and tooling.\nfor label, price, p2a in ((\"interactive\", 2.00, 2.5), (\"flat batch\", 2.00, 1.2), (\"API cut\", 1.00, 2.5)):\n    print(label)\n    for v in (5_000, 20_000, 50_000, 100_000):            # million tokens per month\n        api = api_cost(v, price)\n        sh, n = self_host_cost(v, 3_000, 2_000, p2a, 0.2, 16_000)\n        print(f\"  {v:>7}M  api=${api:>8,.0f}  self=${sh:>8,.0f} ({n:>2} GPUs)  {'self' if sh < api else 'api'} wins\")\n# interactive:  5,000M api=$10,000 self=$25,000 (3)  20,000M api=$40,000 self=$52,000 (12)\n#               50,000M api=$100,000 self=$103,000 (29)  100,000M api=$200,000 self=$190,000 (58)\n# flat batch:   20,000M api=$40,000 self=$34,000 (6) -> self already wins; 50,000M self=$58,000\n# API cut:      100,000M api=$100,000 self=$190,000 (58) -> api wins at every volume shown\n```\n\nRead the three blocks together, starting with the sign test:\n\n| Step | Arithmetic | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| One replica at full load | 2,000 × 2,592,000 ≈ 5.2 billion tokens a month | `c_self` = $3,000 / 5,184M ≈ $0.58 per million at u = 1 |\n| Interactive utilization | u ≈ 1 / (2.5 × 1.2) = 1/3 | `c_self` ≈ $1.74, below the $2 price but only just |\n| Interactive crossover | `V* ≈ 16,000 / (2.00 − 1.74)` | ≈ 62 billion tokens a month |\n| Flat load | u ≈ 0.69 | `c_self` ≈ $0.83; crossover about 14 billion |\n| API price halved | $1.74 is above $1.00 | sign test fails; no volume rescues it |\n\n- **The table agrees with the formula:** self-hosting does not win until somewhere past 50 billion tokens a\n  month, an API bill around $100,000.\n- **Flat load** lets the same GPUs serve roughly twice the tokens.\n- **The GPU price never changed** across the three runs; utilization and the competitor's price did all\n  the work.\n\n### Worked scenario: payback on the switch\nSame illustrative inputs, plus a one-off migration cost `M` of $140,000 (serving path, evals, load tests,\ntraffic migration).\n\n| Case at volume | API per month | Self-host per month | Monthly saving | Payback = M / saving |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Interactive, 100,000M | $200,000 | $190,000 | $10,000 | 14 months |\n| Flat batch, 50,000M | $100,000 | $58,000 | $42,000 | about 3.3 months |\n| Flat batch, 20,000M | $40,000 | $34,000 | $6,000 | about 23 months |\n\n- **The interactive case is past break-even and still a loss** if the model choice will be revisited\n  within a year: the 14-month payback outlives the decision.\n- **The flat 50,000M case pays back in just over a quarter,** so the migration cost is recovered well\n  before a typical model or reservation decision is revisited. It is not immune: a halved API price makes\n  the API $50,000 at this volume against $58,000 self-hosted.\n- **Just past break-even is fragile:** a small monthly saving turns a one-off cost into years of payback.","patterns":["**Measure `T_slo` with a shaped load test.** When: before any GPU commitment. How: replay your real input/output length mix at rising concurrency against the candidate server (vLLM ships a serving benchmark), and record the highest throughput where p95 TTFT and time per output token still meet the target. Monitor the same latencies in production.","**Derive peak-to-average from logs, not intuition.** When: building `N(V)`. How: take per-minute token counts over several weeks and divide a high percentile of the per-minute rate by the mean. Re-check it monthly, because launches and new tenants change the shape.","**Reserve only the base load.** When: you do self-host. How: size reserved replicas to the overnight floor of the daily curve and serve the rest on demand or via the API. Monitor reserved-replica utilization; if it sits low, the reservation is too big.","**Spillover routing in the gateway.** When: running hybrid. How: send traffic to the self-hosted pool until queue depth or p95 crosses a threshold, then route the overflow to the hosted API. Monitor the spill fraction and the self-hosted utilization together.","**Price a managed open-weight endpoint as a control row.** When: the case rests on a cheaper open model. How: run the same evals and price the same model per token on a hosting provider. If it captures most of the saving, there is no `F` to pay.","**Keep the calculator live.** When: always after the decision. How: store it in the repo with inputs fed from production metrics and re-run it on every provider price change and each quarter. Alert when achieved utilization falls below the assumption the decision used."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Non-cost driver: residency, quota, model?\"] -->|\"yes\"| B[\"Decide on it, price the premium\"]\n    A -->|\"no\"| C[\"Measure T_slo and peak-to-average\"]\n    C --> D[\"Compute u and c_self\"]\n    D --> E{\"c_self below p?\"}\n    E -->|\"no\"| F[\"Stay on API or managed endpoint\"]\n    E -->|\"yes\"| G[\"V* = F / (p - c_self)\"]\n    G --> H{\"Volume above V*?\"}\n    H -->|\"no\"| F\n    H -->|\"yes\"| I{\"Payback within horizon?\"}\n    I -->|\"no\"| F\n    I -->|\"yes\"| J[\"Self-host base load, API for bursts\"]","caption":"Follow the decision path: non-cost constraints first, then the sign test, then the crossover volume, then\npayback against the commitment horizon."},"interview":[{"q":"Why does utilization matter more than the GPU's hourly price in a break-even analysis?","a":"- **Effective cost per token is `G / (T_slo · seconds_per_month · u)`,** so cost per token scales with\n  1/u. Going from u = 1 to u = 1/3 triples it, a bigger swing than typical price gaps between comparable\n  GPU offers.\n- **Interactive traffic forces low u,** because you provision for the peak with headroom; flat batch work\n  can run near full, which is why it crosses break-even far earlier."},{"q":"A self-hosted pilot costs three times per token what the spreadsheet promised. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check how `T_slo` was measured:** a max-batch benchmark overstates throughput at the latency target.\n- **Check the request shape:** decode-heavy production traffic delivers far fewer tokens per second than a\n  prefill-heavy benchmark.\n- **Check utilization:** compare provisioned replicas with average load; peak-to-average and headroom often\n  explain most of the gap.\n- **Check `F`:** on-call, upgrades and eval work may have been left out of the spreadsheet entirely."},{"q":"When would you self-host even though the arithmetic says the API is cheaper?","a":"- **When a non-cost driver decides it:** data that cannot leave a region or building, quotas that cap your\n  peak, co-location for latency, or a custom fine-tuned model the vendor will not serve.\n- **Still run the numbers,** so the premium being paid for the constraint is explicit and owned.\n- **Check the middle options first:** a managed open-weight endpoint or provisioned capacity may satisfy\n  the constraint without running the stack."},{"q":"One replica does 2,000 tokens per second at SLO for $3,000 a month, and the API charges $2 per million tokens. What utilization do you need just to pass the sign test?","a":"- **At full load** the replica serves about 5.2 billion tokens a month, so `c_self` ≈ $0.58 per million at\n  u = 1.\n- **The sign test needs `c_self` < $2,** so u must exceed about 0.58 / 2 ≈ 0.29.\n- **Interactive traffic at about 1/3 barely clears it,** so `F` makes the crossover huge (about 62 billion\n  tokens a month with $16,000 of fixed cost), and a price cut to $1 would fail the test outright."},{"q":"The provider cut prices by half six months after you moved to self-hosting. What now?","a":"- **Re-run the sign test at the new `p`:** if `c_self` is now above it, there is no break-even at any\n  volume, and extra traffic will not fix it.\n- **Separate sunk from avoidable cost:** the migration cost is spent, but reserved terms, people and\n  upgrades still recur, so compare forward-looking cost only.\n- **Shrink toward hybrid:** keep only the flattest base load on owned capacity, let reservations lapse at\n  term, and route the rest back to the API."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fixed commitments vs variable spend.** Self-hosting wins when load is high and flat and loses when load is small or spiky; the flip is governed by achievable utilization, not by GPU price.","**Reserved pricing vs flexibility.** It cuts `G` substantially but locks a term; if traffic falls or a better hosted model appears you keep paying for idle capacity. Only worth it with confidence in a year or more of demand.","**Hosted API: frontier quality, zero platform work vs vendor control.** Prices, quotas, regions and deprecations are the vendor's decisions; the trade flips when a quota or residency rule blocks you outright.","**Hybrid serving: best cost per traffic shape vs double surface area.** Two serving paths, two eval baselines and a routing layer that must keep them consistent.","**Open-weight quality parity vs recurring fine-tuning.** Matching hosted quality often needs fine-tuning; that cost is front-loaded and recurs with each base-model generation, which favours the API when model turnover is fast."],"pitfalls":["**Using benchmark peak throughput for capacity.** Symptom: a plan for 20 GPUs becomes 60 once p95 latency targets are enforced in load tests. Fix: measure `T_slo`.","**Omitting people and platform cost.** Symptom: a GPU bill below the API bill and a total cost above it, discovered when the headcount request lands. Fix: write `F` down as FTE fractions at loaded cost.","**Comparing against API list price when committed-use discounts exist.** Symptom: a break-even computed against a price nobody at your volume actually pays. Fix: get a committed-use quote and use it as `p`.","**Treating the crossover as permanent.** Symptom: a decision that looked right at one price per million tokens looks wrong after the next provider price cut. Fix: re-run the sign test on every price change.","**Ignoring the input/output mix.** Symptom: decode-heavy traffic achieves far less than the measured throughput, because decode is bandwidth-bound while the benchmark was prefill-heavy. Fix: load-test with your real length mix.","**Framing it as \"frontier API versus our own GPUs\".** Symptom: a self-hosting project whose whole saving came from the cheaper model, which a managed per-token open-weight endpoint would have delivered without the platform team.","**Arguing cost when the real driver is residency or quotas.** Symptom: weeks of spreadsheet debate while the compliance blocker that actually decides the question goes unaddressed."],"when":"- **Use when** monthly API spend becomes material (a useful threshold: it approaches the fully loaded cost\n  of one engineer), and again whenever a non-cost driver appears: a residency requirement, a quota ceiling\n  during peaks, a vendor deprecation notice.\n- **Collect inputs first:** tokens per month by feature with the input/output split, peak-to-average from\n  logs, the latency target, throughput per replica at that target on a candidate open-weight model with\n  your sequence lengths, the quality delta on your evals, reserved GPU quotes, and a realistic people plan.\n- **Output:** a break-even band under sensitivity and a recommendation that names the premium being paid\n  for whichever side is chosen.\n- **Interviews:** the structural answer is expected: fixed versus variable cost, the utilization-driven\n  effective cost per token, the sign test, and the hybrid shape.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Measures throughput at the latency target on real sequence lengths, extracts the peak-to-average ratio from traffic logs, and keeps the calculator honest with production numbers.","architect":"Owns the decision and its band: sets the utilization assumption, the reservation term, the quality-parity requirement and the hybrid split, and records the non-cost drivers that override the arithmetic.","fde":"Rebuilds the break-even with the customer's volumes, their cloud pricing and their compliance constraints, and explains why their \"GPUs are cheaper\" slide omitted the people and utilization lines."},"check":{"q":"Your API bill is $40,000 a month at a blended $2 per million tokens, so 20 billion tokens a month.\nAn engineer says one GPU does 2,000 tokens per second, so four GPUs at $3,000 a month, $12,000 in total,\nwould replace it. What is wrong with that, and what is the right calculation?","a":"Twenty billion tokens a month is roughly 7,700 tokens per second on average, and four GPUs at\n2,000 tokens per second cover that only if they are saturated every second of the month with no headroom.\nWith a peak-to-average ratio of 2.5 and 20% headroom you must provision about 23,000 tokens per second,\nwhich is twelve replicas, $36,000, before adding the platform engineer, on-call and eval upkeep that make\nit about $52,000, above the $40,000 API bill. The 2,000 figure must also be re-measured at the latency\ntarget on your sequence lengths rather than at maximum batch, and the open-weight model must match the\nhosted one on your evals or the gap must be priced. Then run the sensitivity: a flat batch workload drops\nthe replica count to around six and self-hosting wins, while a committed-use API discount to $1.50 per\nmillion makes the API $30,000 and widens its lead. The honest recommendation is to measure peak-to-average\nand throughput at SLO first, and to consider a hybrid that self-hosts the flat base load and sends bursts\nto the API."},"quiz":[{"q":"For a chosen model and serving stack, which parameter most changes the self-hosted cost per token?","options":["The GPU's hourly list price","The utilization actually achieved after provisioning for peak with headroom","The maximum-batch throughput in the vendor's published benchmark","The network egress price of the cloud region"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Effective cost per token is the replica cost divided by the tokens it actually serves. Provisioning for a bursty peak can leave two-thirds or more of the capacity idle, which typically moves the cost per token more than price differences between comparable GPU offers; a vendor benchmark changes no real cost, and egress is small for text tokens."},{"q":"At realistic utilization the self-hosted effective cost per token comes out above the API's price per token. What does that imply?","options":["Break-even will be reached at a high enough volume","There is no break-even at any volume, because the fixed costs only widen the gap","Break-even is reached at a low volume","More GPUs should be added to lower the cost per token"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If the variable part alone is more expensive than the API, adding fixed costs for people and platform makes self-hosting worse at every volume. Only a change in utilization, GPU price or API price can restore a crossover."},{"q":"Why is a hybrid, self-hosting the base load and sending bursts to the API, so often the cheapest shape?","options":["Hosted APIs are always cheaper per token than any self-hosted setup","Fixed capacity amortizes best on flat, predictable load, while bursts are exactly what per-token pricing handles well","It removes the need to evaluate the open-weight model","Self-hosted GPUs cannot technically serve bursty traffic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each cost structure is matched to the traffic shape it suits: the reserved GPUs run near full utilization on the steady base, and the API absorbs the spiky remainder without idle capacity being paid for. The price is two serving paths to keep consistent."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/finops","sources":["Official docs: FinOps Foundation (FinOps Framework)","Storment & Fuller, 2019 — Cloud FinOps"],"why":"- **Cloud broke purchase-order budgeting.** Traditional budgets assume finance approves spend in advance;\n  in the cloud a line in a config file decides spend, and the invoice arrives a month later.\n- **LLM APIs break it harder.** Spend is per token, so a prompt edit, a retrieval change that doubles\n  context, a retrying agent loop or a traffic spike can move the bill by a multiple with no procurement\n  event and no code reviewer thinking about money.\n- **The invoice says nothing useful.** It is a single line, \"API usage\", with no product, customer, feature\n  or team. Finance cannot allocate it, engineering gets no feedback, and the two sides meet only when the\n  number is already alarming.\n- **The naive responses each fail.** Approval gates for every model or key slow delivery and get bypassed\n  with personal keys, worsening attribution. A one-time \"cost project\" saves a slice, then the bill climbs\n  back because nothing structural changed. Hard spend caps protect the budget by taking the feature down\n  for customers at month end.\n- **What works is what worked for latency and reliability:** timely, attributed data in front of the\n  people making decisions, clear ownership, and a loop that runs every week rather than once a year. Cost\n  becomes an engineering signal like latency rather than a quarterly surprise.","how":"### The loop: inform, optimize, operate\nThe FinOps Foundation frames the practice as a cycle:\n\n| Phase | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| *Inform* | see and allocate spend accurately and quickly |\n| *Optimize* | act on it, through both usage and rate levers |\n| *Operate* | make the first two continuous: budgets, forecasts, reviews, owners and policy |\n\nIt is a loop *because* costs drift: models are updated, prices change, features ship weekly and traffic\nmix moves. A snapshot goes stale in a month.\n\n### Inform: meter at the point of call\n1. **Choose one chokepoint.** Provider invoices lack your context, so attribution happens in your own\n   code, at the single point every call passes through: an AI gateway or an SDK wrapper.\n2. **Record one event per call:** model; input, output and cached token counts; latency; tenant; feature;\n   environment; prompt version; and a request id that joins to your traces.\n3. **Price at record time from a versioned price table,** and store the price used, *because* prices change\n   and a cost history that silently re-prices itself is useless for trend analysis.\n4. **Take token counts from the provider's response usage fields,** not your own tokenizer estimate,\n   *because* tokenizers differ between models and estimates drift.\n5. **Send events to the warehouse** where every other business metric lives.\n6. **Reconcile monthly against the invoice.** The gap is a data-quality KPI; a growing one means a call\n   path is bypassing the gateway or a price in the table is stale.\n7. **Normalize billing data** once GPU, API and SaaS spend must be read together; that is what the FinOps\n   Foundation's FOCUS specification is for.\n\n### Keys, tags, showback and chargeback\n- **The root cause of unattributable spend is one shared API key per company.** The gateway is the\n  structural fix.\n- **Per-team or per-feature keys plus mandatory tags** make unallocated spend visible as a bucket to drive\n  toward zero.\n- **Start with *showback*:** show each team its own spend.\n- **Move to *chargeback*** (actually debiting internal budgets) only once allocation is trusted; chargeback\n  on shaky numbers produces arguments about the numbers instead of decisions about the spend.\n\n### Shared costs: allocate by a driver, not evenly\nNot every dollar has a natural tag: a self-hosted GPU pool serves many features; an embedding index, an\neval suite and the platform team serve everyone; a one-year reservation is paid upfront.\n\n| Shared cost | Allocation driver |\n|---|---|\n| Self-hosted GPU pool | GPU-seconds or tokens served per feature; decode tokens weigh more than prefill tokens, because decode generates them one at a time |\n| Reservations | amortized monthly over the term, not booked in the month they were paid |\n| Platform team and tooling | a stated proxy such as share of requests |\n\n- **Splitting evenly is the tempting shortcut and it is wrong,** *because* it subsidizes the heavy users\n  with the light ones, hiding exactly the unprofitable tenant the exercise was meant to find.\n- **Write the rule down.** Disputes about allocation are disputes about the rule, and a written rule ends\n  them.\n\n### Unit economics: telling growth from waste\n- **Total spend is the wrong headline.** Ask whether cost per unit of value moved: cost per successful task,\n  per active user, per document processed, gross margin per tenant.\n- **Growth vs regression:** a bill that doubled while cost per task stayed flat is growth; a bill that\n  doubled on flat volume is a regression.\n- **Only the unit view distinguishes them,** and only the unit view lets product price the feature.\n\n### Optimize: usage levers and rate levers are different jobs\n| | Usage optimization | Rate optimization |\n|---|---|---|\n| Goal | consume fewer units | pay less per unit |\n| Levers | prompt trimming, prompt and response caching, routing cheap models first, output caps, step and token budgets on agents, retiring idle GPU replicas | committed-use and provisioned throughput discounts, reserved GPUs, negotiated enterprise pricing, moving offline work to discounted batch endpoints (same tokens, lower price) |\n| Owner | engineering | procurement |\n\nKeep them separate, because they belong to different people, move on different timescales, and mixing\nthem hides which one is actually working.\n\n### Operate: budgets, anomalies, forecasts, owners\n- **Budget per feature and team, alerting on *forecast*** (month-to-date burn rate projected to month end),\n  not just on the threshold, because by the time a threshold fires the money is spent.\n- **Forecast from the *recent* daily rate too** (say, the trailing three days), because a step change on\n  day eight is diluted by seven normal days in the month-to-date average and shows in full in the recent\n  rate.\n- **Detect anomalies daily, per dimension.** A feature whose tokens per task tripled overnight is a bug, not\n  a trend.\n- **Name an owner** who reviews each cost centre weekly.\n- **Put guardrails in the gateway:** per-key spend limits, model allowlists, default output caps.\n- **Estimate cost in design review:** expected tokens per task times expected volume, so cost is discussed\n  before the code exists.\n- **Mature gradually.** The FinOps Foundation describes it as crawl, walk, run: a daily report first, then\n  budgets and showback, then forecasts, anomaly detection and chargeback.\n\n### What is specific to AI spend\n- **Tokens are driven by prompt design,** so cost is a code-review concern.\n- **Agents multiply spend:** a ten-step loop is ten calls, and context grows with every step.\n- **Evaluation is real spend:** a large eval suite run against a judge model on every pull request can\n  rival production traffic.\n- **Cached tokens are priced differently,** so cache hit rate is a cost KPI.\n- **For self-hosted models the waste metric is GPU idle time,** which belongs on the same dashboard as token\n  spend.","example":"### Gateway-side recorder with a burn-rate review\nA minimal gateway-side recorder and a daily review that forecasts on burn rate. Prices are placeholders;\nin a real system they are loaded from the provider's current price list and versioned.\n\n```python\nimport time, uuid\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nPRICES = {  # USD per 1M tokens, illustrative; \"in\" = uncached input tokens\n    \"large-model\": {\"in\": 3.00, \"out\": 15.00, \"cached_in\": 0.30},\n    \"small-model\": {\"in\": 0.15, \"out\": 0.60,  \"cached_in\": 0.015},\n}\nEVENTS = []\n\ndef record_call(model, usage, *, tenant, feature, env, prompt_version):\n    p = PRICES[model]\n    cost = (usage[\"in\"] * p[\"in\"] + usage[\"out\"] * p[\"out\"] + usage[\"cached_in\"] * p[\"cached_in\"]) / 1e6\n    event = {\"ts\": time.time(), \"id\": str(uuid.uuid4()), \"model\": model, **usage, \"cost_usd\": cost,\n             \"tenant\": tenant, \"feature\": feature, \"env\": env, \"prompt_version\": prompt_version,\n             \"price_version\": \"2026-10\"}   # store the price used so history stays reproducible\n    EVENTS.append(event)\n    return event\n\ndef daily_review(events, budgets_usd, day_of_month, days_in_month=30):\n    spend = defaultdict(float)\n    for e in events:\n        spend[e[\"feature\"]] += e[\"cost_usd\"]\n    for feature, budget in budgets_usd.items():\n        mtd = spend[feature]\n        forecast = mtd / day_of_month * days_in_month        # burn-rate forecast, not a threshold\n        flag = \"OVER-FORECAST\" if forecast > budget else \"ok\"\n        print(f\"{feature:<10} mtd=${mtd:8.2f}  forecast=${forecast:8.2f}  budget=${budget:6.0f}  {flag}\")\n\n# Illustrative traffic after a retrieval change doubled the context sent by 'search'\nfor _ in range(250_000):\n    record_call(\"small-model\", {\"in\": 2400, \"out\": 150, \"cached_in\": 800},\n                tenant=\"acme\", feature=\"search\", env=\"prod\", prompt_version=\"v12\")\nfor _ in range(50_000):\n    record_call(\"large-model\", {\"in\": 1200, \"out\": 300, \"cached_in\": 0},\n                tenant=\"acme\", feature=\"summarize\", env=\"prod\", prompt_version=\"v3\")\ndaily_review(EVENTS, {\"search\": 300, \"summarize\": 1500}, day_of_month=10)\n# search     mtd=$  115.50  forecast=$  346.50  budget=$   300  OVER-FORECAST\n# summarize  mtd=$  405.00  forecast=$ 1215.00  budget=$  1500  ok\n```\n\n- **The first line is the point.** On day ten search has spent only $115 of its $300 budget, so a\n  threshold alert would stay silent for two more weeks; the burn-rate forecast flags it today.\n- **Tags make the diagnosis one query.** Every event carries `prompt_version` and token counts, so tokens\n  per call by prompt version shows that `v12` doubled input tokens. The fix is a prompt rollback, not a\n  budget increase.\n- **The simulation flatters the month-to-date forecast.** It runs all ten days at the new rate. If `v12`\n  shipped on day eight, the average would mix in seven cheaper days and understate the overrun; a\n  trailing-days forecast shows it in full (next example).\n- **Where the money is:** each search call costs about $0.00046, and 372 of its 462 micro-dollars are input\n  tokens, so context size, not output length, is the lever for that feature.\n\n### Worked scenario: month-to-date vs trailing-days forecast\nIllustrative: search costs $10 a day until `v12` ships on day eight, then $25 a day. The budget is $500.\n\n```python\ndef forecasts(daily, days_in_month=30, trailing=3):\n    day = len(daily)\n    mtd = sum(daily)\n    avg_forecast = mtd / day * days_in_month                     # month-to-date average\n    recent = sum(daily[-trailing:]) / trailing                    # trailing-days rate\n    recent_forecast = mtd + recent * (days_in_month - day)\n    return mtd, avg_forecast, recent_forecast\n\ndaily = [10.0] * 7 + [25.0] * 3          # day 10, step change on day 8\nmtd, avg_f, recent_f = forecasts(daily)\nprint(f\"mtd=${mtd:.0f}  avg forecast=${avg_f:.0f}  trailing-3 forecast=${recent_f:.0f}\")\n# mtd=$145  avg forecast=$435  trailing-3 forecast=$645\n```\n\n| Forecast on day 10 | Value | Against $500 budget |\n|---|---|---|\n| Month-to-date average × 30 | $435 | ok, alert stays silent |\n| Spent so far + trailing-3 rate × 20 remaining days | $645 | over, alert fires today |\n\nRun both and alert on either: the average resists noise, the trailing window catches step changes.","patterns":["**Gateway as the only egress, tags mandatory.** When: from the first shared key. How: route every provider call through one gateway or SDK wrapper, issue per-team or per-feature keys, and reject or quarantine calls missing tenant and feature tags. Monitor the unallocated share of spend weekly.","**Monthly invoice reconciliation.** When: every billing cycle. How: sum metered `cost_usd` per provider and compare with the invoice line. Monitor the gap as a KPI; a rising gap means a bypass path or a stale price version.","**Two-window forecast alerts.** When: any feature with a budget. How: compute both the month-to-date burn-rate forecast and a trailing-three-days forecast per feature, as in the example above, and alert if either exceeds budget. Monitor forecast error at month end.","**Tokens-per-task anomaly detection.** When: daily, per feature and prompt version. How: compare each day's tokens per task with a trailing baseline for the same feature and flag large ratios. Monitor time from anomaly to detection.","**Hard caps for non-production, soft limits for production.** When: dev, eval and load-test keys. How: per-key spend limits in the gateway for non-production keys; forecast alerts plus rate limiting for customer-facing keys. Monitor non-production spend as its own line with an owner.","**Cost estimate as a design-review field.** When: every new feature. How: expected tokens per task times expected volume times the current price table, reviewed again after launch against metered numbers."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"App or agent call\"] --> G[\"AI gateway: key, tags, limits\"]\n    G --> P[\"Provider or GPU pool\"]\n    G --> E[\"Usage event with price version\"]\n    E --> W[(\"Warehouse\")]\n    I[\"Provider invoice\"] -->|\"monthly\"| R[\"Reconcile: gap is a KPI\"]\n    W --> R\n    W --> U[\"Showback and unit cost\"]\n    W --> F[\"Forecasts and anomaly alerts\"]\n    U --> O[\"Owner weekly review\"]\n    F --> O\n    O -->|\"usage or rate lever\"| A","caption":"Every call passes one gateway that tags and prices it; the warehouse feeds reconciliation, unit-cost\nviews and alerts, and owners close the loop with usage or rate changes."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between showback and chargeback, and when do you move from one to the other?","a":"- **Showback** shows each team its own attributed spend; **chargeback** actually debits internal budgets.\n- **Move only once allocation is trusted,** meaning the unallocated bucket is small and shared-cost rules\n  are written down. Chargeback on shaky numbers produces arguments about the numbers instead of decisions\n  about the spend."},{"q":"Thirty teams call three LLM providers with a handful of shared keys. How would you make spend attributable?","a":"- **One chokepoint:** route every call through an AI gateway or SDK wrapper, with per-team or per-feature\n  keys and mandatory tenant, feature, environment and prompt-version tags.\n- **Price at record time:** compute cost from a versioned price table using the provider's usage fields,\n  and store the price version with each event.\n- **Land it with business data:** events go to the warehouse; reconcile monthly against each invoice and\n  normalize sources into one schema (FOCUS) once GPU and SaaS spend join in."},{"q":"When would you not use hard spend caps?","a":"- **On customer-facing production paths,** because a cap that trips mid-month takes the feature down for\n  customers, which usually costs more than the overspend.\n- **Use forecast alerts plus rate limiting there instead,** with an owner who can act the same day.\n- **Keep hard caps for development, eval and load-test keys,** where an outage costs little and runaway\n  experiments are common."},{"q":"Metered spend for the month is $38,000 but the invoice says $45,000. What does the gap tell you?","a":"- **About 16% of spend is unexplained,** which makes every per-feature number suspect until it is closed.\n- **Likely causes:** a call path that bypasses the gateway (a script with a personal key, an eval job), a\n  stale price in the table, or token counts estimated locally instead of read from usage fields.\n- **Track the gap as a KPI** and drive it toward zero before introducing chargeback."},{"q":"How would you split a $30,000-a-month self-hosted GPU pool across three features?","a":"- **By the driver that causes the cost:** GPU-seconds or tokens served per feature, weighting decode tokens\n  above prefill tokens.\n- **Not evenly:** a feature using most of the GPU-seconds should carry most of the cost; an even split\n  subsidizes it and hides an unprofitable tenant or feature.\n- **Amortize any reservation monthly** over its term, and write the rule down so disputes end."}],"tradeoffs":["**Gateway chokepoint: complete attribution and enforcement vs one more hop.** It adds latency and a single point of failure; the trade flips when teams need provider features the gateway cannot pass through.","**Chargeback: real accountability vs needing allocation that teams trust.** Until allocation is nearly complete, showback produces better decisions with fewer arguments.","**Hard per-key caps: stop runaway bills vs outages.** They can take a customer-facing feature down mid-month. Soft forecast alerts plus rate limiting suit production paths; hard caps suit development and evaluation keys.","**Commitments (reserved GPUs, provisioned throughput): lower unit price vs locked volume.** They pay off only when the baseline is predictable for the whole term.","**Fine-grained tags vs call-site discipline.** Tenant, feature and prompt-version tags answer \"which customer is unprofitable\" but demand discipline on every call site; coarse tags are cheap and answer almost nothing."],"pitfalls":["**One shared API key for the whole company.** Symptom: finance asks who spent the money and nobody can answer beyond \"the AI team\". Fix: per-team keys and mandatory tags at the gateway.","**Alerting only on a monthly threshold.** Symptom: an alert fires on the 28th, after the budget is gone and before anyone can react. Fix: forecast alerts.","**Reporting total spend instead of unit cost.** Symptom: a growth month triggers a panic cost cut that damages the product. Fix: lead every review with cost per successful task.","**Hard-coding prices and never updating them.** Symptom: an internal dashboard disagrees with the invoice by a wide margin and loses everyone's trust. Fix: a versioned price table and monthly reconciliation.","**Ignoring non-production spend.** Symptom: production explains half the bill while evals, load tests and developer experiments own the rest with no owner. Fix: separate keys, a budget and an owner per environment.","**Splitting a shared GPU pool or platform cost evenly.** Symptom: a margin report where every tenant looks equally profitable and the heavy tenant's growth never shows up as a cost. Fix: allocate by a written driver such as GPU-seconds.","**Agents without step and token budgets.** Symptom: a single stuck loop becomes the largest line item of the day. Fix: a max-steps and max-tokens limit per run, enforced in code."],"when":"- **Start** the day there is a bill worth caring about: for most teams the first month the API invoice\n  exceeds a few hundred dollars, or the first GPU reservation.\n- **Minimum viable version:** one structured event per call with tags, a versioned price table and a daily\n  report by feature. Budgets, forecasts, anomaly detection and showback follow as spend grows; chargeback\n  comes last.\n- **Measure the practice itself:** percentage of spend allocated, cost per successful task by feature,\n  cache hit rate, GPU utilization, forecast error and time from anomaly to detection.\n- **Day to day:** a cost column in the trace viewer, a cost estimate in design reviews and a weekly\n  ten-minute review.\n- **Interviews:** explain the loop, why attribution must happen at the gateway, the usage-versus-rate\n  distinction, and why unit cost rather than total spend is the headline.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments every call with tags and token counts, keeps the price table versioned, adds step and token budgets to agents, and treats a tokens-per-task regression like a latency regression.","architect":"Owns the gateway as the enforcement point, the allocation model, the budget structure and the policy that no feature ships without a cost estimate and an owner.","fde":"Sets up per-tenant metering and showback inside the customer's environment, and turns the first surprising invoice into a cost-per-task conversation about margin rather than a cancellation."},"check":{"q":"The monthly LLM bill jumped from $20,000 to $45,000 and leadership wants a 30% cut by Friday. What\ndo you do first, and why might cutting be the wrong move?","a":"Attribute before acting. Break the $25,000 delta down by feature, tenant, model and prompt version,\nand compute tokens per task and cost per successful task before and after the jump. Three outcomes are\npossible. If volume grew and unit cost is flat, the increase is growth, and the right response is to show\nmargin per task and leave the feature alone. If tokens per task jumped in one feature, it is a regression,\ntypically a retrieval change, a prompt edit or an agent loop retrying, and fixing it recovers most of the\ndelta without touching anything else. If the model or price mix changed, the levers are routing traffic\nback to cheaper models (usage) and committed-use pricing or a tier change (rate). Then install the guardrails that were missing: per-feature budgets\nwith burn-rate forecasts, daily anomaly alerts on tokens per task, per-key limits and a cost estimate in\ndesign review, so the next jump is caught in a day rather than on the invoice."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must token and cost attribution happen in your own gateway rather than from the provider's invoice?","options":["Because provider invoices arrive too late to be useful","Because the invoice carries none of your context: tenant, feature, environment or prompt version","Because providers round costs to the nearest dollar","Because gateways compute costs faster than providers do"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The provider sees API keys and tokens, not your product structure. Only the code that makes the call knows which customer and feature it serves, so the tags have to be attached there and the cost computed alongside them."},{"q":"Which of these is a usage-optimization lever rather than a rate-optimization lever?","options":["Signing a committed-use discount with the provider","Negotiating enterprise pricing for the large model","Trimming the prompt and capping output length so each task consumes fewer tokens","Reserving GPU instances for a one-year term"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rate levers change the price paid per unit and belong to procurement. Trimming and capping reduce the number of billable units consumed, which is an engineering change to usage; keeping the two apart shows which lever is working."},{"q":"Total spend doubled this month while cost per successful task stayed flat. What is the FinOps reading?","options":["Waste doubled and spend should be cut immediately","Usage doubled; check margin per task and whether the growth is wanted, rather than cutting","Provider prices doubled without notice","Cost attribution is broken and the numbers cannot be trusted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Flat unit cost with doubled total means twice as many successful tasks were delivered at the same efficiency. That is growth, and the decision belongs to product and pricing, not to a cost cut that would reduce the value delivered."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/tco","sources":[],"why":"- **Decisions get framed by the most visible price:** dollars per million tokens, per GPU hour, per seat.\n  Each is real, and each describes only one slice of what the option will cost.\n- **Each headline price hides lines.** A per-token price omits the engineer who keeps prompts working when\n  the provider retires the model. A GPU hourly rate omits the serving stack, the on-call rotation and the\n  three-year hardware refresh. A per-seat SaaS price omits integration, the security review, the annual\n  price escalator and what it costs to get your data back out.\n- **The failure mode is predictable.** Option A wins on headline price; then the unwritten costs arrive as\n  \"unplanned\" work: a migration sprint when a model is deprecated, a re-embedding job when the embedding\n  model changes, a quarter of platform work to make a self-hosted cluster reliable. Six months in, the\n  cheaper option is the expensive one, and nobody can say whether the decision was wrong or just unlucky,\n  because nothing was written down.\n- **\"Add 20% for overhead\" fails** because hidden costs are not proportional to the visible one.\n  Engineering cost is roughly fixed regardless of volume, licence cost scales with seats, token cost\n  scales with usage, and migration cost arrives in lumps on someone else's schedule.\n- **What TCO buys:** all of those on one timeline, so the comparison is honest and the assumptions can be\n  checked later.","how":"### 1. Fix the outcome and the horizon\n- **Same outcome:** the same task, at the same quality bar on your evals, at the same latency and\n  availability target.\n- **Unequal quality gets priced:** if the cheaper option scores lower, add the cost of closing the gap\n  (fine-tuning, a reranker, human review) or express both in cost per successful task.\n- **Horizon is usually three years,** because one-off build costs amortize over it and it matches the\n  longest common committed-use term (hardware itself is often depreciated over three to five years).\n- **Hosted-API exception:** a horizon longer than the model generation you build on is fiction, so keep it\n  short and model the migration as a cost inside it.\n\n### 2. Enumerate costs in four buckets\n| Bucket | What goes in |\n|---|---|\n| **Acquire** (one-off) | build effort; integration with identity, data and logging systems; procurement and security review; building the eval set; initial data preparation and indexing; hardware purchase |\n| **Operate** (recurring) | tokens or GPU hours, licences, vector database and storage, observability, network egress, support contracts, and the fractional people who run it, carry the pager and answer user tickets |\n| **Operate, on-prem extras** | power and cooling (facility power is roughly IT power times the site's PUE, power usage effectiveness), space, spares and hardware refresh |\n| **Change** (recurring but lumpy) | forced model migrations and the re-evaluation they need, prompt re-tuning, re-embedding a corpus when the embedding model changes (the old vectors are unusable with the new model), serving-stack upgrades, price changes at renewal |\n| **Exit** (contingent) | data export, re-integration with a replacement, contract minimums or unused commitments, parallel running during cut-over |\n\nExit is included because it bounds how locked in you are.\n\n### 3. Cost people honestly\n- **People are usually the biggest line,** and the one most often left out because it does not appear on a\n  cloud bill.\n- **Use a loaded cost per engineer-year** (salary plus benefits, overhead, tooling) and estimate fractions\n  of FTE per activity.\n- **Include opportunity cost** when the same engineers would otherwise ship product: an option that\n  consumes a platform team is not free because the team is already on payroll.\n\n### 4. Count only what the decision changes\n- **TCO for a decision is *incremental*:** include a cost only if it differs between the options from today\n  onward.\n- **Sunk costs belong in neither column.** GPUs bought last year or a prototype already built are gone\n  whichever option you pick, *because* the money is spent; \"we already paid for the cluster\" argues for\n  using it only through its resale or opportunity value.\n- **Shared costs that exist either way cancel out** (the identity system, the data warehouse), so leave\n  them out of both, which keeps the model small.\n- **What remains** is the money and people the choice actually commits.\n\n### 5. Put it on a timeline and discount it\n- **Lay costs out by period.** Capital purchases are depreciated over their useful life for accounting, but\n  for a decision compare cash flows.\n- **Discount future cash** at a rate your finance team provides: `NPV = Σ_t cost_t / (1 + r)^t`.\n- **Why discounting matters:** options differ in timing. A build is front-loaded, a growing subscription is\n  back-loaded, and undiscounted totals overstate the back-loaded one.\n\n### 6. Model uncertainty as scenarios\n1. **Run a base case** rather than trusting one number; volume, adoption, provider price moves and model\n   deprecation dates are all uncertain.\n2. **Vary the two or three assumptions that move the answer most** (adoption, price, team size).\n3. **Attach expected costs to risk events** as probability times impact.\n4. **Report a range** and a statement of which assumption flips the decision.\n\n### How it relates to its neighbours\n| Neighbour | Relationship to TCO |\n|---|---|\n| Break-even analysis | one slice of TCO along the volume axis |\n| Cost modeling | supplies the per-request operate costs |\n| Unit economics | divides TCO by successful outcomes |\n| FinOps | checks the TCO model against actual spend once the option is live |","example":"### Build on an API or buy SaaS?\nBuild a contract-review assistant on a hosted LLM API, or buy a vendor SaaS product priced per seat?\nEvery figure below is illustrative, in thousands of dollars.\n\n```python\nFTE, RATE = 200, 0.10               # loaded engineer-year; discount rate from finance\n\ndef build_on_api(users):\n    upfront = 1.0 * FTE + 30        # 2 engineers x 6 months, plus building the eval set\n    run = [u * 0.12                 # tokens at $120 per active user-year\n           + 0.75 * FTE             # maintenance, on-call, prompt upkeep\n           + 24                     # vector DB, observability, gateway\n           + 40                     # one forced model migration and re-eval per year\n           for u in users]\n    return [upfront] + run\n\ndef buy_saas(users, seat=0.36, escalator=0.07):\n    upfront = 60                    # integration, SSO, security review\n    run = [u * seat * (1 + escalator) ** y + 0.25 * FTE for y, u in enumerate(users)]\n    return [upfront] + run          # licence with annual escalator, plus admin time\n\ndef tco(flows, rate=0.0):\n    return sum(c / (1 + rate) ** t for t, c in enumerate(flows))\n\nfor label, users in ((\"growth\", [500, 1000, 1500]), (\"flat\", [500, 500, 500])):\n    b, s = build_on_api(users), buy_saas(users)\n    print(f\"{label}: build {tco(b):.0f} (NPV {tco(b, RATE):.0f})  buy {tco(s):.0f} (NPV {tco(s, RATE):.0f})\")\n# growth: build 1232 (NPV 1051)  buy 1393 (NPV 1131)\n# flat: build 1052 (NPV 911)  buy 789 (NPV 662)\n```\n\nWhat the numbers say:\n- **Tokens are a minority of build cost.** Even in year three they are $180k of $394k, and over the three\n  years the 0.75 FTE of upkeep ($450k) outweighs tokens ($360k). A 20% token discount would save about\n  $72k; trimming upkeep by a quarter of an FTE would save $150k.\n- **The decision flips on adoption.** Per-seat pricing scales with users while the build is mostly fixed,\n  so build wins if usage triples and buy wins clearly if it stays flat. Adoption is the assumption to test\n  before committing.\n- **Discounting halves build's advantage in the growth case** ($161k undiscounted, $80k NPV), because the\n  subscription's large payments come late.\n- **A strong write-up goes further:** add exit cost to the buy side and the option value of reusing the\n  built pipeline for a second feature, then say which one the recommendation depends on.\n\n### Worked scenario: find the assumption that flips it\nAppend this to the code above (it reuses `tco`, `buy_saas`, `build_on_api` and `RATE`) to sweep the two assumptions most likely to be wrong: year-three adoption\nand the vendor's seat price.\n\n```python\nfor y3 in range(500, 2001, 250):                       # year-3 active users, linear ramp from 500\n    users = [500, (500 + y3) / 2, y3]\n    gap = tco(buy_saas(users), RATE) - tco(build_on_api(users), RATE)\n    print(f\"year-3 users {y3:>5}: buy minus build NPV = {gap:+6.0f}k -> {'build' if gap > 0 else 'buy'}\")\nfor seat in (0.30, 0.36, 0.45):                         # seat price, thousands per user-year\n    users = [500, 1000, 1500]\n    gap = tco(buy_saas(users, seat=seat), RATE) - tco(build_on_api(users), RATE)\n    print(f\"seat ${seat*1000:.0f}/yr: buy minus build NPV = {gap:+6.0f}k\")\n```\n\n| Year-3 users | Buy minus build (NPV, $k) | Cheaper |\n|---|---|---|\n| 500 (flat) | −249 | buy |\n| 1,000 | −85 | buy |\n| 1,250 | −3 | buy, a tie |\n| 1,500 | +80 | build |\n| 2,000 | +244 | build |\n\n- **The flip sits near 1,250 year-three users,** so the recommendation is really a bet on adoption; a\n  pilot that measures it is worth more than refining any other input.\n- **Seat price matters too:** at $300 a seat-year buy wins by $78k in the growth case; at $450 build wins\n  by $316k. Negotiating price before deciding can change the answer.","patterns":["**TCO as a decision record.** When: any commitment longer than a few months. How: a short document with the outcome, horizon, the four buckets, each assumption with its owner, and the flip assumption named. Monitor: re-open it at renewal or when a provider changes price or retires a model.","**Track change cost as a measured line.** When: from the first model migration. How: log engineer-days for each model swap, re-evaluation and re-embedding job, and use the real average in the next TCO. Monitor migration frequency and days per migration.","**Run an exit drill before signing.** When: buying SaaS or committing to a provider. How: test a full export of documents, embeddings, logs and configuration, and price the re-integration effort and any contract minimums. Monitor that the export still works at each renewal.","**Commit to the low scenario, not the forecast.** When: signing committed-use or reserved capacity. How: size the commitment to the bottom of the adoption range and buy the rest on demand. Monitor commitment utilization monthly.","**Show risk events in their own column.** When: presenting to stakeholders. How: list each event (a deprecation, a price rise at renewal, a forced exit) as probability times impact, separate from firm costs, so a reader can disagree with a probability without discarding the model.","**Reconcile with FinOps actuals.** When: quarterly after launch. How: compare the operate and change lines with metered spend and logged effort, and update the assumptions that drifted most."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Fix outcome, quality bar, horizon\"] --> B[\"Acquire costs\"]\n    A --> C[\"Operate costs\"]\n    A --> D[\"Change costs\"]\n    A --> E[\"Exit costs\"]\n    B --> F[\"Drop sunk and shared costs\"]\n    C --> F\n    D --> F\n    E --> F\n    F --> G[\"Timeline and NPV\"]\n    G --> H[\"Scenarios on top assumptions\"]\n    H --> I[\"Range plus flip assumption\"]\n    I -.->|\"FinOps actuals at renewal\"| A","caption":"Follow the steps top to bottom; the loop at the end is FinOps actuals correcting the assumptions at\nrenewal."},"interview":[{"q":"What does TCO include beyond the invoice, and why does \"add 20% for overhead\" not work?","a":"- **Four buckets:** acquire (build, integration, security review, eval set), operate (tokens or GPUs plus\n  the people who run it), change (forced migrations, re-embedding, upgrades) and exit (export,\n  re-integration, unused commitments).\n- **A flat uplift fails** because these lines scale differently: people are roughly fixed, licences scale\n  with seats, tokens with usage, and migrations arrive in lumps on the provider's schedule."},{"q":"A manager says self-hosting is nearly free because \"we already own the GPUs\". How do you respond?","a":"- **The purchase is sunk:** it is spent whichever option is chosen, so it belongs in neither column.\n- **What still counts is incremental:** power, space, spares, the people to run and upgrade the serving\n  stack, and the quality gap against the hosted option.\n- **The GPUs' real value is their opportunity value,** what they could earn by being resold or used for\n  another workload, and that is the number to put in the comparison."},{"q":"When is a full TCO model not worth building?","a":"- **For cheap, reversible decisions:** a pilot that can be switched off in weeks costs less to run than to\n  model, so run it and measure.\n- **When one constraint decides it anyway,** such as data that cannot leave a region; still write a short\n  estimate so the premium being paid is known.\n- **Rule of thumb from practice:** the full model pays off once commitments exceed a quarter or so."},{"q":"Why discount, and what is a $100k cost in year three worth today at 10%?","a":"- **Options differ in timing:** builds are front-loaded and subscriptions back-loaded, so undiscounted\n  totals overstate the back-loaded option.\n- **The arithmetic:** `NPV = Σ_t cost_t / (1 + r)^t`, so $100k / 1.1³ ≈ $100k / 1.331 ≈ $75k.\n- **The rate is a finance input,** and a high rate makes deferring cost look better than it is, so show\n  results at more than one rate if the decision is close."},{"q":"How do you choose the horizon for a product built on a hosted model API?","a":"- **Default is three years,** because build costs amortize and it matches common commitment terms.\n- **But do not run past the model generation you build on;** keep the horizon short and model at least one\n  forced migration and re-evaluation as a cost inside it.\n- **Then treat migration frequency as a scenario input,** measured from how long the last swap actually\n  took."}],"tradeoffs":["**Full model vs one-page estimate.** A full TCO model exposes hidden costs but takes days to build; for a small, reversible decision a one-page estimate is enough, and the full model pays off only when commitments exceed a quarter or so.","**Longer horizon: amortizes build cost vs outlives the model.** Longer horizons favour building, but in AI they stretch past the life of the current model generation, so assumptions beyond about two years become guesses.","**Discounting: fairer timing comparison vs a finance-chosen rate.** It compares front-loaded and back-loaded options fairly, yet a high rate can make deferring cost look better than it is.","**Risk-weighted exit and migration costs: visible lock-in vs judgement calls.** Probabilities are judgement, so present them separately from firm costs instead of folding them in silently.","**Committed-use and reserved pricing: lower unit cost vs fixed obligation.** It pays only if utilization materializes, and flips to a loss when adoption undershoots."],"pitfalls":["**Comparing the invoice line only,** such as tokens versus GPU hours. Symptom: a \"cheaper\" option whose first quarter is consumed by unplanned platform and migration work. Fix: fill all four buckets.","**Leaving out people because they are \"already on payroll\".** Symptom: roadmap features slip while the team keeps the self-hosted or custom-built stack alive. Fix: FTE fractions at loaded cost, plus opportunity cost.","**Comparing options at unequal quality.** Symptom: a cheap option that needs a human review queue, or a fine-tuning project, that nobody costed. Fix: price the gap or compare cost per successful task.","**Ignoring change costs** such as model deprecations and embedding-model upgrades. Symptom: an urgent re-embedding job or migration sprint arrives on the provider's schedule rather than yours. Fix: budget at least one migration per horizon from logged past effort.","**Presenting one number with false precision.** Symptom: when one assumption, usually adoption, turns out wrong, stakeholders lose trust in the whole analysis instead of updating the input that moved. Fix: report a range and name the flip assumption.","**Counting sunk costs as a reason to keep something.** Symptom: a business case for keeping an underused cluster that only works if its purchase price is counted as a saving.","**Never revisiting the model after launch.** Symptom: without FinOps actuals to compare against, the next TCO repeats the same blind spots."],"when":"- **Use when** a decision commits money or people for more than a few months: build versus buy, hosted API\n  versus self-hosting, choosing a vector database or serving platform, signing a committed-use contract, or\n  moving workloads on-prem.\n- **Avoid when** the experiment is cheap and reversible; run the pilot and measure instead.\n- **Measure** loaded people cost, cost per successful task, migration frequency and adoption, because those\n  dominate.\n- **Revisit** at renewal or when a provider changes price or retires a model.\n- **Interviews:** the signal is naming the non-invoice lines unprompted and saying which assumption flips\n  the recommendation.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Supplies the honest numbers: tokens per task, FTE fractions for upkeep, and how long the last model migration and re-evaluation actually took.","architect":"Owns the comparison frame: equal outcome, horizon, cost buckets, scenarios, and designing for low exit cost so the TCO's lock-in line stays small.","fde":"Builds the customer's TCO for adopting the product, including their integration, security review and change-management effort, which often decides the deal more than licence price."},"check":{"q":"A team proposes self-hosting an open-weight model because GPU cost per token is a third of the API\nprice. What would you ask before agreeing, in terms of total cost of ownership?","a":"First, where \"a third\" came from. If it is the GPU hourly price divided by benchmark throughput, it\nassumes the GPU is busy every second; bursty interactive traffic provisioned for peak with headroom often\nruns near a third utilization, which alone turns a third of the price into roughly the same price, before\nanything else is added. Then whether the comparison is at equal quality on our evals, and if not what\nclosing the gap costs. Then the operate costs beyond GPUs: fractional platform and on-call engineers at\nloaded cost, serving-stack upgrades and observability. Then\nchange costs: how often we will swap models and what each re-evaluation costs. Then exit and commitment\nrisk: reserved capacity we may not fill. I would want this over a two-to-three-year horizon with\nscenarios for volume and API price cuts, and the assumption that flips the answer named explicitly."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should two options be compared at equal quality on your evals before comparing TCO?","options":["Because vendors are required to disclose quality differences in their pricing","Because a quality gap creates real costs, such as fine-tuning or human review, that otherwise go uncounted","Because lower-quality models always use more tokens per request","Because TCO is only defined for options with identical architectures"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The cheaper option often needs extra work to reach the same outcome. Comparing at unequal quality hides that work; equalize quality or compare cost per successful task instead."},{"q":"In a build-on-API option, token spend is a small share of three-year TCO. What does that imply?","options":["Negotiating a lower token price is the most effective way to cut TCO","The option is under-utilized and should be moved to self-hosting","People and change costs dominate, so reducing upkeep effort moves the total more than token price does","Discounting should be removed because it distorts small cost lines"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Effort on a line should follow its share of the total. When upkeep FTE is the biggest line, automation and stable interfaces save more than a token discount; self-hosting would usually add people cost."},{"q":"Discounting future costs at 10% narrows the gap between a front-loaded build and a growing subscription. Why?","options":["Discounting inflates upfront costs to reflect risk","The subscription's largest payments come in later years, which discounting weights less than early ones","Discounting applies only to licence fees, not engineering costs","Discounting removes the annual price escalator from the subscription"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each future cost is divided by (1 + r)^t, so late cash counts for less. The subscription grows with adoption and escalators, so its back-loaded payments shrink most; the upfront build cost is untouched."}]}]});
